/* Pearson Edexcel GCSE Mathematics (1MA1) — room kits built by hand in a Claude Code session: written by
   Opus 5 subagents to the contract in src/kit-validator.js, every question re-solved and the lesson read against
   the specification map by a fresh Opus 5 subagent before shipping. See .claude/skills/course-builder/references/depth.md. */
module.exports = { ID: 'EDX-1MA1', KITS: {"number-structure":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"number-structure","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Every one of the three 1MA1 papers opens on number, and Number carries 22–28% of the Foundation marks. This room holds the machinery the rest of the course runs on: ordering, the four operations, priority of operations, primes and the HCF and LCM, systematic listing, powers and roots, exact answers and standard form. Paper 1 is non-calculator, so the written method and the exact form of the answer are both credited, and a bare answer can lose every method mark. Almost every later topic — ratio, area, probability — is arithmetic wearing a different hat, so a slip here costs marks in rooms that have nothing to do with number.","idea":[{"h":"Ordering numbers and the inequality symbols","t":"Order integers, decimals and fractions by first putting them all in one form, usually decimals, then comparing place value by place value. With negatives, the further a number is from zero the smaller it is, so −7/5 < −1.2. Write the comparison with =, ≠, <, >, ≤ or ≥, reading the symbol as pointing at the smaller number. Examiners ask for a list 'smallest first' or for the right symbol between two values, and a list written in the wrong direction scores nothing.","code":"N1"},{"h":"The four operations and place value","t":"Add, subtract, multiply and divide integers, decimals, proper and improper fractions and mixed numbers, positive and negative, by formal written methods. With decimals, multiply as whole numbers and then count the decimal places: 5.3 × 0.24 has three places altogether, so 1272 becomes 1.272. Subtracting a negative adds. Fractions are added over a common denominator once any mixed number has been written as an improper fraction. On Paper 1 the written method is what carries the method marks.","code":"N2"},{"h":"Inverse operations and priority of operations","t":"Brackets first, then indices (powers and roots), then division and multiplication left to right, then addition and subtraction left to right. A calculator obeys this order too, so the calculation has to be written down correctly in the first place. Each operation is undone by its inverse — + by −, × by ÷, a power by its root — which is how a division is checked and how a calculation is simplified by cancelling before it is worked out.","code":"N3"},{"h":"Primes, factors, multiples, HCF and LCM","t":"A prime has exactly two factors, itself and 1. Every integer above 1 has one prime factorisation and no other — the unique factorisation theorem — written in product notation, for example 720 = 2⁴ × 3² × 5. From two factorisations the HCF is the product of the lowest power of each shared prime and the LCM the product of the highest power of every prime that appears. Exam questions hide these behind contexts: identical bags or rows want the HCF, two events meeting again want the LCM.","code":"N4"},{"h":"Systematic listing strategies","t":"To count every possibility without missing or repeating one, hold one choice fixed and run through the next in a set order, then move the fixed choice on: cheese–tea–flapjack, cheese–tea–brownie, cheese–juice–flapjack, and so on. The marks are for the system, not for the total alone, so the list itself is written out and its order is visible. An unordered scribble that happens to reach the right total is not a complete answer.","code":"N5"},{"h":"Positive integer powers and real roots","t":"A power is repeated multiplication and a root undoes it: ∛64 = 4 because 4³ = 64. Recognise the powers of 2 (2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128), of 3 (3, 9, 27, 81, 243), of 4 (4, 16, 64, 256) and of 5 (5, 25, 125, 625) on sight, because on Paper 1 a question expects them to be written down rather than worked out. The squares up to 15² = 225 and the cubes of 2, 3, 4 and 5 are worth knowing for the same reason.","code":"N6"},{"h":"Calculating with roots and integer indices","t":"Powers of the same base multiply by adding the indices and divide by subtracting them, so 7⁵ × 7³ ÷ 7⁶ = 7⁸ ÷ 7⁶ = 7². A zero index gives 1, and a negative index means the reciprocal, so 5⁻² = 1/5² = 1/25. Roots can be combined within a calculation — √72 ÷ √2 = √36 = 6 — which turns an awkward pair of roots into a whole number. The index form, not a rounded decimal, is usually what the accuracy mark is for.","code":"N7"},{"h":"Exact calculation with fractions and multiples of π","t":"An exact answer is a fraction in its lowest terms or a multiple of π, not a rounded decimal. A circle of radius 6 cm has area π × 6² = 36π cm², and 36π cm² is exact where 113.1 cm² is not. Multiples of π are handled like any other multiple: 3 × 5π = 15π. Paper 1 is non-calculator, so questions there ask for the exact form, and a decimal loses the accuracy mark even when it has been correctly rounded.","code":"N8"},{"h":"Standard form","t":"Standard form is A × 10ⁿ, where A is at least 1 and less than 10 and n is an integer; n is positive for large numbers and negative for small ones, so 0.000 000 065 = 6.5 × 10⁻⁸. To multiply, multiply the A values and add the indices, then adjust the answer back into standard form: 24 × 10¹ is rewritten as 2.4 × 10². To compare two numbers written in standard form, look at the power of 10 first and only then at A.","code":"N9"}],"examples":[{"title":"Comparing signed temperatures","setup":"A weather station logs four overnight temperatures: Monday −2.5 °C, Tuesday −9/4 °C, Wednesday 0.8 °C and Thursday −3/5 °C. Name the warmest night and the coldest night, and then write the correct symbol, < or >, between Monday's reading and Tuesday's reading.","steps":["Change each fraction into a decimal so that all four readings are in one form: −9/4 = −2.25 and −3/5 = −0.6.","Every negative reading is below every positive one, and Wednesday alone is positive, so Wednesday at 0.8 °C was the warmest night.","Of −2.5, −2.25 and −0.6, the reading furthest below zero is −2.5, so Monday was the coldest night.","Monday is −2.5 and Tuesday is −2.25, so Monday's is the smaller; the symbol points at the smaller value, giving −2.5 < −9/4."],"cues":["Are the four readings all written in the same form yet?","How does any negative reading compare with a positive one?","Which position on a thermometer counts as the coldest?","Which value does an inequality symbol point at?"]},{"title":"Checking and undoing with inverse operations","setup":"A stallholder shares a bill of £966 equally between 23 traders and writes down £42 for each of them. Use an inverse operation to decide whether £42 is right, and then find the number that makes 6 × ☐ + 9 = 87 true by undoing the operations in turn.","steps":["The inverse of dividing by 23 is multiplying by 23, so the share is tested with 42 × 23.","42 × 23 = 966, which is the whole bill, so the share of £42 each is right.","In 6 × ☐ + 9 the addition is done last, so it is undone first: 87 − 9 = 78, leaving 6 × ☐ = 78.","Undoing the multiplication as well, 78 ÷ 6 = 13, so the missing number is 13."],"cues":["How can a division be tested without dividing again?","Does the test come back to the whole bill?","Which operation is applied last in 6 × ☐ + 9?","Which operation is still left to undo?"]},{"title":"Prime factorisation, HCF and LCM","setup":"Two numbers are 90 and 126. Without a calculator, write each of them as a product of powers of its prime factors, then use those two factorisations to work out the highest common factor and the lowest common multiple of the pair.","steps":["Divide 90 by primes in turn: 90 = 2 × 45 = 2 × 3 × 15 = 2 × 3 × 3 × 5, so 90 = 2 × 3² × 5.","Do the same for the other: 126 = 2 × 63 = 2 × 3 × 21 = 2 × 3 × 3 × 7, so 126 = 2 × 3² × 7.","HCF: take the lowest power of each prime that is in both lists, 2 and 3², so the HCF = 2 × 9 = 18.","LCM: take the highest power of every prime that appears, 2, 3², 5 and 7, so the LCM = 2 × 9 × 5 × 7 = 630."],"cues":["Which prime is worth trying first on an even number?","Which primes would you expect this second number to share?","For a common factor, is the lower or the higher power needed?","For a common multiple, which primes must be included?"]},{"title":"Listing every possibility in order","setup":"A café meal deal is one sandwich chosen from cheese, ham and tuna, one drink chosen from tea and juice, and one cake chosen from a flapjack and a brownie. By listing the possible deals in a systematic order, work out how many different meal deals there are, and how many of them contain juice.","steps":["Hold cheese and tea, and vary the cake: cheese–tea–flapjack, cheese–tea–brownie.","Keep cheese and move the drink on: cheese–juice–flapjack, cheese–juice–brownie, so cheese gives 4 deals.","Repeat the same four for ham: ham–tea–flapjack, ham–tea–brownie, ham–juice–flapjack, ham–juice–brownie.","Tuna gives four in the same way, so the total is 4 + 4 + 4 = 12 different meal deals.","Juice appears in 2 of the 4 deals for each sandwich, so 2 + 2 + 2 = 6 deals contain juice."],"cues":["Which choice is being held still while another varies?","What has to change next once the cakes are used up?","Why will ham produce the same number as cheese?","How many sandwiches are there to account for?","In each sandwich's list, how many deals had juice?"]},{"title":"Indices and roots without a calculator","setup":"Without a calculator, work out the value of 2⁴ × 2³ ÷ 2⁵ as a whole number, and also the value of √72 ÷ √2.","steps":["The multiplication adds the indices: 2⁴ × 2³ = 2⁷.","The division subtracts the index: 2⁷ ÷ 2⁵ = 2⁷⁻⁵ = 2², which is 4.","For the roots, combine them into a single root: √72 ÷ √2 = √(72 ÷ 2).","Divide inside the root: 72 ÷ 2 = 36, and √36 = 6."],"cues":["What happens to the indices when powers of one base multiply?","And what happens to the indices when they divide?","Can a division of two roots be written as one root?","Is the number now under the root a square number?"]},{"title":"Multiplying in standard form","setup":"A single grain of sand has a mass of 6 × 10⁻⁵ kg. A bucket holds 4 × 10⁶ grains of sand. Without a calculator, work out the total mass of the sand in the bucket, giving your answer in kilograms in standard form.","steps":["Multiply the number parts: 6 × 4 = 24.","Multiply the powers of ten by adding the indices: 10⁻⁵ × 10⁶ = 10¹.","Put the two together: 24 × 10¹, and 24 is not between 1 and 10, so this is not yet standard form.","Rewrite 24 as 2.4 × 10, giving 2.4 × 10 × 10¹ = 2.4 × 10² kg."],"cues":["Which two parts of the numbers can be multiplied separately?","What do you do with indices when powers of ten multiply?","Does the front number obey the rule for standard form?","How does moving the decimal point change the index?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Compare −0.4 and −2/5 using the correct symbol.","a":"−0.4 = −2/5, because −2/5 written as a decimal is −0.4"},{"q":"Work out 20 − 4 × 3 + 2³ without a calculator.","a":"16 (2³ = 8, then 20 − 12 + 8)"},{"q":"Write 48 as a product of powers of its prime factors.","a":"2⁴ × 3"},{"q":"Write 3.6 × 10³ as an ordinary number.","a":"3600"}]},"room":{"facts":["Standard form is A × 10ⁿ with 1 ≤ A < 10 and n an integer; a number smaller than 1 has a negative n.","Laws of indices: aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ, aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ, a⁰ = 1 and a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ.","Priority of operations: brackets, then indices, then division and multiplication left to right, then addition and subtraction left to right.","HCF = the product of the lowest power of each prime the numbers share; LCM = the product of the highest power of every prime that appears in either.","Powers of 2: 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128. Powers of 3: 3, 9, 27, 81, 243. Powers of 4: 4, 16, 64, 256. Powers of 5: 5, 25, 125, 625. Cubes: 2³ = 8, 3³ = 27, 4³ = 64, 5³ = 125.","To multiply in standard form, multiply the A values, add the indices of 10, then adjust so that 1 ≤ A < 10.","Subtracting a negative adds: a − (−b) = a + b; and multiplying two negatives gives a positive."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Write these five numbers in order, starting with the smallest: −1.2, 3/5, −7/5, 0.55, 1/2","a":"−7/5, −1.2, 1/2, 0.55, 3/5","sol":"Write the fractions as decimals: 3/5 = 0.6, −7/5 = −1.4 and 1/2 = 0.5 (M1). The negatives come first and −1.4 < −1.2; then 0.5 < 0.55 < 0.6, so the order is −7/5, −1.2, 1/2, 0.55, 3/5 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Numbers are only easy to compare when they are all written in the same form.","Divide the top of each fraction by the bottom to get a decimal, then compare place value by place value.","Once everything is a decimal, the two negative values are placed first, and the one further from zero is the smaller of them."],"codes":["N1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 4.6 × 0.35","a":"1.61","sol":"Multiply as whole numbers by a formal written method: 46 × 35 = 1610 (M1). There is one decimal place in 4.6 and two in 0.35, three altogether, so 1610 becomes 1.61 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The digits of the answer do not depend on where the decimal points are.","Multiply the two numbers as whole numbers first, then decide where the decimal point belongs.","Work out 46 × 35 by a formal written method, then count how many decimal places the two original numbers have between them."],"codes":["N2"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 30 − 2 × (8 − 3)² ÷ 5","a":"20","sol":"Brackets first: 8 − 3 = 5, then the index: 5² = 25 (M1). Multiplication and division left to right: 2 × 25 = 50 and 50 ÷ 5 = 10; the subtraction is last, 30 − 10 = 20 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["There is a fixed order for these operations, and it is not simply left to right.","Brackets, then the power, then the multiplication and division left to right, and the subtraction last of all.","Evaluate the bracket first, and square the result of that before anything else happens."],"codes":["N3"]},{"q":"Write 84 as a product of powers of its prime factors. A calculator may be used.","a":"2² × 3 × 7","sol":"Divide by primes in turn: 84 = 2 × 42 = 2 × 2 × 21 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 7 (M1). Collecting the repeated prime in product notation gives 84 = 2² × 3 × 7 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Every whole number above 1 is built from primes in exactly one way.","Divide by the smallest prime that goes in exactly, then keep dividing each quotient by a prime until you reach 1.","84 is even, so begin by dividing by 2 and carry on from the quotient; collect any repeated prime as a power at the end."],"codes":["N4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out the value of 2⁵ ÷ 2³ + √81 − ∛27","a":"10","sol":"Divide the powers by subtracting the indices: 2⁵ ÷ 2³ = 2² = 4 (M1). √81 = 9 and ∛27 = 3 (B1). Adding and subtracting left to right, 4 + 9 − 3 = 10 (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The two powers share a base, so that division can be done in index form.","Divide the powers by subtracting the indices, work out each root, then add and subtract left to right.","2⁵ ÷ 2³ becomes a single power of 2; √81 asks which number squared gives 81, and ∛27 which number cubed gives 27."],"codes":["N6","N7"]},{"q":"A virus particle measures 0.000 000 12 m across. Write this measurement in standard form. A calculator may be used.","a":"1.2 × 10⁻⁷ m","sol":"The digits give a number between 1 and 10: 1.2 (M1). The decimal point has to move 7 places to the right from 0.000 000 12 to reach 1.2, and the measurement is smaller than 1, so n = −7 and the answer is 1.2 × 10⁻⁷ m (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Standard form needs exactly one non-zero digit in front of the decimal point.","Write the digits as a number between 1 and 10, then count how many places the decimal point has to move.","Count the places between the decimal point in the measurement and the position just after its first non-zero digit; a number smaller than 1 takes a negative index."],"codes":["N9"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 4⁻², giving your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms.","a":"1/16","sol":"A negative index means the reciprocal of the matching positive power: 4⁻² = 1/4² (M1). 4² = 16, so 4⁻² = 1/16 (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["A negative index does not make the answer a negative number.","A negative index means take the reciprocal of the matching positive power.","Rewrite the expression as 1 divided by the matching positive power of 4, then evaluate that power."],"codes":["N7"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Ali makes two-digit numbers using two different digits chosen from 3, 5 and 7. By listing them in a systematic order, find (a) how many such two-digit numbers there are, and (b) the largest of them.","a":"(a) 6   (b) 75","sol":"Hold the tens digit and vary the units digit: with 3 in the tens, 35 and 37 (M1). With 5 in the tens, 53 and 57; with 7 in the tens, 73 and 75 (M1). The list is complete with no repeats, so (a) there are 6 numbers and (b) the largest is 75 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Each number uses two different digits, so no digit may be repeated within it.","Hold the tens digit still and run through the possible units digits, then move the tens digit on.","Write down the numbers whose tens digit is the smallest of the three first, then those with the next tens digit, and so on."],"codes":["N5"]},{"q":"Buses to Leeds leave a stop every 12 minutes and buses to York leave the same stop every 18 minutes. Both leave together at 09:00. Work out the time at which they next leave together. A calculator may be used.","a":"09:36","sol":"They leave together again after a number of minutes that is a multiple of both gaps, so the lowest common multiple is needed (M1). 12 = 2² × 3 and 18 = 2 × 3², so the LCM = 2² × 3² = 36 minutes (A1). 09:00 plus 36 minutes is 09:36 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["They meet again at a time that is a whole number of gaps along both routes.","Find the lowest common multiple of the two gaps in minutes, then add it to the starting time.","Write each gap as a product of prime powers, then take the highest power of every prime that appears."],"codes":["N4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A circle has a radius of 7 cm. Work out its area, giving your answer as a multiple of π. The formulae sheet printed with the paper gives area of a circle = πr².","a":"49π cm²","sol":"Substitute r = 7 into the printed formula: area = π × 7² (M1). 7² = 49, so the exact area is 49π cm² (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The formulae sheet printed with the paper already gives the area of a circle.","Substitute the radius into πr² and leave π in the answer instead of multiplying it out.","Square the radius first, then write that result in front of π with the correct square unit."],"codes":["N8"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 2/5 + 1 1/4, giving your answer as a mixed number in its lowest terms.","a":"1 13/20","sol":"Write the mixed number as an improper fraction: 1 1/4 = 5/4 (B1). Use the common denominator 20: 2/5 = 8/20 and 5/4 = 25/20 (M1). 8/20 + 25/20 = 33/20 = 1 13/20 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A mixed number is much easier to add once it is written as a single fraction.","Change the mixed number to an improper fraction, then rewrite both fractions over a common denominator.","The denominators are 5 and 4, so use their lowest common multiple as the common denominator."],"codes":["N2","N8"]},{"q":"A gardener has 60 tulip bulbs and 84 daffodil bulbs. She plants them all in identical rows, every row holding the same number of tulips and the same number of daffodils, with none left over, and she wants as many rows as possible. Work out (a) the number of rows she makes and (b) the number of tulip bulbs in each row. A calculator may be used.","a":"(a) 12 rows   (b) 5 tulip bulbs","sol":"The number of rows must divide both 60 and 84 exactly, so the greatest possible number of rows is their highest common factor (M1). 60 = 2² × 3 × 5 and 84 = 2² × 3 × 7 (M1). The lowest power of each shared prime gives HCF = 2² × 3 = 12, so (a) she makes 12 rows (A1). Each row then holds 60 ÷ 12 = 5 tulip bulbs (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Every row is identical and no bulbs at all are left over.","The number of rows has to divide both totals exactly, and the greatest number that does is the highest common factor.","Write both totals as products of prime powers, then take the lowest power of each prime they share."],"codes":["N4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Ellie has a £2 coin, a 50p coin, a 20p coin and a 10p coin. She chooses exactly two of the four coins. By listing the possible pairs in a systematic order, work out (a) how many different pairs she can choose and (b) the total value of the most valuable pair.","a":"(a) 6 pairs   (b) £2.50","sol":"Hold the £2 coin and pair it with each of the others: £2 and 50p, £2 and 20p, £2 and 10p (M1). Then hold the 50p: 50p and 20p, 50p and 10p; then the 20p: 20p and 10p (M1). The list has no repeats, so (a) 3 + 2 + 1 = 6 different pairs (A1). The most valuable pair is the two largest coins, £2 + 50p = £2.50 (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Two coins are chosen from four, and the order in which she picks them up does not matter.","Hold the most valuable coin and pair it with each of the others in turn, then move on to the next coin down.","List the pairs that contain the £2 coin first, then the pairs that contain the 50p coin but not the £2 coin, and so on."],"codes":["N5","N2"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Three stars are 4.1 × 10¹³ km, 9.5 × 10¹² km and 3.8 × 10¹³ km from Earth. Work out (a) the three distances in order, nearest first, and (b) how much further away the furthest star is than the nearest, in km in standard form.","a":"(a) 9.5 × 10¹² km, 3.8 × 10¹³ km, 4.1 × 10¹³ km   (b) 3.15 × 10¹³ km","sol":"Compare the powers of 10 first: 10¹² is smaller than 10¹³, so 9.5 × 10¹² is the nearest; among the other two, 3.8 < 4.1 (M1). So (a) the order is 9.5 × 10¹² km, 3.8 × 10¹³ km, 4.1 × 10¹³ km (A1). To subtract, write both with the same power of 10: 9.5 × 10¹² = 0.95 × 10¹³ (M1). Then 4.1 × 10¹³ − 0.95 × 10¹³ = 3.15 × 10¹³ km (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["In standard form the power of 10 decides the size before the number in front of it does.","Compare the indices first, and only compare the front numbers when the indices are equal.","For the subtraction, rewrite the smaller distance so that it has the same power of 10 as the larger one."],"codes":["N9","N1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A number n is given by n = 2³ × 3² × 5. Work out (a) the value of n and (b) the smallest whole number that n must be multiplied by so that the product is a square number.","a":"(a) 360   (b) 10","sol":"Evaluate the powers: 2³ = 8 and 3² = 9, so (a) n = 8 × 9 × 5 = 360 (B1). A square number has an even index for every prime in its factorisation, and here the indices of 2 and of 5 are odd (M1). Multiplying by 2 × 5 gives 2⁴ × 3² × 5² = 3600 = 60², so (b) the smallest multiplier is 10 (A1).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["A square number is one whose square root is a whole number.","Evaluate the powers to get n, then look at whether each index in the factorisation is even or odd.","In 2³ × 3² × 5 the indices are 3, 2 and 1, and a square number needs every index to be even."],"codes":["N4","N6"]},{"q":"A shop sells pens in boxes of 48. A school orders 25 boxes at £6.40 for each box and pays £20 for delivery. Work out the cost of one pen, in pence. A calculator may be used.","a":"15p","sol":"Cost of the boxes: 25 × £6.40 = £160 (M1). Total cost including delivery: £160 + £20 = £180 (A1). Number of pens: 25 × 48 = 1200 (M1). Cost of one pen: £180 ÷ 1200 = £0.15 (M1), which is 15p (A1).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The delivery charge is paid once for the whole order, not once for each box.","Work out the cost of the boxes, add the delivery charge, then divide by the total number of pens.","Multiply the number of boxes by the price of one box; separately, multiply the number of boxes by the number of pens in a box."],"codes":["N2"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 4.05 × 10⁴ as an ordinary number.","a":"40 500"},{"q":"Work out 6 + 4 × 5²","a":"106"},{"q":"Write down the lowest common multiple of 6 and 8.","a":"24"},{"q":"Work out 2⁻³, giving your answer as a fraction.","a":"1/8"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The inequality symbols =, ≠, <, >, ≤, ≥","back":"= equals; ≠ is not equal to; < is less than; > is greater than; ≤ is less than or equal to; ≥ is greater than or equal to. The symbol always points at the smaller value.","code":"N1","open":false},{"front":"Ordering negative numbers","back":"The further a negative number lies from zero, the smaller it is, so −7 < −3 < −0.5 < 0 < 0.5. Every negative number is smaller than every positive one.","code":"N1","open":false},{"front":"Name two ways of showing that 0.6 is greater than 4/7","back":"Write both as decimals, since 4/7 = 0.571… and 0.571… < 0.6; write both over the common denominator 35, since 0.6 = 21/35 and 4/7 = 20/35; multiply out, since 0.6 × 7 = 4.2 and 4.2 > 4.","code":"N1","open":true},{"front":"Place value when multiplying decimals","back":"Multiply the digits as whole numbers, then give the answer as many decimal places as the two numbers have between them: 0.3 × 0.04 = 0.012.","code":"N2","open":false},{"front":"Subtracting a negative number","back":"Subtracting a negative is the same as adding: −7 − (−12) = −7 + 12 = 5. Two negative signs next to each other become a plus.","code":"N2","open":false},{"front":"Priority of operations (BIDMAS)","back":"Brackets, then indices (powers and roots), then division and multiplication left to right, then addition and subtraction left to right.","code":"N3","open":false},{"front":"Inverse operations","back":"Each operation is undone by its inverse: + and −, × and ÷, a power and its root. Use the inverse to check: 372 ÷ 12 = 31 because 31 × 12 = 372.","code":"N3","open":false},{"front":"Prime number","back":"A whole number greater than 1 with exactly two factors, itself and 1. The primes begin 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19. 1 is not prime.","code":"N4","open":false},{"front":"Prime factorisation and the unique factorisation theorem","back":"Every integer greater than 1 is a product of primes in exactly one way, written in product notation: 720 = 2⁴ × 3² × 5.","code":"N4","open":false},{"front":"Reading the HCF and the LCM from two prime factorisations","back":"HCF: multiply the lowest power of each prime that appears in both. LCM: multiply the highest power of every prime that appears in either.","code":"N4","open":false},{"front":"A systematic listing strategy","back":"Hold one choice fixed and vary the next in a set order, then move the fixed choice on. The list is then complete, with no gaps and no repeats.","code":"N5","open":false},{"front":"The powers and roots to recognise on sight","back":"2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128; 3, 9, 27, 81, 243; 4, 16, 64, 256; 5, 25, 125, 625. A root undoes its power: ∛64 = 4 because 4³ = 64.","code":"N6","open":false},{"front":"The laws of indices for integer indices","back":"aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ; aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ; a⁰ = 1; a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ. The base must be the same before indices may be added or subtracted.","code":"N7","open":false},{"front":"An exact answer","back":"A fraction in its lowest terms or a multiple of π, never a rounded decimal: a circle of radius 3 cm has area 9π cm², not 28.3 cm².","code":"N8","open":false},{"front":"Standard form A × 10ⁿ","back":"A is at least 1 and less than 10, and n is an integer: positive for large numbers, negative for small ones. 0.000 45 = 4.5 × 10⁻⁴.","code":"N9","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: area of a circle = πr², where r is the radius — so an exact area such as 49π cm² can be written straight from the sheet.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d is the diameter.","Those two circle results are the only things on this room's syllabus that the Foundation Exam Aid prints; everything below has to be remembered.","Remember: the laws of indices — aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ, aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ, a⁰ = 1, a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ (N7). The Exam Aid does not print them at either tier.","Remember: standard form is A × 10ⁿ with 1 ≤ A < 10 and n an integer, and to multiply you multiply the A values, add the indices of 10, then adjust A back into range (N9).","Remember: HCF = the product of the lowest power of each prime shared by the two factorisations; LCM = the product of the highest power of every prime that appears (N4).","Remember: the priority of operations — brackets, indices, division and multiplication left to right, then addition and subtraction left to right (N3).","Remember: the squares up to 15² = 225, the cubes 2³ = 8, 3³ = 27, 4³ = 64 and 5³ = 125, and the powers of 2 up to 2⁷ = 128 (N6).","Remember: when multiplying decimals the answer carries as many decimal places as the two numbers have between them, and a − (−b) = a + b (N2).","For reference, the rest of the Foundation Exam Aid — none of it needed in this room — is the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, Pythagoras' theorem, the three trigonometric ratios, the compound interest formula and P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B)."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T11:42:40Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Every answer key is correct — all 16 bank questions, the 4 exit questions, the 4 lesson checks and all 6 worked examples re-solved independently in Python, with mark tallies matching each tariff and the Formulae sheet matching the 2026 Foundation Exam Aid word for word — so the only work before publishing is to stop lesson.idea from printing the answers to questions 1, 5, 6 and 9."}}},"number-fdp":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"number-fdp","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Fractions, decimals and percentages are the working language of the rest of this course. A sale price, a share of a ratio, a rate of pay, a probability and a share of a survey are all the same move: one quantity acted on by a multiplier. This room gives you the three conversions that unlock that move — a terminating decimal into its fraction and back, a ratio into the fraction of the whole, and a fraction or percentage used as an operator. Paper 1 is taken without a calculator, so these conversions have to be done by hand and answers left exact, as a fraction in its lowest terms; on Papers 2 and 3 the same multipliers are what you key in. Get them wrong here and every later percentage, proportion and rate question goes wrong with them.","idea":[{"h":"Terminating decimals and their fractions","t":"A terminating decimal stops, so its last digit names a denominator: tenths, hundredths, thousandths. Write the digits over that place value and cancel by the highest common factor — 0.28 = 28/100 = 7/25, and 1.6 = 16/10 = 8/5. To go back, divide numerator by denominator: 9/40 = 9 ÷ 40 = 0.225. Examiners ask for this in three shapes: convert and give the fraction in its lowest terms, convert a fraction to a decimal, or order a mixed list of fractions and decimals by putting them all into the same form first. Knowing 1/2, 1/4, 3/4, 1/5, 1/8 and 1/20 by sight saves the division every time.","code":"N10"},{"h":"Fractions hiding inside a ratio","t":"A ratio counts equal parts, so a ratio a : b describes a + b parts altogether and each quantity is a fraction of that whole: 2 : 3 makes 2/5 and 3/5. This is why a ratio question and a fraction question can be answered by the same working — find the total number of parts, then take that many parts of the amount. The move also runs backwards: if 3/7 of a class walk to school, the rest are 4/7 and the ratio walk : bus is 3 : 4. Watch for part : part against part : whole, because 3 : 4 and 3/7 describe the same class.","code":"N11"},{"h":"Fractions and percentages as operators","t":"An operator is something that acts on a quantity and scales it. The word 'of' means multiply: 3/4 of 60 kg = 3/4 × 60 = 45 kg, and 15% of 240 = 0.15 × 240 = 36. A change is the same idea in one step: an increase of p% leaves you with 100% + p%, so the multiplier is 1 + p/100 (15% gives × 1.15), and a decrease leaves 100% − p%, so the multiplier is 1 − p/100 (35% gives × 0.65). One multiplication, not two steps, is what the mark scheme looks for, and by hand it is often easier as a fraction: × 0.75 is × 3/4.","code":"N12"}],"examples":[{"title":"A terminating decimal written as a fraction","setup":"A bolt is 0.28 m long. Write 0.28 as a fraction in its lowest terms.","steps":["The last digit 8 sits in the hundredths column, so 0.28 = 28/100.","Both parts divide by 4: 28 ÷ 4 = 7 and 100 ÷ 4 = 25.","So 0.28 = 7/25, and 7 and 25 share no common factor, so this is lowest terms."],"cues":["Which place value does the last digit sit in?","What is the highest common factor of 28 and 100?","Can the cancelled fraction be reduced any further?"]},{"title":"Comparing a fraction with a decimal","setup":"Two ribbons are measured in different ways. Ribbon A is 7/8 of a metre long. Ribbon B is 0.87 m long. Which ribbon is longer, and by how many metres?","steps":["Change Ribbon A into a decimal by short division: 7 ÷ 8 = 0.875.","Pad Ribbon B to the same number of decimal places: 0.87 becomes 0.870.","Digit by digit the tenths and hundredths match, but in the thousandths 5 is bigger than 0, so 0.875 is the larger value.","0.875 − 0.870 = 0.005, so Ribbon A is longer, by 0.005 m."],"cues":["Which form should both lengths be in before they are compared?","Why is it safer to give both lengths the same number of decimal places?","Which column decides the comparison here?","How is the gap between two decimals found?"]},{"title":"From a ratio to a fraction of the whole","setup":"A fruit bowl holds only apples and pears, in the ratio 4 : 5. There are 36 pieces of fruit in the bowl altogether. What fraction of the fruit are apples, and how many apples are there?","steps":["Add the parts: 4 + 5 = 9, so the fruit is shared into 9 equal parts.","Apples take 4 of those 9 parts, so the fraction that are apples is 4/9, and that fraction acts on the 36 pieces of fruit.","One part is 36 ÷ 9 = 4 pieces of fruit, so 4/9 of the bowl is 4 × 4 = 16 apples."],"cues":["How many equal parts does this ratio describe?","How many of those parts are apples, and what does that fraction act on?","How many pieces of fruit make up one part?"]},{"title":"A percentage increase in one multiplication","setup":"A train ticket costs £48. The price is to be increased by 15%. Use a multiplier to work out the new price.","steps":["An increase of 15% keeps the whole 100% and adds 15% on top, so the multiplier is 1 + 15/100 = 1.15.","New price = old price × multiplier = 48 × 1.15.","48 × 1.15 = 48 + 4.80 + 2.40 = 55.20, so the new price is £55.20."],"cues":["What percentage of the old price is the new price?","Which single calculation now gives the new price?","What are 10% and 5% of £48?"]},{"title":"A percentage decrease done as a fraction","setup":"A coat costs £84. In a sale, every price is reduced by 25%. Work out the sale price, using a multiplier written as a fraction in its lowest terms.","steps":["Taking 25% off leaves 100% − 25% = 75% of the price, so the multiplier is 0.75.","As a fraction 0.75 = 75/100 = 3/4 after dividing both parts by 25, so the sale price is 84 × 3/4.","84 ÷ 4 = 21 and 21 × 3 = 63, so the sale price is £63."],"cues":["What percentage of the price does the shopper actually pay?","How could this multiplier be written so the arithmetic is easy by hand?","What is one quarter of £84?"]},{"title":"From a fraction back to a ratio","setup":"In a class of 28 students, 3/7 walk to school and the rest travel by bus. Write the number who walk to the number who travel by bus as a ratio in its simplest form, and work out how many students are in each group.","steps":["Subtract from the whole: 7/7 − 3/7 = 4/7 do not walk, giving walk : bus = 3 : 4.","One seventh of the class is 28 ÷ 7 = 4 students, so 3/7 of 28 = 4 × 3 = 12 walk.","28 − 12 = 16 travel by bus, and 12 : 16 divided through by 4 is 3 : 4, which agrees with the ratio."],"cues":["What fraction of the class does not walk?","How many students make up one seventh of the class?","How can the two group sizes be used to check the ratio?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write 0.4 as a fraction in its lowest terms.","a":"2/5"},{"q":"Write 7/20 as a decimal.","a":"0.35"},{"q":"Counters are red : blue in the ratio 3 : 5. What fraction of the counters are blue?","a":"5/8"},{"q":"Work out 2/3 of 54 m.","a":"36 m"},{"q":"Write down the single multiplier that increases an amount by 22%.","a":"1.22"}]},"room":{"facts":["Decimal to fraction: put the digits over the place value of the last digit, then cancel — 0.28 = 28/100 = 7/25.","Fraction to decimal: divide the numerator by the denominator — 9/40 = 9 ÷ 40 = 0.225.","A ratio a : b has a + b equal parts, so the first quantity is a/(a + b) of the whole and the second is b/(a + b).","A fraction of the whole gives a ratio: if a/b have the property, property : rest = a : (b − a).","'Of' means multiply: a/b of Q = a/b × Q, and p% of Q = (p/100) × Q.","Multiplier for an increase of p% is 1 + p/100; for a decrease of p% it is 1 − p/100 (a 35% decrease is × 0.65).","Equivalents to know by sight: 1/2 = 0.5, 1/4 = 0.25, 3/4 = 0.75, 1/5 = 0.2, 1/8 = 0.125, 3/8 = 0.375, 1/20 = 0.05, 1/25 = 0.04."],"questions":[{"q":"Write 1.6 as an improper fraction in its lowest terms. (non-calculator)","a":"8/5","sol":"The last digit sits in the tenths column, so 1.6 = 16/10. M1 for 16/10 (or for 1 whole and 6/10). Both parts divide by 2: 16 ÷ 2 = 8 and 10 ÷ 2 = 5, giving 8/5. A1 for 8/5 (16/10 uncancelled scores only the method mark).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The digit after the point tells you which place value names the denominator.","Write all the digits over that place value, then cancel by the highest common factor.","The first line is 1.6 = 16/10; the cancelling comes after it."],"codes":["N10"]},{"q":"Write 9/40 as a decimal. (non-calculator)","a":"0.225","sol":"9/40 means 9 ÷ 40. Short division gives 9.000 ÷ 40 = 0.225 (or multiply both parts by 25: 9/40 = 225/1000 = 0.225). B1 for 0.225.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The line in a fraction is a division sign.","Divide the numerator by the denominator, or scale the denominator up to a power of 10.","The first line is 9 ÷ 40, worked as 9.000 ÷ 40."],"codes":["N10"]},{"q":"Work out 3/5 of 85 kg. (non-calculator)","a":"51 kg","sol":"'Of' means multiply, so 3/5 of 85 = 3/5 × 85. One fifth is 85 ÷ 5 = 17, so three fifths is 17 × 3. M1 for 85 ÷ 5 = 17 or for 3/5 × 85 seen. A1 for 51 kg, with the unit.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A fraction in front of a quantity is acting on it.","Divide by the denominator to get one part, then multiply by the numerator.","The first line is 85 ÷ 5, and the numerator is used after that."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"Write down the single number an amount is multiplied by to increase it by 7%. (non-calculator)","a":"1.07","sol":"After a 7% increase the amount is 100% + 7% = 107% of what it was, and 107% = 107/100. B1 for 1.07.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["After the increase, what percentage of the original amount is left standing?","A percentage only works as a multiplier once it is written as a decimal.","The same route on different numbers: a 9% increase gives 100% + 9% = 109%, so the multiplier is 1.09."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"A shelf holds fiction and non-fiction books in the ratio 6 : 5. What fraction of the books on the shelf are non-fiction? Give your answer in its simplest form. (non-calculator)","a":"5/11","sol":"The ratio splits the books into 6 + 5 = 11 equal parts. M1 for 11 parts seen. Non-fiction takes 5 of those 11 parts, so the fraction is 5/11. A1 for 5/11.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A ratio counts equal parts, and the whole shelf is all of them.","Add the parts to get the whole, then count the parts you want.","The first line is 6 + 5, and the non-fiction parts go on top."],"codes":["N11"]},{"q":"Priya is paid £9.60 per hour. Her rate of pay is increased by 5%. Work out her new rate of pay per hour. (a calculator may be used)","a":"£10.08","sol":"An increase of 5% gives 100% + 5% = 105%, so the multiplier is 1.05. New rate = 9.60 × 1.05 = 10.08. M1 for 1.05 or for 9.60 × 1.05 (or for 5% of 9.60 = 0.48). A1 for £10.08.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["One multiplication can do the whole increase.","Work out the multiplier for a 5% increase, then apply it to the old rate.","The first line is old rate × multiplier, with the multiplier found from 100% + 5%."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"In a car park, 0.45 of the vehicles are red. Write the ratio of red vehicles to vehicles that are not red, in its simplest form. (non-calculator)","a":"9 : 11","sol":"0.45 = 45/100 = 9/20 of the vehicles are red. M1 for 45/100 or 9/20. The rest are 20/20 − 9/20 = 11/20 (or 1 − 0.45 = 0.55 = 11/20). M1 for 11/20 or 0.55. In twentieths the vehicles split 9 parts to 11 parts, so red : not red = 9 : 11. A1 for 9 : 11.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A decimal of the whole can be read as a fraction of the whole.","Turn the decimal into a fraction in its lowest terms, then find the fraction that is left over.","The first line is 0.45 = 45/100, and the two fractions over the same denominator give the ratio."],"codes":["N10","N11"]},{"q":"A laptop costs £540. In a sale the price is reduced by 35%. Work out the sale price. (a calculator may be used)","a":"£351","sol":"A reduction of 35% leaves 100% − 35% = 65%, so the multiplier is 0.65. M1 for 0.65 (or for 35% of 540 = 189). Sale price = 540 × 0.65. M1 for 540 × 0.65 (or 540 − 189). This gives 351. A1 for £351.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The shopper still pays for most of the laptop — how much of it?","Find the percentage that is left and use it as a multiplier on the old price.","The first line is 100% − 35%, written as a decimal multiplier."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"A jar contains only 20p coins and 50p coins, in the ratio 3 : 2. There are 45 coins in the jar. How many 50p coins are in the jar? (non-calculator)","a":"18","sol":"The ratio gives 3 + 2 = 5 equal parts, so the 50p coins are 2/5 of the coins. M1 for 5 parts or for the fraction 2/5. One fifth of the jar is 45 ÷ 5 = 9 coins. M1 for 45 ÷ 5 = 9. So 2/5 of 45 = 9 × 2 = 18. A1 for 18.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The 50p coins are a fraction of all the coins, not of the 20p coins.","Turn the ratio into the fraction that is 50p coins, then take that fraction of 45.","The first line is 3 + 2 = 5 parts, and 45 is then divided by the number of parts."],"codes":["N11","N12"]},{"q":"A café bakes 120 cakes. It sells 5/8 of them in the morning, then sells 40% of the cakes that are left during the afternoon. How many cakes are unsold at the end of the day? (a calculator may be used)","a":"27","sol":"Morning: 5/8 of 120 = (120 ÷ 8) × 5 = 75 cakes sold. M1 for 5/8 × 120 = 75. Left at midday: 120 − 75 = 45. M1 for 45. Afternoon: 40% are sold, so 60% of the 45 are left, giving 0.6 × 45. M1 for 0.6 × 45 (or 0.4 × 45 = 18 followed by 45 − 18). This gives 27. A1 for 27 cakes.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The afternoon percentage acts on what is left, not on the whole batch.","Work out the morning sales first, subtract them, then apply a multiplier to what remains.","The first line is 5/8 × 120, and the remainder is what the afternoon multiplier acts on."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"Put these four numbers in order, smallest first: 0.7, 5/8, 0.66, 17/25. (non-calculator)","a":"5/8, 0.66, 17/25, 0.7","sol":"Put everything in one form. 5/8 = 5 ÷ 8 = 0.625. M1 for 0.625. 17/25 = 68/100 = 0.68. M1 for 0.68. Comparing 0.625, 0.66, 0.68 and 0.7 gives 0.625 < 0.66 < 0.68 < 0.7, so in the forms given the order is 5/8, 0.66, 17/25, 0.7. A1 for the correct order, written in the original forms.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Numbers can only be compared when they are written the same way.","Convert both fractions to decimals, then compare place value by place value.","The first line is 5 ÷ 8, and the second fraction is scaled to hundredths."],"codes":["N10"]},{"q":"Ali and Ben share a piece of work. Ali does 0.375 of the work and Ben does the rest. They are paid £560 for the job, shared between them in the same proportions as the work. How much is Ben paid? (non-calculator)","a":"£350","sol":"Ali: 0.375 = 375/1000 = 3/8 of the work. M1 for 3/8. Ben does the rest, 1 − 3/8 = 5/8 (so Ali : Ben = 3 : 5). M1 for 5/8 or for the ratio 3 : 5. Ben's pay = 5/8 of 560, and 560 ÷ 8 = 70. M1 for 560 ÷ 8 = 70. So 70 × 5 = 350. A1 for £350.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The decimal tells you Ali's share of one whole job.","Turn the decimal into a fraction, take it from the whole to get Ben's fraction, then apply that fraction to the money.","The first line is 0.375 = 375/1000, cancelled, and the whole is 1."],"codes":["N10","N11","N12"]},{"q":"A shop buys 240 bottles of juice for 90p each. It sells 3/4 of the bottles at £1.50 each, and sells the rest at a price that is 30% less than £1.50. Work out the shop's total profit on the 240 bottles. (a calculator may be used)","a":"£117","sol":"Cost = 240 × 0.90 = £216. M1 for 240 × 0.90. Bottles at full price = 3/4 × 240 = 180, so 240 − 180 = 60 are left. M1 for 180 and 60. Full-price takings = 180 × 1.50 = £270. M1 for 270. The reduced price is 1.50 × 0.7 = £1.05, so the last bottles take 60 × 1.05 = £63. M1 for 63. Total takings = 270 + 63 = £333, and profit = 333 − 216 = £117. A1 for £117.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Profit compares what the shop takes in with what it paid out.","Work out the cost, then the takings from each group of bottles separately, using a multiplier for the reduced price.","The first line is 240 × 0.90 for the cost, and the bottles then split 3/4 to the rest."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"A youth club has 96 members. The ratio of members aged under 16 to members aged 16 or over is 5 : 3. A quarter of the under-16 members are girls, and 1/3 of the members aged 16 or over are girls. How many members of the club are girls? (non-calculator)","a":"27","sol":"The ratio gives 5 + 3 = 8 equal parts, so the under-16 members are 5/8 of the club and the rest are 3/8. M1 for 5/8 (or 8 parts with 96 ÷ 8 = 12). Under 16: 5/8 of 96 = (96 ÷ 8) × 5 = 60 members; 16 or over: 96 − 60 = 36 members. M1 for 60 and 36. Girls under 16 = 1/4 of 60 = 15. M1 for 15. Girls aged 16 or over = 1/3 of 36 = 12. M1 for 12. Total girls = 15 + 12 = 27. A1 for 27.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The ratio splits the club before either fraction is used.","Find the size of each age group first, then apply each group's own fraction to that group.","The first line is 5 + 3 parts, giving the fraction of the club that is under 16."],"codes":["N11","N12"]},{"q":"The same pair of trainers is on offer in two shops. In shop A they cost £80 and the price is reduced by 1/4. In shop B they cost £76 and the price is reduced by 20%. Which shop sells the trainers more cheaply, and by how much? (non-calculator)","a":"Shop A, by £0.80","sol":"Shop A: a reduction of 1/4 leaves 3/4, so the price is 80 × 3/4. M1 for 80 × 3/4 (or 1/4 of 80 = 20 taken off). Shop B: a reduction of 20% leaves 80%, so the price is 76 × 0.8. M1 for 76 × 0.8 (or 20% of 76 = 15.20 taken off). The prices are £60 and £60.80. A1 for both £60 and £60.80. 60.80 − 60 = 0.80, so shop A is cheaper by £0.80. A1 for the comparison with the difference.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A fraction off and a percentage off are the same kind of instruction.","Turn each reduction into the fraction or decimal of the price that is still paid, then compare the two sale prices.","The first line is 80 × 3/4 for shop A, with shop B needing its own multiplier."],"codes":["N10","N12"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 0.24 as a fraction in its lowest terms.","a":"6/25"},{"q":"Write 3/16 as a decimal.","a":"0.1875"},{"q":"Sweets are red : green in the ratio 2 : 7. What fraction of the sweets are green?","a":"7/9"},{"q":"Write down the single multiplier that decreases an amount by 18%.","a":"0.82"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Terminating decimal","back":"A decimal that stops after a fixed number of decimal places, such as 0.375. Written over a power of 10 it cancels, so in lowest terms its denominator has no prime factors other than 2 and 5: 0.28 = 7/25.","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"Decimal to fraction: the method","back":"Write the digits over the place value of the last digit (tenths, hundredths, thousandths), then cancel by the highest common factor: 0.28 = 28/100 = 7/25.","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"Fraction to decimal: the method","back":"Divide the numerator by the denominator: 3/8 = 3 ÷ 8 = 0.375. Scaling to a power of 10 also works: 9/40 = 225/1000 = 0.225.","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"3.5 written as an improper fraction","back":"3.5 = 35/10 = 7/2. The 3 is 30/10 and the 0.5 is 5/10, and 35/10 cancels by 5.","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"The decimal equivalents worth knowing by sight","back":"1/2 = 0.5, 1/4 = 0.25, 3/4 = 0.75, 1/5 = 0.2, 1/8 = 0.125, 3/8 = 0.375, 1/10 = 0.1, 1/20 = 0.05, 1/25 = 0.04, 1/40 = 0.025.","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"Give one example of a terminating decimal written as a fraction in its lowest terms","back":"0.45 = 9/20; 0.6 = 3/5; 0.125 = 1/8. Any decimal correctly converted and fully cancelled is acceptable.","code":"N10","open":true},{"front":"Number of parts in the ratio a : b","back":"a + b equal parts. One part is the whole amount divided by a + b, which is the step most ratio questions turn on.","code":"N11","open":false},{"front":"Fraction of the whole from a ratio","back":"In the ratio a : b the first quantity is a/(a + b) of the whole and the second is b/(a + b). So 2 : 3 gives 2/5 and 3/5.","code":"N11","open":false},{"front":"Ratio from a fraction of the whole","back":"If a/b of a set has a property, the rest is (b − a)/b, so property : rest = a : (b − a). 3/7 of a class gives 3 : 4.","code":"N11","open":false},{"front":"Part : part against part : whole","back":"3 : 4 counts one group against the other; 3/7 counts one group against everybody. Check which the question gives before using it.","code":"N11","open":false},{"front":"Fraction as an operator","back":"'Of' means multiply: a/b of Q = a/b × Q. Divide by the denominator for one part, then multiply by the numerator: 3/4 of 60 kg = 45 kg.","code":"N12","open":false},{"front":"Percentage as an operator","back":"p% of Q = (p/100) × Q. So 15% of 240 = 0.15 × 240 = 36.","code":"N12","open":false},{"front":"Multiplier for an increase of p%","back":"1 + p/100. An increase of 15% is × 1.15, because the new amount is 115% of the old one.","code":"N12","open":false},{"front":"Multiplier for a decrease of p%","back":"1 − p/100. A decrease of 35% is × 0.65, because 65% of the amount is left.","code":"N12","open":false},{"front":"Multiplier written as a fraction","back":"Any multiplier can be a fraction: × 0.75 is × 3/4 and × 1.2 is × 6/5. Without a calculator the fraction is usually the quicker route.","code":"N12","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Decimal to fraction: digits over the place value of the last digit, then cancel — 0.28 = 28/100 = 7/25. Not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Fraction to decimal: numerator ÷ denominator — 9/40 = 9 ÷ 40 = 0.225. Not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Decimal to improper fraction and back: 3.5 = 35/10 = 7/2. Not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Fraction of the whole from a ratio a : b — first quantity = a/(a + b), second = b/(a + b), and one part = whole ÷ (a + b). Not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Ratio from a fraction — if a/b of a set has a property, property : rest = a : (b − a). Not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","'Of' as an operator — a/b of Q = a/b × Q and p% of Q = (p/100) × Q. Not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Multiplier for a p% increase = 1 + p/100 (15% gives × 1.15); for a p% decrease = 1 − p/100 (35% gives × 0.65). Not on the Exam Aid: remember both.","Decimal equivalents that save a division: 1/2 = 0.5, 1/4 = 0.25, 3/4 = 0.75, 1/5 = 0.2, 1/8 = 0.125, 3/8 = 0.375, 1/10 = 0.1, 1/20 = 0.05, 1/25 = 0.04, 1/40 = 0.025. Nowhere on the Exam Aid: remember them.","The one multiplier result the Foundation Exam Aid (W87990A) does print is Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, for interest compounded over n periods — the repeated-multiplier case, which belongs to the growth and decay room, not to the single-step multipliers here.","Nothing else in this room is printed: the Foundation sheet gives only the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, the circumference and area of a circle, a² + b² = c², sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b, the compound interest formula and P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B), all of which belong to other rooms."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T11:41:22Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.93,"notes":"Answer keys, mark tallies and solution lines are all correct and the Foundation formulae-sheet claims match Exam Aid W87990A exactly — publish as it is, and only trim the hints on the 7% multiplier question if you want students to do any of it themselves."}}},"number-measures":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"number-measures","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Measures and accuracy marks are spread right across all three papers of 1MA1, and they are rarely the whole question: a speed, a density or a price per unit sits inside a ratio or proportion problem, a rounding instruction sits at the end of a calculator question, and an error interval sits inside a reasoning question about whether something will fit. Every answer in the exam has to be given to the accuracy asked for and with its unit, so the marks in this room protect marks everywhere else. Paper 1 is non-calculator, so estimation by rounding to 1 significant figure is also how you check your own arithmetic, and on Papers 2 and 3 it is how you catch a mistyped calculator key before it costs you the accuracy mark.","idea":[{"h":"Standard units and compound measures","t":"Use the standard units of mass (g, kg, tonnes), length (mm, cm, m, km), capacity (ml, litres), time (seconds, minutes, hours) and money (£ and pence), and name the unit with every answer — an answer with no unit loses the accuracy mark. A compound measure is one quantity divided by another: average speed = distance ÷ time, density = mass ÷ volume, price per unit = total price ÷ number of units. Its unit is written the same way: km/h, m/s, g/cm³, £ per litre. The two quantities must be in matching units before you divide, so 45 minutes becomes 0.75 hours and 1.79 kg becomes 1790 g. Decimal quantities are expected: 2.5 hours, not 2 hours 30 minutes, inside a division.","code":"N13"},{"h":"Estimating and checking by approximation","t":"To estimate, round every number in the calculation to 1 significant figure, then work out the easy calculation that is left, writing ≈ rather than =. The method mark is for the rounded calculation being written down, so a bare estimate scores nothing. Dividing by a number below 1 makes the answer bigger: ÷ 0.5 doubles, ÷ 0.2 multiplies by 5. Estimation is also how a calculator answer is checked — if the display reads 42.4 and the estimate is 400, a key has been mistyped. Questions often ask you to say whether an answer is sensible, so finish with a sentence comparing the estimate with the value given, not just the estimate itself.","code":"N14"},{"h":"Rounding and error intervals","t":"Rounding to a number of decimal places counts places after the point; rounding to significant figures starts counting at the first non-zero digit, so 0.04862 to 2 significant figures is 0.049. Look at the next digit along: 5 or more rounds up, 4 or less leaves the digit alone. An appropriate degree of accuracy usually means money to the nearest penny, or the same accuracy as the values you started with. A rounded measurement does not give the exact value, so the error interval says what it could have been: to the nearest unit u the interval is half a unit either side, written with ≤ at the lower end and < at the upper end because a value on the upper limit would round up. Truncation chops the digits off instead of rounding, so a value truncated to 8.3 lies in 8.3 ≤ n < 8.4.","code":"N15"},{"h":"Limits of accuracy","t":"A rounded or truncated measurement stands for every value in its interval, and the limits of accuracy are the two ends of that interval: a mass of 25 kg to the nearest kilogram has a lower limit of 24.5 kg and an upper limit of 25.5 kg, and the true mass is at least 24.5 kg but never quite 25.5 kg. Interpreting the limits means saying what they mean for the situation: the shortest a shelf could be, the most a lift could be carrying, whether a claimed value is possible at all. In a reasoning question the useful limit is usually the one that works against you — the shortest length when something has to fit, the largest load when something has a safe limit — and the conclusion has to be written down in words, with the comparison that justifies it.","code":"N16"}],"examples":[{"title":"Average speed as a compound measure","setup":"A car travels 150 km in 2.5 hours. Work out its average speed in km/h, giving the answer as a decimal quantity with its unit.","steps":["Average speed = distance ÷ time, so the calculation is 150 ÷ 2.5.","Doubling both parts clears the decimal: 150 ÷ 2.5 = 300 ÷ 5, which is the line the method mark is awarded for.","300 ÷ 5 = 60, so the average speed is 60 km/h."],"cues":["Which quantity is being shared out over which?","How could the divisor be made a whole number?","What unit belongs on a distance in km over a time in hours?"]},{"title":"Estimating to check a calculator answer","setup":"A calculator shows 39.7 × 5.13 ÷ 0.48 = 424.29 (to 2 decimal places). Estimate the value of 39.7 × 5.13 ÷ 0.48 by rounding each number to 1 significant figure, and decide whether the calculator answer is sensible.","steps":["Round each value to 1 significant figure: 39.7 ≈ 40, 5.13 ≈ 5 and 0.48 ≈ 0.5, choosing the nearest easy numbers.","The estimate is 40 × 5 ÷ 0.5; do the multiplication first, 40 × 5 = 200.","Dividing by 0.5 doubles a number, so 200 ÷ 0.5 = 400.","400 is close to the value the calculator displayed, so that answer is sensible."],"cues":["What is the first significant figure of each number?","Which part of the rounded calculation is easiest to do first?","What does dividing by a half do to a number?","How does the estimate compare with the display?"]},{"title":"Writing an error interval","setup":"A length is measured as 8 cm to the nearest centimetre. Write down the error interval for the length x, using inequality notation.","steps":["To the nearest centimetre means the value was rounded, so it lies within half a centimetre either side: the gap is 1 ÷ 2 = 0.5 cm.","Smallest value: 8 − 0.5 = 7.5 cm, and 7.5 cm itself rounds up to 8 cm, so 7.5 is included.","Largest value: 8 + 0.5 = 8.5 cm, but 8.5 cm would round up to 9 cm, so 8.5 is not included.","So the error interval is 7.5 ≤ x < 8.5, in cm."],"cues":["How far either side of the reading can the true value be?","What is the smallest length that still rounds to this reading?","Does the value at the top of the range round up or down?","Which end of the interval needs the strict inequality?"]},{"title":"Interpreting limits of accuracy","setup":"A time is measured as 12.5 seconds, correct to the nearest 0.1 second. State the limits of accuracy of the measurement, and say whether the true time could have been 12.44 seconds.","steps":["Nearest 0.1 second means the reading was rounded, so the true value lies within 0.1 ÷ 2 = 0.05 s of it.","Lower limit: 12.5 − 0.05 = 12.45 s. Upper limit: 12.5 + 0.05 = 12.55 s.","As an interval, 12.45 ≤ t < 12.55, so the true time is at least 12.45 s and below 12.55 s.","The value suggested in the question is below 12.45 s, so no — the true time could not have been that small."],"cues":["Half of which unit gives the gap either side?","What are the two ends of the range?","How is that range written in inequality notation?","Does the suggested value lie inside the range?"]},{"title":"Price per litre and best value","setup":"A 750 ml bottle of juice costs £2.19. A 2 litre carton of the same juice costs £5.60. Work out the price per litre of each, to the nearest penny, and say which is the better value.","steps":["750 ml = 750 ÷ 1000 = 0.75 litres, so the bottle gives 0.75 litres for £2.19.","Bottle: price per litre = 2.19 ÷ 0.75 = £2.92.","Carton: price per litre = 5.60 ÷ 2 = £2.80.","£2.80 per litre is less than £2.92 per litre, so the carton is the better value."],"cues":["What has to happen before the two sizes can be compared?","Which quantity is divided by which for a price per litre?","What is the same calculation for the larger container?","Which price per litre is the smaller?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A width is 4.7 cm to the nearest 0.1 cm. Write down the error interval for the width w.","a":"4.65 ≤ w < 4.75 (cm)"},{"q":"Estimate 396 ÷ 1.96 by rounding each number to 1 significant figure.","a":"200 (from 400 ÷ 2)"},{"q":"A runner covers 250 m in 50 seconds. Work out the average speed in m/s.","a":"5 m/s"},{"q":"Round 2.0649 to 2 decimal places.","a":"2.06"}]},"room":{"facts":["Average speed = distance ÷ time, so distance = speed × time and time = distance ÷ speed. Distance in km with time in hours gives km/h; metres with seconds gives m/s.","Density = mass ÷ volume (g/cm³ or kg/m³), and price per unit = total price ÷ number of units. Both quantities must be in matching units before dividing.","To estimate: round every number to 1 significant figure, then do the easy calculation, writing ≈ not =. Dividing by 0.5 doubles a number; dividing by 0.2 multiplies it by 5.","Metric units: 1 km = 1000 m, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 litre = 1000 ml, 1 ml = 1 cm³, 1 hour = 60 minutes.","Rounding: look at the next digit along — 5 or more rounds up, 4 or less leaves the digit alone. Significant figures are counted from the first non-zero digit.","A measurement given to the nearest u has error interval (value − u/2) ≤ x < (value + u/2); a measurement truncated at that place has value ≤ x < value + u."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Round 0.04862 correct to 2 significant figures.","a":"0.049","sol":"Significant figures are counted from the first non-zero digit, so the first is 4 and the second is 8. The next digit is 6, which is 5 or more, so the 8 rounds up to 9 and the zeros holding the place value are kept: 0.049 (B1). Writing 0.05 (1 significant figure) or 0.048 scores nothing.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The zeros in front do not count as significant figures.","Find the first non-zero digit, count two digits from there, then look at the digit after them.","Your first line is to mark which digit is the second significant figure."],"codes":["N15"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A water tank holds 47 000 litres, correct to the nearest thousand litres. Write down the error interval for the volume V litres.","a":"46 500 ≤ V < 47 500","sol":"To the nearest thousand the true volume lies within half a thousand of the reading, and half of 1000 is 500. 47 000 − 500 = 46 500 and 47 000 + 500 = 47 500 (B1 for both values seen). A volume of 46 500 litres rounds up to 47 000 so it is included, while 47 500 litres rounds up to 48 000 so it is not: 46 500 ≤ V < 47 500 (B1 for the fully correct inequality).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The figure given is not exact — it stands for a whole range of volumes.","Work out half of the unit the value was rounded to, then go that far either side.","Your first line is half of the rounding unit; then take it away from, and add it to, the value."],"codes":["N15"]},{"q":"A part-time worker is paid £58.50 for 4.5 hours of work. Work out her rate of pay in £ per hour. A calculator may be used.","a":"£13 per hour","sol":"Rate of pay is a compound measure: pay ÷ time worked = 58.50 ÷ 4.5 (M1). This gives 13, so the rate of pay is £13 per hour (A1; the unit is needed for the accuracy mark).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A rate of pay measures money against the time worked.","Divide the total pay by the number of hours worked.","Your first line is total pay ÷ number of hours."],"codes":["N13"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Estimate the value of 4.8 × 19.7 by rounding each number to 1 significant figure.","a":"100","sol":"Rounding each number to 1 significant figure gives 4.8 ≈ 5 and 19.7 ≈ 20, so the calculation becomes 5 × 20 (M1 for both roundings and the product written down). 5 × 20 = 100 (A1). The exact value is 94.56, so the estimate is a sensible check.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["You are not meant to work out the exact product here.","Replace each number by the nearest number with one non-zero digit.","Your first line writes the rounded calculation, with ≈ instead of =."],"codes":["N14"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A bag of sand has a mass of 25 kg, correct to the nearest kilogram. Write down the smallest possible mass of the bag.","a":"24.5 kg","sol":"To the nearest kilogram the true mass lies within 0.5 kg either side of 25 kg, so the lower limit is 25 − 0.5 = 24.5 kg (B1, unit needed). A mass of exactly 24.5 kg rounds up to 25 kg, so it is the smallest mass possible.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The mass on the label is a rounded value, not the exact mass.","For a value rounded to the nearest kilogram, the true mass can be up to half a kilogram either side.","Your first line is half of the unit the mass was rounded to."],"codes":["N16"]},{"q":"A solid block of copper has a mass of 1.79 kg and a volume of 200 cm³. Work out the density of the copper in g/cm³. A calculator may be used.","a":"8.95 g/cm³","sol":"The unit asked for uses grams, so convert the mass first: 1.79 kg = 1.79 × 1000 = 1790 g (M1). Density = mass ÷ volume = 1790 ÷ 200 (M1). This gives 8.95, so the density is 8.95 g/cm³ (A1, unit needed).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Check the units the answer has to be in before you divide anything.","Convert the mass into grams, then divide mass by volume.","Your first line converts kilograms into grams by multiplying by 1000."],"codes":["N13"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Estimate the value of 29.3² ÷ 5.8.","a":"150","sol":"Round each number to 1 significant figure: 29.3 ≈ 30 and 5.8 ≈ 6 (M1). Square first: 30² = 900 (M1). Then 900 ÷ 6 = 150 (A1). The exact value is 148.0 to 1 decimal place, so the estimate is a good check.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Pick numbers that make both the squaring and the dividing easy.","Round both values to 1 significant figure, then follow the order of operations.","Your first line is the rounded calculation written with the square still to be worked out."],"codes":["N14"]},{"q":"A train travels 84 km in 45 minutes. Work out its average speed in km/h. A calculator may be used.","a":"112 km/h","sol":"The unit is km/h, so the time must be in hours: 45 minutes = 45 ÷ 60 = 0.75 hours (M1). Average speed = distance ÷ time = 84 ÷ 0.75 (M1). This gives 112, so the average speed is 112 km/h (A1, unit needed).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The two quantities have to be in units that match the answer.","Turn the time into a decimal number of hours, then divide distance by time.","Your first line divides the number of minutes by 60."],"codes":["N13"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A number n is truncated to 1 decimal place, giving 8.3. Write down the error interval for n.","a":"8.3 ≤ n < 8.4","sol":"Truncating chops off the digits after the first decimal place instead of rounding, so every value from 8.3 up to (but not including) 8.4 is truncated to 8.3 — for example 8.39 truncates to 8.3 (B1 for 8.3 and 8.4 identified as the limits). The lower limit is included and the upper limit is not: 8.3 ≤ n < 8.4 (B1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Truncating and rounding do not give the same interval.","Ask what the largest and smallest numbers are whose digits start this way.","Your first line writes down the two values the interval runs between."],"codes":["N15"]},{"q":"A car uses 41.6 litres of fuel to travel 508 km. Work out the fuel used per 100 km, correct to 3 significant figures. A calculator may be used.","a":"8.19 litres per 100 km","sol":"Fuel per kilometre = 41.6 ÷ 508 = 0.0818897… litres (M1). Fuel per 100 km = 0.0818897… × 100 = 8.18897… (M1). The fourth significant figure is 8, which is 5 or more, so this rounds to 8.19 litres per 100 km (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This is a compound measure: an amount of fuel measured against a distance.","Find the fuel used for one kilometre first, then scale it up.","Your first line divides the volume of fuel by the distance travelled."],"codes":["N13","N15"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A bottle is labelled as containing 500 ml, correct to the nearest 10 ml. Dan says the bottle could contain 506 ml. Is Dan right? You must give a reason.","a":"No — the volume must be less than 505 ml","sol":"To the nearest 10 ml the true volume lies within 10 ÷ 2 = 5 ml either side, so the error interval is 495 ≤ v < 505 in ml (B1). 506 ml is above the upper limit of 505 ml, so Dan is wrong (B1 for the conclusion with the comparison).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The label is a rounded value, so the contents lie in a range.","Work out the two limits the rounding allows, then test Dan's value against them.","Your first line is half of the unit the volume was rounded to."],"codes":["N16"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A shelf is 2.4 m long, correct to the nearest 0.1 m. Ben wants to cut 8 pieces, each exactly 0.3 m long, from the shelf. Can Ben be certain that he has enough shelf? You must show your working.","a":"No — the shelf could be only 2.35 m, which is less than the 2.4 m needed","sol":"Length of shelf needed: 8 × 0.3 = 2.4 m (M1). The shelf is rounded to the nearest 0.1 m, so the lower limit of its length is 2.4 − 0.05 = 2.35 m (M1). Since 2.35 m is less than the 2.4 m needed, the shelf might be too short, so Ben cannot be certain (A1 for the comparison and the conclusion).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Two lengths have to be compared: what is needed and what is certainly there.","Work out the total length of the cuts, then the shortest the shelf could possibly be.","Your first line multiplies the number of pieces by the length of one piece."],"codes":["N16"]},{"q":"A lorry travels 210 km at an average speed of 60 km/h, and then 90 km at an average speed of 45 km/h. Work out the average speed for the whole journey, correct to 3 significant figures. A calculator may be used.","a":"54.5 km/h","sol":"First stage: time = distance ÷ speed = 210 ÷ 60 = 3.5 hours (M1). Second stage: 90 ÷ 45 = 2 hours (M1). Total distance = 210 + 90 = 300 km and total time = 3.5 + 2 = 5.5 hours, so average speed = 300 ÷ 5.5 (M1). This gives 54.545…, which is 54.5 km/h to 3 significant figures (A1). Averaging the two speeds instead gives 52.5 km/h and scores no accuracy mark.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["An average speed for a whole journey is never the average of the two speeds.","Find the time for each stage, then use the total distance and the total time.","Your first line rearranges the speed formula to time = distance ÷ speed."],"codes":["N13","N15"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A club has 312 members. Each member pays £48.50 a year. The treasurer says the yearly income will be about £20 000. Use estimation to decide whether the treasurer is right. You must show your working.","a":"No — the income is about £15 000","sol":"Round each value to 1 significant figure: 312 ≈ 300 and £48.50 ≈ £50 (M1). Estimated income = 300 × 50 (M1). 3 × 5 = 15 and there are three zeros to replace, so the estimate is £15 000 (A1 for £15 000 together with the conclusion that £20 000 is too high). The exact value is £15 132, so the estimate is a fair one.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["An estimate is enough here — the exact total is not needed.","Round the number of members and the yearly payment to 1 significant figure, then multiply.","Your first line writes the two rounded values as a product, using ≈."],"codes":["N14"]},{"q":"A gold bar is a cuboid measuring 12 cm by 6 cm by 4 cm. The density of the gold is 19.3 g/cm³. Work out the mass of the bar in kilograms, correct to 3 significant figures. A calculator may be used.","a":"5.56 kg","sol":"Volume of the cuboid = 12 × 6 × 4 = 288 cm³ (M1). Rearranging density = mass ÷ volume gives mass = density × volume = 19.3 × 288 (M1), which is 5558.4 g (A1). Converting, 5558.4 ÷ 1000 = 5.5584 kg, which is 5.56 kg to 3 significant figures (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The density tells you the mass of each cubic centimetre of gold.","Find the volume of the cuboid first, then use the density formula rearranged for mass.","Your first line multiplies the three dimensions of the cuboid together."],"codes":["N13","N15"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Round 0.008361 correct to 2 significant figures.","a":"0.0084"},{"q":"A length L is 18 cm to the nearest centimetre. Write down the error interval for L.","a":"17.5 ≤ L < 18.5"},{"q":"A cyclist covers 400 m in 50 seconds. Work out the average speed in m/s.","a":"8 m/s"},{"q":"Estimate 8.13 × 29.6 by rounding each number to 1 significant figure.","a":"240 (from 8 × 30)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Compound measure","back":"A measure made by dividing one quantity by another, such as speed, density or price per litre. Its unit is written the same way: km/h, g/cm³, £ per litre.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Average speed","back":"Average speed = distance ÷ time. Rearranged: distance = speed × time, time = distance ÷ speed. Units must match — km with hours gives km/h, metres with seconds gives m/s.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Density","back":"Density = mass ÷ volume, so mass = density × volume. Measured in g/cm³ or kg/m³. Convert the mass and volume into the units the answer needs before dividing.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Price per unit (unit pricing)","back":"Price per unit = total price ÷ number of units, for example £ per litre or pence per gram. The better value is the one with the smaller price per unit.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Standard metric conversions","back":"1 km = 1000 m, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 litre = 1000 ml, 1 ml = 1 cm³, £1 = 100 p.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Writing a time as a decimal number of hours","back":"Divide the minutes by 60: 45 minutes = 45 ÷ 60 = 0.75 hours, 15 minutes = 0.25 hours, 20 minutes = 1/3 hour. Needed before dividing to get a speed in km/h.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Estimating a calculation","back":"Round every number to 1 significant figure, then work out the easy calculation, writing ≈ not =. The rounded calculation must be written down: a bare estimate earns no method mark.","code":"N14","open":false},{"front":"Worst case of a rounded measurement","back":"The value at the wrong end of one measurement's error interval: a rope given as 5 m to the nearest metre could be only 4.5 m, so it may not reach; a 5 kg load could be nearly 5.5 kg.","code":"N16","open":false},{"front":"Give one way to check that a calculator answer is about right","back":"Estimate by rounding each value to 1 significant figure and compare; check the size of the answer against the situation (a person's mass is not 700 kg); redo the calculation with the inverse operation.","code":"N14","open":true},{"front":"Significant figures","back":"Counted from the first non-zero digit. In 0.04862 the first significant figure is 4; to 2 significant figures it is 0.049. Zeros in front only hold place value and are not significant.","code":"N15","open":false},{"front":"The rounding rule","back":"Look at the digit after the place you are rounding to: 5 or more rounds the digit up, 4 or less leaves it as it is. Digits after the rounding place are dropped, place-holding zeros kept.","code":"N15","open":false},{"front":"Error interval for a rounded measurement","back":"A value x given as a to the nearest u lies in (a − u/2) ≤ x < (a + u/2). A length of 8 cm to the nearest cm gives 7.5 ≤ x < 8.5.","code":"N15","open":false},{"front":"Truncation","back":"Chopping off the later digits instead of rounding. A number truncated to 8.3 lies in 8.3 ≤ n < 8.4, so the stated value is always the lower limit of the interval.","code":"N15","open":false},{"front":"Appropriate degree of accuracy","back":"Money to the nearest penny (2 decimal places), and other answers to about the accuracy of the values given, usually 3 significant figures from a calculator. Never round part-way through a calculation.","code":"N15","open":false},{"front":"Limits of accuracy","back":"The values a single rounded measurement lies between, read off its error interval: a mass of 25 kg to the nearest kilogram lies in 24.5 ≤ m < 25.5, so it is at least 24.5 kg and under 25.5 kg.","code":"N16","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Preamble: the Exam Aid formulae sheet printed as an insert with every Foundation tier 1MA1 paper gives only the eight results listed next, and none of them belongs to this room, so every result marked Remember must be written from memory.","Given on the Exam Aid: area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, where a and b are the parallel sides and h their perpendicular separation.","Given on the Exam Aid: volume of a prism = area of cross section × length.","Given on the Exam Aid: circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d the diameter.","Given on the Exam Aid: area of a circle = πr².","Given on the Exam Aid: Pythagoras' theorem a² + b² = c², in a right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is c.","Given on the Exam Aid: sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b, in a right-angled triangle ABC whose hypotenuse is c.","Given on the Exam Aid: compound interest total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, where P is the principal, r the interest rate per period and n the number of periods.","Given on the Exam Aid: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B).","Remember: average speed = distance ÷ time — not given on the Exam Aid, which prints no compound measure.","Remember: distance = speed × time and time = distance ÷ speed, the two rearrangements of the speed formula.","Remember: density = mass ÷ volume, so mass = density × volume and volume = mass ÷ density.","Remember: price per unit = total price ÷ number of units; the better value is the smaller price per unit.","Remember: to estimate, round every number to 1 significant figure and work out the easy calculation, writing ≈ not =.","Remember: a value a given to the nearest u has error interval (a − u/2) ≤ x < (a + u/2), with ≤ at the lower end and < at the upper end.","Remember: a value a truncated at a place value of u has interval a ≤ x < a + u, so the value given is the lower limit.","Remember: 1 km = 1000 m, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 litre = 1000 ml, 1 ml = 1 cm³.","Remember: minutes become a decimal number of hours by dividing by 60, so 45 minutes = 0.75 hours.","Remember: every answer in this room carries its unit — km/h, g/cm³, £ per litre, cm — because the accuracy mark depends on it."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T11:40:27Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Answer keys, solutions, marks and units are sound throughout — all 15 bank questions, both four-item checklists and all five worked examples re-solved and confirmed, nine questions correctly flagged non-calculator with hand-workable numbers, and the Formulae sheet matches the 2026 Foundation Exam Aid exactly — so it is publishable as it stands; only the discrete-population interval in question 1 and the packed first formulae item are worth a teacher's pencil."}}},"algebra-notation":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"algebra-notation","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Every other piece of algebra you meet is written in the notation of this room. Solving an equation, using a formula in a mensuration or ratio question, reading a straight-line graph or testing a sequence rule all assume you can write a × b as ab, put a negative value in brackets before squaring it, tell an expression from an equation, an identity from a formula, collect like terms, expand and factorise, and turn a formula round to give a different letter. Marks here are lost far more often to careless notation — 0.25x written where x/4 is wanted, −4² worked out as though it were (−4)², a minus sign dropped after a bracket — than to any hard idea, and much of it is examined without a calculator, where the written method is what earns the method mark.","idea":[{"h":"The conventions of algebraic notation","t":"Algebra has a shorthand and examiners expect it. A multiplication sign is left out, so a × b is written ab and 3 × y is written 3y; repeated addition of the same letter is written as a multiple, so y + y + y is 3y; repeated multiplication is written as a power, so a × a is a², a × a × a is a³ and a × a × b is a²b. A division is written as a fraction: a ÷ b is a/b. The number multiplying a letter is its coefficient, and it is written as a fraction rather than a decimal, so x/4, never 0.25x. Brackets mean that everything inside is treated as one quantity first, so 3(x + 2) is three lots of the whole of x + 2. Questions ask you to write an expression in this form, and every later answer is marked in it.","code":"A1"},{"h":"Substituting numerical values","t":"To find a value from a formula or an expression, replace each letter by the number it stands for and then work the arithmetic out in the usual priority: brackets, then powers, then × and ÷, then + and −. The trap is negative values: write each one in brackets as you substitute, because (−4)² = 16 while −4² means −(4²) = −16. Scientific formulae such as v = u + at are substituted the same way, and the answer is given with its unit and to the accuracy asked for. The method mark is for the substitution written down, so put the line with the numbers in it on the page before you evaluate anything.","code":"A2"},{"h":"Expression, equation, formula, identity, inequality","t":"These five words are examined directly. An expression, such as 5y − 2, has no equals sign; it can be simplified or evaluated but not solved. An equation, such as 5y − 2 = 13, has an equals sign and is true only for particular values. An identity, written with ≡, is true for every value: 2(x + 3) ≡ 2x + 6. A formula, such as A = πr² or v = u + at, gives a rule for working out one quantity, its subject, from others. An inequality, such as y > 2, compares two expressions with <, ≤, > or ≥. Within an expression, a term is a part separated by + or −, and a factor is something the expression divides by exactly.","code":"A3"},{"h":"Simplifying and manipulating expressions","t":"Four techniques do most of the work. Collect like terms: only terms with exactly the same letters and powers can be joined, and each term keeps the sign in front of it. Multiply a single term over a bracket: every term inside is multiplied, signs included. Take out common factors: divide each term by the highest common factor and write it outside the bracket, and check by expanding. Expand products of two binomials: every term in the first bracket multiplies every term in the second, giving four products to collect. The same statement covers factorising x² + bx + c, where the two numbers multiply to c and add to b, the difference of two squares x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a), and the laws of indices: xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ, xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ, (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ and x⁰ = 1. 'Simplify fully' and 'factorise fully' mean a partly finished answer scores no accuracy mark.","code":"A4"},{"h":"Standard formulae and changing the subject","t":"A formula has a subject — the letter standing alone on one side — and you are often asked for a different one. Work out what has been done to the letter you want, then undo those operations in reverse order, doing the same thing to both sides each time, until that letter stands alone and appears only once. To make t the subject of v = u + at, take u from both sides and then divide by a; to make r the subject of A = πr², divide by π and then take the square root. Standard formulae such as the area of a circle or a trapezium are printed on the Exam Aid, so the marks are for the rearranging and the substituting, not for the recall.","code":"A5"},{"h":"Showing two expressions are always equal","t":"An equation is true for particular values of the letter; an identity is true for all of them. To show two expressions are equivalent, take one side, expand every bracket and collect every like term, and show the result is exactly the other side — then say so. That single sentence is what turns working into an argument, and it is where the reasoning mark sits. Testing one value is not enough: it shows the two sides agree there, not everywhere. Once shown, the statement may be written with ≡ instead of =. The same expand-and-collect chain proves results about numbers, such as showing an expression is always even or always a multiple of 5.","code":"A6"},{"h":"An expression as a function: input and output","t":"An expression such as 3x − 1 can be read as a machine: an input x goes in, it is multiplied by 3 and then 1 is taken away, and an output comes out. Substituting an input gives the output. Running the machine backwards — undoing each operation in reverse order — takes an output back to the input that produced it, which is the same inverse-operation thinking used to change the subject of a formula. Questions give a function machine, a mapping written x → 3x − 1, or a plain expression, and ask for an output from a given input or the input that gives a stated output.","code":"A7"}],"examples":[{"title":"Substituting a negative value into a scientific formula","setup":"The kinetic energy of a moving object is E = ½mv², where m is its mass in kilograms and v is its velocity in m/s. A trolley of mass m = 6 kg is rolling backwards, so its velocity is v = −4 m/s. Work out the value of E, in joules.","steps":["Replace each letter by its value, keeping the negative one in brackets: E = ½ × 6 × (−4)²","Powers are worked out before multiplying, and (−4)² = −4 × −4 = 16","Now multiply from the left: ½ × 6 = 3, so E = 3 × 16","E = 48 J"],"cues":["Why must the velocity be written inside brackets here?","Which part of this calculation is done first?","What is left to work out once the square is known?","What unit must be attached to the value?"]},{"title":"Expanding and simplifying a product of two brackets","setup":"Expand and simplify the product (2x + 5)(x − 4), writing the answer as a quadratic expression with its terms in descending powers of x.","steps":["Every term in the left bracket multiplies every term in the right: 2x × x = 2x², 2x × (−4) = −8x, 5 × x = 5x, 5 × (−4) = −20","Written in a row, the four products give 2x² − 8x + 5x − 20","The x terms are like terms and combine: −8x + 5x = −3x","So (2x + 5)(x − 4) = 2x² − 3x − 20"],"cues":["How many separate products will multiplying out give?","Which of the four products are negative?","Which two of the four terms can be joined together?","Will the coefficient of x end up positive or negative?"]},{"title":"Changing the subject of a perimeter formula","setup":"The perimeter P of a rectangle of length l and width w is given by the formula P = 2(l + w). Rearrange this formula to make w the subject.","steps":["The bracket is multiplied by 2 last, so undo that first: divide both sides by 2 to get P/2 = l + w","The length is added to w, so subtract l from both sides: P/2 − l = w","Turn the formula round so the subject is written first: w = P/2 − l"],"cues":["Which operation is applied to the bracket last?","What is still attached to w after the first move?","On which side should the new subject be written?"]},{"title":"Deciding whether a statement is an identity","setup":"Priya says that 5(x + 2) − 3(x − 1) is the same as 2x + 13 for every value of x. Show whether Priya is right, and say whether her statement is an equation or an identity.","steps":["Multiply out the left bracket: 5(x + 2) = 5x + 10","Multiply out the right bracket, remembering the sign in front of it: −3(x − 1) = −3x + 3","Collect like terms: 5x − 3x = 2x and 10 + 3 = 13, so the whole expression is 2x + 13","That is exactly the expression Priya gave, and it came from simplifying, not from choosing a value, so she is right for every value of x","A statement true for every value is an identity, written 5(x + 2) − 3(x − 1) ≡ 2x + 13"],"cues":["What must happen to both brackets before anything can be compared?","What sign will the number term from the second bracket have?","Which terms in the four can be joined together?","Why is testing x = 1 not enough to settle this?","Which word describes a statement true for all values?"]},{"title":"Factorising fully by taking out the common factor","setup":"Factorise fully 6a²b + 15ab², giving your answer as a product of a single term and a bracket.","steps":["The highest common factor of 6 and 15 is 3, and both terms contain at least one a and one b, so the common factor is 3ab","Divide each term by that factor to find what goes inside the bracket: 6a²b ÷ 3ab = 2a and 15ab² ÷ 3ab = 5b","Write the common factor outside and the results inside: 6a²b + 15ab² = 3ab(2a + 5b)","Expanding checks it: 3ab × 2a = 6a²b and 3ab × 5b = 15ab², so the factorisation is right and nothing is left to take out"],"cues":["What number and what letters divide into both terms?","How do you find what belongs inside the bracket?","Where does the common factor go in the answer?","How can a factorisation be checked in one line?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write 7 × y × y × y in conventional algebraic notation.","a":"7y³"},{"q":"Work out the value of 3a − b² when a = 5 and b = −3.","a":"6"},{"q":"Is 4(x + 1) = 4x + 4 an equation or an identity? Give the reason.","a":"An identity — both sides simplify to 4x + 4, so it is true for every value of x"},{"q":"Make h the subject of the formula A = ½bh.","a":"h = 2A/b"},{"q":"Simplify 8c + 5d − 3c − 9d.","a":"5c − 4d"}]},"room":{"facts":["The notation conventions: ab means a × b; 3y means y + y + y and 3 × y; a² means a × a; a²b means a × a × b; a/b means a ÷ b; brackets group a whole quantity.","A coefficient is written as a fraction, not a decimal: x/4, never 0.25x.","Substitution order: brackets, then powers, then × and ÷, then + and −, with every negative value put in brackets, so (−4)² = 16 but −4² = −16.","An expression has no equals sign; an equation is true for particular values; an identity (≡) is true for all values; a formula gives a rule for its subject; an inequality compares with <, ≤, > or ≥.","Laws of indices: xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ; xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ; (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ; x⁰ = 1.","x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q) where p × q = c and p + q = b; the difference of two squares is x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a).","To change the subject, apply the inverse operation to both sides in turn until the wanted letter stands alone on one side and appears only once."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Write each of these in conventional algebraic notation: (i) t + t + t + t, (ii) m × m × n.","a":"(i) 4t (ii) m²n","sol":"(i) Four lots of t is 4 × t, and the multiplication sign is left out with the number written in front: 4t (B1). (ii) m × m is written as a power, m², and the remaining letter is written next to it with no sign: m²n (B1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Repeated addition of one letter, and repeated multiplication of one letter, each have a shorter standard form.","Count how many lots of the letter the first one has, and how many times the letter multiplies itself in the second.","Write the first as a number in front of t with the sign left out, and the second as a power of m written next to n."],"codes":["A1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write a ÷ b in conventional algebraic notation, and rewrite the expression 0.25x so that its coefficient is written as a fraction.","a":"a/b and x/4","sol":"A division is written as a fraction with the number being divided on top: a ÷ b = a/b (B1). A coefficient is written as a fraction rather than a decimal, and 0.25 = 25/100 = 1/4, so 0.25x is written x/4 (B1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Divisions and decimal coefficients each have a preferred written form in algebra.","A fraction bar does the same job as ÷, and a decimal coefficient is the same as a fraction of the letter.","Write the division with a horizontal bar, and convert the decimal to a fraction in its lowest terms before attaching the letter."],"codes":["A1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Here are four statements: 5y − 2, 5y − 2 = 13, C = 5y, y > 2. Write down which one is a formula, and write down what kind of statement 5y − 2 = 13 is.","a":"C = 5y is the formula; 5y − 2 = 13 is an equation","sol":"A formula gives a rule for working out one named quantity from another, so it has a subject standing alone on one side: C = 5y (B1). 5y − 2 = 13 has an equals sign but is true only for the particular value y = 3, so it is an equation, not an identity (B1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Look at the signs first: two of the four have an equals sign, one has an inequality sign and one has neither.","A formula works out one named quantity from others; an equation with an equals sign holds only for particular values.","Ask of each statement in turn whether it has an equals sign, and if it does, whether it tells you how to work out a named quantity."],"codes":["A3"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify 7a + 4b − 3a + 2b.","a":"4a + 6b","sol":"Collect the a terms, keeping the sign in front of each: 7a − 3a = 4a (B1). Collect the b terms: 4b + 2b = 6b (B1). The two results are unlike terms, so the answer is left as 4a + 6b. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Only terms with exactly the same letter can be joined together.","Deal with the a terms and the b terms separately, and keep the sign that sits in front of each term with it.","Rewrite the expression with the a terms next to each other and the b terms next to each other before combining anything."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) The formula v = u + at gives the velocity v, in m/s, of an object t seconds after it starts. Work out v when u = 3.2 m/s, a = 1.5 m/s² and t = 4 s.","a":"9.2 m/s","sol":"Replace each letter by its value: v = 3.2 + 1.5 × 4 (M1). Multiplication comes before addition, so 1.5 × 4 = 6 and v = 3.2 + 6 = 9.2 m/s (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Each letter in the formula stands for one of the values listed in the question.","Once the values are in place, the priority of operations decides which part is worked out first.","Write the formula out with each letter replaced by its value, then evaluate the product before the addition."],"codes":["A2"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A function takes an input x and gives the output 4x − 5. Write down the output when the input is 6, and the output when the input is −1.","a":"19 and −9","sol":"Substitute x = 6: 4 × 6 − 5 = 24 − 5 = 19 (B1). Substitute x = −1, keeping the negative input in brackets: 4 × (−1) − 5 = −4 − 5 = −9 (B1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The expression works like a machine: whatever goes in is multiplied, then something is taken away.","Put each input in place of x and carry out the two operations in the order the expression sets.","Write 4 × (the input) − 5 for each of the two inputs, putting a negative input in brackets."],"codes":["A7","A2"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A rectangle has length (x + 7) cm and width (x − 2) cm. Write an expression, in its simplest form, for the area of the rectangle in cm².","a":"x² + 5x − 14","sol":"Area = length × width = (x + 7)(x − 2). Every term in the first bracket multiplies every term in the second: x² − 2x + 7x − 14 (M1 for at least three of the four products correct). Collect the like terms, −2x + 7x = 5x, giving an area of x² + 5x − 14 cm² (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The area of a rectangle is worked out the same way whether its sides are numbers or expressions.","Multiplying two brackets means each term of one multiplies each term of the other, giving four products to collect.","Write the area as the product of the two bracketed sides, then multiply them out term by term before collecting."],"codes":["A4","A1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Factorise x² + 7x + 12.","a":"(x + 3)(x + 4)","sol":"The expression factorises as (x + p)(x + q) where p × q = 12 and p + q = 7. The pairs of factors of 12 are 1 and 12, 2 and 6, 3 and 4, and only the last pair adds to 7 (M1). So x² + 7x + 12 = (x + 3)(x + 4) (A1), which expanding confirms. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["A quadratic in this form comes from multiplying two brackets, each beginning with x.","The two numbers in the brackets multiply to give the number term and add to give the coefficient of x.","List the pairs of whole numbers whose product is the number term, then test which pair has the right sum."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify (p⁵ × p⁴) ÷ p³.","a":"p⁶","sol":"Multiplying powers of the same letter adds the indices: p⁵ × p⁴ = p⁵⁺⁴ = p⁹ (M1). Dividing subtracts them: p⁹ ÷ p³ = p⁹⁻³ = p⁶ (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["All three powers are powers of the same letter, so the laws of indices apply throughout.","One law handles the multiplication and a second handles the division; work the bracket out first.","Add the indices of the two powers being multiplied, then subtract the index of the power being divided by."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out the value of 5x² − 3y when x = −3 and y = −2.","a":"51","sol":"Substitute, keeping each negative value in brackets: 5 × (−3)² − 3 × (−2). Powers first: (−3)² = 9, so the first term is 5 × 9 = 45, and the second is −3 × (−2) = +6 (M1 for either term correct). Adding them: 45 + 6 = 51 (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["A squared negative number and a subtracted negative number both need care in this one.","Replace the letters first, with brackets round each negative value, then follow the priority of operations.","Write 5 × (the x value in brackets)² − 3 × (the y value in brackets) before working anything out."],"codes":["A2"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) Make t the subject of the formula v = u + at.","a":"t = (v − u)/a","sol":"The term at is added to u, so subtract u from both sides: v − u = at (M1). t is multiplied by a, so divide both sides by a: (v − u)/a = t (A1). Written with the subject first, t = (v − u)/a. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The letter you want is inside a product that is being added to another letter.","Undo the operations in the reverse of the order they were applied, doing the same to both sides each time.","Take the letter that is added to the at term away from both sides first, then deal with what multiplies t."],"codes":["A5"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Show that 3(2y − 1) + 5 is equal to 6y + 2 for every value of y, and name the kind of statement this makes.","a":"3(2y − 1) + 5 = 6y − 3 + 5 = 6y + 2, so the statement is an identity","sol":"Expand the bracket: 3 × 2y = 6y and 3 × (−1) = −3, giving 6y − 3 + 5 (M1). Collect the number terms, −3 + 5 = 2, so the left-hand side simplifies to 6y + 2, which is exactly the right-hand side (A1). The two sides agree for every value of y, not just particular ones, so the statement is an identity and may be written 3(2y − 1) + 5 ≡ 6y + 2 (B1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Showing two expressions are always equal means simplifying one of them until it matches the other.","Multiply the bracket out, collect the number terms, and compare the result with the expression on the right.","Multiply each term inside the bracket by the number in front of it, leaving the + 5 where it stands."],"codes":["A6","A4","A3"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) The formula C = 5/9 (F − 32) changes a temperature F, in degrees Fahrenheit, into the temperature C, in degrees Celsius. Rearrange the formula to make F the subject, and use it to work out F when C = 25.","a":"F = 9C/5 + 32, and F = 77","sol":"The bracket is multiplied by 5/9, so multiply both sides by its reciprocal 9/5: 9C/5 = F − 32 (M1). Add 32 to both sides: F = 9C/5 + 32 (A1). Substitute C = 25: F = 9 × 25 ÷ 5 + 32 = 45 + 32 (M1). F = 77 (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["F sits inside a bracket that is being multiplied by a fraction, so that fraction has to come off first.","Undo the multiplication by the fraction by multiplying both sides by its reciprocal, then undo the subtraction inside the bracket.","Multiply both sides by 9/5 to clear the fraction, then deal with the number that is being subtracted from F."],"codes":["A5","A2"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The sides of a triangle are (3x + 1) cm, (2x − 3) cm and (x + 5) cm. Write an expression in its simplest form for the perimeter of the triangle, and work out the perimeter when x = 4.","a":"Perimeter = 6x + 3; when x = 4 the perimeter is 27 cm","sol":"The perimeter is the sum of the three sides: (3x + 1) + (2x − 3) + (x + 5) (M1). Collect the x terms, 3x + 2x + x = 6x, and the number terms, 1 − 3 + 5 = 3, giving 6x + 3 (A1). Substitute x = 4 into the simplified expression: 6 × 4 + 3 (M1). This gives 24 + 3 = 27 cm (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A perimeter is found in the same way whether the sides are numbers or expressions.","Add the three side expressions and collect like terms, then substitute the given value into the simplified expression.","Write the three side expressions in a row joined by + signs, then group the x terms and the number terms."],"codes":["A4","A2","A1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Priya adds 3(2n + 1) to 4(n − 3), where n is a whole number. Show that her result is always an odd number.","a":"The sum is 10n − 9, which is always odd because 10n is always even and an even number minus an odd number is odd","sol":"Expand both brackets: 3(2n + 1) = 6n + 3 and 4(n − 3) = 4n − 12 (M1). Add them and collect like terms: 6n + 4n = 10n and 3 − 12 = −9, so the sum is 10n − 9 (A1). 10n is a multiple of 10, so it is even for every whole number n (B1). An even number take away an odd number is odd, so 10n − 9 is odd whatever whole number n is (B1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Nothing can be decided while the brackets are still there, so the two expressions must first become one.","Expand and collect, then look at whether each part of the result is always even or always odd.","Multiply out each bracket by the number in front of it and write the four terms in a row before collecting."],"codes":["A6","A4"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) The area of a circle is given by A = πr², where r is the radius. Make r the subject of this formula, and use it to find the radius of a circle of area 120 cm², giving your answer to 1 decimal place.","a":"r = √(A/π), and r = 6.2 cm","sol":"Divide both sides by π to leave the square on its own: A/π = r² (M1). Take the positive square root of both sides, since a radius is a length: r = √(A/π) (A1). Substitute A = 120: r = √(120 ÷ π) = √38.197… (M1). r = 6.180… = 6.2 cm to 1 decimal place (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The area formula itself is given to you, so the work is in turning it round and then substituting.","Undo the multiplication by π first, and then undo the squaring by the inverse operation.","Divide both sides by π so that r² stands alone, then apply the inverse of squaring to both sides."],"codes":["A5","A2"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 4 × p × p × q in conventional algebraic notation.","a":"4p²q"},{"q":"Work out the value of 2x² + y when x = −5 and y = 3.","a":"53"},{"q":"Factorise x² − 36.","a":"(x + 6)(x − 6)"},{"q":"Make b the subject of the formula P = a + 2b.","a":"b = (P − a)/2"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The conventions of algebraic notation","back":"ab means a × b; 3y means y + y + y and 3 × y; a² means a × a; a²b means a × a × b; a/b means a ÷ b.","code":"A1","open":false},{"front":"Coefficient","back":"The number multiplying a letter in a term: in 7x it is 7, in −3x² it is −3. A coefficient is written as a fraction, x/4, not as a decimal, 0.25x.","code":"A1","open":false},{"front":"Term (in an algebraic expression)","back":"A part of an expression separated from the next by + or −. In 5x² − 3x + 7 the terms are 5x², −3x and 7.","code":"A3","open":false},{"front":"Factor of an expression","back":"Something the expression divides by exactly. 3 and (x + 2) are factors of 3x + 6, because 3x + 6 = 3(x + 2).","code":"A3","open":false},{"front":"Expression","back":"A collection of terms with no equals sign, such as 4x − 9. It can be simplified or evaluated, but it cannot be solved.","code":"A3","open":false},{"front":"Equation","back":"A statement with an equals sign that is true only for particular values, such as 3x + 1 = 7, which holds only when x = 2.","code":"A3","open":false},{"front":"Identity","back":"A statement true for every value of the letter, written with ≡, such as 2(x + 3) ≡ 2x + 6. Both sides simplify to the same expression.","code":"A6","open":false},{"front":"Formula","back":"A rule connecting quantities, with its subject standing alone on one side, such as A = πr² or v = u + at. Substituting the other letters gives the subject's value.","code":"A5","open":false},{"front":"Inequality","back":"A statement comparing two expressions with <, ≤, > or ≥, such as y > 2, which is true for a range of values rather than for one.","code":"A3","open":false},{"front":"Priority of operations when substituting","back":"Brackets, then powers, then × and ÷, then + and −. Write a negative value in brackets first: (−4)² = 16, but −4² = −16.","code":"A2","open":false},{"front":"Laws of indices","back":"xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ; xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ; (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ; x⁰ = 1.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Difference of two squares","back":"x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a). So x² − 25 factorises as (x + 5)(x − 5).","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Factorising x² + bx + c","back":"Find two numbers with product c and sum b, then write (x + p)(x + q). For x² + 9x + 20 the numbers are 4 and 5.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Changing the subject of a formula","back":"Apply the inverse operation to both sides in turn until the wanted letter stands alone on one side and appears only once.","code":"A5","open":false},{"front":"An expression read as a function","back":"3x − 1 acts as a machine: an input x gives an output. Substituting finds the output; undoing the operations in reverse traces an output back to its input.","code":"A7","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Preamble: none of this room's own notation, index and factorising results is printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid (W87990A), the insert in every Foundation paper, so every item below marked Remember must be written from memory; the items marked Printed are given to you on that sheet, so a question that substitutes into one of them, or rearranges it, prints it for you.","Remember: ab means a × b — the multiplication sign is left out. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: 3y means y + y + y, and also 3 × y. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: a² means a × a. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: a³ means a × a × a. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: a²b means a × a × b. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: a/b means a ÷ b — a division is written as a fraction. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: a coefficient is written as a fraction, x/4, never as a decimal, 0.25x. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: brackets hold a whole quantity together, so 3(x + 2) means three lots of the whole of x + 2. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: the priority of operations when you substitute — brackets, then powers, then × and ÷, then + and −. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: put a negative value in brackets when you substitute it, so that (−4)² = 16 while −4² = −16. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ. The Foundation sheet says nothing about the laws of indices, so remember it.","Remember: xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: x⁰ = 1. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q), where p × q = c and p + q = b. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: the difference of two squares, x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a). Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: to change the subject, apply the inverse operation to both sides in turn until the wanted letter stands alone and appears only once. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: area of a triangle = ½ × base × height, the formula behind A = ½bh. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, where a and b are the parallel sides and h their perpendicular separation.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: volume of a prism = area of cross section × length.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d the diameter.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: area of a circle = πr², the formula this room's stretch question asks you to rearrange.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: Pythagoras' theorem a² + b² = c², in a right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is c.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: sin A = a/c, in a right-angled triangle ABC whose hypotenuse is c and whose side a is opposite angle A.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: cos A = b/c, where b is the side adjacent to angle A and c the hypotenuse.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: tan A = a/b, where a is opposite angle A and b adjacent to it.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, where P is the principal, r the interest rate per period and n the number of periods.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B)."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T11:43:19.036509+00:00","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.86,"notes":"Every answer key, solution line and mark tally checks out (all 16 bank questions, the 4 exit questions, the 5 lesson checks and all 5 worked examples re-solved in sympy), A1–A7 are covered in order with nothing beyond Foundation — the only repairs needed are to the wording of the Formulae sheet extra."}}},"algebra-graphs":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"algebra-graphs","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Graphs turn algebra into a picture, and the picture is where the marks are. Algebra carries 17–23% of the Foundation papers, and any of the three papers can ask for a line plotted, a gradient read off, an equation found from two points, the roots and turning point of a quadratic, one of the four standard shapes recognised, or a speed taken from a distance–time graph. These are marks worth having: the methods are short and repeatable, and the same idea — the gradient is the rate, the intercept is the starting value — comes back in the ratio, sequences and equations rooms and again in science. This room gives you the four-quadrant grid, y = mx + c read in both directions, the three features of a parabola, the shapes of the standard functions and the reading of a real graph, with the line of working the mark scheme rewards written out each time.","idea":[{"h":"Coordinates in all four quadrants","t":"The x axis and the y axis cut the plane into four quadrants. A point is always written (x, y): x first, read across, then y, read up or down. In the first quadrant both coordinates are positive; in the second x is negative and y positive; in the third both are negative; in the fourth x is positive and y negative. Because the axes carry negative numbers, a distance is the difference of the coordinates: the horizontal line from (−4, 3) to (2, 3) has length 2 − (−4) = 6. The midpoint of a segment is the mean of the x coordinates paired with the mean of the y coordinates, and a described move changes one coordinate at a time — 5 left takes 5 off x, 4 down takes 4 off y. Examiners ask you to plot a point, read one off, give the point reached after a move, and find a midpoint.","code":"A8"},{"h":"Straight-line graphs and their equations","t":"Any equation that can be rearranged into y = mx + c has a straight line for its graph. To plot one, choose three x values (include a negative one), work out y for each, plot the three points and rule a line through them — the third point is the check that the first two are right. Working the other way, the equation comes from the gradient and the intercept: with two points, find m = change in y ÷ change in x, write y = mx + c with that m in it, substitute one of the points to find c, then test the other point. Parallel lines share the same m, so the line through (0, 1) parallel to y = 4x + 7 is y = 4x + 1. A point lies on a line only if its coordinates satisfy the equation, and that is how a missing coordinate is found.","code":"A9"},{"h":"Gradients and intercepts of linear functions","t":"In y = mx + c the gradient m says how much y changes for every 1 that x increases by, and c says where the line cuts the y axis, at the point (0, c). Read both straight off the equation once it is in that form: y = 6 − 5x is y = −5x + 6, so the gradient is −5, the line slopes down, and it crosses the y axis at (0, 6). From a drawn line, choose two points whose coordinates can be read exactly and divide the change in y by the change in x. In a real context the gradient is a rate — pounds per gigabyte, kilometres per hour, degrees per minute — and the intercept is the starting value, the fixed charge or the amount already there. The mark is for the statement made in the context, with its unit, not for the bare number.","code":"A10"},{"h":"Roots, intercepts and turning points of quadratics","t":"A quadratic has a parabola for its graph, and three features are asked for. The roots are the x values where the curve meets the x axis, so they come from putting y = 0 and factorising: x² − 4x + 3 = (x − 1)(x − 3), a bracket is zero at x = 1 and at x = 3, and the curve crosses at (1, 0) and (3, 0). The y intercept is the value at x = 0, which is the number on the end of the expression. The turning point is the vertex and it sits on the line of symmetry; when the curve has two roots that line runs exactly halfway between them, so take the mean of the roots and substitute it back for the y coordinate. When the curve does not cross the x axis there are no roots to average, so complete the table of values and take two x values that give the same y — the line of symmetry is halfway between those two instead. From a drawn graph the same three features are read off to the accuracy of the grid, and a question may ask for one read and one deduced.","code":"A11"},{"h":"Recognising and sketching the standard graphs","t":"Four shapes must be known by sight. y = mx + c is a straight line, sloping up when m is positive and down when m is negative. y = ax² + bx + c is a parabola, a U-shape when a is positive and an upside-down U when a is negative, symmetrical about a vertical line through its turning point. A simple cubic such as y = x³ is an S-shaped curve through the origin, rising from bottom left to top right because the x³ term is positive, and it flattens at the origin rather than turning there. The reciprocal y = 1/x, with x ≠ 0, is two separate branches, one in the first quadrant and one in the third, and it never touches either axis because x = 0 gives no value of y and no x makes y equal 0. A sketch needs the right shape and the intercepts marked; it is not plotted point by point.","code":"A12"},{"h":"Graphs in real contexts","t":"In a real graph the axes carry quantities, so every value read off has a unit and every gradient is a rate. On a distance–time graph the gradient is the speed: a steeper line is faster, and a horizontal line means the object is not moving. On a speed–time graph the gradient is the acceleration, in m/s² when speed is in m/s and time in seconds, and a downward slope is a deceleration. Other contexts behave the same way: pounds against gigabytes gives a price per gigabyte, degrees against minutes gives a cooling rate. Curved graphs, reciprocal graphs included, are read for approximate answers — find the value on one axis, go across or up to the curve and read the other. Always give the answer in the units of the context.","code":"A14"}],"examples":[{"title":"Midpoint and a move on a four-quadrant grid","setup":"A(−4, 3) and B(2, −5) are plotted on a grid whose axes both run from −6 to 6. M is the midpoint of AB. Work out the coordinates of M, then write down the coordinates of the point P reached by starting at M and moving 3 squares right and 6 squares up.","steps":["The x coordinate of a midpoint is the mean of the two x coordinates: (−4 + 2) ÷ 2 = −2 ÷ 2 = −1.","The y coordinate is the mean of the two y coordinates: (3 + (−5)) ÷ 2 = −2 ÷ 2 = −1, so M is (−1, −1), the line the method mark is given for.","Moving right adds to the x coordinate: −1 + 3 = 2; moving up adds to the y coordinate: −1 + 6 = 5.","Both coordinates of P must fit the axes given: 2 and 5 each lie between −6 and 6, so P(2, 5) is on the grid."],"cues":["How do you get from two x coordinates to the one exactly between them?","What is the mean of 3 and −5?","Which coordinate does a move to the right change, and by how much?","The grid runs from −6 to 6 on both axes — does the point reached still fit on it?"]},{"title":"The equation of the line through two points","setup":"A straight line passes through the points (1, 4) and (3, 10). Find the equation of the line in the form y = mx + c.","steps":["Gradient m = change in y ÷ change in x = (10 − 4) ÷ (3 − 1) = 6 ÷ 2 = 3.","So the line is y = 3x + c, which is the line the method mark is given for.","Substitute the point (1, 4) into it: 4 = 3 × 1 + c.","3 × 1 = 3, so 4 = 3 + c and c = 4 − 3 = 1.","The equation is y = 3x + 1, and the other point checks: 3 × 3 + 1 = 10."],"cues":["Which two differences do you need before you can find the gradient?","With the gradient known, what part of y = mx + c is still missing?","Which pair of values can you put into the equation to pin down c?","What is left once 3 × 1 has been worked out?","How can you test the equation on the second point?"]},{"title":"Reading a quadratic: intercept, roots and turning point","setup":"The curve y = x² − 4x + 3 is drawn for values of x from −1 to 5. Write down the coordinates of the point where the curve crosses the y axis, find the coordinates of the two points where it crosses the x axis, and work out the coordinates of its turning point.","steps":["On the y axis x = 0, so y = 0² − 4 × 0 + 3 = 3 and the curve crosses at (0, 3).","On the x axis y = 0, so factorise: x² − 4x + 3 = (x − 1)(x − 3) = 0, the line the method mark is given for.","A product is zero only when a bracket is zero, so x = 1 or x = 3, and the crossings are (1, 0) and (3, 0).","A parabola is symmetrical, so the turning point is halfway between the roots: x = (1 + 3) ÷ 2 = 2.","At x = 2, y = 2² − 4 × 2 + 3 = 4 − 8 + 3 = −1, so the curve crosses the y axis at (0, 3), crosses the x axis at (1, 0) and (3, 0), and has its turning point at (2, −1)."],"cues":["What is x worth at every point of the y axis?","Which two numbers multiply to 3 and add to −4?","What must be true of a bracket if the product is zero?","Where does the line of symmetry sit compared with the two roots?","What is y when that x value is substituted?"]},{"title":"Speed and a stop on a distance–time graph","setup":"A van's journey is shown on a distance–time graph, with time in hours across and distance from the depot in km up. The graph is a straight line from (0, 0) to (1.5, 60), a horizontal line from (1.5, 60) to (2, 60), and a straight line from (2, 60) down to (3.5, 0). Work out the speed on the first stage in km/h, say how long the van was stopped, and work out the speed on the return stage.","steps":["Speed is the gradient here, and on the first stage the van covers 60 − 0 = 60 km in 1.5 − 0 = 1.5 hours.","First stage speed = 60 ÷ 1.5 = 40 km/h, and this division is the line the method mark is given for.","Along the flat section the distance does not change, so the van is stopped for 2 − 1.5 = 0.5 hours, or 30 minutes.","The return stage runs from 2 hours to 3.5 hours, taking 1.5 hours, and the distance falls by 60 km back to 0 km.","Return stage speed = 60 ÷ 1.5 = 40 km/h, so the van did 40 km/h on the first stage, was stopped for 30 minutes and returned at 40 km/h."],"cues":["Which two readings do you take off the axes for the first stage?","How do you turn a distance and a time into a speed?","What is happening to the distance while the line is flat?","How long does the return stage take?","What speed does that distance over that time give?"]},{"title":"A line parallel to a given line","setup":"The line L has equation y = 4 − 3x. Write down the gradient of L and the coordinates of the point where L crosses the y axis, then find the equation of the line that is parallel to L and passes through (2, 5).","steps":["Rearranged into the form y = mx + c, L is y = −3x + 4.","So the gradient of L is −3, and L crosses the y axis at (0, 4).","Parallel lines have equal gradients, so the new line is y = −3x + c, the line the method mark is given for.","Substituting x = 2 and y = 5 gives 5 = −3 × 2 + c, that is 5 = −6 + c.","So c = 5 + 6 = 11 and the parallel line is y = −3x + 11."],"cues":["What has to happen to 4 − 3x before m and c can be read off?","Which number in that form is the gradient, and which fixes the crossing point?","What does the new line share with L?","Which values go into the new equation to pin down its c?","What is c once −6 is moved across?"]},{"title":"The reciprocal graph y = 1/x","setup":"A table of values is being completed for y = 1/x, where x cannot be 0. The x values in the table are −2, −1, −0.5, 0.5, 1 and 2. Work out the six y values, say which two quadrants the branches of the curve lie in, and explain why the curve has no point on the y axis.","steps":["Each y value is 1 ÷ x: 1 ÷ (−2) = −0.5, 1 ÷ (−1) = −1 and 1 ÷ (−0.5) = −2.","For the positive x values: 1 ÷ 0.5 = 2, 1 ÷ 1 = 1 and 1 ÷ 2 = 0.5.","Every negative x gives a negative y, so those points sit in the third quadrant, and every positive x gives a positive y, so the other branch sits in the first quadrant.","x = 0 would mean 1 ÷ 0, which has no value, so the curve has no point on the y axis and simply gets closer to it without ever touching it — so the six y values are −0.5, −1, −2, 2, 1 and 0.5, the branches lie in the third and first quadrants, and the curve never reaches the y axis."],"cues":["What does the equation tell you to do to each x value?","What are the y values for the three positive x values?","What sign does y take when x is negative?","Why can x = 0 not be put into this equation?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which quadrant does the point (−3, −4) lie in?","a":"The third quadrant"},{"q":"Work out the gradient of the line through (2, 1) and (6, 9).","a":"2"},{"q":"Find the roots of y = x² − x − 6.","a":"x = 3 and x = −2"},{"q":"A distance–time graph is a straight line from (0, 0) to (4 hours, 30 km). What speed does it show?","a":"7.5 km/h"},{"q":"Describe the shape of the graph of y = 1/x, where x ≠ 0.","a":"Two separate branches, one in the first quadrant and one in the third, neither touching the axes"}]},"room":{"facts":["Gradient = change in y ÷ change in x = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁); the gradient is positive for a line sloping up to the right, negative for one sloping down and 0 for a horizontal line.","In y = mx + c, m is the gradient and c is the y intercept, so the line crosses the y axis at (0, c).","Parallel lines have equal gradients: same m, different c.","The midpoint of the segment joining (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂) is ((x₁ + x₂)/2, (y₁ + y₂)/2).","A graph crosses the y axis at the value it takes when x = 0, and the x axis wherever y = 0.","For a quadratic that has two roots, factorise and set each bracket to zero for them; the turning point lies on the line of symmetry, halfway between the roots, so substitute the mean of the roots back in for its y coordinate. When the curve has no roots, use two x values that give the same y and take the mean of those instead.","Speed is the gradient of a distance–time graph and acceleration is the gradient of a speed–time graph; a horizontal distance–time line means the object is at rest.","Standard shapes: y = mx + c a straight line, y = ax² + bx + c a parabola (U-shaped when a > 0), y = x³ an S-shaped curve rising through the origin and flattening there, y = 1/x two branches that never meet either axis."],"questions":[{"q":"Start at the point (3, 2). Move 5 squares left and 4 squares down. Write down the coordinates of the point you reach. (non-calculator)","a":"(−2, −2)","sol":"A move left or right changes only the x coordinate and a move up or down only the y coordinate. Left 5: 3 − 5 = −2. Down 4: 2 − 4 = −2. B1 for (−2, −2), written as a coordinate pair.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A sideways move and a vertical move touch different coordinates.","Take the sideways move off the x coordinate and the downward move off the y coordinate.","The first line writes the x coordinate with the sideways move subtracted, and then the same for the y coordinate."],"codes":["A8"]},{"q":"The temperature T °C inside an oven t minutes after it is switched on is shown by a straight line on a graph, and the line has equation T = 15t + 20. (a) Write down what the 15 tells you about the oven. (b) Write down what the 20 tells you about the oven. (non-calculator)","a":"(a) The temperature rises by 15 °C every minute (b) The oven was at 20 °C when it was switched on","sol":"Comparing T = 15t + 20 with y = mx + c, the gradient is 15 and the intercept is 20. (a) The gradient is a rate, so the temperature goes up by 15 °C for every extra minute. B1 for the rise per minute, with the unit. (b) The intercept is the value at t = 0, so the oven was at 20 °C when it was switched on. B1 for the starting temperature.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["In a real context one of the two numbers in the equation is a rate and the other is a starting value.","Decide which number is the gradient and which is the intercept, then say what each one means in degrees and minutes.","The first line sets the equation beside y = mx + c and labels which number is m and which is c, before either is put into words."],"codes":["A10"]},{"q":"Work out the coordinates of the point where the line y = 3x − 12 crosses the x axis. (non-calculator)","a":"(4, 0)","sol":"Every point on the x axis has y = 0, so 3x − 12 = 0. M1 for 3x − 12 = 0. Adding 12 to both sides gives 3x = 12, and dividing by 3 gives x = 4, so the line crosses at (4, 0). A1 for (4, 0) as a coordinate pair.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what the y coordinate is worth at every point of the x axis.","Put that value in place of y, then solve the equation you are left with for x.","The first line sets the expression in x equal to the y value the x axis forces, before it is solved."],"codes":["A9"]},{"q":"The curve y = x² − 6x + 8 crosses the x axis at two points. Work out the coordinates of both points. (non-calculator)","a":"(2, 0) and (4, 0)","sol":"On the x axis y = 0, so x² − 6x + 8 = 0. Two numbers multiplying to 8 and adding to −6 are −2 and −4, so (x − 2)(x − 4) = 0. M1 for (x − 2)(x − 4). A product is zero only when a bracket is zero, so x = 2 or x = 4. A1 for (2, 0) and (4, 0) as coordinate pairs.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what y is worth at every point of the x axis.","Set the expression equal to 0 and factorise it into two brackets.","The first line is x² − 6x + 8 = 0, then the pair of brackets whose numbers multiply to 8."],"codes":["A11"]},{"q":"Here are four equations. A: y = 2x + 1   B: y = x²   C: y = x³   D: y = 1/x (x ≠ 0). Write down the letter of the equation whose graph is a parabola, and the letter of the equation whose graph is in two separate branches that never touch the axes. (non-calculator)","a":"Parabola: B; two branches: D","sol":"A squared term with no higher power gives a parabola, so B. B1 for B. The reciprocal function has two branches, one in the first quadrant and one in the third, approaching both axes without meeting them, so D. B1 for D.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The highest power of x in an equation decides the shape of its graph.","Sort the four equations by their highest power, and treat 1/x as the odd one out.","The first line writes beside each equation the highest power of x it contains, noting that in 1/x the x sits underneath instead; the family of each graph follows from that."],"codes":["A12"]},{"q":"The point (k, 13) lies on the line y = 5x − 2. Work out the value of k. (non-calculator)","a":"k = 3","sol":"A point on a line satisfies its equation, so putting x = k and y = 13 gives 13 = 5k − 2. M1 for 13 = 5k − 2. Adding 2 to both sides gives 15 = 5k, and dividing by 5 gives k = 3. A1 for 3.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["If a point is on a line, its two coordinates fit the equation.","Put the coordinates into the equation and undo the operations to reach k.","The first line puts the y value on the left and 5k − 2 on the right."],"codes":["A9"]},{"q":"A candle burns at a steady rate. Its height h cm is plotted against the time t hours since it was lit, and the points lie on a straight line through (1, 17) and (4, 11). Find the equation of the line in the form h = mt + c, and write down the height of the candle when it was lit. (non-calculator)","a":"h = −2t + 19; the candle was 19 cm tall when it was lit","sol":"Gradient = change in h ÷ change in t = (11 − 17) ÷ (4 − 1). M1 for a correct difference in h over a correct difference in t. That is −6 ÷ 3 = −2. A1 for m = −2. Substituting (1, 17) into h = −2t + c gives 17 = −2 × 1 + c, that is 17 = −2 + c. M1 for substituting either point into h = −2t + c. So c = 19, the line is h = −2t + 19, and the height when lit is the value at t = 0, namely 19 cm. A1 for h = −2t + 19 with a height of 19 cm (check on the second point: −2 × 4 + 19 = 11).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Two points on the line fix how fast the candle shortens, and the height when it was lit is its height before any time has passed.","Find the gradient from the two differences, use one point to find c, then read the starting height off the equation.","The first line is the change in height over the change in time between the two given points."],"codes":["A9","A10"]},{"q":"The curve y = x² − 2x − 15 has a turning point. Work out its coordinates. (non-calculator)","a":"(1, −16)","sol":"Find the roots first: −15 = (−5) × 3 and −2 = −5 + 3, so x² − 2x − 15 = (x − 5)(x + 3). M1 for (x − 5)(x + 3). Setting each bracket to zero gives x = 5 and x = −3. A1 for both roots. A parabola is symmetrical, so the turning point lies halfway between the roots: x = (5 + (−3)) ÷ 2 = 1. M1 for taking the mean of the roots and substituting it. At x = 1, y = 1 − 2 − 15 = −16. A1 for (1, −16).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The turning point sits on the curve's line of symmetry.","Get the roots by factorising, then use the symmetry of the parabola to place the line of symmetry.","The first line is the factorised form of x² − 2x − 15 set equal to 0."],"codes":["A11"]},{"q":"Water is run into a paddling pool. The volume V litres in the pool is plotted against the time t minutes, and the graph is a straight line from (0, 0) to (6, 48), then a straight line from (6, 48) to (14, 72). (a) Work out the rate, in litres per minute, at which the pool filled during the first 6 minutes. (b) Work out how many litres were in the pool after 10 minutes. (a calculator may be used)","a":"(a) 8 litres per minute (b) 60 litres","sol":"(a) On the first section the volume rises by 48 − 0 = 48 litres in 6 − 0 = 6 minutes. M1 for a correct volume over a correct time. The rate is 48 ÷ 6 = 8. A1 for 8 litres per minute. (b) On the second section the volume rises by 72 − 48 = 24 litres in 14 − 6 = 8 minutes, a rate of 24 ÷ 8 = 3 litres per minute. M1 for the rate of the second section, or for reading up from t = 10 on that section. Ten minutes is 4 minutes into that section, so the pool holds 48 + 4 × 3 = 60 litres. A1 for 60 litres.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The steepness of each section says how fast the water is going in, and the two sections are not equally steep.","Work out the litres per minute for each section, then count on from the volume at the start of the section that contains 10 minutes.","The first line is the volume added over the time taken for the first section."],"codes":["A14"]},{"q":"A broadband bill is shown by a straight line on a graph, with the number of extra gigabytes g used along the horizontal axis and the bill C in pounds up the vertical axis. The line passes through (0, 18) and (50, 33). Find the equation of the line in the form C = mg + c, and say what the gradient tells you about the bill. (a calculator may be used)","a":"C = 0.3g + 18; the gradient means each extra gigabyte adds £0.30 to the bill","sol":"Gradient = (33 − 18) ÷ (50 − 0) = 15 ÷ 50. M1 for a correct change in C over a correct change in g. That is 0.3. A1 for 0.3. The line meets the vertical axis at (0, 18), so c = 18 and the equation is C = 0.3g + 18. A1 for C = 0.3g + 18. The gradient is a rate: every extra gigabyte adds £0.30 (30p) to the bill. B1 for the interpretation in context.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["On a real graph the gradient is a rate and the intercept is what you pay before using anything.","Work out the gradient from the two points, read c off the vertical axis, then say what the gradient means in pounds per gigabyte.","The first line is the change in C over the change in g between the two given points."],"codes":["A10","A14"]},{"q":"(a) Complete the table of values for y = x² − 2x − 3.   x: −2, −1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4   y: 5, ▢, −3, ▢, −3, 0, ▢   (b) On a grid with x from −2 to 4 and y from −5 to 6, plot the seven points from your table and join them with a smooth curve. Write down the coordinates of the two points where your curve crosses the x axis. (c) Write down the coordinates of the turning point of your graph. (non-calculator)","a":"(a) The missing y values are 0, −4 and 5 (b) (−1, 0) and (3, 0) (c) (1, −4)","sol":"(a) Substitute each missing x value into y = x² − 2x − 3. At x = −1: (−1)² − 2 × (−1) − 3 = 1 + 2 − 3 = 0. At x = 1: 1 − 2 − 3 = −4. At x = 4: 16 − 8 − 3 = 5. B2 for all three of 0, −4 and 5 (B1 for any two of them correct). (b) Plot the seven points (−2, 5), (−1, 0), (0, −3), (1, −4), (2, −3), (3, 0) and (4, 5) and join them with one smooth curve, not with ruled segments. M1 for the seven points plotted and joined by a smooth parabola, follow through from the candidate's table. The curve crosses the x axis where y = 0, which the table gives at x = −1 and x = 3. A1 for (−1, 0) and (3, 0). (c) The lowest point of the curve is at x = 1, where y = −4. B1 for (1, −4) as a coordinate pair.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Every y value in the table comes from putting that x into the equation, and squaring a negative number gives a positive one.","Work out x² − 2x − 3 for each missing x, plot all seven points and join them with a single smooth curve, then look for the lowest point that curve reaches.","The first line is y = (−1)² − 2 × (−1) − 3 for the first missing value, and the same substitution for the other two."],"codes":["A12","A11"]},{"q":"A conversion graph is used to change between pounds (£) and Danish kroner. The graph is a straight line from (0, 0) to (£40, 344 kroner). (a) Use the graph to change £25 into kroner. (b) Use the graph to change 129 kroner into pounds. (a calculator may be used)","a":"(a) 215 kroner (b) £15","sol":"The line passes through the origin, so the two amounts are in direct proportion and the point given fixes the whole graph: £1 is worth 344 ÷ 40 = 8.6 kroner. M1 for 344 ÷ 40, or for any correct scaling of the given point. (a) £25 is worth 25 × 8.6 = 215 kroner. A1 for 215 kroner. (b) Reading the graph the other way, 129 ÷ 8.6 = 15. B1 for £15.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A straight line through the origin means the two currencies are in direct proportion, so one point on it fixes every other reading.","Use the given point to find what one pound is worth in kroner, then multiply for part (a) and divide for part (b).","The first line divides the kroner at the given point by the pounds at the same point."],"codes":["A14"]},{"q":"A taxi fare is shown by a straight line on a graph, with the distance m miles along the horizontal axis and the fare £C up the vertical axis. The line passes through (0, 3.20) and (8, 17.60). (a) Find the equation of the line in the form C = am + b. (b) The firm then raises the charge per mile by 25% but leaves the fixed charge unchanged. Write down the equation of the new line. (a calculator may be used)","a":"(a) C = 1.8m + 3.2 (b) C = 2.25m + 3.2","sol":"(a) Gradient = (17.60 − 3.20) ÷ (8 − 0) = 14.40 ÷ 8. M1 for a correct change in C over a correct change in m. That is 1.8. A1 for 1.8. The fixed charge is the fare at m = 0, so b = 3.20 and C = 1.8m + 3.2. A1 for C = 1.8m + 3.2. (b) A 25% increase is a multiplier of 1.25, so the new charge per mile is 1.8 × 1.25. M1 for 1.8 × 1.25 (or 1.8 + 0.45). That is 2.25, and the fixed charge does not change, so C = 2.25m + 3.2. A1 for C = 2.25m + 3.2.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The charge per mile and the fixed charge are the two numbers in the equation of the line.","Find the gradient from the two points and the fixed charge from the vertical axis, then apply the percentage increase to the gradient only.","The first line is the change in C over the change in m between the two given points."],"codes":["A9","A10","A14"]},{"q":"The curve y = x² − 8x + 12 crosses the x axis at A and B, and crosses the y axis at C. (a) Work out the length of AB. (b) Write down the coordinates of C. (c) Work out how far the turning point of the curve is below the x axis. (non-calculator)","a":"(a) AB = 4 (b) C = (0, 12) (c) 4 units below the x axis","sol":"(a) On the x axis y = 0: x² − 8x + 12 = (x − 2)(x − 6) = 0. M1 for (x − 2)(x − 6). The roots are x = 2 and x = 6, so A is (2, 0) and B is (6, 0) and AB = 6 − 2 = 4. A1 for 4. (b) At x = 0, y = 12, so C is (0, 12). B1 for (0, 12). (c) The turning point lies halfway between the roots, at x = (2 + 6) ÷ 2 = 4, and there y = 4² − 8 × 4 + 12 = 16 − 32 + 12 = −4. M1 for substituting the mean of the roots into the equation. The turning point is (4, −4), so it is 4 units below the x axis. A1 for 4 units below.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Two of the three parts come straight from the roots, and the third from the curve's symmetry.","Factorise to get the roots, take the difference for AB, put x = 0 in for C, and use the mean of the roots for the turning point.","The first line is the factorised form of x² − 8x + 12 set equal to 0."],"codes":["A11","A8"]},{"q":"A tram sets off from rest. Its speed–time graph is a straight line from (0 s, 0 m/s) up to (25 s, 15 m/s), then a horizontal line at 15 m/s until t = 85 s, then a straight line down to (100 s, 0 m/s). (a) Work out the acceleration over the first 25 seconds, in m/s². (b) Work out the deceleration over the last part of the journey, in m/s². (c) Write the tram's top speed in km/h. (a calculator may be used)","a":"(a) 0.6 m/s² (b) 1 m/s² (c) 54 km/h","sol":"(a) Acceleration is the gradient of a speed–time graph: (15 − 0) ÷ (25 − 0) = 15 ÷ 25. M1 for a correct change in speed over a correct time. That is 0.6. A1 for 0.6 m/s². (b) The speed falls from 15 m/s to 0 m/s in 100 − 85 = 15 s, so the gradient is −15 ÷ 15 = −1, a deceleration of 1 m/s². B1 for 1 m/s² (accept −1 m/s²). (c) In one hour the tram at top speed would cover 15 × 3600 = 54 000 m. M1 for × 3600 then ÷ 1000, or for × 3.6. 54 000 m = 54 km, so the speed is 54 km/h. A1 for 54 km/h.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["On these axes the steepness of the line is not a speed but a change in speed.","Divide the change in speed by the time it took for each sloping section, then change metres per second into kilometres per hour.","The first line is the change in speed over the change in time for the first section."],"codes":["A14","A10"]},{"q":"A student says, 'The graph of y = x² + 2x + 3 crosses the x axis twice.' By working out the coordinates of the lowest point of the curve, show that the student is wrong. (non-calculator)","a":"The lowest point is (−1, 2), which is above the x axis, so the curve never crosses it and the student is wrong","sol":"At x = 0, y = 3, and at x = −2, y = 4 − 4 + 3 = 3, so the curve is at the same height at two x values. M1 for two x values with equal y values. A parabola is symmetrical, so the line of symmetry is halfway between them: x = (0 + (−2)) ÷ 2 = −1. A1 for x = −1. At x = −1, y = 1 − 2 + 3 = 2, so the lowest point is (−1, 2). A1 for (−1, 2). The coefficient of x² is positive, so the curve opens upwards and never goes below y = 2; since 2 is above the x axis, the curve cannot cross it, so the student is wrong. B1 for the conclusion with that reason.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["This expression will not factorise, but a parabola's symmetry can still be used.","Find two x values that give the same y, then the line of symmetry is midway between them.","The first line works out y at x = 0 and at one other x value that gives the same height."],"codes":["A11","A12"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write down the gradient of the line y = 5 − 2x.","a":"−2"},{"q":"The point (4, p) lies on the line y = 2x − 1. Work out p.","a":"p = 7"},{"q":"Write down the coordinates of the point where the curve y = x² − 5x + 6 crosses the y axis.","a":"(0, 6)"},{"q":"A distance–time graph is a straight line from (0, 0) to (2 hours, 90 km). What speed does it show?","a":"45 km/h"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The four quadrants","back":"The axes cut the plane into four regions: first (+, +), second (−, +), third (−, −), fourth (+, −), numbered anticlockwise from the top right. A point is always written (x, y), across then up.","code":"A8","open":false},{"front":"Midpoint of the segment joining (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂)","back":"((x₁ + x₂)/2, (y₁ + y₂)/2): the mean of the two x coordinates paired with the mean of the two y coordinates. Not printed on either Exam Aid.","code":"A8","open":false},{"front":"Gradient of a straight line","back":"Change in y ÷ change in x = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁). Positive slopes up to the right, negative slopes down, 0 is horizontal. Not printed on either Exam Aid.","code":"A10","open":false},{"front":"The form y = mx + c","back":"m is the gradient, the change in y for every 1 added to x; c is the y intercept, so the line crosses the y axis at (0, c).","code":"A10","open":false},{"front":"Condition for two straight lines to be parallel","back":"Equal gradients: the same m in y = mx + c, with different c. The line through (0, 1) parallel to y = 4x + 7 is y = 4x + 1.","code":"A9","open":false},{"front":"Method for the equation of a line through two points","back":"Work out m = change in y ÷ change in x, write y = mx + c with that m, substitute one point's coordinates to find c, then check the equation on the other point.","code":"A9","open":false},{"front":"Roots of a quadratic","back":"The x values where the curve meets the x axis, where y = 0. Factorise and set each bracket to zero: (x − 1)(x + 5) = 0 gives x = 1 and x = −5.","code":"A11","open":false},{"front":"y intercept of a graph","back":"The value of y when x = 0. For y = x² + 3x − 10 it is −10, so the curve crosses the y axis at (0, −10).","code":"A11","open":false},{"front":"Turning point of a quadratic","back":"The vertex, sitting on the line of symmetry halfway between the roots. Take the mean of the roots for its x coordinate, then substitute that x back into the equation for its y coordinate.","code":"A11","open":false},{"front":"Shape of the graph of y = ax² + bx + c","back":"A parabola: a U-shape when a is positive, an upside-down U when a is negative, symmetrical about a vertical line through its turning point.","code":"A12","open":false},{"front":"Shape of the graph of y = x³","back":"An S-shaped curve rising from bottom left to top right through the origin, flattening at the origin instead of turning, with rotational symmetry about the origin.","code":"A12","open":false},{"front":"The reciprocal graph y = 1/x, x ≠ 0","back":"Two separate branches, one in the first quadrant and one in the third. It touches neither axis: x = 0 gives no value of y, and no x makes y equal 0.","code":"A12","open":false},{"front":"One feature of the graph of y = 1/x that no straight-line graph has","back":"It comes in two separate branches; it crosses neither axis; its steepness changes from point to point instead of staying the same everywhere.","code":"A12","open":true},{"front":"Gradient of a distance–time graph","back":"The speed, in the units of the axes (km/h for km against hours). A steeper line is faster; a horizontal line means the object is stopped.","code":"A14","open":false},{"front":"Gradient of a speed–time graph","back":"The acceleration, in m/s² when speed is in m/s and time in seconds. A downward slope is a deceleration; a horizontal line is constant speed.","code":"A14","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["What the Exam Aid prints for this room: nothing, at either tier, so every result below must be remembered. The Foundation sheet (W87990A) prints only the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, the circumference and area of a circle, a² + b² = c², sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b, Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ and P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B), and the Higher sheet (W87991A) adds the quadratic formula x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/2a, the sine and cosine rules, Area of triangle = ½ab sin C and P(A and B) = P(A given B) P(B) — every one of them belonging to another room. The quadratic formula is not Foundation content, so the roots in this room come from factorising, not from the printed formula.","Gradient of a straight line: m = change in y ÷ change in x = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁).","Equation of a straight line: y = mx + c, where m is the gradient and c the y intercept, the line crossing the y axis at (0, c).","Parallel lines: equal gradients, m₁ = m₂, with different c.","Midpoint of the segment joining (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂): ((x₁ + x₂)/2, (y₁ + y₂)/2).","Intercepts: put x = 0 for the y intercept, y = 0 for the x intercepts (the roots).","Roots and turning point of a quadratic: factorise and set each bracket to 0 for the roots; the line of symmetry is x = the mean of the roots, and substituting that x gives the turning point.","Speed = distance ÷ time, read as the gradient of a distance–time graph.","Acceleration = change in speed ÷ time, read as the gradient of a speed–time graph."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T12:11:04Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Every answer key in the bank, the exit ticket and the lesson check re-solved independently in node and all 25 agree, including the awkward ones (0.3 g⁻¹ on the broadband line, 1.8 → 2.25 after the 25% rise, 129 ÷ 8.6 = 15 exactly, 15 m/s = 54 km/h, and the (−1, 2) vertex found by symmetry rather than completing the square); the room stays inside Foundation content — no A15 gradient-of-a-curve or area-under-a-graph, no quadratic formula, no completing the square, and every described graph is given by straight sections or a table, so nothing depends on an image — 11 of the 16 questions are non-calculator with hand-sized numbers, the six idea codes A8–A12 and A14 appear in the lesson in specification order and are all examined, hints stop at the first line of working, and the only real repair needed is the dead last step of the first worked example."}}},"algebra-equations":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"algebra-equations","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Solving is where algebra earns its keep: once a situation has been turned into an equation, the answer follows from a method you can learn, write down and check. This room holds the solving methods the Foundation papers return to again and again — inverse operations for a linear equation, including one with the unknown on both sides, factorising for a quadratic, elimination or substitution for a pair of simultaneous equations, and the same moves once more for an inequality, where multiplying or dividing by a negative turns the sign round. It also holds the step that decides most problem-solving questions: naming the unknown and writing down the relation the words describe. Prices, perimeters, ages, numbers of pupils and consecutive numbers are all set as equations, and a graph gives the same answers, because the solution of one equation is where two graphs cross and the roots of a quadratic are where its curve cuts the x axis. Every answer here can be checked by substituting it back into the original statement, so a mark you have earned need never be lost.","idea":[{"h":"Solving a linear equation by inverse operations","t":"An equation says that two expressions have the same value, and solving it means finding the value of the unknown that makes that true. Keep the equation balanced: whatever you do to one side you do to the other. Undo the operations in reverse order — take the number away before dividing by the coefficient, and clear any bracket or fraction first, by expanding or by multiplying every term by the denominator. When the unknown is on both sides, as in 9x + 1 = 5x + 13, subtract the smaller x term from both sides so the unknown is on one side only, then collect the numbers on the other. The same equation can be solved approximately from a graph: draw y = each side on the same axes and read off the x coordinate of the point where the two graphs cross. Always substitute your value back into the original equation to check.","code":"A17"},{"h":"Solving a quadratic equation by factorising","t":"A quadratic equation must first be written with zero on one side, in the form x² + bx + c = 0; if it reads x² + 4x = 21 you subtract 21 from both sides before doing anything else. Then factorise: find two numbers that multiply to give the constant term and add to give the coefficient of x, so that the left-hand side becomes a product of two brackets. Now use the fact that a product can only be zero when one of the things multiplied is zero, so set each bracket equal to zero in turn. That gives two one-step equations and therefore two roots, and both must be written down unless the situation rules one out. On a graph the same roots are the x coordinates of the points where the curve y = x² + bx + c crosses the x axis, which is how approximate solutions are found when the quadratic will not factorise.","code":"A18"},{"h":"Solving two simultaneous linear equations","t":"Two equations in two unknowns are solved together, because only one pair of values satisfies both. To eliminate, multiply one or both equations until one unknown has coefficients of the same size, then add the equations if the signs of those terms are opposite and subtract if they are the same. That leaves one equation in one unknown; solve it, then back-substitute into either original equation to get the second value, and check the pair in the equation you did not use. To substitute instead, rearrange one equation to give one unknown in terms of the other and put that expression into the second equation. The pair of values is the point (x, y) where the graphs of the two lines cross, so a drawn pair of lines gives an approximate solution and a useful check on the algebra.","code":"A19"},{"h":"Turning a situation into an equation and back again","t":"Most marks in a problem-solving question are for the translation, not the arithmetic. Name the unknown and say what it stands for, with its unit — let x be the cost of one pen in pence, let n be the number of pupils. Write every other quantity in terms of that letter: a stamp costing 30p more than another is (x + 30) pence, the length of a rectangle 3 cm longer than its width is (w + 3) cm. Then write the equals sign the situation gives you: a total paid, a perimeter, two things that must be equal, or a product. Solve the equation, then interpret the solution: answer the question that was actually asked, in context and with its unit, and reject any root the situation makes impossible, such as a negative length or a negative number of people. Where two quantities are unknown, two separate statements give two simultaneous equations.","code":"A21"},{"h":"Solving a linear inequality and showing the solution set","t":"An inequality is solved with exactly the same moves as an equation — add, subtract, multiply or divide both sides — with one extra rule: multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative number reverses the inequality sign, so from −3x > 9 you get x < −3. Adding and subtracting never change the sign. The answer is not a single value but a solution set, and it must be written with the inequality sign, as x ≤ 4, not as x = 4. On a number line the end value is marked with a closed (filled) circle for ≤ or ≥, because that value is included, and with an open circle for < or >, because it is not; a line with an arrow then runs towards the values that satisfy the inequality. If a question asks for integer solutions, list the whole numbers inside the set, taking care at the end value.","code":"A22"}],"examples":[{"title":"A linear equation with the unknown on both sides","setup":"Solve the equation 5x − 3 = 2x + 9, showing your working. No calculator is allowed.","steps":["Subtract 2x from both sides so the unknown appears on one side only: 3x − 3 = 9","Add 3 to both sides so that the x term stands alone: 3x = 12","Divide both sides by 3, the line the mark scheme credits as the method: x = 4","Check by substituting back: 5 × 4 − 3 = 17 and 2 × 4 + 9 = 17, so the solution x = 4 is correct"],"cues":["Which x term should be removed, and from which side?","What is still attached to the x term on the left?","What single operation is left to do?","How can you be certain the value is right?"]},{"title":"A quadratic equation solved by factorising","setup":"Solve the equation x² + 5x + 6 = 0 by factorising, giving both solutions. No calculator is allowed.","steps":["Two numbers are needed that multiply to give 6 and add to give 5: they are 2 and 3","Write the left-hand side as a product of two brackets, the line that earns the method mark: (x + 2)(x + 3) = 0","A product is zero only when one of the factors is zero, so x + 2 = 0 or x + 3 = 0","Solving each in turn: x = −2 or x = −3"],"cues":["What pair of conditions must the two numbers satisfy?","How are those two numbers used to rewrite the expression?","What does a product of zero tell you about the factors?","What values come out of the two small equations?"]},{"title":"Simultaneous equations by elimination, read on the graph","setup":"Solve the simultaneous equations 3x + 2y = 12 and x − y = 1. Give the values of x and y, and say what the pair of values means on the graphs of the two lines. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Multiply the second equation through by 2 so that the y terms are the same size: 2x − 2y = 2","The y terms now have opposite signs, so adding the two equations removes y: 5x = 14","Divide both sides by 5: x = 2.8","Substitute into x − y = 1: 2.8 − y = 1, so y = 1.8","The solution x = 2.8, y = 1.8 is the point (2.8, 1.8) at which the graphs of the two lines cross"],"cues":["Which unknown is easier to match here, and by what multiplier?","Should the two equations now be added or subtracted?","What is left to do to the equation in x alone?","Which original equation is quickest for finding the second value?","What does the pair of values look like on a pair of axes?"]},{"title":"Deriving an equation from a described shape","setup":"A rectangle measures 3 cm more in length than in width, and the distance all the way round it is 26 cm. Form an equation for the width and solve it, then state the width and the length in cm. No calculator is allowed.","steps":["Let the width be w cm, so the length is (w + 3) cm and the four sides add to w + (w + 3) + w + (w + 3)","Collect the terms and set the total equal to the distance round the shape, the line that earns the method mark: 4w + 6 = 26","Subtract 6 from both sides: 4w = 20","Divide both sides by 4: w = 5","The width is 5 cm and the length is 5 + 3 = 8 cm"],"cues":["Which side should be called w, and how is the other written?","What does the sum of the four sides have to equal?","Which of the two operations on the left should be undone first?","What is still being done to w, and what undoes it?","What was actually asked for, and in what unit?"]},{"title":"An inequality and its solution set on a number line","setup":"Solve the inequality 4x − 5 ≤ 11, and describe exactly how the solution set is drawn on a number line. No calculator is allowed.","steps":["Add 5 to both sides, exactly as you would for an equation: 4x ≤ 16","Divide both sides by 4; that divisor is positive, so the inequality sign does not change: x ≤ 4","On the number line, put a closed (filled) circle at 4, because that value itself satisfies x ≤ 4","Draw a line with an arrow from the circle towards the smaller numbers, so the solution set x ≤ 4 covers every value from 4 downwards"],"cues":["What is the first move, and does the sign change?","Is the divisor positive or negative, and why does that matter?","Open circle or closed circle here, and what decides it?","Which way should the arrow point?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Solve 3x + 8 = 29.","a":"x = 7"},{"q":"Solve (x + 4)(x − 7) = 0.","a":"x = −4 or x = 7"},{"q":"Solve x² − 2x − 15 = 0 by factorising.","a":"x = 5 or x = −3"},{"q":"Solve the simultaneous equations x + y = 9 and x − y = 3.","a":"x = 6, y = 3"},{"q":"Solve 2x − 1 > 7 and write down the smallest integer that satisfies it.","a":"x > 4, and the smallest integer is 5"},{"q":"A number is doubled and then 5 is added; the result is 31. Form an equation and find the number.","a":"2n + 5 = 31, so n = 13"}]},"room":{"facts":["An equation stays true if the same thing is done to both sides; solve by inverse operations, undoing in reverse order (+ undoes −, × undoes ÷).","When the unknown is on both sides, subtract the smaller x term from both sides first, then collect the numbers on the other side.","A quadratic must be rearranged into the form x² + bx + c = 0 before it is factorised: x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q), where p × q = c and p + q = b.","If a product is zero then one of the factors is zero: (x + p)(x + q) = 0 gives x = −p or x = −q, so a quadratic has two roots.","Elimination: scale one or both equations until an unknown has coefficients of the same size, add when their signs are opposite and subtract when they are the same, then back-substitute for the other unknown.","A graph gives the same solutions: the solution of one equation is the x coordinate where the two graphs cross, two simultaneous linear equations are solved at the point where the lines cross, and the roots of a quadratic are where its curve cuts the x axis.","An inequality is solved like an equation, but multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative number reverses the sign; on a number line use a closed circle for ≤ or ≥ and an open circle for < or >."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve 4x + 7 = 23.","a":"x = 4","sol":"Subtract 7 from both sides so that only the x term is left: 4x = 16 (M1). Divide both sides by 4: x = 4 (A1). Check: 4 × 4 + 7 = 23. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Something has been done to x twice; solving means undoing both, in the opposite order.","Get the term in x by itself first, then remove the number it is multiplied by.","Subtract 7 from both sides, then divide both sides by the coefficient of x."],"codes":["A17"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve 7x + 2 = 4x + 17.","a":"x = 5","sol":"Subtract 4x from both sides so the unknown is on one side only: 3x + 2 = 17 (M1). Subtract 2 from both sides: 3x = 15 (M1). Divide both sides by 3: x = 5 (A1). Check: 7 × 5 + 2 = 37 and 4 × 5 + 17 = 37. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The unknown appears on both sides, so the first job is to get it onto one side only.","Take the smaller number of x from both sides, then deal with the numbers in the same way.","Subtract 4x from both sides, then move the constant across before dividing."],"codes":["A17"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve (x − 3)(x + 8) = 0.","a":"x = 3 or x = −8","sol":"A product is zero only when one of its factors is zero, so x − 3 = 0 or x + 8 = 0 (M1). Solving each in turn gives x = 3 or x = −8 (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Two quantities have been multiplied together and the result is zero.","That can only happen if at least one of the two quantities is itself zero.","Set each bracket in turn equal to zero and solve the two one-step equations that gives."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve the inequality 3x + 4 < 19.","a":"x < 5","sol":"Subtract 4 from both sides: 3x < 15 (M1). Divide both sides by 3; the divisor is positive, so the inequality sign is unchanged: x < 5 (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["An inequality is solved with the same moves as an equation; only the sign in the middle is different.","Undo the addition first and then the multiplication, watching whether the sign has to turn round.","Subtract 4 from both sides, then divide by the coefficient of x and keep the inequality sign in your answer."],"codes":["A22"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve the simultaneous equations x + y = 15 and x − y = 3.","a":"x = 9, y = 6","sol":"The y terms are + y and − y, so adding the two equations eliminates y: 2x = 18 (M1). Divide by 2: x = 9 (A1). Substitute into x + y = 15: 9 + y = 15, so y = 6 (A1). Check in x − y = 3: 9 − 6 = 3. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Look at the y terms: one is added and the other is subtracted.","Combining the two equations in the right way will make one of the unknowns disappear.","Add the two left-hand sides and the two right-hand sides, then use either original equation for the second value."],"codes":["A19"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve 5(2x − 3) = 3x + 13.","a":"x = 4","sol":"Expand the bracket: 10x − 15 = 3x + 13 (M1). Subtract 3x from both sides and add 15 to both sides: 7x = 28 (M1). Divide by 7: x = 4 (A1). Check: 5 × (8 − 3) = 25 and 3 × 4 + 13 = 25. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The bracket has to go before any terms can be collected.","Multiply everything inside the bracket by the number outside, then gather the x terms on one side and the numbers on the other.","Multiply both terms inside the bracket by 5, then subtract the smaller x term from both sides."],"codes":["A17"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve x² + 3x − 28 = 0.","a":"x = −7 or x = 4","sol":"Two numbers that multiply to −28 and add to 3 are 7 and −4, so the equation factorises as (x + 7)(x − 4) = 0 (M1). Set each bracket equal to zero: x + 7 = 0 or x − 4 = 0 (M1). Solving gives x = −7 or x = 4 (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The equation already has zero on one side, so it is ready to be written as a product.","Look for two numbers that multiply to give the constant term and add to give the coefficient of x.","Write out the factor pairs of the constant term and look for the pair whose difference is the coefficient of x, remembering that one of the two numbers must be negative."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve x² + 6x = 16.","a":"x = −8 or x = 2","sol":"Subtract 16 from both sides so that one side is zero: x² + 6x − 16 = 0 (M1). Two numbers that multiply to −16 and add to 6 are 8 and −2, so (x + 8)(x − 2) = 0 (M1). Each bracket in turn is zero, giving x = −8 or x = 2 (A1). Check: (−8)² + 6 × (−8) = 64 − 48 = 16 and 2² + 6 × 2 = 16. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A quadratic cannot be factorised for solving while there is a number on the right-hand side.","Move that number across first, then factorise the quadratic that is left.","Subtract 16 from both sides to make the right-hand side zero, then look for two numbers multiplying to the new constant term and adding to 6."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"(a calculator may be used) Solve the simultaneous equations 3x + 4y = 18 and 5x − 2y = 4.","a":"x = 2, y = 3","sol":"Multiply the second equation through by 2 so that the y terms are the same size: 10x − 4y = 8 (M1). The y terms now have opposite signs, so add this to 3x + 4y = 18: 13x = 26 (M1). Divide by 13: x = 2 (A1). Substitute into 3x + 4y = 18: 6 + 4y = 18, so 4y = 12 and y = 3 (A1). Check in 5x − 2y = 4: 10 − 6 = 4. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Neither unknown has matching coefficients yet, so one of the equations has to be scaled first.","Double the second equation so the y terms are the same size, then combine the two equations.","Multiply every term of 5x − 2y = 4 by 2, then decide whether to add or subtract by looking at the signs of the y terms."],"codes":["A19"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A pen costs x pence. A notebook costs 40 pence more than a pen. Ellie buys 3 pens and 2 notebooks and pays £3.55 altogether. Form an equation in x and solve it to find the cost of one pen.","a":"55p (x = 55)","sol":"Work in pence throughout. A notebook costs (x + 40) pence, so 3 pens and 2 notebooks give 3x + 2(x + 40) = 355 (M1). Expand the bracket and collect: 5x + 80 = 355 (M1). Subtract 80 from both sides: 5x = 275 (M1). Divide by 5: x = 55, so one pen costs 55p (A1). Check: 3 × 55 + 2 × 95 = 165 + 190 = 355 pence = £3.55. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Work in pence throughout so that every number in the equation is a whole number.","Write the cost of one notebook in terms of x, then write the cost of three pens and two notebooks added together.","Add three lots of x to two lots of (x + 40), set that total equal to the amount paid in pence, and expand the bracket."],"codes":["A21","A17"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve the inequality 5 − 2x < 13 and write down the smallest integer that satisfies it.","a":"x > −4, and the smallest integer is −3","sol":"Subtract 5 from both sides: −2x < 8 (M1). Divide both sides by −2; dividing by a negative number reverses the inequality sign: x > −4 (A1). The integers greater than −4 begin at −3, so the smallest integer in the solution set is −3 (B1). Check: x = −3 gives 5 + 6 = 11, which is less than 13, while x = −4 gives exactly 13. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The term in x here is negative, and that changes one thing before the answer is written down.","Solve it as you would an equation, but watch what happens to the sign when you divide by a negative number.","Subtract 5 from both sides, then divide by the coefficient of x and turn the inequality sign round."],"codes":["A22"]},{"q":"(a calculator may be used) In a café, 2 teas and 3 coffees cost £9.60 altogether, and 4 teas and 1 coffee cost £8.20 altogether. Work out the cost of one tea and the cost of one coffee.","a":"A tea costs £1.50 and a coffee costs £2.20","sol":"Let £t be the cost of a tea and £c the cost of a coffee, so 2t + 3c = 9.60 and 4t + c = 8.20 (M1). Multiply the second equation by 3 so that the c terms match: 12t + 3c = 24.60 (M1). Subtract the first equation from this: 10t = 15 (M1). Divide by 10: t = 1.50, so a tea costs £1.50 (A1). Substitute into 4t + c = 8.20: 6.00 + c = 8.20, so c = 2.20 and a coffee costs £2.20 (A1). Check: 2 × 1.50 + 3 × 2.20 = 3.00 + 6.60 = £9.60. Total 5 marks.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["There are two unknown prices here, so two separate statements are needed before anything can be solved.","Let t and c stand for the two prices in pounds, write one equation for each order, then eliminate one of them.","Write an equation for each order in pounds, then multiply the second one through so that its c term is the same size as the c term in the first."],"codes":["A21","A19"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The product of two consecutive positive whole numbers is 72. By forming and solving a quadratic equation, find the two numbers.","a":"8 and 9","sol":"Let the smaller number be n, so the next one is (n + 1) and n(n + 1) = 72 (M1). Expand and subtract 72 from both sides: n² + n − 72 = 0 (M1). Two numbers that multiply to −72 and add to 1 are 9 and −8, so (n + 9)(n − 8) = 0 (M1). This gives n = −9 or n = 8, and the numbers are positive, so n = 8 (A1). The two numbers are therefore 8 and 9 (A1). Check: 8 × 9 = 72. Total 5 marks.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Consecutive whole numbers differ by 1, so one letter is enough to describe both.","Write the product of the two numbers in terms of that letter, set it equal to the given product, and rearrange into a quadratic.","Call the smaller number n, multiply n by (n + 1), then move every term to one side so that the other side is zero."],"codes":["A21","A18"]},{"q":"(a calculator may be used) The lines y = 2x − 3 and y = 12 − x are drawn on the same axes. (a) Use algebra to find the coordinates of the point where the two lines cross. (b) Hence write down the solution of the equation 2x − 3 = 12 − x.","a":"(a) (5, 7)  (b) x = 5","sol":"(a) At the point where the lines cross, the two expressions for y are equal: 2x − 3 = 12 − x (M1). Add x to both sides and add 3 to both sides: 3x = 15, so x = 5 (M1). Substituting into y = 2x − 3 gives y = 7, and 12 − 5 = 7 confirms it, so the lines cross at (5, 7) (A1). (b) At that crossing point both expressions take the same value, so its x coordinate is the value of x that makes 2x − 3 = 12 − x true, and the solution is therefore x = 5 (B1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["At a crossing point, a value of x gives the same y on both lines.","Put the two expressions for y equal to each other, solve for x, and then use either line to find y.","Write 2x − 3 = 12 − x, then collect the x terms on one side and the numbers on the other."],"codes":["A17","A19"]},{"q":"(a calculator may be used) A school trip costs £45 for each pupil plus a fixed coach charge of £320. The school can spend at most £2000 on the trip. By forming and solving an inequality, work out the greatest number of pupils who can go.","a":"37 pupils","sol":"Let n be the number of pupils. The total cost is (45n + 320) pounds and it can be at most £2000, so 45n + 320 ≤ 2000 (M1). Subtract 320 from both sides: 45n ≤ 1680 (M1). Divide both sides by 45: n ≤ 37.33… (A1). The number of pupils must be a whole number, so the greatest number who can go is 37 (A1). Check: 45 × 37 + 320 = £1985, which is within budget, while 38 pupils would cost £2030. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The budget is a limit rather than an exact amount, so the statement you write will not be an equation.","Write the total cost for n pupils, say that it is at most the budget, and solve for n.","Form the inequality with the cost per pupil times n plus the fixed charge on the left, then subtract the fixed charge from both sides."],"codes":["A21","A22"]},{"q":"(a calculator may be used) In a sale a shop reduces the price of a coat by 20%. Jack pays £68 for the coat in the sale. Let £x be the price of the coat before the sale. Form an equation and solve it to find the price before the sale, and work out how much Jack saved.","a":"£85 before the sale, a saving of £17","sol":"A reduction of 20% leaves 80% of the original price, so 0.8x = 68 (M1). Divide both sides by 0.8: x = 68 ÷ 0.8 (M1). This gives x = 85, so the coat cost £85 before the sale (A1). The saving is 85 − 68 = £17 (B1). Check: 20% of £85 = £17 and £85 − £17 = £68. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The £68 is what is left after the reduction, not the size of the reduction.","Write the sale price as a multiplier of the original price, form an equation and solve it.","Express the sale price as a decimal multiplier of x, set that expression equal to the price paid, then divide both sides by the multiplier."],"codes":["A21","A17"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Solve 6x − 5 = 3x + 13.","a":"x = 6"},{"q":"Solve x² + 2x − 15 = 0.","a":"x = 3 or x = −5"},{"q":"Solve the simultaneous equations 2x + y = 11 and x − y = 1.","a":"x = 4, y = 3"},{"q":"Solve the inequality 3 − 4x ≥ 15.","a":"x ≤ −3"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Solving an equation","back":"Finding the value of the unknown that makes the two sides equal. Every step does the same thing to both sides, so the equation stays balanced and stays true.","code":"A17","open":false},{"front":"Inverse operations","back":"The operation that undoes another: + undoes −, × undoes ÷, and a square is undone by a square root, but x² = k has two roots, x = ±√k. Undo a linear equation's operations in reverse order.","code":"A17","open":false},{"front":"First move when the unknown is on both sides","back":"Subtract the smaller x term from both sides, so the unknown is left on one side only: from 9x + 1 = 5x + 13, subtracting 5x leaves 4x + 1 = 13.","code":"A17","open":false},{"front":"Approximate solution of an equation from a graph","back":"Draw y = each side of the equation on one pair of axes; the x coordinate of the point where the two graphs cross is the solution, read off to the accuracy of the scale.","code":"A17","open":false},{"front":"Standard form of a quadratic equation","back":"ax² + bx + c = 0, with every term on one side and 0 on the other. A quadratic must be rearranged into this form before it is factorised and solved.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"The zero product property","back":"If two quantities multiply to give 0, one of them must be 0. So (x + p)(x + q) = 0 gives x = −p or x = −q.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"Factorising x² + bx + c","back":"x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q), where p × q = c and p + q = b: two numbers multiplying to the constant term and adding to the coefficient of x.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"Roots of a quadratic from its graph","back":"The solutions of x² + bx + c = 0 are the x coordinates of the points where the curve y = x² + bx + c crosses the x axis, which is how approximate roots are read off.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"Elimination method for simultaneous equations","back":"Scale one or both equations until an unknown has coefficients of the same size, then add if their signs are opposite and subtract if they are the same; solve, then back-substitute.","code":"A19","open":false},{"front":"Substitution method for simultaneous equations","back":"Rearrange one equation to give one unknown in terms of the other, put that expression into the second equation, solve for one unknown, then back-substitute for the other.","code":"A19","open":false},{"front":"Simultaneous equations on a graph","back":"The solution of two simultaneous linear equations is the point (x, y) where the graphs of the two lines cross; parallel lines never cross, so such a pair has no solution.","code":"A19","open":false},{"front":"Deriving an equation from a situation","back":"Name the unknown and its unit, write every other quantity in terms of that letter, then write down the equals sign the situation gives: a total, a perimeter or a product.","code":"A21","open":false},{"front":"Interpreting the solution in context","back":"Turn the value of the unknown back into the answer asked for, with its unit, and reject any solution the situation makes impossible, such as a negative length or a negative number of people.","code":"A21","open":false},{"front":"Multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number","back":"The inequality sign reverses: from −3x > 9, dividing both sides by −3 gives x < −3. Adding, subtracting, and multiplying or dividing by a positive number leave the sign unchanged.","code":"A22","open":false},{"front":"Number line convention for a solution set","back":"A closed (filled) circle at the end value for ≤ or ≥, an open circle for < or >, with a line and arrow drawn towards the values that satisfy the inequality.","code":"A22","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Nothing this room needs is printed on the Foundation Exam Aid (W87990A). Its only sections are area of a trapezium, volume of a prism, circumference and area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem and the three trigonometric ratios, compound interest and P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B). Every rule below has to be remembered.","Recall: an equation stays true if the same operation is done to both sides; solve by inverse operations, clearing brackets and fractions first.","Recall: when the unknown is on both sides, subtract the smaller x term from both sides before collecting the numbers on the other side.","Recall: a quadratic is solved from the form x² + bx + c = 0 — rearrange until one side is zero before factorising.","Recall: x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q), where p × q = c and p + q = b.","Recall: if a product is zero then a factor is zero — (x + p)(x + q) = 0 gives x = −p or x = −q, so there are two roots.","Recall: elimination — scale one or both equations so an unknown has coefficients of the same size, add when the signs are opposite and subtract when they are the same, then back-substitute.","Recall: substitution — rearrange one equation for one unknown and put that expression into the other equation.","Recall: the graphical readings — the solution of one equation is the x coordinate where the two graphs cross, two simultaneous linear equations are solved where the lines cross, and the roots of a quadratic are where its curve cuts the x axis.","Recall: an inequality is solved like an equation, but the sign reverses when both sides are multiplied or divided by a negative number.","Recall: the number line convention — closed (filled) circle for ≤ or ≥, open circle for < or >, with the arrow drawn towards the solution set.","The quadratic formula x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/2a is printed on the Higher Exam Aid (W87991A) only; it is not on the Foundation sheet, and it is not needed in this room, where quadratic equations are solved by factorising.","If a question in this room sets its equation inside a context that uses one of the printed results — the area of a circle, say, or the area of a trapezium — that formula is given to you on the sheet, but the equation you build from it and the method you solve it by are not."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T11:52:32Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Answer keys, solutions and mark tallies are all sound and the Formulae sheet matches the Foundation Exam Aid (W87990A) exactly, so this is publishable as it stands; the only things worth a teacher's pen are the duplicated x² + 6x = 16 and the \"square root undoes a square\" card."}}},"algebra-sequences":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"algebra-sequences","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Sequences are set on all three papers at both tiers, and they are the cheapest algebra marks on the paper if you know what to look for. A question may hand you a rule and ask for terms, hand you terms and ask for the rule, or hide either inside a pattern of tiles, matchsticks or weekly savings. The same three moves cover them all: apply a term-to-term rule repeatedly, substitute a position into an nth-term expression, or read the common difference off a linear sequence and build dn + (a - d). Because an nth-term expression tests any value in one line, it also settles the reasoning questions examiners like — is 200 in this sequence, which term is the first to go negative, which numbers belong to both sequences — and those carry the AO2 and AO3 marks.","idea":[{"h":"Generating terms: term-to-term and position-to-term rules","t":"A term-to-term rule tells you how to get from one term to the next, so it is useless without a starting term and you must work through every term in order: 'first term 9, term-to-term rule subtract 5' gives 9, 4, −1, −6, −11. A position-to-term rule, usually written as 'the nth term is …', gives any term directly from its position: with nth term 9n − 4 the 8th term is 9 × 8 − 4 = 68 and the 20th is 9 × 20 − 4 = 176, with no need for the 19 terms in between. Exam questions name which rule you have been given, so read the wording before you start; the marks for generating terms are usually B marks, but a single arithmetic slip early in a term-to-term chain spoils every term after it, so write each step down rather than doing it in your head.","code":"A23"},{"h":"The sequences you are expected to recognise","t":"Five families come up by name. Triangular numbers 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21, 28, … go up by 1 more each time; square numbers 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, … are n × n; cube numbers 1, 8, 27, 64, 125, … are n × n × n. A Fibonacci-type sequence adds the two previous terms, so it needs two starting values and can be run backwards by subtracting. A geometric progression multiplies by a constant common ratio r, found by dividing any term by the one before it, and the sequence rⁿ is r, r², r³, … . A quadratic sequence is the one whose differences are not constant: work out the first differences, and if those go up or down by the same amount each time the second difference is constant and you continue by extending the differences first. Checking the differences is the move that tells you which family you are in.","code":"A24"},{"h":"Deducing the nth term of a linear sequence","t":"A linear (arithmetic) sequence has a constant difference d between consecutive terms, and that difference is the coefficient of n: the sequence behaves like the multiples of d, shifted. Compare 4, 10, 16, 22 with the multiples of 6 — 6, 12, 18, 24 — and every term is 2 less, so the nth term is 6n − 2. In general the nth term is dn + (a − d), where a is the first term, and a − d is the term that would sit at position zero. A decreasing sequence has a negative d: 30, 25, 20, 15 gives 35 − 5n. Always check your expression on a term you have not used. The expression is what answers the harder questions: substitute a position to get a term, or set the expression equal to a value and solve for n — a positive whole number means the value is in the sequence, anything else means it is not.","code":"A25"}],"examples":[{"title":"Generating terms from a two-step term-to-term rule","setup":"A sequence has first term 5. Its term-to-term rule is 'multiply by 2, then subtract 3'. Work out the 4th term of the sequence.","steps":["Apply the rule to the first term: 5 × 2 = 10, then 10 − 3 = 7, so the 2nd term is 7","The rule now acts on 7: 7 × 2 = 14, then 14 − 3 = 11, so the 3rd term is 11","The rule acts on 11: 11 × 2 = 22, then 22 − 3 = 19, so the 4th term is 19","Check by listing the terms in order: 5, 7, 11, 19 — the rule has been applied three times, so 19 does sit at position 4"],"cues":["Which order are the two operations done in?","Which number does the rule act on next?","How many more times must the rule be applied?","How can you be sure the rule has been applied the right number of times?"]},{"title":"Finding a later term of a geometric progression","setup":"A sequence begins 2, 6, 18, 54, … . Show that it is a geometric progression, and work out its 7th term.","steps":["Divide each term by the one before it: 6 ÷ 2 = 3, 18 ÷ 6 = 3, 54 ÷ 18 = 3, so the common ratio is 3 and the sequence is geometric","Multiply on to the 5th term: 54 × 3 = 162","The 6th term is 162 × 3 = 486","One more multiplication gives 486 × 3 = 1458, so the term asked for is 1458"],"cues":["What is the same about each pair of neighbouring terms?","What must be done to the last term given to move on one place?","Which position has been reached now?","How many more multiplications are needed?"]},{"title":"Continuing a quadratic sequence","setup":"Here are the first four terms of a quadratic sequence: 3, 8, 15, 24. Work out the next two terms of the sequence.","steps":["Work out the first differences: 8 − 3 = 5, 15 − 8 = 7, 24 − 15 = 9","The differences themselves go up by 2 each time, so the next two first differences are 11 and 13","Add the next difference to the last term: 24 + 11 = 35","Add the difference after that: 35 + 13 = 48, so the next two terms are 35 and 48"],"cues":["What should be worked out first when the gaps are not equal?","What pattern do those gaps themselves follow?","Which term does the next gap get added to?","What remains to be done to reach a second new term?"]},{"title":"Deducing the nth term of a linear sequence","setup":"Here are the first four terms of a linear sequence: 9, 14, 19, 24. Find an expression, in terms of n, for the nth term of this sequence.","steps":["The gap between terms is constant: 14 − 9 = 5, so the coefficient of n is 5","The multiples of 5 are 5, 10, 15, 20, while the sequence reads 9, 14, 19, 24","Each term of the sequence is 4 more than the matching multiple of 5","nth term = 5n + 4"],"cues":["What is the same about every gap between neighbouring terms?","What should the sequence be compared with once the gap is known?","How does each term compare with the multiple above it?","How is that comparison written as an expression in n?"]},{"title":"Testing whether a number is a term of a sequence","setup":"The nth term of a sequence is 6n − 5. Alfie says that 121 is a term of this sequence. Decide whether Alfie is right, showing your working.","steps":["A number is a term only if some whole-number position gives it, so set 6n − 5 = 121","Add 5 to both sides: 6n = 126","Divide both sides by 6: n = 21","n = 21 is a positive whole number, so 121 is the 21st term and Alfie is right"],"cues":["What has to be true about the position for a number to be in the sequence?","What is the first move towards getting n on its own?","What undoes the multiplication by 6?","What does a whole-number position tell you about Alfie's claim?"]},{"title":"The nth term from two terms that are not next to each other","setup":"In a linear sequence the 3rd term is 20 and the 7th term is 44. Find an expression, in terms of n, for the nth term of the sequence.","steps":["From the 3rd to the 7th term is 4 steps, and the terms rise by 44 − 20 = 24 altogether","So each step adds 24 ÷ 4 = 6, and the coefficient of n is 6","Step back from the 3rd term to the 1st term: 20 − 6 − 6 = 8, so the first term is a = 8","The constant is the first term minus the common difference, so nth term = 6n + (8 − 6), that is 6n + 2"],"cues":["How many steps separate the two terms given?","What does the total rise divided by the number of steps give?","Which term of the sequence is still missing before an expression can be written?","How are the first term and the common difference combined into the nth term?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A sequence has first term 5 and term-to-term rule 'add 6'. Write down the first four terms.","a":"5, 11, 17, 23"},{"q":"Write down the first five square numbers and the first five triangular numbers.","a":"Square: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25. Triangular: 1, 3, 6, 10, 15"},{"q":"Find an expression for the nth term of the linear sequence 7, 10, 13, 16, …","a":"3n + 4"},{"q":"Is 84 a term of the sequence whose nth term is 5n + 4?","a":"Yes: 5n + 4 = 84 gives n = 16, so 84 is the 16th term"},{"q":"The first three terms of a geometric progression are 4, 12, 36. Write down the common ratio and work out the 5th term.","a":"Common ratio 3; 5th term 324"}]},"room":{"facts":["A term-to-term rule needs the previous term and is applied over and over from a given first term; a position-to-term (nth term) rule gives any term straight from its position n.","For a linear sequence the nth term is dn + (a − d), where d is the common difference and a is the first term: the difference is the coefficient of n and the constant is the term that would sit at position zero.","Triangular numbers: 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21, 28, 36, 45, 55. Square numbers: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100. Cube numbers: 1, 8, 27, 64, 125, 216, 343, 512, 729, 1000.","In a Fibonacci-type sequence each term is the sum of the two terms before it, so two starting terms are needed; running it backwards means subtracting.","In a geometric progression each term is the previous term multiplied by the common ratio r, and r = any term ÷ the term before it; the sequence rⁿ reads r, r², r³, r⁴, …","A linear sequence has a constant first difference; a quadratic sequence has a constant second difference, so its first differences go up or down by the same amount each time.","To test whether a value V is in a sequence, solve nth term = V: a positive whole-number n means V is the nth term, and any other value of n means V is not in the sequence."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) A sequence has first term 7 and term-to-term rule 'subtract 4'. Write down the first five terms of the sequence.","a":"7, 3, −1, −5, −9","sol":"The rule is applied to each term in turn, starting from 7: 7 − 4 = 3, 3 − 4 = −1, −1 − 4 = −5, −5 − 4 = −9 (M1 for repeatedly subtracting 4, shown by at least three correct terms). The first five terms are 7, 3, −1, −5, −9 (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A term-to-term rule only ever acts on the term you have just written.","Apply the rule again and again, using each new term as the starting point for the next.","Take 4 away from the first term, then take 4 away from whatever that gives, and keep going until five terms are written."],"codes":["A23"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The nth term of a sequence is 5n − 2. Work out the 8th term and the 20th term of this sequence.","a":"8th term 38; 20th term 98","sol":"A position-to-term rule gives a term directly, so substitute the position for n. For the 8th term, 5 × 8 − 2 = 40 − 2 = 38 (B1). For the 20th term, 5 × 20 − 2 = 100 − 2 = 98 (B1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The letter n stands for the position of the term in the sequence.","There is no need to write out the earlier terms — an nth term rule reaches any position in one step.","Replace n by 8 in 5n − 2, then replace n by 20 in the same expression."],"codes":["A23"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Here are the first five triangular numbers: 1, 3, 6, 10, 15. Write down the next two triangular numbers.","a":"21 and 28","sol":"The gaps between triangular numbers are 2, 3, 4, 5, going up by 1 each time, so the next gaps are 6 and 7 (M1 for using gaps that increase by 1, or for adding the next counting number). 15 + 6 = 21 and 21 + 7 = 28 (A1 for both). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Triangular numbers are built by adding one more dot in each new row.","Work out the gap between each pair of terms and look at how the gaps themselves change.","The gaps so far are 2, 3, 4 and 5, so work out what has to be added to 15 next."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Find an expression, in terms of n, for the nth term of the linear sequence 6, 13, 20, 27, …","a":"7n − 1","sol":"The common difference is 13 − 6 = 7, so the coefficient of n is 7 and the sequence is compared with the multiples of 7: 7, 14, 21, 28 (M1 for a common difference of 7 or for 7n seen). Each term of the sequence is 1 less than the matching multiple of 7, so the nth term is 7n − 1 (A1). Check with n = 3: 7 × 3 − 1 = 20. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["In a sequence that goes up by the same amount each time, that amount is built into the rule.","Compare the sequence term by term with the multiples of the common difference.","Work out 13 − 6 to get the common difference, then write the multiples of that number under the sequence and compare them term by term."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write down the first four cube numbers.","a":"1, 8, 27, 64","sol":"A cube number is a whole number multiplied by itself and then by itself again: 1 × 1 × 1 = 1, 2 × 2 × 2 = 8, 3 × 3 × 3 = 27, 4 × 4 × 4 = 64, giving 1, 8, 27, 64 (B1 for all four in order). Total 1 mark.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A cube number counts the small cubes in a solid cube.","Take each of the counting numbers 1, 2, 3, 4 in turn and use it three times as a factor.","Work out 1 × 1 × 1, then 2 × 2 × 2, and carry on."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"The sequence 2, 5, 12.5, 31.25, … is a geometric progression. Write down the common ratio and work out the next term. A calculator may be used.","a":"Common ratio 2.5; next term 78.125","sol":"The common ratio is any term divided by the term before it: 5 ÷ 2 = 2.5, and 12.5 ÷ 5 = 2.5 confirms it (B1). The next term is the last one multiplied by the ratio: 31.25 × 2.5 = 78.125 (B1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["In a geometric progression the same thing is done to every term to reach the next one.","Dividing a term by the term before it gives the number that is being multiplied by each time.","Work out 5 ÷ 2, check it against 12.5 ÷ 5, then use that ratio on the last term given."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The nth term of a sequence is 4n + 3. Explain why 200 is not a term of this sequence.","a":"4n + 3 = 200 gives n = 49.25, which is not a whole number, so 200 is not a term","sol":"If 200 were a term there would be a whole-number position n with 4n + 3 = 200, so 4n = 200 − 3 = 197 (M1 for forming the equation or for 200 − 3 followed by ÷ 4). Then n = 197 ÷ 4 = 49.25, which is not a whole number, so no position gives 200 and 200 is not a term of the sequence (A1 for 49.25 with the conclusion). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Every term of the sequence sits at a whole-number position.","Assume 200 is a term and see what position it would have to have.","Set the nth term expression equal to 200 and take 3 from both sides."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Here are the first four terms of a quadratic sequence: 2, 9, 20, 35. Work out the next two terms.","a":"54 and 77","sol":"The first differences are 9 − 2 = 7, 20 − 9 = 11 and 35 − 20 = 15, which go up by 4 each time, so the second difference is 4 and the next first differences are 19 and 23 (M1 for the first differences with a constant second difference of 4). Then 35 + 19 = 54 (A1) and 54 + 23 = 77 (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The gaps between these terms are not equal, so look at how the gaps change.","Work out the first differences, then the differences of those differences.","Write 7, 11, 15 under the sequence and continue that row before returning to the terms."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"The nth term of a sequence is 60 − 7n. Work out the first term of the sequence that is negative, and write down its position. A calculator may be used.","a":"−3, which is the 9th term","sol":"Substitute increasing positions: n = 8 gives 60 − 56 = 4, which is still positive, and n = 9 gives 60 − 63 = −3 (M1 for testing successive positions, or for solving 60 − 7n < 0 to get n > 8.57…). So the first negative term is at position 9 (A1) and its value is −3 (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Each step up in position takes 7 away from the term, so the terms fall steadily.","Find the position at which 7n first overtakes 60, then work out the term there.","Substitute n = 8 into 60 − 7n and see whether the answer has passed zero yet."],"codes":["A23","A25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) In a Fibonacci-type sequence each term is the sum of the two terms before it. The 3rd term is 11 and the 4th term is 18. Work out the 1st term.","a":"4","sol":"The 4th term is the 2nd term plus the 3rd term, so the 2nd term is 18 − 11 (M1 for subtracting to run the sequence backwards). The 2nd term is 7 (A1). The 3rd term is the 1st term plus the 2nd term, so the 1st term is 11 − 7 = 4 (A1). Check: 4, 7, 11, 18. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The rule that builds the sequence forwards can be undone to work backwards.","Adding gives a later term, so the opposite operation recovers an earlier one.","The 2nd term and the 3rd term add to give the 4th term, so work out 18 − 11 first."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Find an expression, in terms of n, for the nth term of the sequence 27, 23, 19, 15, … and use it to work out the 12th term.","a":"31 − 4n; 12th term −17","sol":"The sequence falls by 4 each time, so the common difference is −4 and the coefficient of n is −4 (M1). The term at position zero is 27 + 4 = 31, so the nth term is 31 − 4n (A1). Substituting n = 12 gives 31 − 4 × 12 = 31 − 48 = −17 (B1, follow through on a correct expression). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The sequence is decreasing, so think about what that does to the number in front of n.","The common difference is the coefficient of n, and the constant is the term one step before the first.","Work out 23 − 27 to get the common difference, then compare the sequence term by term with the multiples of that number."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Patterns are made from matchsticks. Pattern 1 uses 4 matchsticks, pattern 2 uses 7 matchsticks and pattern 3 uses 10 matchsticks. Priya has 150 matchsticks. Find an expression for the number of matchsticks in pattern n, then work out the largest pattern Priya can make and how many matchsticks she has left over.","a":"3n + 1; pattern 49, with 2 matchsticks left over","sol":"Each new pattern uses 3 more matchsticks than the one before, so the common difference is 3 (M1) and, since pattern 1 uses 4, the expression is 3n + 1 (A1). Priya needs 3n + 1 to be at most 150, so 3n ≤ 149 and n ≤ 49.66… , or by trial 3 × 49 + 1 = 148 while 3 × 50 + 1 = 151 is too many (M1). The largest pattern is pattern 49, which uses 148 matchsticks, leaving 150 − 148 = 2 matchsticks (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The numbers of matchsticks form a linear sequence, so it has an nth term rule.","Find the rule first, then ask which position it can reach without going past 150.","Work out 7 − 4 to get the step between one pattern and the next, then compare the multiples of that step with 4, 7, 10."],"codes":["A25","A23"]},{"q":"The first three terms of a geometric progression are 5, 15, 45. Work out the first term of this progression that is greater than 10 000, and say which position it has. A calculator may be used.","a":"10 935, the 8th term","sol":"The common ratio is 15 ÷ 5 = 3 (M1). Multiplying on by 3 each time gives 135, 405, 1215, 3645, 10935 as the 4th to 8th terms (M1 for continuing until 10 000 is passed). The 7th term, 3645, is still below 10 000, so the first term greater than 10 000 is 10 935, the 8th term (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Each term of a geometric progression is a fixed multiple of the one before it.","Find that multiplier, then keep multiplying and count the positions as you go.","Work out 15 ÷ 5 and check it with 45 ÷ 15, then multiply 45 by that ratio."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Sequence A has nth term 2n + 1. Sequence B is the sequence of triangular numbers. Write down all the numbers less than 30 that appear in both sequence A and sequence B.","a":"3, 15 and 21","sol":"The triangular numbers below 30 are 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21, 28 (M1). Sequence A is 3, 5, 7, 9, …, the odd numbers from 3 upwards, so a triangular number is in A only if it is odd and at least 3 (M1); this rules out 6, 10 and 28 for being even, and 1 because 2n + 1 = 1 would need position n = 0. The numbers in both sequences are 3, 15 and 21 (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Two sequences share a number when it can be found in each of them separately.","List one sequence up to the limit given, then test each of its numbers against the other rule.","Write out the triangular numbers as far as 30 before you look at the rule for sequence A at all."],"codes":["A24","A23"]},{"q":"Sequence P has nth term 4n + 1 and sequence Q has nth term 46 − 5n. There is one position at which the two sequences have the same term. Work out that position and the value of the term. A calculator may be used.","a":"Position 5; the term is 21","sol":"The terms are equal when 4n + 1 = 46 − 5n (M1). Adding 5n to both sides and subtracting 1 gives 9n = 45 (M1). So n = 5 (A1). Substituting back, P gives 4 × 5 + 1 = 21 and Q gives 46 − 25 = 21, so the common term is 21 (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["'The same term' means the two expressions have the same value at that position.","Set the two nth term expressions equal to each other and solve for n.","Write 4n + 1 = 46 − 5n and collect the n terms on one side."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"Ella runs 2.5 km in week 1 of a training plan, and each week after that she runs 0.4 km further than in the week before. Find an expression, in terms of n, for the distance she runs in week n, and work out the first week in which she runs more than 10 km. A calculator may be used.","a":"0.4n + 2.1 km; week 20","sol":"The distances form a linear sequence with common difference 0.4 and first term 2.5, so the week-n distance is 2.5 + 0.4(n − 1) (M1), which simplifies to 0.4n + 2.1 km (A1). Setting 0.4n + 2.1 greater than 10 gives 0.4n greater than 7.9 and n greater than 19.75, or by trial week 19 gives 0.4 × 19 + 2.1 = 9.7 km, which is not enough (M1). The first week over 10 km is week 20, when she runs 0.4 × 20 + 2.1 = 10.1 km (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The weekly distances go up by the same amount each week, so they form a linear sequence.","Build the nth term from the common difference, then find the first position where it passes 10.","Count how many weekly increases have happened by week n, then add that many of them to the 2.5 km of week 1."],"codes":["A25","A23"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write down the next two terms of the sequence 5, 9, 13, 17, …","a":"21 and 25"},{"q":"The nth term of a sequence is 4n − 1. Which term of the sequence is equal to 59?","a":"The 15th term"},{"q":"Write down the next two terms of the Fibonacci-type sequence 2, 5, 7, 12, …","a":"19 and 31"},{"q":"Find an expression, in terms of n, for the nth term of the sequence 11, 8, 5, 2, …","a":"14 − 3n"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Sequence","back":"An ordered list of numbers, called terms, that follows a rule. The position of a term is counted n = 1, 2, 3, … from the first term.","code":"A23","open":false},{"front":"Term-to-term rule","back":"A rule that produces the next term from the previous one, such as 'add 3' or 'multiply by 2 then subtract 1'. A first term must be given, and the rule is applied repeatedly.","code":"A23","open":false},{"front":"Position-to-term rule (the nth term)","back":"A rule that gives any term directly from its position n. With nth term 4n + 1 the 10th term is 4 × 10 + 1 = 41, with no need for the earlier terms.","code":"A23","open":false},{"front":"Linear (arithmetic) sequence","back":"A sequence with a constant difference between consecutive terms, so its nth term has the form dn + c and its terms lie on a straight line.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"The nth term of a linear sequence","back":"nth term = dn + (a − d), where d is the common difference and a is the first term. The difference is the coefficient of n; the constant is the term at position zero.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"Common difference","back":"The fixed amount added to each term of a linear sequence to reach the next. It is negative when the sequence decreases, as in 40, 33, 26, … where d = −7.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"Testing whether a value is a term of a linear sequence","back":"Set the nth term expression equal to the value and solve for n. A positive whole number means the value is the nth term; any other value of n means it is not in the sequence.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"Triangular numbers","back":"1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21, 28, 36, 45, 55 — the dots that build a triangle. The gaps go up by 1 each time: +2, +3, +4, +5, …","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Square numbers","back":"1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100 — each is n × n, the number of dots in a square array.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Cube numbers","back":"1, 8, 27, 64, 125, 216, 343, 512, 729, 1000 — each is n × n × n, the number of unit cubes in a cube.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Fibonacci-type sequence","back":"A sequence in which each term is the sum of the two terms before it, so two starting terms are needed: 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, … Subtracting runs it backwards.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Geometric progression","back":"A sequence in which each term is the previous term multiplied by a constant common ratio r. The sequence rⁿ reads r, r², r³, r⁴, … for n = 1, 2, 3, 4, …","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Common ratio of a geometric progression","back":"r = any term ÷ the term before it, and the value is the same for every such pair. For 2, 6, 18, 54 the common ratio is 6 ÷ 2 = 3.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Quadratic sequence","back":"A sequence whose first differences are not constant but change by the same amount each time, so the second difference is constant. In 4, 7, 12, 19 the differences 3, 5, 7 rise by 2.","code":"A24","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Nothing in this room is printed on either tier's Exam Aid formulae sheet (W87990A Foundation, W87991A Higher). Every rule below must be written from memory.","Recall: nth term of a linear sequence = dn + (a − d), where d is the common difference and a is the first term.","Recall: the triangular numbers 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21, 28, 36, 45, 55; the square numbers 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100; the cube numbers 1, 8, 27, 64, 125, 216, 343, 512, 729, 1000.","Recall: in a Fibonacci-type sequence each term is the sum of the two terms before it; in a geometric progression each term is the previous term × r, and the sequence rⁿ reads r, r², r³, r⁴, …","Recall: common ratio r = any term ÷ the term before it; common difference d = any term − the term before it.","Recall: a linear sequence has a constant first difference and a quadratic sequence has a constant second difference — the test that tells you which method to use.","Recall: a value V is a term of a sequence only if solving nth term = V gives a positive whole number n, and that value of n is its position.","What the Foundation Exam Aid does print, none of it needed in this room: area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h; volume of a prism = area of cross section × length; circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd; area of a circle = πr²; a² + b² = c²; sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b; total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ; P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B).","The Higher Exam Aid prints all of those and, in addition, the quadratic formula x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/2a, the sine rule, the cosine rule, area of a triangle = ½ab sin C and P(A and B) = P(A given B) P(B). No sequence rule appears on either sheet."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T11:57:01Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.95,"notes":"Every answer key, solution line, mark split, card and formulae-sheet claim checks out — the Foundation and Higher Exam Aid lists match W87990A/W87991A exactly and the room correctly never asks for the nth term of a quadratic sequence (Higher-only A25) — so it is publishable as it stands, with only the difficulty label on the geometric-progression question worth a second look."}}},"ratio":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"ratio","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Ratio, proportion and rates of change is the largest single block of Foundation content — between 22% and 28% of the course at this tier — and it is where the exam meets real life: sharing money, mixing concrete, map scales, best buys, speed and density, percentage change, interest and depreciation. Almost every AO3 problem-solving question on a Foundation paper sits in this room or passes through it, because a ratio, a multiplier or a rate is nearly always the step that turns a wordy situation into a calculation. The techniques are short, so what goes wrong is units, which quantity divides which, and leaving out the intermediate value the method mark is waiting for.","idea":[{"code":"R1","h":"Converting standard and compound units","t":"Changing units is multiplying or dividing by the conversion factor: 1 m = 100 cm, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 hour = 60 minutes. Area squares that factor (1 m² = 100² = 10 000 cm²) and volume cubes it (1 m³ = 100³ = 1 000 000 cm³), which is the single most common slip in this room. A compound unit is converted one part at a time: to turn 25 m/s into km/h, multiply by 3600 for the seconds and divide by 1000 for the metres. Examiners give the method mark for the conversion line, so write it down rather than doing it in your head."},{"code":"R2","h":"Scale factors, scale diagrams and maps","t":"A scale written as 1 : n means one unit on the drawing stands for n of the same units in real life, so a measured length × n gives the real length and a real length ÷ n gives the drawing length. A scale written as a statement — 1 cm to 2.5 m — is used the same way. Work in a single unit first (change metres to centimetres, or centimetres to metres) and only convert to the unit asked for at the end. Questions ask for a real distance in kilometres from a map, or for the length to draw on a plan, and the unit conversion carries its own mark."},{"code":"R3","h":"One quantity as a fraction of another","t":"To write one quantity as a fraction of another, put the first over the second with both in the same unit, then cancel: 40 cm as a fraction of 3 m is 40/300 = 2/15. The fraction is allowed to be greater than 1 — 7 as a fraction of 4 is 7/4 — and an improper fraction is a perfectly good answer unless the question asks for a mixed number. The examined slip is comparing unlike units, so convert before you divide; the conversion line is the method mark and the cancelled fraction is the accuracy mark."},{"code":"R4","h":"Ratio notation and simplest form","t":"A ratio compares parts using a colon, with both parts in the same unit and no units written in the ratio itself. Simplest form means dividing every part by their highest common factor so the parts are whole numbers with no common factor: 20 : 35 = 4 : 7. Where a question asks for the form 1 : n, divide both parts by the first part, and n may then be a decimal. A ratio containing a decimal or a fraction is cleared by multiplying every part by the same number, so 0.75 : 1 becomes 3 : 4."},{"code":"R5","h":"Dividing a quantity in a given ratio","t":"Sharing in the ratio a : b means cutting the quantity into a + b equal parts. Find one part by dividing the whole by the total number of parts, then multiply up for each share; the division line is where the method mark falls, so write the value of one part down. A part:whole ratio works the same way once you read off how many parts each side holds. Adding your two shares back to the original total is a free check, and mixing, concentration and scaling problems are the same calculation dressed in context."},{"code":"R6","h":"Multiplicative relationships as ratios or fractions","t":"'One quantity is so many times another' is a ratio in disguise: if A is 1.5 times B then A : B = 1.5 : 1 = 3 : 2, and as a fraction A/B = 3/2. Either form can be fed into the next step of a calculation, and the direction matters — A : B is not B : A. Multiplying both parts by the same number clears a decimal or a fraction from the ratio. Questions state the relationship in words and ask for it as a ratio in its simplest form, or give the ratio and ask how many times bigger one quantity is."},{"code":"R7","h":"Proportion as equality of ratios","t":"Two pairs of quantities are in proportion when their ratios are equal, so 3 : 5 and 12 : 20 are in proportion because both simplify to 3 : 5. That equality is the tool for a missing value: scale one ratio up or down until one part matches, and the other part follows — in 3 : 5 = x : 35 the second parts scale by 7, so x scales by 7 too. In problems the equality is often hidden in a sentence about parts of a group, and the step examiners look for is the scaling factor written down."},{"code":"R8","h":"Ratios, fractions and linear functions","t":"A ratio, a fraction and a straight-line graph through the origin all say the same thing. If x : y = a : b then y/x = b/a and y = (b/a)x, whose graph is a straight line through the origin with gradient b/a. Reading the gradient off such a graph gives the ratio, and a table of values that is in a fixed ratio must plot as a straight line through the origin. This link is what lets a proportion question be answered from a graph, from a table or from algebra, and the exam moves between the three."},{"code":"R9","h":"Percentages and percentage change","t":"Per cent means 'parts per hundred', so p% is the fraction p/100 and the decimal p ÷ 100. One quantity as a percentage of another is first ÷ second × 100. Change is handled multiplicatively: a p% increase multiplies by 1 + p/100 and a p% decrease by 1 − p/100, percentages over 100% are allowed, and an original-value question is undone by dividing by that multiplier rather than by adding the percentage back. Simple interest pays the same amount each year, worked out on the starting amount only, so it is principal × rate ÷ 100 × years."},{"code":"R10","h":"Direct and inverse proportion","t":"In direct proportion two quantities scale together, so the ratio between them is fixed: find the value of one unit — one book, one pen, one litre — then multiply, and the unitary line is the method mark. In inverse proportion their product is fixed, so one rises as the other falls: six pumps taking 14 hours is 84 pump-hours of work however many pumps are used. The trap is assuming every relationship is direct, so ask whether doubling one quantity should double the other or halve it before you calculate."},{"code":"R11","h":"Compound units","t":"A compound unit is one quantity per unit of another: speed = distance ÷ time, density = mass ÷ volume, pressure = force ÷ area, pay = rate × hours, unit price = cost ÷ amount. None of these formulae is printed on the Exam Aid, so all must be remembered, together with the two rearrangements each one gives. The unit written on the answer states which division was done — g/cm³ is grams divided by cubic centimetres — so check the given quantities are in those units before dividing, and quote the unit with the answer or lose the accuracy mark."},{"code":"R12","h":"Comparing lengths, areas and volumes by ratio","t":"Corresponding lengths of similar figures are in a fixed ratio, and that ratio is exactly what a scale factor records. Because area is two lengths multiplied and volume is three, a length ratio a : b gives an area ratio a² : b² and a volume ratio a³ : b³: lengths 2 : 5 give volumes 8 : 125. The same fixed ratio of corresponding sides is what a trigonometric ratio measures in similar right-angled triangles. Questions ask for the area or volume ratio from a length ratio, or work back from an area ratio to the lengths."},{"code":"R13","h":"Equations of direct and inverse proportion","t":"Direct proportion is written y = kx and inverse proportion y = k/x, and saying X is inversely proportional to Y is the same as saying X is proportional to 1/Y. The number k is the constant of proportionality: substitute the one pair of values the question gives to find it, write the completed equation, then use that equation for any further value asked for. Interpreting the equation is the Foundation skill — reading off that doubling x halves y when y = k/x — and the substitution line earns the first method mark."},{"code":"R14","h":"Gradient as a rate of change","t":"The gradient of a straight-line graph is the change in the vertical quantity for each unit of the horizontal one, so it is a rate with units of y per unit of x: pounds per day on a cost graph, metres per second on a distance–time graph. A graph of direct proportion is a straight line through the origin, and its gradient is the rate; a graph of inverse proportion is a curve falling away from both axes and never touching them. Interpreting means writing the value with its units and saying what it means in the situation."},{"code":"R16","h":"Growth and decay problems","t":"Repeated percentage change is repeated multiplication: apply the multiplier once for each period, so n periods give the multiplier raised to the power n. £2000 growing at 3% a year for 5 years is 2000 × 1.03⁵, and a van losing 12% a year for two years is multiplied by 0.88². Compound interest is the growth case and depreciation the decay case, and where the rate changes the multipliers are simply multiplied together in order. Read whether the answer wanted is the final amount or the total change, and subtract if it is the change."}],"examples":[{"title":"Sharing a quantity in a given ratio","setup":"A box of 84 chocolates is shared between Ravi and Sam in the ratio 3 : 4. Work out how many chocolates Ravi gets and how many Sam gets.","steps":["The ratio 3 : 4 cuts the whole box into 3 + 4 = 7 equal parts.","One part is worth 84 ÷ 7 = 12 chocolates.","Ravi has 3 parts, so Ravi gets 3 × 12 = 36 chocolates.","Sam has 4 parts, so Sam gets 4 × 12 = 48 chocolates: Ravi gets 36 chocolates and Sam gets 48 chocolates, and 36 + 48 = 84 confirms the share."],"cues":["How many equal parts is the box cut into?","What is one part worth in chocolates?","How many of those parts belong to Ravi?","How many parts are left for Sam, and how can the total be checked?"]},{"title":"Finding the price before a reduction","setup":"In a sale, the price of a coat is reduced by 15%. The sale price of the coat is £102. Work out the price of the coat before the sale.","steps":["A reduction of 15% leaves 100% − 15% = 85% of the original price.","As a multiplier that is 0.85, so original price × 0.85 = 102.","Dividing undoes the multiplication: original price = 102 ÷ 0.85.","The coat cost £120 before the sale, and 120 × 0.85 = 102 checks it."],"cues":["What percentage of the original price does £102 represent?","What single number multiplies the old price to give the new one?","Which operation undoes multiplying by that number?","How can the answer be checked against the sale price?"]},{"title":"Compound interest over several years","setup":"Kemi invests £4500 in a savings account paying 2.5% compound interest per year. She leaves the money in the account for 6 years and takes nothing out. Work out the value of her investment at the end of the 6 years, giving your answer to the nearest penny.","steps":["Each year the amount in the account is multiplied by 1 + 2.5/100 = 1.025.","Six years means six multiplications, so value = 4500 × 1.025⁶ — the form the Exam Aid prints as Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ.","1.025⁶ = 1.159693… so the value is 4500 × 1.159693… = 5218.6203…","To the nearest penny the investment is worth £5218.62."],"cues":["By what single number does one year's interest multiply the amount?","How many times is that multiplier applied?","What does that power come to on a calculator?","How should a money answer like this be rounded?"]},{"title":"Density from mass and volume","setup":"A solid block of copper has a mass of 1.79 kg and a volume of 200 cm³. Work out the density of the copper in g/cm³.","steps":["The answer is wanted in grams per cubic centimetre, so change the mass: 1.79 kg = 1.79 × 1000 = 1790 g.","Density = mass ÷ volume, which here is 1790 ÷ 200.","1790 ÷ 200 = 8.95, so the density is 8.95 g/cm³."],"cues":["Which unit must the mass be in before you divide?","Which quantity is divided by which?","What unit must be written on the answer?"]},{"title":"Inverse proportion with a fixed amount of work","setup":"Six identical pumps, all working at the same rate, empty a tank in 14 hours. Work out how long 4 of these pumps would take to empty the same tank.","steps":["Emptying the tank is a fixed amount of work: 6 pumps × 14 hours = 84 pump-hours.","One pump on its own would therefore need 84 hours — fewer pumps means more time, so this is inverse proportion.","Four pumps share that work: 84 ÷ 4 = 21 hours."],"cues":["What stays the same however many pumps are used?","How long would a single pump need?","How is the one-pump time shared between four pumps?"]},{"title":"A real distance from a map scale","setup":"A map is drawn to a scale of 1 : 50 000. On the map, two villages are 7.4 cm apart. Work out the real distance between the villages in kilometres.","steps":["Each 1 cm measured stands for 50 000 cm on the ground, so multiply the measurement by 50 000.","Real distance = 7.4 × 50 000 = 370 000 cm.","370 000 cm ÷ 100 = 3700 m.","3700 m ÷ 1000 = 3.7 km."],"cues":["What does one centimetre on this map stand for?","What is the real distance in centimetres?","How many centimetres make one metre?","How many metres make one kilometre?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Convert 3 m² into cm².","a":"30 000 cm² (the linear factor 100 is squared)"},{"q":"£45 is shared in the ratio 4 : 5. Work out the larger share.","a":"£25"},{"q":"X is inversely proportional to Y, and X = 12 when Y = 3. Work out X when Y = 9.","a":"X = 4 (since X = 36/Y)"},{"q":"A car travels 45 km in 30 minutes. Work out its average speed in km/h.","a":"90 km/h"}]},"room":{"facts":["One part of a share = quantity ÷ (total number of parts): to share in the ratio a : b, divide by a + b, then multiply each share up.","Simplest form: divide every part of the ratio by their highest common factor, with both parts in the same unit and no units written in the ratio.","Speed = distance ÷ time, density = mass ÷ volume, pressure = force ÷ area — none of these is on the Exam Aid, and each gives two rearrangements.","A p% increase multiplies by 1 + p/100 and a p% decrease by 1 − p/100; to find the original value, divide the new value by that multiplier.","Simple interest = principal × rate ÷ 100 × number of years; compound interest applies the multiplier once per year instead.","Direct proportion: y = kx, a straight line through the origin with gradient k. Inverse proportion: y = k/x, so xy is constant.","Similar shapes with lengths in the ratio a : b have areas in the ratio a² : b² and volumes in the ratio a³ : b³.","Unit conversions: 1 m = 100 cm, 1 m² = 10 000 cm², 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 hour = 3600 s, and 1 m/s = 3.6 km/h.","A scale of 1 : n means map length × n = real length in the same unit, and real length ÷ n = map length."],"questions":[{"q":"Write the ratio 18 : 30 in its simplest form. (non-calculator)","a":"3 : 5","m":1,"d":1,"codes":["R4"],"sol":"The highest common factor of 18 and 30 is 6, and both parts are divided by it: 18 ÷ 6 = 3 and 30 ÷ 6 = 5, so 18 : 30 = 3 : 5. B1 for 3 : 5 (3 : 5 only; 9 : 15 is not simplest form).","hints":["Simplest form means the two parts share no factor above 1.","Divide both parts of the ratio by their highest common factor.","The first line divides each part by the same whole number."]},{"q":"Write 0.8 m : 50 cm as a ratio of two integers in its simplest form. (non-calculator)","a":"8 : 5","m":2,"d":1,"codes":["R4","R1"],"sol":"Both parts must be in the same unit first: 0.8 m = 0.8 × 100 = 80 cm, so the ratio is 80 : 50. M1 for 80 : 50 (or for 0.8 : 0.5 with both in metres). Both parts divide by 10: 80 ÷ 10 = 8 and 50 ÷ 10 = 5. A1 for 8 : 5.","hints":["A ratio compares two quantities measured in the same unit.","Convert one quantity so that both are in centimetres, then cancel.","The first line rewrites the metres as centimetres before any cancelling."]},{"q":"Express 45 minutes as a fraction of 2 hours. Give your answer in its lowest terms. (non-calculator)","a":"3/8","m":2,"d":1,"codes":["R3","R1"],"sol":"Put both times in the same unit: 2 hours = 2 × 60 = 120 minutes, so the fraction is 45/120. M1 for 45/120. Both parts divide by 15: 45 ÷ 15 = 3 and 120 ÷ 15 = 8. A1 for 3/8.","hints":["The two quantities must be measured in the same unit before one can be written over the other.","Change the hours into minutes, put the first quantity over the second, then cancel.","The first line converts the 2 hours into minutes."]},{"q":"Priya is paid £11.40 for each hour she works. Work out her pay for 7.5 hours of work. (a calculator may be used)","a":"£85.50","m":2,"d":1,"codes":["R11"],"sol":"A rate of pay is money per hour, so pay = rate × number of hours = 11.40 × 7.5. M1 for 11.40 × 7.5. This gives 85.5, written as money to the penny. A1 for £85.50.","hints":["A rate of pay tells you what one hour of work is worth.","Multiply the hourly rate by the number of hours worked.","The first line multiplies the rate per hour by the hours, and the answer is then written as money."]},{"q":"P is 2.5 times Q. Write the ratio P : Q in its simplest form, using integers. (non-calculator)","a":"5 : 2","m":2,"d":1,"codes":["R6","R4"],"sol":"'P is 2.5 times Q' means that for every 1 of Q there is 2.5 of P, so P : Q = 2.5 : 1. M1 for 2.5 : 1 (or for P/Q = 2.5). Multiplying both parts by 2 clears the decimal: 2.5 × 2 = 5 and 1 × 2 = 2. A1 for 5 : 2.","hints":["'So many times as big' can be written straight down as a comparison of two quantities.","Write the relationship against a single unit of Q, then clear the decimal from the ratio.","The first line writes the ratio with 1 as its second part."]},{"q":"Mortar is made by mixing cement and sand in the ratio 2 : 7 by mass. A builder makes 810 kg of mortar. Work out the mass of sand he uses. (a calculator may be used)","a":"630 kg","m":3,"d":2,"codes":["R5"],"sol":"The ratio 2 : 7 cuts the mortar into 2 + 7 = 9 equal parts. M1 for 9 parts seen. One part is 810 ÷ 9 = 90 kg. M1 for 810 ÷ 9 (= 90). Sand is 7 of those parts: 7 × 90 = 630. A1 for 630 kg, with the unit. (Check: cement is 2 × 90 = 180 kg and 180 + 630 = 810 kg.)","hints":["The two numbers in the ratio count equal parts of the whole mix.","Find how much one part weighs, then take as many parts as the sand needs.","The first line adds the parts of the ratio to find how many there are altogether."]},{"q":"Nadia invests £950 in an account that pays 2.4% simple interest per year. She leaves the money in the account for 5 years. Work out the total interest she earns. (a calculator may be used)","a":"£114","m":3,"d":2,"codes":["R9"],"sol":"Simple interest is worked out on the £950 only, so the same interest is paid each year. One year's interest is 950 × 2.4 ÷ 100 = £22.80. M1 for 950 × 0.024 (or 22.80 seen). Over 5 years the interest is 22.80 × 5. M1 for × 5. A1 for £114 (accept £114.00).","hints":["Simple interest is worked out on the starting amount every year, never on the interest already earned.","Find one year's interest, then account for all the years.","The first line finds the interest for a single year from the amount invested and the rate."]},{"q":"7 identical books have a total mass of 1218 g. Work out the total mass of 12 of these books. (a calculator may be used)","a":"2088 g","m":3,"d":2,"codes":["R10"],"sol":"The books are identical, so mass is directly proportional to the number of books. One book has mass 1218 ÷ 7 = 174 g. M1 for 1218 ÷ 7 (= 174). Twelve books have mass 174 × 12. M1 for × 12. A1 for 2088 g (accept 2.088 kg).","hints":["Identical books means the mass grows in step with the number of books.","Work out what one book weighs before scaling up.","The first line divides the total mass by the number of books it belongs to."]},{"q":"A car is travelling at a steady speed of 18 m/s. Change this speed into kilometres per hour. (non-calculator)","a":"64.8 km/h","m":3,"d":2,"codes":["R1","R11"],"sol":"There are 60 × 60 = 3600 seconds in an hour, so in one hour the car covers 18 × 3600 = 64 800 m. M1 for × 3600 (or for × 60 twice). There are 1000 m in a kilometre, so 64 800 m = 64 800 ÷ 1000 = 64.8 km. M1 for ÷ 1000. A1 for 64.8 km/h, with the unit.","hints":["A compound unit is changed one part at a time — the distance part and the time part.","Find how far the car goes in one hour, then rewrite that distance in kilometres.","The first line converts the distance travelled each second into the distance travelled each hour."]},{"q":"Two similar cylinders have heights in the ratio 3 : 4. Work out the ratio of their volumes, in its simplest form. (a calculator may be used)","a":"27 : 64","m":2,"d":2,"codes":["R12"],"sol":"Corresponding lengths of similar solids are in the ratio 3 : 4, and volume is three lengths multiplied together, so the volume ratio is the cube of the length ratio. M1 for cubing both parts (3³ : 4³ seen, or a scale factor of 4/3 cubed). 3³ = 27 and 4³ = 64, and 27 and 64 have no common factor. A1 for 27 : 64.","hints":["Volume is built from three lengths multiplied together.","Do to each part of the length ratio what volume does to a length.","The first line raises each part of the length ratio to the power that volume needs."]},{"q":"The cost £C of hiring a floor sander for d days is shown by a straight-line graph through the origin. The line passes through the point (6, 27). Interpret the gradient of the line by working out the cost of hiring the sander for one day. (a calculator may be used)","a":"£4.50 per day","m":2,"d":2,"codes":["R14","R8"],"sol":"The line passes through the origin, so C is directly proportional to d and C = kd, where the gradient k is the cost of one day. From the origin (0, 0) to the given point (6, 27) the change in C is 27 and the change in d is 6, so the gradient = 27 ÷ 6. M1 for 27 ÷ 6 (or for 27 = 6k). This gives 4.5, and the units of the gradient are pounds per day. A1 for £4.50 per day (the unit must be seen or implied by the interpretation).","hints":["On a straight-line graph through the origin, steepness measures how much one quantity changes for each unit of the other.","Divide the change in cost by the change in the number of days between the origin and the given point.","The first line is the change in C divided by the change in d for that point."]},{"q":"A garden path is 45 m long. It is drawn on a plan with a scale of 1 : 500. Work out the length of the path on the plan, in centimetres. (a calculator may be used)","a":"9 cm","m":3,"d":2,"codes":["R2","R1"],"sol":"A scale of 1 : 500 compares lengths in the same unit, and the answer is wanted in centimetres, so work in centimetres: 45 m = 45 × 100 = 4500 cm. M1 for 4500 cm. Going from real to plan divides by the scale: 4500 ÷ 500. M1 for ÷ 500. A1 for 9 cm.","hints":["A scale of 1 : 500 compares two lengths measured in the same unit.","Put the real length into the unit the answer is wanted in, then go from real length to plan length.","The first line rewrites the real length of the path in centimetres."]},{"q":"A van is bought for £18 000. Its value falls by 15% during the first year and by 12% during each of the next two years. Work out the value of the van at the end of the three years. (a calculator may be used)","a":"£11 848.32","m":3,"d":3,"codes":["R16","R9"],"sol":"A fall of 15% multiplies by 1 − 0.15 = 0.85 and a fall of 12% multiplies by 1 − 0.12 = 0.88. M1 for 0.85 and 0.88 both seen as multipliers. The 12% fall happens twice, so value = 18 000 × 0.85 × 0.88². M1 for the complete method. 18 000 × 0.85 = 15 300, 15 300 × 0.88 = 13 464 and 13 464 × 0.88 = 11 848.32. A1 for £11 848.32.","hints":["A percentage fall can be done in one multiplication instead of a subtraction.","Apply the multiplier for each year in turn, remembering that the later rate is used more than once.","The first line writes down the multiplier for each of the two different percentage falls."]},{"q":"Washing powder is sold in two sizes. A 1.5 kg box costs £4.20 and a 2.6 kg box costs £7.02. Work out which box is better value for money, showing the cost per kilogram of each box. (a calculator may be used)","a":"The 2.6 kg box (£2.70 per kg, against £2.80 per kg)","m":3,"d":3,"codes":["R10","R11"],"sol":"Compare the boxes by the cost of the same amount of powder — one kilogram. Small box: 4.20 ÷ 1.5 = 2.80. M1 for 4.20 ÷ 1.5 or for 7.02 ÷ 2.6. Large box: 7.02 ÷ 2.6 = 2.70. A1 for £2.80 per kg and £2.70 per kg. Since £2.70 is less than £2.80, each kilogram costs less from the larger box. B1 dep on the M1 and the A1, so both unit costs must be seen, for the conclusion naming the 2.6 kg box.","hints":["Two boxes can only be compared once they are measured against the same amount of powder.","Work out what one kilogram costs from each box, then compare the two costs.","The first line divides the cost of a box by the mass of powder in it."]},{"q":"In a table tennis club the ratio of boys to girls is 3 : 5. There are 24 more girls than boys. Work out the total number of members of the club. (non-calculator)","a":"96","m":3,"d":3,"codes":["R5","R7"],"sol":"Boys are 3 parts and girls are 5 parts, so the girls have 5 − 3 = 2 parts more than the boys, and those 2 parts are the 24 extra girls. M1 for 2 parts = 24. One part is 24 ÷ 2 = 12 members. M1 for 24 ÷ 2 (= 12). The club has 3 + 5 = 8 parts altogether, so the total is 8 × 12 = 96. A1 for 96. (Check: 36 boys and 60 girls differ by 24.)","hints":["The difference between the two groups is also measured in parts of the ratio.","Work out how many parts the 24 extra girls represent, then find the value of one part.","The first line subtracts the smaller part of the ratio from the larger one."]},{"q":"The pressure P atmospheres of a fixed mass of gas is inversely proportional to its volume V cm³, so P = k/V. At a volume of 250 cm³ the pressure is 1.8 atmospheres. The gas is squeezed until its pressure reaches 3 atmospheres. Work out the decrease in the volume of the gas. (a calculator may be used)","a":"100 cm³","m":4,"d":3,"codes":["R13","R10"],"sol":"Substituting the given pair into P = k/V gives 1.8 = k/250. M1 for 1.8 = k/250 (or k = 1.8 × 250). So k = 450 and the equation is P = 450/V. A1 for k = 450. At a pressure of 3 atmospheres the volume is V = 450 ÷ 3. M1 for 450 ÷ 3 (= 150). That gives 150 cm³, so the volume has fallen from 250 cm³ to 150 cm³ and the decrease is 250 − 150 = 100. A1 for 100 cm³, with the unit. (Check: 3 × 150 = 450 = 1.8 × 250.)","hints":["Inverse proportion means the pressure and the volume multiply to the same fixed number every time.","Use the pair of values given to complete the equation, then work out the new volume before comparing it with the old one.","The first line substitutes the given pressure and volume into P = k/V."]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write the ratio 24 : 36 in its simplest form.","a":"2 : 3"},{"q":"Express 12 as a percentage of 48.","a":"25%"},{"q":"Write down the single number an amount is multiplied by to decrease it by 6%.","a":"0.94"},{"q":"A cyclist travels 24 km in 1 hour 30 minutes. Work out the average speed in km/h.","a":"16 km/h"}]},"cards":[{"code":"R4","open":false,"front":"Ratio in its simplest form","back":"Put both parts in the same unit, then divide every part by their highest common factor: 20 : 35 = 4 : 7. The parts are whole numbers and no unit is written in the ratio."},{"code":"R5","open":false,"front":"Sharing a quantity in the ratio a : b","back":"The quantity is cut into a + b equal parts, so one part = quantity ÷ (a + b). The two shares are a × one part and b × one part, and they add back to the quantity."},{"code":"R3","open":false,"front":"One quantity as a fraction of another","back":"Put the first quantity over the second with both in the same unit, then cancel: 40 cm out of 3 m is 40/300 = 2/15. The fraction may be greater than 1."},{"code":"R1","open":false,"front":"Converting length, area, volume and compound units","back":"Length uses the conversion factor, area its square (1 m² = 10 000 cm²) and volume its cube (1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³). A compound unit is converted one part at a time."},{"code":"R2","open":false,"front":"A scale written as 1 : n","back":"One unit on the drawing stands for n of the same units in real life: map length × n = real length, real length ÷ n = map length. 1 : 25 000 means 1 cm stands for 250 m."},{"code":"R6","open":false,"front":"A multiplicative relationship written as a ratio","back":"If A is k times B then A : B = k : 1 and A/B = k. Clear a decimal by multiplying both parts: 1.5 : 1 = 3 : 2. The order A : B matters."},{"code":"R7","open":false,"front":"Proportion as equality of ratios","back":"Two pairs are in proportion when their ratios are equal: 3 : 5 and 12 : 20 both simplify to 3 : 5. A missing value is found by scaling one ratio until a part matches the other."},{"code":"R8","open":false,"front":"A ratio written as a linear function","back":"If x : y = a : b then y = (b/a)x. The graph is a straight line through the origin whose gradient b/a is the ratio written as a fraction."},{"code":"R9","open":false,"front":"Percentage multiplier","back":"A p% increase multiplies by 1 + p/100 and a p% decrease by 1 − p/100, so +12% is × 1.12 and −12% is × 0.88. To find the original value, divide the new value by the multiplier."},{"code":"R10","open":false,"front":"Direct and inverse proportion compared","back":"Direct: y ÷ x is constant, so find the value of one unit then multiply. Inverse: x × y is constant, so as one quantity rises the other falls in the same proportion."},{"code":"R11","open":false,"front":"Compound units: speed, density and pressure","back":"Speed = distance ÷ time, density = mass ÷ volume, pressure = force ÷ area. The unit on the answer names the division: m/s, g/cm³, N/m². None is printed on the Exam Aid."},{"code":"R12","open":false,"front":"Length, area and volume ratios of similar shapes","back":"Lengths in the ratio a : b give areas in the ratio a² : b² and volumes in the ratio a³ : b³. A scale factor k multiplies area by k² and volume by k³."},{"code":"R13","open":false,"front":"Constant of proportionality k","back":"Direct proportion is y = kx; inverse proportion is y = k/x, which is y proportional to 1/x. Substitute one given pair of values to find k, then use the completed equation."},{"code":"R14","open":false,"front":"Gradient of a straight-line graph as a rate of change","back":"The gradient is the change in the vertical quantity for each unit of the horizontal one, with units of y per unit of x — pounds per day, metres per second."},{"code":"R16","open":false,"front":"Multiplier for growth and decay over n periods","back":"Apply the multiplier once per period, so after n periods the amount is P × mⁿ: 3% growth for 5 years is P × 1.03⁵ and 12% decay for 2 years is P × 0.88²."}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Compound interest — Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, where P is the principal, r the rate per period and n the number of periods. PRINTED on the Foundation Exam Aid (W87990A) under 'Compound Interest', so it need not be recalled; the equivalent working, amount = P × multiplierⁿ, earns the same marks.","Speed = distance ÷ time, and its rearrangements distance = speed × time and time = distance ÷ speed. NOT on the Foundation sheet: remember it.","Density = mass ÷ volume, and its rearrangements mass = density × volume and volume = mass ÷ density. NOT on the Foundation sheet: remember it.","Pressure = force ÷ area, and its rearrangements force = pressure × area and area = force ÷ pressure. NOT on the Foundation sheet: remember it.","Simple interest = P × r/100 × n, the same interest each period on the principal only. NOT on the sheet — only the compound formula is printed, so the two must be told apart from the wording of the question.","One part of a share in the ratio a : b = quantity ÷ (a + b), and the shares are a × one part and b × one part. Not a printed formula: remember it.","Percentage multipliers — increase of p%: × (1 + p/100); decrease of p%: × (1 − p/100); original value = new value ÷ multiplier; one quantity as a percentage of another = first ÷ second × 100. NOT on the sheet: remember all four.","Direct proportion y = kx and inverse proportion y = k/x, with k found by substituting one given pair of values. NOT on the sheet: remember both forms.","Similar shapes — lengths a : b give areas a² : b² and volumes a³ : b³; a scale factor k scales area by k² and volume by k³. NOT on the sheet: remember it.","Unit conversions — 1 m = 100 cm, 1 m² = 10 000 cm², 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 hour = 3600 s, 1 m/s = 3.6 km/h. NOT on the sheet: remember them.","Map and drawing scales — a scale of 1 : n gives real length = map length × n and map length = real length ÷ n, both in the same unit. Not a printed formula: remember it.","Gradient of a straight line graph = change in y ÷ change in x, and for a line through the origin that gradient is the rate of change — the amount of y for each 1 unit of x, with its units written as one unit per the other. NOT on the Foundation sheet (nor on the Higher sheet): remember it.","Nothing else in this room is printed. The rest of the Foundation Exam Aid — area of a trapezium, volume of a prism, circumference and area of a circle, a² + b² = c², sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b, and P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B) — belongs to other rooms. The Higher sheet prints the same compound interest formula and adds the quadratic formula, the sine and cosine rules, ½ab sin C and P(A and B) = P(A given B) P(B), none of which are needed here."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T12:01:01.258Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Every answer key, solution line, mark tally, worked example, card and formulae-sheet claim checks out — all 16 bank questions, the 4 exit questions and the 4 lesson check questions re-solved clean, six questions are non-calculator with hand-workable numbers, difficulties split 5/7/4, and the only gaps are a missing gradient entry on the formulae sheet and a sol line that refers to a graph the student is not given."}}},"geometry-properties":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"geometry-properties","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Geometry and measures carries 12–18% of the Foundation tier marks, and this room holds the language the rest of geometry is written in: the angle facts, the properties of the special quadrilaterals, the congruence criteria and the full descriptions of the four transformations that the mensuration, trigonometry and vector rooms all lean on. It is also where the AO2 reasoning marks live — an angle question asks for the fact behind each step, and a mark scheme gives no credit for a correct size with no reason. All three papers draw from it, and Paper 1 is non-calculator, so the working and the reasons must both be written down.","idea":[{"h":"The words, marks and letters a diagram is written in","t":"Geometry questions are written in the board's own language, so the terms must be exact: point, line, vertex, edge, plane, parallel lines (marked with arrows), perpendicular lines (marked with a right-angle square) and equal sides (marked with matching dashes). A polygon is regular when all its sides and all its angles are equal, and questions also describe shapes by their reflection or rotation symmetry. Triangles use the standard convention: capital letters A, B and C name the vertices and the angles there, and the matching lower-case letter names the side opposite, so side a is BC. The three-letter notation ∠ABC always means the angle at the middle letter, B. When a question says draw a diagram from this description, the marks are for the arrows, dashes, right-angle squares and labels, not for a neat sketch.","code":"G1"},{"h":"Compasses, a straight edge, and the regions they fence off","t":"Three constructions are required and all of them are done with compasses and a straight edge only, with every arc left showing — rubbing the arcs out loses the marks. The perpendicular bisector of AB: open the compasses to more than half of AB, draw arcs from A and from B on both sides, join the two crossing points. A perpendicular to a line from or at a point: arcs from the point cutting the line twice, then the perpendicular bisector of the piece between them. The bisector of an angle: an arc from the vertex cutting both arms, then equal arcs from those two points, joined to the vertex. Each construction is also a locus: the perpendicular bisector is the set of points the same distance from A and from B, the angle bisector the set the same distance from two lines, and a circle of radius d the set a fixed distance d from a point. The perpendicular distance from a point to a line is the shortest distance to that line.","code":"G2"},{"h":"The angle facts, and quoting one at every step","t":"Angles at a point add up to 360°; angles at a point on a straight line add up to 180°; vertically opposite angles, where two straight lines cross, are equal. Where a transversal crosses parallel lines, alternate angles (the Z shape) are equal, corresponding angles (the F shape) are equal, and co-interior or allied angles (the C shape) add up to 180°. The angles of a triangle add up to 180°, and splitting an n-sided polygon into n − 2 triangles from one vertex gives an interior angle sum of (n − 2) × 180°. Walking once round any polygon turns through a full turn, so its exterior angles add up to 360°, and in a regular n-sided polygon each exterior angle is 360° ÷ n and each interior angle is 180° − 360° ÷ n. In the exam the size alone is not the whole answer: each step must name the fact it rests on, in words such as alternate angles are equal.","code":"G3"},{"h":"What each special quadrilateral guarantees","t":"Each name carries a fixed package of facts about sides, angles, diagonals and symmetry, and the package is what a question expects you to quote. A square has four equal sides and four right angles, with diagonals that are equal and bisect each other at right angles. A rectangle has four right angles and equal diagonals that bisect each other. A parallelogram has both pairs of opposite sides parallel and equal, opposite angles equal, co-interior angles adding to 180° and diagonals that bisect each other. A rhombus is a parallelogram with four equal sides, so its diagonals bisect each other at right angles and bisect the angles. A trapezium has exactly one pair of parallel sides. A kite has two pairs of equal adjacent sides, one pair of equal opposite angles (the pair between the unequal sides) and one line of symmetry. All four interior angles of any quadrilateral add up to 360°.","code":"G4"},{"h":"Four sets of facts that fix a triangle exactly","t":"Two triangles are congruent when one is an exact copy of the other — same side lengths and same angles, however it has been turned or flipped. Only four sets of information guarantee it: SSS, three pairs of equal sides; SAS, two pairs of equal sides with the equal angle between them in both triangles; ASA, two pairs of equal angles and a pair of equal sides in matching positions; and RHS, a right angle, equal hypotenuses and one other pair of equal sides. Two sides and an angle that is not the included angle is not a criterion, and three equal angles gives similar shapes, not congruent ones. A full answer names the criterion and pairs the vertices in corresponding order, so triangle ABC is congruent to triangle PQR means A matches P, B matches Q and C matches R.","code":"G5"},{"h":"Turning facts into a proof","t":"A proof is a chain of statements in which every link names the property it rests on, ending with the result written out. Because the answer is given in a show that or prove question, no step may be left out and the conclusion must be stated. The standard chains are short: the base angles of an isosceles triangle are equal because the line from the apex to the midpoint of the base makes two triangles congruent by SSS, so the corresponding angles match; the exterior angle of a triangle equals the sum of the two opposite interior angles because both the triangle's angle sum and the angles on the straight line come to 180°. The same toolkit — angle facts, congruence, similarity and the properties of quadrilaterals — is used to conjecture and derive new results, including Pythagoras' theorem. Verifying one case or working back from the answer is not a proof.","code":"G6"},{"h":"The four transformations and the data each one needs","t":"Rotation, reflection and translation keep the shape and size, so object and image are congruent; enlargement keeps the shape but changes the size, so they are similar. Describe fully means giving every piece of data the transformation needs and nothing else: a rotation needs the centre, the angle and the direction (clockwise or anticlockwise); a reflection needs the equation of the mirror line, such as x = 1 or y = −x; a translation needs the column vector, the two numbers written one above the other for the movement right and up; an enlargement needs the centre and the scale factor, which may be a fraction such as 1/2, making the image smaller. Naming two transformations for one mapping scores nothing. On coordinate axes a quick check of the matching side lengths tells you whether the size has changed, and rays drawn through pairs of corresponding vertices meet at the centre of an enlargement.","code":"G7"},{"h":"Naming the parts of a circle correctly","t":"Circle questions are written in named parts, and using the wrong name loses the communication mark even when the arithmetic is right. The centre is the fixed point; the radius joins the centre to the circumference; the diameter is a chord through the centre, twice the radius; the circumference is the whole distance round the edge. A chord joins any two points on the circumference; a tangent is a straight line touching the circle at exactly one point; an arc is a part of the circumference. A sector is the region between two radii and an arc — a slice — while a segment is the region between a chord and an arc. The plural of radius is radii, and a question that says the sector bounded by OA and OB is telling you which two radii to use.","code":"G9"},{"h":"Doing geometry with coordinates","t":"On axes, three tools turn a geometrical question into arithmetic. The midpoint of a line segment is the mean of the x-coordinates and the mean of the y-coordinates. The length of a segment comes from its horizontal and vertical steps: length = √(horizontal step² + vertical step²), and on Paper 1 the answer is left as a surd such as √17. The gradient is the vertical step divided by the horizontal step, and equal gradients mean parallel sides. Together they establish named properties: equal lengths for a rhombus, equal gradients for a parallelogram, and a shared midpoint for diagonals that bisect each other. A missing vertex is found by keeping a property — for example adding the same steps that go from A to B onto D to reach C in a parallelogram.","code":"G11"},{"h":"Counting the surfaces, edges and vertices of a solid","t":"A face is a flat surface, an edge is where two faces meet and a vertex is a corner where edges meet; a cylinder, cone and sphere also have curved surfaces, which are not called faces. A cuboid has 6 rectangular faces, 12 edges and 8 vertices, with opposite faces congruent. A prism has two congruent parallel end faces joined by rectangles, so a triangular prism has 5 faces, 9 edges and 6 vertices. A square-based pyramid has 5 faces, 8 edges and 5 vertices, its apex being the vertex where the four triangles meet. A cylinder has 2 flat circular faces, a curved surface and 2 edges; a cone has 1 flat circular face, a curved surface, 1 edge and an apex; a sphere has a single curved surface and no edges or vertices.","code":"G12"},{"h":"Drawing a solid from three directions","t":"A plan is the view looking straight down on the solid from above; the front elevation is the view looking straight at the front, and the side elevation the view from the side. All three are drawn accurately on squared paper to the same scale, as outlines with any internal edges that can be seen shown as lines — and nothing is drawn in perspective, so a face seen straight on keeps its true shape and size. A cylinder standing upright has a circle for its plan and a rectangle for each elevation; a square-based pyramid has a square plan with its diagonals and a triangle for each elevation. Read the other way, a set of three views identifies the solid, and for a solid built from cubes the views tell you how many cubes tall each column is.","code":"G13"}],"examples":[{"title":"An angle on a pair of parallel lines","setup":"A straight line crosses two parallel lines. At the upper crossing, the angle measured between the crossing line and the parallel line, inside the pair of parallel lines and on the right, is 128°. At the lower crossing, angle x is also inside the pair of parallel lines and on the right. Work out the size of angle x and give the reason for your answer.","steps":["Both angles lie between the parallel lines on the same side of the crossing line, so they are co-interior (allied) angles, and co-interior angles on parallel lines add up to 180°: x + 128 = 180.","Subtracting 128 from each side makes x the subject: x = 180 − 128, which is the line the method mark is awarded for.","180 − 128 = 52, so x = 52°, because co-interior angles on parallel lines add up to 180°."],"cues":["Where do these two angles sit in relation to the parallel lines and the line crossing them?","How is x made the subject of that equation?","What does the subtraction come to, and what must be written beside it?"]},{"title":"The fourth angle of a kite","setup":"ABCD is a kite in which AB = AD and CB = CD. The angle at A, angle BAD, is 70° and the angle at B, angle ABC, is 105°. Work out the size of the angle at C, angle BCD.","steps":["In a kite the pair of angles lying between the unequal sides are equal, so angle ADC = angle ABC = 105°.","The interior angles of any quadrilateral add up to 360°, so 70 + 105 + 105 + angle BCD = 360.","The three known angles come to 70 + 105 + 105 = 280, so angle BCD = 360 − 280, which is the line the method mark is awarded for.","360 − 280 = 80, so angle BCD = 80°."],"cues":["Which two angles of a kite are always equal to each other?","What do the four angles inside a quadrilateral come to?","What do the three angles you now know add up to?","What is left for the angle at C?"]},{"title":"Each interior angle of a regular polygon","setup":"A regular polygon has 12 sides. Work out the size of each of its interior angles, in degrees.","steps":["Walking once round any polygon turns through a full turn, so its exterior angles add up to 360°, and in a regular polygon they are all equal: each exterior angle is 360 ÷ 12.","360 ÷ 12 = 30, so each exterior angle is 30°.","At each vertex the interior angle and the exterior angle lie on a straight line, so the interior angle is 180 − 30, which is the line the method mark is awarded for.","180 − 30 = 150, so each interior angle is 150°."],"cues":["What do the exterior angles of any polygon add up to?","How big is one of those equal exterior angles?","What links the interior and exterior angles at the same corner?","What is the final subtraction?"]},{"title":"Describing a transformation fully","setup":"On a coordinate grid, triangle P has vertices (2, 1), (4, 1) and (2, 4). Triangle Q has vertices (−1, 2), (−1, 4) and (−4, 2). A single transformation maps triangle P onto triangle Q. Describe that transformation fully.","steps":["Matching sides have equal lengths — P has sides of 2 and 3 units and so does Q — so the image is congruent and it cannot be an enlargement.","In P the side of 2 units is horizontal, while in Q it is vertical, so the shape has been turned: try a quarter turn about the origin.","A rotation of 90° anticlockwise about the origin sends (x, y) to (−y, x): (2, 1) → (−1, 2), (4, 1) → (−1, 4) and (2, 4) → (−4, 2), which are exactly the vertices of Q.","A full description gives the centre, the angle and the direction: a rotation of 90° anticlockwise about the origin (0, 0)."],"cues":["Are the two triangles the same size as each other?","Has the shape been slid, flipped over or turned?","Where does the vertex (2, 1) land under that quarter turn?","What three pieces of information does a rotation need?"]},{"title":"Midpoint and length from coordinates","setup":"Point P has coordinates (−2, 3) and point Q has coordinates (6, 9). M is the midpoint of the line segment PQ. Work out the coordinates of M, and the length of PQ.","steps":["The midpoint is the mean of the two x-coordinates and the mean of the two y-coordinates: M = ((−2 + 6) ÷ 2, (3 + 9) ÷ 2).","(−2 + 6) ÷ 2 = 2 and (3 + 9) ÷ 2 = 6, so M is the point (2, 6).","For the length, the horizontal step from P to Q is 6 − (−2) = 8 and the vertical step is 9 − 3 = 6.","The two steps and the segment make a right-angled triangle, so PQ² = 8² + 6² = 64 + 36 = 100, which is the line the method mark is awarded for.","PQ = √100 = 10, so the length of PQ is 10 units."],"cues":["How is the middle of two coordinates found?","What are the two means worth?","How far across and how far up is it from P to Q?","How do those two steps give the length of the segment itself?","What is the square root of that total?"]},{"title":"Proving the base angles of an isosceles triangle are equal","setup":"Triangle ABC has AB = AC. M is the midpoint of BC, and the line AM is drawn. Prove that triangle ABM is congruent to triangle ACM, and hence that angle ABC = angle ACB.","steps":["M is the midpoint of BC, so BM = CM: that is the first pair of corresponding sides.","AM is a side of both triangles, so AM is common to them: that is the second pair.","With AB = AC given as the third pair, all three pairs of corresponding sides are equal, so triangle ABM is congruent to triangle ACM by SSS.","Corresponding angles of congruent triangles are equal, and angle ABM corresponds to angle ACM, so angle ABC = angle ACB: the base angles of an isosceles triangle are equal."],"cues":["What does the word midpoint tell you about BM and CM?","Which side appears in both of the small triangles?","Which of the four criteria do these three pairs match?","What follows for the two angles at B and at C?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What do the angles at a point add up to?","a":"360°"},{"q":"Work out the size of each interior angle of a regular octagon.","a":"135° (each exterior angle is 360 ÷ 8 = 45°, and 180 − 45 = 135)"},{"q":"What three pieces of information fully describe a rotation?","a":"The centre, the angle and the direction (clockwise or anticlockwise)"},{"q":"What is the locus of the points that are the same distance from two points A and B?","a":"The perpendicular bisector of AB"},{"q":"How many faces, edges and vertices does a triangular prism have?","a":"5 faces, 9 edges and 6 vertices"}]},"room":{"facts":["Angles at a point add up to 360°; angles at a point on a straight line add up to 180°; vertically opposite angles are equal.","On parallel lines: alternate angles are equal, corresponding angles are equal, and co-interior (allied) angles add up to 180°.","The angles of a triangle add up to 180°; the interior angles of an n-sided polygon add up to (n − 2) × 180°.","The exterior angles of any polygon add up to 360°, so each exterior angle of a regular n-sided polygon is 360° ÷ n and each interior angle is 180° − 360° ÷ n.","The interior angles of any quadrilateral add up to 360°; a rhombus has four equal sides with diagonals that bisect each other at right angles, and a kite has one pair of equal opposite angles.","The four congruence criteria are SSS, SAS, ASA and RHS; two sides and a non-included angle is not one of them.","A full description gives the centre, angle and direction of a rotation; the mirror line of a reflection; the column vector of a translation; the centre and scale factor of an enlargement.","The perpendicular bisector of AB is the locus of points equidistant from A and B, the bisector of an angle is the locus of points equidistant from its two arms, and the perpendicular distance from a point to a line is the shortest distance to that line.","From coordinates: midpoint = the mean of the x-coordinates and of the y-coordinates; length = √(horizontal step² + vertical step²); gradient = vertical step ÷ horizontal step."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Three angles meet at a point on a straight line. Two of them are 63° and 48°, and the third is angle x. Work out the size of angle x.","a":"69°","sol":"Angles at a point on a straight line add up to 180°, so x = 180 − 63 − 48; taking the two known angles together, 63 + 48 = 111, so x = 180 − 111 (M1 for a complete method). 180 − 111 = 69, so x = 69° (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["All three angles sit on the same straight line, so together they fill the same amount of turn every time.","Add the two angles you are given, then take that total away from the turn along a straight line.","Your first line is the angle sum of a straight line with the two known angles subtracted from it."],"codes":["G3"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out the sum of the interior angles of a hexagon.","a":"720°","sol":"A hexagon has 6 sides, and the interior angles of an n-sided polygon add up to (n − 2) × 180°, so the calculation is (6 − 2) × 180 (M1). 4 × 180 = 720, so the interior angles add up to 720° (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A hexagon can be cut into triangles from one vertex, and you know what each triangle's angles come to.","Use the rule for an n-sided polygon: two fewer than the number of sides, multiplied by the angle sum of a triangle.","Your first line is (number of sides − 2) × 180."],"codes":["G3"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A circle has centre O. Write down the mathematical name of (i) a straight line that touches the circle at exactly one point, and (ii) a part of the circumference between two points on the circle.","a":"(i) a tangent (ii) an arc","sol":"(i) A straight line that meets the circle at exactly one point without crossing it is a tangent (B1). (ii) A piece of the circumference itself is an arc; the straight line joining the same two points would be a chord, and the region trapped between that chord and the arc is a segment (B1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["One of these just brushes the edge of the circle; the other is a piece of the edge itself.","Decide which named part crosses the circle and which only meets it once, then whether the second part asked for is curved or straight.","Your first line is the name for a line meeting the circle once, then the name for a curved piece of the edge."],"codes":["G9"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) ABCD is a rhombus and angle ABC = 116°. Work out the size of angle BCD.","a":"64°","sol":"A rhombus is a parallelogram, so AB is parallel to DC and angles ABC and BCD are co-interior (allied) angles on those parallel lines, which add up to 180°. So angle BCD = 180 − 116 (M1). 180 − 116 = 64, so angle BCD = 64° (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A rhombus is a parallelogram with four equal sides, so its sides come in parallel pairs.","Angles ABC and BCD lie between the same pair of parallel sides, on the same side of BC.","Your first line is the sum of two co-interior angles with the known angle taken away from it."],"codes":["G4"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Triangle ABC is labelled using the standard convention. (i) Write down the lower-case letter used for the side opposite angle A, and say which two vertices that side joins. (ii) Write down the vertex at which the angle ∠ABC is measured.","a":"(i) side a, which joins B and C (ii) at vertex B","sol":"(i) The convention labels each side with the lower-case letter of the angle it faces, so the side opposite angle A is side a, and the side facing A is the one joining the other two vertices, B and C (B1). (ii) In the three-letter notation ∠ABC the middle letter names the vertex, so the angle is at B, between the arms BA and BC (B1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["There is a convention that pairs every side of a triangle with the angle it faces.","For (i), find the side of the triangle that does not touch vertex A and ask how the convention letters it; for (ii), decide which of the three letters in ∠ABC names the vertex where the two arms meet.","Your first line is the lower-case letter matching angle A, followed by the two vertices that side runs between."],"codes":["G1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A straight line crosses two parallel lines. At the first crossing the angle a = 63°. At the second crossing, angle b is the alternate angle to a, and angle c lies next to b at a point on a straight line. Work out the size of angle c. You must give a reason at each step.","a":"117°","sol":"Alternate angles on parallel lines are equal, so b = 63° with that reason quoted (B1). Angles b and c are angles at a point on a straight line, which add up to 180°, so c = 180 − 63 (M1). 180 − 63 = 117, so c = 117° (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The lines being parallel forces one angle at the second crossing to match the angle you are given.","Carry the 63° across to the second crossing as the angle in the alternate, Z-shaped position, then use the two angles sitting on the straight line.","Your first line is the value of b written with its reason; then take b from the angle sum of a straight line."],"codes":["G3"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Each exterior angle of a regular polygon is 24°. Work out the number of sides of the polygon and the size of each interior angle.","a":"15 sides, and each interior angle is 156°","sol":"The exterior angles of any polygon add up to 360°, and in a regular polygon they are all equal, so the number of sides is 360 ÷ 24 (M1). 360 ÷ 24 = 15, so the polygon has 15 sides (A1). At each vertex the interior and exterior angles lie on a straight line, so each interior angle is 180 − 24 = 156° (B1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The exterior angles are what you turn through as you walk once round the shape.","Divide the total of all the exterior angles by the size of one of them, then use the straight line at a single vertex for the second part.","Your first line is the total of the exterior angles divided by the size of one exterior angle."],"codes":["G3"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Triangle A has vertices (1, 1), (3, 1) and (1, 2). Triangle B has vertices (−1, 1), (−3, 1) and (−1, 2). Triangle A is mapped onto triangle B by a reflection. (i) Write down the equation of the mirror line. (ii) Write down the coordinates of the image of the point (5, 4) under that same reflection.","a":"(i) x = 0 (the y-axis) (ii) (−5, 4)","sol":"(i) Pairing the vertices, (1, 1) → (−1, 1), (3, 1) → (−3, 1) and (1, 2) → (−1, 2): every y-coordinate is unchanged and every x-coordinate has changed sign only, so each point and its image are the same distance from the vertical axis and the mirror line is the y-axis, x = 0 (B1). (ii) Reflecting in x = 0 leaves the y-coordinate alone and changes the sign of the x-coordinate, so the image of (5, 4) is (−5, 4) (B1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Compare each vertex with its image: what has changed and what has stayed the same?","The y-coordinates are untouched and the x-coordinates have only changed sign, so find the line every point and its image are equally far from, then move the extra point across it in the same way.","Your first line is the three pairs of object and image coordinates written side by side."],"codes":["G7","G11"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Shape P has vertices (1, 1), (2, 1) and (1, 3). Shape Q has vertices (3, 3), (6, 3) and (3, 9). Sam says that shape P has been enlarged by scale factor 3, centre (1, 1), to give shape Q. Is Sam right? Give the correct scale factor and centre with your answer.","a":"No — the scale factor is 3, but the centre is (0, 0), not (1, 1)","sol":"Matching lengths have been multiplied by the same number: the base of P is 2 − 1 = 1 unit and the base of Q is 6 − 3 = 3 units, while the height of P is 3 − 1 = 2 units and the height of Q is 9 − 3 = 6 units, so the scale factor is 3 and Sam is right about that much (B1). Both coordinates of every vertex have been multiplied by 3, so the rays drawn through pairs of corresponding vertices all meet at the origin: the centre is (0, 0) (B1). A point at the centre of an enlargement does not move, and an enlargement of scale factor 3 about (1, 1) would send (2, 1) to (4, 1) and (1, 3) to (1, 7), which are not vertices of Q, so Sam is wrong about the centre (B1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Check the two halves of Sam's claim separately: the multiplier first, then the centre.","Divide a length on Q by the matching length on P to find the multiplier, then remember that the point at the centre of an enlargement is the one point that does not move.","Your first line is a length of one side of Q divided by the length of the matching side of P."],"codes":["G7","G11"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. In triangle ABC, AB = 7 cm, BC = 5 cm and angle ABC = 48°. In triangle PQR, PQ = 7 cm, QR = 5 cm and angle PQR = 48°. Name the congruence criterion that proves the two triangles are congruent, and say why that information is enough.","a":"SAS, because the equal 48° angle is the one between the two pairs of equal sides.","sol":"Two pairs of sides are equal, AB = PQ = 7 cm and BC = QR = 5 cm, and in both triangles the equal 48° angle is the one between those two sides, so the criterion is SAS (B1). Two sides with the angle between them fix the third side and the remaining two angles, so no other triangle can be drawn from that information; if the 48° angle were not the included angle it would not be a criterion (B1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Check where the equal angle sits inside each triangle.","Two sides and the angle between them fix a triangle exactly, so decide whether the given angle lies between the two given sides.","Your first line writes down the two pairs of equal sides and says where the 48° angle sits in each triangle."],"codes":["G5"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. In triangle ABC, a point P must be less than 4 cm from vertex A and nearer to side AB than to side AC. Write down the two boundaries of the region in which P must lie, and state how each is drawn.","a":"An arc of radius 4 cm drawn with compasses centred on A, and the bisector of angle BAC constructed with compasses and a straight edge","sol":"Less than 4 cm from A is bounded by the circle of radius 4 cm centred on A, drawn as an arc with the compasses opened to 4 cm (B1). Nearer to AB than to AC is bounded by the locus of points equidistant from the two lines, which is the bisector of angle BAC, constructed with compasses and a straight edge — an arc from A cutting both arms, then equal arcs from those two points joined back to A, with all arcs left showing (B1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Each condition on its own describes a set of points with its own boundary.","A fixed distance from a single point gives a curved boundary; being the same distance from two lines gives a straight one.","Your first line is the boundary for the distance condition, drawn with the compasses centred on A."],"codes":["G2"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A solid square-based pyramid stands on a table. Write down the number of edges it has, and the shape of its front elevation.","a":"8 edges; the front elevation is a triangle","sol":"The square base contributes 4 edges, and one sloping edge runs from each of the 4 corners of the base up to the apex, so the pyramid has 4 + 4 = 8 edges (B1). Looking straight at the pyramid from the front, the outline is the base line with two sloping edges rising to the apex above it, so the front elevation is a triangle (B1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Count what the base contributes first, then what joins the base to the top.","Every corner of the base has one sloping edge to the apex; for the elevation, picture the outline you see looking straight at the solid.","Your first line is the number of edges round the base, added to the number of sloping edges."],"codes":["G12","G13"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A quadrilateral ABCD has vertices A(0, 0), B(4, 1), C(5, 5) and D(1, 4). Show that ABCD is a rhombus.","a":"AB = BC = CD = DA = √17, with AB parallel to DC and AD parallel to BC, so ABCD is a rhombus","sol":"From A to B the steps are 4 across and 1 up, so AB = √(4² + 1²) = √17 (M1 for a correct length method). From B to C the steps are 1 and 4, from C to D they are 4 and 1, and from D to A they are 1 and 4, so BC = CD = DA = √17 as well: all four sides are equal (A1). Four equal sides already make ABCD a rhombus, and the gradients confirm it is a parallelogram as well: gradient of AB = 1/4 and gradient of DC = (5 − 4)/(5 − 1) = 1/4, while gradient of AD = 4/1 = 4 and gradient of BC = (5 − 1)/(5 − 4) = 4 (M1). Both pairs of opposite sides are parallel and all four sides are equal, so ABCD is a parallelogram with four equal sides, which is a rhombus (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A rhombus is a parallelogram with four equal sides, so work out the four side lengths and then check the opposite sides for equal gradients.","Find each side length from its horizontal and vertical steps, then check that opposite sides have matching gradients.","Your first line is the horizontal and vertical steps from A to B put into the length calculation."],"codes":["G11","G4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) In triangle ABC the interior angle at A is a and the interior angle at B is b. The side BC is extended beyond C to a point D. Prove that angle ACD = a + b.","a":"a + b + angle ACB = 180° and angle ACB + angle ACD = 180°, so angle ACD = a + b","sol":"The angles of a triangle add up to 180°, so a + b + angle ACB = 180° (M1). B, C and D lie on a straight line, and angles at a point on a straight line add up to 180°, so angle ACB + angle ACD = 180° (M1). Both left-hand sides equal 180°, so a + b + angle ACB = angle ACB + angle ACD; subtracting angle ACB from each side gives angle ACD = a + b, as required (A1 for the complete chain with both reasons quoted).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Two different facts meet at vertex C: one about the triangle, one about the line through B, C and D.","Write the triangle's angle sum as one equation and the angles at C on the straight line as another, then compare the two.","Your first line is the angle sum of triangle ABC written as an equation in a, b and angle ACB."],"codes":["G6","G3"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. ABCD is a parallelogram and the diagonal AC is drawn. Prove that triangle ABC is congruent to triangle CDA.","a":"Congruent by SSS","sol":"Opposite sides of a parallelogram are equal, so AB = CD (B1) and BC = DA (B1). AC is a side of both triangles, so AC = CA is common to them (B1). Three pairs of corresponding sides are equal — AB = CD, BC = DA and AC = CA — so triangle ABC is congruent to triangle CDA by SSS, with the vertices paired in that order (B1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A congruence proof needs three matching pairs, and the diagonal belongs to both triangles.","Use the property that opposite sides of a parallelogram are equal, then look for the side the two triangles share.","Your first line is one pair of equal opposite sides of the parallelogram, written with its reason."],"codes":["G5","G6","G4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A regular pentagon and a regular hexagon are placed flat on a table so that they share one whole edge. Work out the size of the gap between them at one end of the shared edge — that is, the angle at that point which lies outside both shapes.","a":"132°","sol":"Each interior angle of a regular pentagon is (5 − 2) × 180 ÷ 5 (M1), and 540 ÷ 5 = 108° (A1). Each interior angle of a regular hexagon is (6 − 2) × 180 ÷ 6 = 720 ÷ 6 = 120° (B1). The pentagon's angle, the hexagon's angle and the gap meet at a point and so add up to 360°, giving 360 − 108 − 120 = 132° (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Three angles meet at the point where the two shapes touch.","Work out each shape's interior angle at that corner, then use the full turn about the point.","Your first line is the interior angle of a regular pentagon, from either the exterior angle method or (n − 2) × 180 ÷ n."],"codes":["G3","G4"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Work out the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon.","a":"540°"},{"q":"Each exterior angle of a regular polygon is 45°. Work out the number of sides.","a":"8"},{"q":"Two right-angled triangles have equal hypotenuses and one other pair of equal sides. Name the congruence criterion this is.","a":"RHS"},{"q":"A solid cone stands on its circular base. Write down the shape of its plan.","a":"A circle"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Standard labelling of a triangle","back":"Capital letters name the vertices and the angles there; each side takes the lower-case letter of the angle opposite it, so side a is BC. The notation ∠ABC means the angle at the middle letter, B.","code":"G1","open":false},{"front":"Perpendicular bisector of a line segment","back":"Open the compasses to more than half of AB, draw arcs from A and from B, and join the two crossing points. It is the locus of points the same distance from A as from B. Leave the arcs showing.","code":"G2","open":false},{"front":"Bisector of an angle","back":"From the vertex draw an arc cutting both arms; from those two points draw equal arcs; join their crossing point to the vertex. It is the locus of points the same distance from the two arms.","code":"G2","open":false},{"front":"Angles at a point, on a straight line, and vertically opposite","back":"Angles at a point add up to 360°. Angles at a point on a straight line add up to 180°. Vertically opposite angles, where two straight lines cross, are equal.","code":"G3","open":false},{"front":"Alternate, corresponding and co-interior angles","back":"On parallel lines: alternate angles (Z shape) are equal; corresponding angles (F shape) are equal; co-interior or allied angles (C shape) add up to 180°. The lines must be parallel for any of these.","code":"G3","open":false},{"front":"Interior and exterior angles of a polygon","back":"The interior angles of an n-sided polygon add up to (n − 2) × 180°. The exterior angles of any polygon add up to 360°, so each exterior angle of a regular n-sided polygon is 360° ÷ n.","code":"G3","open":false},{"front":"Rhombus","back":"A parallelogram with four equal sides: opposite sides parallel, opposite angles equal, co-interior angles adding to 180°, diagonals bisecting each other at right angles and bisecting the angles. Two lines of symmetry, rotational symmetry of order 2.","code":"G4","open":false},{"front":"Kite","back":"Two pairs of equal adjacent sides. The pair of angles between the unequal sides are equal; one diagonal bisects the other at right angles and bisects the two angles it joins. One line of symmetry.","code":"G4","open":false},{"front":"The four congruence criteria","back":"SSS, three sides; SAS, two sides and the angle between them; ASA, two angles and a side in matching positions; RHS, right angle, hypotenuse and one other side. Two sides and a non-included angle is not a criterion.","code":"G5","open":false},{"front":"Base angles of an isosceles triangle","back":"In a triangle with two equal sides the angles opposite them are equal. The line from the apex to the midpoint of the base makes two triangles congruent by SSS, so those base angles correspond and must be equal.","code":"G6","open":false},{"front":"Describing a transformation fully","back":"Rotation: centre, angle and direction. Reflection: the equation of the mirror line. Translation: the column vector. Enlargement: centre and scale factor. One transformation only — naming two for one mapping scores nothing.","code":"G7","open":false},{"front":"Parts of a circle","back":"Radius: centre to circumference. Diameter: a chord through the centre. Chord: joins two points on the circumference. Tangent: touches at one point. Arc: part of the circumference. Sector: between two radii. Segment: between a chord and an arc.","code":"G9","open":false},{"front":"Length, midpoint and gradient from coordinates","back":"Midpoint: the mean of the x-coordinates and the mean of the y-coordinates. Length: √(horizontal step² + vertical step²). Gradient: vertical step ÷ horizontal step. Equal gradients mean the sides are parallel.","code":"G11","open":false},{"front":"Give one example of a solid and say how many faces, edges and vertices it has","back":"A cuboid has 6 faces, 12 edges and 8 vertices; a triangular prism has 5 faces, 9 edges and 6 vertices; a square-based pyramid has 5 faces, 8 edges and 5 vertices.","code":"G12","open":true},{"front":"Plan and elevations","back":"The plan is the view from directly above; the front elevation is the view from the front and the side elevation the view from the side. All three are drawn to the same scale on squared paper.","code":"G13","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Preamble: this room is set at Foundation standard, so the sheet printed with the paper is the Foundation tier Exam Aid. It prints only the eight results listed next, and of those just one belongs to this room: Pythagoras' theorem, which G6 asks you to derive, is printed for you. Every angle fact, quadrilateral property, congruence criterion, transformation description and coordinate method below is marked Remember and must be written from memory.","Given on the Exam Aid: area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, where a and b are the parallel sides and h their perpendicular separation.","Given on the Exam Aid: volume of a prism = area of cross section × length.","Given on the Exam Aid: circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d the diameter — the names radius and diameter are this room's work, but the two formulae are printed for you.","Given on the Exam Aid: area of a circle = πr².","Given on the Exam Aid: Pythagoras' theorem a² + b² = c², in a right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is c — so the length of a line segment on coordinate axes can be found from a printed result.","Given on the Exam Aid: sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b, in a right-angled triangle ABC whose hypotenuse is c.","Given on the Exam Aid: compound interest, total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ.","Given on the Exam Aid: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B).","Remember: angles at a point add up to 360°; angles at a point on a straight line add up to 180°; vertically opposite angles are equal.","Remember: on parallel lines, alternate angles are equal, corresponding angles are equal, and co-interior (allied) angles add up to 180°.","Remember: the angles of a triangle add up to 180°, and the interior angles of an n-sided polygon add up to (n − 2) × 180°.","Remember: the exterior angles of any polygon add up to 360°, so each exterior angle of a regular n-sided polygon is 360° ÷ n and each interior angle is 180° − 360° ÷ n.","Remember: the interior angles of any quadrilateral add up to 360°.","Remember: the properties of the special quadrilaterals — square, rectangle, parallelogram, trapezium, kite and rhombus — including which diagonals are equal, which bisect each other and which meet at right angles.","Remember: the four congruence criteria SSS, SAS, ASA and RHS, and that two sides with a non-included angle is not one of them.","Remember: a full description of a transformation — rotation (centre, angle, direction), reflection (equation of the mirror line), translation (column vector), enlargement (centre, scale factor).","Remember: the perpendicular bisector of AB is the locus of points equidistant from A and B; the bisector of an angle is the locus of points equidistant from its two arms; the perpendicular distance from a point to a line is the shortest distance to that line.","Remember: from coordinates, midpoint = the mean of the x-coordinates and the mean of the y-coordinates, length = √(horizontal step² + vertical step²) and gradient = vertical step ÷ horizontal step.","Remember: the faces, edges and vertices of the standard solids — cuboid 6, 12, 8; triangular prism 5, 9, 6; square-based pyramid 5, 8, 5 — and that a cylinder, cone and sphere have curved surfaces, which are not counted as faces."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T12:10:36.511303Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.86,"notes":"All 16 bank questions, the 4 exit-ticket items and the 5 check items were re-solved independently in node and every answer key and every line of every sol is correct, so the faults left are presentational: fix the formulae-sheet preamble's Pythagoras claim, the give-away hint on Q5 (index 4) and the half-answer in Q10's (index 9) `a`, and this is publishable."}}},"geometry-mensuration":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"geometry-mensuration","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Geometry and measures is 12–18% of the Foundation tier, and mensuration — areas, perimeters, volumes, circles, sectors, similarity and right-angled trigonometry — is where nearly all of those marks are won. It is also the part of the paper that most often appears inside another question: a cost per square metre needs an area first, a capacity in litres needs a volume in cm³ first, and a reasoning question about whether something fits needs a length from Pythagoras' theorem. The Exam Aid printed with every paper gives you the trapezium, the prism, the circle, Pythagoras' theorem and the three trigonometric ratios, so the marks are lost not on recall but on choosing the right formula, keeping every length in the same unit, and writing the unit on the answer — cm for a length, cm² for an area, cm³ for a volume. On Paper 1 there is no calculator, so answers are left as multiples of π or as surds, and the exact trigonometric values have to be known by heart.","idea":[{"h":"Standard units and keeping them consistent","t":"Length is measured in mm, cm, m and km; area in mm², cm², m² and km²; volume in mm³, cm³ and m³; capacity in ml and litres; mass in g, kg and tonnes; time in seconds, minutes and hours; money in £ and pence. Every quantity in one calculation must be in the same unit before you start, so a cylinder with radius 40 cm and height 1.2 m is worked in centimetres by writing 1.2 m as 120 cm. The links you need are 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 km = 1000 m, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 litre = 1000 ml and 1 ml = 1 cm³, so 1 litre = 1000 cm³. The unit belongs on the answer: an area given as '40' rather than '40 cm²' loses the accuracy mark, and examiners give the unit its own line in the mark scheme.","code":"G14"},{"h":"Measuring, scale drawings and bearings","t":"Lines are measured to the nearest millimetre with a ruler and angles to the nearest degree with a protractor, and answers are accepted inside a small tolerance either side. A scale tells you what one unit on the drawing stands for in real life: a scale of 1 : 25 000 means 1 cm on the map is 25 000 cm on the ground, so you multiply by 25 000 and then convert the answer into sensible units. A bearing is a direction measured clockwise from north and is always written with three figures, so a bearing of 70° is written 070°. North lines are parallel, so the bearing of A from B and the bearing of B from A differ by exactly 180°: add 180 when the first bearing is under 180°, and subtract 180 when it is 180° or more.","code":"G15"},{"h":"Areas of triangles and quadrilaterals, and the volume of a prism","t":"Area of a rectangle = length × width, area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height, and area of a parallelogram = base × perpendicular height; only the area of a trapezium, ½(a + b)h, is printed on the Exam Aid. The height in every one of these is the perpendicular height, not a sloping side, which is the commonest error in the question. A prism is a solid with the same cross section all the way along, and its volume = area of cross section × length — that result is on the Exam Aid too. A cuboid is a prism (volume = length × width × height) and so is a cylinder, whose cross section is a circle, giving volume = πr²h. Areas are in square units and volumes in cubic units.","code":"G16"},{"h":"Circles, composite shapes and curved solids","t":"For a circle of radius r and diameter d, circumference = 2πr = πd and area = πr²; both are on the Exam Aid, but you must know that the radius is half the diameter, because squaring the diameter by mistake is what loses the mark. A composite shape is split into parts whose areas you know — a rectangle plus a semicircle, or a large circle with a smaller one cut out — and the parts are then added or subtracted. Do the same with a composite solid. The sphere, cone and pyramid formulae are not on the Exam Aid: Pearson prints them inside any question that needs them, so read the question for the formula rather than trying to recall it. When the shape is cut out of another, the perimeter is the outside edge only, so an edge that has become internal is not counted.","code":"G17"},{"h":"Arcs and sectors","t":"A sector is the slice of a circle between two radii, and the angle θ at the centre says what fraction of the whole circle it is: that fraction is θ/360. So the arc length is θ/360 × 2πr and the sector area is θ/360 × πr², and neither formula is printed on the Exam Aid — both have to be remembered. Write the fraction down as a separate line, because that is where the first method mark falls. The perimeter of a sector is the arc plus the two radii, so it is θ/360 × 2πr + 2r, not just the arc. The relation can be reversed: if the arc length or the area is given, divide by the circumference or by the area of the whole circle to get θ/360, then multiply by 360 to find the angle.","code":"G18"},{"h":"Congruence and similarity","t":"Two figures are congruent if they are identical in every measurement — same side lengths, same angles — even if one has been turned or reflected. Two figures are similar if one is an enlargement of the other: the angles are equal and every pair of corresponding lengths is in the same ratio. That ratio is the scale factor, found by dividing a length on the new figure by the matching length on the original, and once you have it every other length follows by multiplying (or dividing, going the other way). The hardest part is pairing the sides correctly, so match them by the angles they lie between or by the order of the letters in the two names. A perimeter is a length, so it too is multiplied by the scale factor.","code":"G19"},{"h":"Pythagoras' theorem and the trigonometric ratios","t":"In a right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is c, a² + b² = c². Add the squares to find the hypotenuse; subtract to find a shorter side; then take the square root. The trigonometric ratios are sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse and tan θ = opposite/adjacent — remembered as SOH CAH TOA. Both are printed on the Exam Aid, so the marks are for choosing correctly: label the hypotenuse first (always opposite the right angle), then the side opposite the angle you are using, and whatever is left is the adjacent side. Use Pythagoras' theorem when three sides are involved and no angle, and a trigonometric ratio whenever an angle appears. To find the angle itself, use the inverse: sin⁻¹, cos⁻¹ or tan⁻¹.","code":"G20"},{"h":"The exact trigonometric values","t":"Paper 1 has no calculator, so the values of sine, cosine and tangent for the special angles must be known by heart: sin 0° = 0, sin 30° = 1/2, sin 45° = √2/2, sin 60° = √3/2, sin 90° = 1; cos 0° = 1, cos 30° = √3/2, cos 45° = √2/2, cos 60° = 1/2, cos 90° = 0; tan 0° = 0, tan 30° = √3/3, tan 45° = 1, tan 60° = √3. They come from two triangles: a square of side 1 cut along its diagonal gives 45° with sides 1, 1 and √2, and an equilateral triangle of side 2 cut in half gives 30° and 60° with sides 1, √3 and 2. None of them is on the Exam Aid, and an answer is left in surd form rather than rounded.","code":"G21"}],"examples":[{"title":"Volume of a cylinder as a prism","setup":"A cylindrical tin of beans has radius 4 cm and height 11 cm. Work out its volume, correct to 3 significant figures. A calculator may be used.","steps":["A cylinder is a right prism, so its volume = area of cross section × length, and the cross section here is a circle.","Area of that circle = π × 4² = 16π cm².","Volume = area of cross section × height = π × 4² × 11, the substitution the method mark is awarded for.","π × 16 × 11 = 552.920… cm³, which is 553 cm³ correct to 3 significant figures."],"cues":["Which 2D shape is the cross section of a cylinder?","Which formula gives the area of that cross section?","What is the cross-section area multiplied by?","How many figures should the final answer be given to?"]},{"title":"Perimeter of a composite shape","setup":"A shape is made from a rectangle 12 cm long and 7 cm wide, with a semicircle of diameter 7 cm joined to one of its 7 cm sides. Work out the perimeter of the whole shape, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["The outside edge is three straight sides plus the curved edge; the fourth straight side has become internal where the semicircle joins, so it is not counted.","The three straight sides add to 12 + 7 + 12 = 31 cm.","The curved edge is half a circumference: ½ × π × 7 = 10.995… cm, the line the method mark is awarded for.","Perimeter = 31 + 10.995… = 41.995… cm, which is 42.0 cm correct to 1 decimal place."],"cues":["Which edges of the rectangle are still on the outside of the shape?","What do those straight edges add up to?","How do you find the length of half a circle's edge?","How are the straight part and the curved part combined?"]},{"title":"Arc length of a sector","setup":"A sector of a circle has radius 9 cm and an angle of 40° at the centre. Work out the length of its arc, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["The angle at the centre gives the fraction of the whole circle: 40/360, which cancels to 1/9.","The whole circumference is 2 × π × 9 = 18π cm.","Arc length = 40/360 × 18π = 2π cm, the line the method mark is awarded for.","2π = 6.283… , so the arc is 6.3 cm correct to 1 decimal place."],"cues":["What fraction of a full turn is the angle at the centre?","Which measurement of the whole circle is the arc a piece of?","How do the fraction and the circumference go together?","What does that exact answer come to as a decimal?"]},{"title":"Finding an angle with a trigonometric ratio","setup":"A ladder 6 m long leans against a vertical wall with its foot on horizontal ground, 2.1 m from the base of the wall. Work out the angle the ladder makes with the ground, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Call the angle at the ground θ. The ladder is the hypotenuse and the distance along the ground is the side adjacent to θ.","Adjacent with hypotenuse means cosine: cos θ = 2.1/6, the line the method mark is awarded for.","2.1 ÷ 6 = 0.35, so cos θ = 0.35.","θ = cos⁻¹(0.35) = 69.512… , so the ladder makes an angle of 69.5° with the ground."],"cues":["Which of the three sides is the hypotenuse here?","Which ratio uses the adjacent side and the hypotenuse?","What does that fraction come to as a decimal?","Which calculator key turns a cosine back into its angle?"]},{"title":"Similar triangles in a shadow problem","setup":"A vertical stick 1.5 m tall casts a shadow 2 m long on level ground. At the same moment a tree standing on the same ground casts a shadow 14 m long. Work out the height of the tree.","steps":["Both objects stand upright with the sun at the same angle, so the two right-angled triangles are similar and corresponding sides are in the same ratio.","The shadows are a pair of corresponding sides, so the scale factor from the stick to the tree is 14 ÷ 2 = 7.","The heights correspond in the same way, so the tree's height = 1.5 × 7, the line the method mark is awarded for.","1.5 × 7 = 10.5, so the tree is 10.5 m tall."],"cues":["Why must the two triangles be the same shape?","Which pair of sides has both lengths given?","What does the scale factor do to the stick's height?","What is that multiplication worth, and in what unit?"]},{"title":"An exact length from an exact trigonometric value","setup":"(non-calculator) A right-angled triangle has a hypotenuse of 12 cm and one of its acute angles is 60°. Work out the exact length of the side opposite the 60° angle, leaving your answer in surd form.","steps":["Opposite with hypotenuse means sine, so sin 60° = x/12, where x cm is the side wanted.","Multiplying both sides by 12 gives x = 12 × sin 60°.","The exact value is sin 60° = √3/2, so x = 12 × √3/2, the line the method mark is awarded for.","12 ÷ 2 = 6, so x = 6√3 cm."],"cues":["Which ratio links the opposite side to the hypotenuse?","How is x freed from the fraction?","What is the exact value of that sine, with no calculator?","How does the 2 underneath cancel down?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A prism has a cross section of area 14 cm² and a length of 6 cm. Work out its volume.","a":"84 cm³"},{"q":"A right-angled triangle has shorter sides 7 cm and 24 cm. Work out the length of the hypotenuse.","a":"25 cm"},{"q":"Write down the exact value of tan 45°.","a":"1"},{"q":"A shape is enlarged by scale factor 3. A side that was 5 cm long becomes how long?","a":"15 cm"}]},"room":{"facts":["Area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height; area of a parallelogram = base × perpendicular height; area of a rectangle = length × width. None of these three is printed on the Exam Aid.","Volume of a cuboid = length × width × height and volume of a cylinder = πr²h; both follow from volume of a prism = area of cross section × length, which is printed on the Exam Aid.","Arc length = θ/360 × 2πr and area of a sector = θ/360 × πr², where θ is the angle at the centre. Neither is on the Exam Aid; the perimeter of a sector is the arc plus 2r.","Exact values: sin 30° = 1/2, sin 45° = √2/2, sin 60° = √3/2; cos 30° = √3/2, cos 45° = √2/2, cos 60° = 1/2; tan 30° = √3/3, tan 45° = 1, tan 60° = √3; sin 0° = 0, cos 0° = 1, sin 90° = 1, cos 90° = 0, tan 0° = 0.","A bearing is written with three figures, measured clockwise from north, so 70° is written 070°; the bearing of A from B differs from the bearing of B from A by 180°.","Unit links: 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 km = 1000 m, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 litre = 1000 ml, 1 ml = 1 cm³ and so 1 litre = 1000 cm³.","In similar figures every pair of corresponding lengths is in the same ratio, the scale factor; congruent figures are identical in every length and angle."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) A bottle holds 1.5 litres of juice. How many millilitres of juice does the bottle hold?","a":"1500 ml","sol":"1 litre = 1000 ml, so a number of litres is converted to millilitres by multiplying by 1000: 1.5 × 1000 = 1500 ml (B1, with the unit).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A millilitre is much smaller than a litre, so the number of them must be much bigger.","Use the link between litres and millilitres, then multiply rather than divide.","Your first line is the conversion 1 litre = 1000 ml."],"codes":["G14"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The parallel sides of a trapezium are 6 cm and 10 cm long, and the perpendicular distance between them is 5 cm. Work out the area of the trapezium.","a":"40 cm²","sol":"Area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, which is printed on the Exam Aid, with a = 6, b = 10 and h = 5: ½ × (6 + 10) × 5 (M1 for a correct substitution). 6 + 10 = 16, half of 16 is 8, and 8 × 5 = 40, so the area is 40 cm² (A1; the square unit is part of the answer).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The formula you need is one of the results the paper prints for you.","Add the two parallel sides, halve that total, then multiply by the perpendicular distance.","Your first line is ½(a + b)h with the two parallel sides inside the bracket."],"codes":["G16"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A circle has a radius of 6 cm. Work out the area of the circle, giving your answer as a multiple of π.","a":"36π cm²","sol":"Area of a circle = πr² with r = 6: π × 6² (M1). 6² = 36, so the area is 36π cm² (A1). Leaving the answer as a multiple of π keeps it exact, which is what a non-calculator paper asks for.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["There are two circle formulae — this one squares a length, so the answer is an area.","Substitute the radius into πr² and square the radius before anything else.","Your first line is π × r² with the radius in place of r."],"codes":["G17"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write down the exact value of cos 60°.","a":"1/2","sol":"cos 60° = 1/2 (B1). It comes from halving an equilateral triangle of side 2, which leaves a right-angled triangle with sides 1, √3 and 2, so the side adjacent to the 60° angle is 1 and the hypotenuse is 2.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["This is one of the values you are expected to know by heart; it is not on the formulae sheet.","Cutting an equilateral triangle in half down its line of symmetry leaves a right-angled triangle that still has a 60° angle in it; that half-triangle is where the value comes from.","Your first line is cos 60° = adjacent ÷ hypotenuse, so decide which side of that half-triangle is adjacent to the 60° angle and which is the hypotenuse, then compare their lengths as an exact fraction."],"codes":["G21"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The two shorter sides of a right-angled triangle are 5 cm and 12 cm. Work out the length of the hypotenuse.","a":"13 cm","sol":"Pythagoras' theorem, a² + b² = c², is printed on the Exam Aid. The hypotenuse is the longest side, so c² = 5² + 12² = 25 + 144 = 169 (M1). c = √169 = 13, so the hypotenuse is 13 cm (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The hypotenuse is the side opposite the right angle, and it is the longest side.","Square both of the shorter sides and add them, then undo the squaring.","Your first line is c² = a² + b² with the two short sides in place of a and b."],"codes":["G20"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The cross section of a prism is a triangle with base 6 cm and perpendicular height 5 cm. The prism is 12 cm long. Work out the volume of the prism.","a":"180 cm³","sol":"Volume of a prism = area of cross section × length (printed on the Exam Aid). Area of the triangular cross section = ½ × 6 × 5 = 15 cm² (M1). Volume = 15 × 12 (M1) = 180 cm³ (A1, with the cubic unit).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A prism is the same shape all the way along, so start with the shape of the end face.","Find the area of the triangle first, then use the prism rule.","Your first line is the area of a triangle, ½ × base × perpendicular height."],"codes":["G16"]},{"q":"A sector of a circle has a radius of 15 cm and an angle of 72° at the centre. Work out the area of the sector, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"141.4 cm²","sol":"The sector is 72/360 of the whole circle, and 72/360 = 1/5 (M1 for the fraction). Area of the whole circle = π × 15² = 225π cm², so the sector area = 72/360 × 225π = 45π cm² (M1). 45π = 141.371… , so the area is 141.4 cm² correct to 1 decimal place (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The angle at the centre tells you what share of the circle the sector is.","Work out the area of the whole circle, then take that share of it.","Your first line is the fraction, angle at the centre over 360."],"codes":["G18"]},{"q":"A metal washer is made from a circle of radius 8 cm with a circular hole of radius 3 cm cut out of its centre. Work out the area of the washer, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"172.8 cm²","sol":"Area of the large circle = π × 8² = 64π cm² (M1). Area of the hole = π × 3² = 9π cm², and the washer is what is left: 64π − 9π = 55π cm² (M1 for subtracting the two circle areas). 55π = 172.787… , so the area is 172.8 cm² correct to 1 decimal place (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The washer is one circle with a smaller circle missing from it.","Find both circle areas separately, then deal with the part that has been removed.","Your first line is πr² for the larger circle."],"codes":["G17"]},{"q":"In a right-angled triangle the hypotenuse is 9.4 cm and one of the acute angles is 35°. Work out the length of the side opposite the 35° angle, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"5.4 cm","sol":"The side wanted is opposite the 35° angle and 9.4 cm is the hypotenuse, so the ratio is sine: sin 35° = x/9.4 (M1). Multiplying both sides by 9.4 gives x = 9.4 × sin 35° (M1). = 5.391… , so the side is 5.4 cm correct to 1 decimal place (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Label the sides relative to the 35° angle before choosing anything.","Opposite and hypotenuse together point to one of SOH CAH TOA.","Your first line is sin 35° = opposite ÷ hypotenuse."],"codes":["G20"]},{"q":"A photograph is 15 cm wide and 10 cm high. It is enlarged so that the enlargement is mathematically similar to the original and 24 cm wide. Work out the height of the enlargement. A calculator may be used.","a":"16 cm","sol":"The widths are corresponding lengths, so the scale factor = 24 ÷ 15 = 1.6 (M1). Every length is multiplied by the scale factor, so the height = 10 × 1.6 = 16 cm (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The enlargement is the same shape, so every length has grown in the same ratio.","Use the pair of lengths you know both of to find the scale factor.","Your first line is new width ÷ old width."],"codes":["G19"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The bearing of B from A is 070°. Work out the bearing of A from B.","a":"250°","sol":"North lines at A and at B are parallel, so the two bearings differ by 180°. The given bearing is less than 180°, so add: 070 + 180 (M1). = 250, so the bearing of A from B is 250° (A1). A bearing is always written with three figures.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The north lines at the two points are parallel, so the two directions are linked.","A back bearing differs from the bearing by half a turn — add 180 if the bearing is under 180°, otherwise subtract.","Your first line is the given bearing + 180."],"codes":["G15"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A map has a scale of 1 : 25 000. Two churches are 8 cm apart on the map. Work out the real distance between the churches, in kilometres.","a":"2 km","sol":"A scale of 1 : 25 000 means 1 cm on the map stands for 25 000 cm on the ground, so the real distance = 8 × 25 000 = 200 000 cm (M1). Convert: 200 000 ÷ 100 = 2000 m (M1 for a correct conversion to m or km), and 2000 ÷ 1000 = 2, so the distance is 2 km (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The scale says how many real centimetres one map centimetre stands for.","Multiply by the scale first and worry about the units afterwards.","Your first line is map distance × 25 000, giving an answer in centimetres."],"codes":["G15","G14"]},{"q":"A cylindrical water butt has a radius of 40 cm and a height of 1.2 m. Work out how many litres of water it holds when it is full, correct to the nearest litre. (1 litre = 1000 cm³.) A calculator may be used.","a":"603 litres","sol":"Work in one unit: 1.2 m = 120 cm (B1). A cylinder is a right prism, so volume = πr²h = π × 40² × 120 (M1) = 603 185.8 cm³ (A1). 1 litre = 1000 cm³, so 603 185.8 ÷ 1000 = 603.18… litres, which is 603 litres to the nearest litre (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The two measurements are not in the same unit yet, and a volume in cm³ is not a capacity in litres.","Convert the height, find the volume as area of cross section × length, then convert the volume.","Your first line is the height written in centimetres."],"codes":["G16","G17","G14"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A solid sphere of radius 3 cm is melted down and recast as a solid cone of base radius 3 cm and height h cm, with no metal lost. Work out the value of h. [Volume of a sphere = 4/3 πr³. Volume of a cone = 1/3 πr²h.]","a":"h = 12","sol":"Volume of the sphere = 4/3 × π × 3³ = 4/3 × π × 27 (M1) = 36π cm³ (A1). Volume of the cone = 1/3 × π × 3² × h = 3πh cm³ (M1). No metal is lost, so the volumes are equal: 3πh = 36π. Dividing both sides by 3π gives h = 12 (A1), so the cone is 12 cm tall.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Melting and recasting changes the shape but not the amount of metal.","Write each volume in terms of π using the formulae given, then set them equal.","Your first line is the sphere volume formula with the radius substituted."],"codes":["G17"]},{"q":"A ship sails 8 km due north from A to B, then 15 km due east from B to C. Work out the bearing of C from A, correct to the nearest degree. A calculator may be used.","a":"062°","sol":"North and east are perpendicular, so ABC is a right-angled triangle with the right angle at B; measured from the north line at A, the side opposite the bearing angle is the 15 km and the adjacent side is the 8 km (M1). Opposite with adjacent means tangent: tan θ = 15/8 (M1). θ = tan⁻¹(1.875) = 61.927… (A1). The bearing is measured clockwise from north at A and written with three figures, so the bearing of C from A is 062° (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Sketch the journey — the two legs meet at a right angle, and the bearing is the angle at A from north.","In that right-angled triangle you know the two shorter sides, so pick the ratio that uses both of them.","Your first line is tan θ = opposite ÷ adjacent, with θ the angle at A measured from north."],"codes":["G15","G20"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Triangle ABC is right-angled at B, with AB = 9 cm and BC = 12 cm. Triangle XYZ is mathematically similar to triangle ABC, and XY corresponds to AB. XY = 21 cm. Work out the perimeter of triangle XYZ.","a":"84 cm","sol":"First find AC by Pythagoras' theorem: AC² = 9² + 12² = 81 + 144 = 225 (M1), so AC = √225 = 15 cm (A1). Perimeter of ABC = 9 + 12 + 15 = 36 cm. The scale factor from ABC to XYZ is XY ÷ AB = 21 ÷ 9 = 7/3, and a perimeter is a length, so perimeter of XYZ = 36 × 7/3 (M1) = 84 cm (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["You cannot find the perimeter of either triangle until you know all three sides of the first one.","Use Pythagoras' theorem on ABC, add its three sides, then enlarge the total.","Your first line is AC² = AB² + BC² with the two given lengths substituted."],"codes":["G19","G20"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Work out the area of a triangle with base 8 cm and perpendicular height 5 cm.","a":"20 cm²"},{"q":"A circle has a diameter of 10 cm. Write down its circumference as a multiple of π.","a":"10π cm"},{"q":"A sector of a circle has radius 8 cm and an angle of 90° at the centre. Work out its area as a multiple of π.","a":"16π cm²"},{"q":"The bearing of Q from P is 115°. Work out the bearing of P from Q.","a":"295°"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Metric unit conversions","back":"1 cm = 10 mm, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 km = 1000 m, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 litre = 1000 ml and 1 ml = 1 cm³, so 1 litre = 1000 cm³.","code":"G14","open":false},{"front":"Three-figure bearing","back":"A direction measured clockwise from north, always written with three figures, so 70° is written 070°. The bearing of A from B and of B from A differ by 180°.","code":"G15","open":false},{"front":"Scale of a map or scale drawing","back":"A scale of 1 : n means one unit on the drawing stands for n real units. Multiply a drawing length by n for the real length; divide to go the other way.","code":"G15","open":false},{"front":"Area of a triangle","back":"Area = ½ × base × perpendicular height. The height must be perpendicular to the base, not a sloping side. Not printed on the Exam Aid, so it must be remembered.","code":"G16","open":false},{"front":"Area of a parallelogram and of a trapezium","back":"Parallelogram: area = base × perpendicular height, and it is not on the Exam Aid. Trapezium: area = ½(a + b)h with a and b the parallel sides and h their perpendicular separation, and it is on the Exam Aid.","code":"G16","open":false},{"front":"Volume of a prism","back":"Volume = area of cross section × length, for any solid with the same cross section all the way along. Printed on the Exam Aid. A cuboid is a prism: volume = length × width × height.","code":"G16","open":false},{"front":"Volume of a cylinder","back":"A cylinder is a prism with a circular cross section, so volume = πr²h. Not printed on the Exam Aid — it comes from combining the prism rule with the area of a circle.","code":"G16","open":false},{"front":"Circumference of a circle","back":"Circumference = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d the diameter. Printed on the Exam Aid. The radius is half the diameter, so check which one the question gives.","code":"G17","open":false},{"front":"Area of a circle","back":"Area = πr², where r is the radius. Printed on the Exam Aid. Square the radius, never the diameter, and give the answer in square units or as a multiple of π.","code":"G17","open":false},{"front":"The sphere and cone formulae","back":"Volume of a sphere = 4/3 πr³, surface area of a sphere = 4πr², volume of a cone = 1/3 πr²h, curved surface area of a cone = πrl. Not on the Exam Aid; printed inside the question.","code":"G17","open":false},{"front":"Arc length of a sector","back":"Arc length = θ/360 × 2πr, where θ is the angle at the centre. The perimeter of the sector is the arc plus the two radii: θ/360 × 2πr + 2r. Not on the Exam Aid.","code":"G18","open":false},{"front":"Area of a sector","back":"Area of a sector = θ/360 × πr², where θ is the angle at the centre. Reverse it to find θ: divide the sector area by πr², then multiply by 360. Not on the Exam Aid.","code":"G18","open":false},{"front":"Congruent and similar figures","back":"Congruent figures are identical in every length and angle, however they are turned or reflected. Similar figures have equal angles and all corresponding lengths in the same ratio, the scale factor = new length ÷ original length.","code":"G19","open":false},{"front":"Pythagoras' theorem and the trigonometric ratios","back":"In a right-angled triangle with hypotenuse c: a² + b² = c². For an angle θ: sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse, tan θ = opposite/adjacent. All four are on the Exam Aid.","code":"G20","open":false},{"front":"The exact trigonometric values","back":"sin: 0, 1/2, √2/2, √3/2, 1 for 0°, 30°, 45°, 60°, 90°. cos: 1, √3/2, √2/2, 1/2, 0 for the same angles. tan: 0, √3/3, 1, √3 for 0°, 30°, 45°, 60°. Not on the Exam Aid.","code":"G21","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["The Exam Aid formulae sheet is printed as an insert in every Foundation tier 1MA1 paper. Six of the results it prints belong to this room and are listed first; everything marked Remember is not on the sheet and must be written from memory. (The sheet also prints compound interest and P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B), which belong to other rooms.)","Given on the Exam Aid: area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, where a and b are the parallel sides and h their perpendicular separation.","Given on the Exam Aid: volume of a prism = area of cross section × length.","Given on the Exam Aid: circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d the diameter.","Given on the Exam Aid: area of a circle = πr².","Given on the Exam Aid: Pythagoras' theorem a² + b² = c², in a right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is c.","Given on the Exam Aid: sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b, in a right-angled triangle ABC whose hypotenuse is c — that is, sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse and tan θ = opposite/adjacent.","Printed inside the question, not on the Exam Aid: volume of a sphere = 4/3 πr³ and surface area of a sphere = 4πr².","Printed inside the question, not on the Exam Aid: volume of a cone = 1/3 πr²h and curved surface area of a cone = πrl.","Remember: area of a rectangle = length × width.","Remember: area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height.","Remember: area of a parallelogram = base × perpendicular height.","Remember: volume of a cuboid = length × width × height.","Remember: volume of a cylinder = πr²h.","Remember: arc length of a sector = θ/360 × 2πr, where θ is the angle at the centre.","Remember: area of a sector = θ/360 × πr², where θ is the angle at the centre.","Remember: perimeter of a sector = θ/360 × 2πr + 2r — the arc plus the two radii.","Remember: in similar figures, scale factor = corresponding length on the new figure ÷ corresponding length on the original.","Remember: sin 0° = 0, sin 30° = 1/2, sin 45° = √2/2, sin 60° = √3/2, sin 90° = 1.","Remember: cos 0° = 1, cos 30° = √3/2, cos 45° = √2/2, cos 60° = 1/2, cos 90° = 0.","Remember: tan 0° = 0, tan 30° = √3/3, tan 45° = 1, tan 60° = √3.","Remember: a bearing is measured clockwise from north and written with three figures; the bearing of A from B differs from the bearing of B from A by 180°.","Remember: 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 km = 1000 m, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 litre = 1000 ml and 1 ml = 1 cm³, so 1 litre = 1000 cm³."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T11:57:26Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Answer keys, solutions, formulae-sheet claims and Foundation-tier scope are all sound — every one of the 16 bank questions, 4 exit tickets and 4 lesson checks re-solved to exactly the stated answer, form and unit, and the six \"Given on the Exam Aid\" results match the 2026 Foundation Exam Aid (W87990A) exactly, with nothing Higher-only anywhere in the room; only the shadow worked example's missing method-mark line and one redundant example step need a teacher's pen."}}},"geometry-vectors":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"geometry-vectors","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Vectors are the one part of Geometry and measures where the answer is a pair of numbers rather than a single value, and both statements in this room are assessed at Foundation tier as well as Higher. A transformation question that asks you to describe a mapping fully is only complete when the word translation is written alongside the column vector, so students who give one without the other throw away half of an easy tariff. The same column vectors turn up inside coordinate questions, inside questions about a shape on a grid, and inside problems where two moves have to be combined into one. The arithmetic itself is directed-number arithmetic, which is where nearly all of these marks are lost: a sign the wrong way round turns a correct method into a wrong answer. Paper 1 is a non-calculator paper, so every addition, subtraction and scalar multiplication in this room has to be done and shown by hand.","idea":[{"h":"Translations written as column vectors","t":"A translation slides every point of a shape the same distance in the same direction; the shape keeps its size, its shape and the way round it is, and only its position changes. One column vector fixes the translation completely: the top number is the movement across the page, positive for right and negative for left, and the bottom number is the movement up the page, positive for up and negative for down. In these notes a column vector is typed on one line as (across, up), so (−5, 2) means 5 squares left and 2 squares up. To find the vector that maps an object onto its image, pick a vertex and the matching vertex of the image and subtract: across = x of the image − x of the object, up = y of the image − y of the object. Checking on a second pair of matching vertices catches a sign error. Describe fully means name the transformation and give its vector; naming two transformations for one mapping scores nothing.","code":"G24"},{"h":"Adding, subtracting and scaling vectors","t":"Column vectors are combined one row at a time. To add, add the two across-components and, separately, the two up-components: (a, b) + (c, d) = (a + c, b + d). To subtract, subtract the matching components, remembering that taking away a negative adds: 5 − (−3) = 8. To multiply by a scalar k, multiply both components: k(a, b) = (ka, kb). On a diagram a vector is a directed line segment — an arrow that goes across by the top number and up by the bottom number, with the arrowhead giving the direction of travel. Drawn this way, a + b is the journey along a followed by the journey along b, ka is an arrow k times as long in the same direction, and −a is the same arrow reversed. The method mark is for the line that shows the components being combined, so write that line out even when the answer is short.","code":"G25"}],"examples":[{"title":"Describing the translation that maps one triangle onto another","setup":"On a coordinate grid, triangle A has vertices (1, 2), (3, 2) and (1, 5). Triangle B has vertices (−2, 4), (0, 4) and (−2, 7). Triangle A is mapped onto triangle B by a single transformation. Describe that transformation fully.","steps":["The two triangles are the same size, the same shape and the same way round, so every point has slid by the same amount.","Vertex (1, 2) of A and vertex (−2, 4) of B are matching corners, because each is the bottom left corner of its triangle.","Across: x of the image − x of the object = −2 − 1 = −3, so the slide is 3 squares to the left.","Up: y of the image − y of the object = 4 − 2 = 2, so the slide is 2 squares up.","Testing the same movement on another vertex: (1, 5) moves to (1 − 3, 5 + 2) = (−2, 7), which is a vertex of B.","So the transformation is a translation by the column vector (−3, 2), with −3 on top and 2 beneath."],"cues":["What has changed about the triangle, and what has not?","Which corner of B matches the corner (1, 2) of A?","Is the shape moving to the left or to the right?","Is the shape moving up the grid or down it?","How can the movement be tested on a second corner?","What two things must a full description give here?"]},{"title":"Carrying out a translation given by a column vector","setup":"Triangle T has vertices (2, 3), (5, 3) and (2, 7). Triangle T is translated by the column vector (3, −2), which has 3 on top and −2 beneath. Work out the coordinates of the three vertices of the image of T.","steps":["The top number adds 3 to every x-coordinate; the bottom number adds −2 to every y-coordinate, which takes 2 away.","Vertex (2, 3) lands on (2 + 3, 3 − 2) = (5, 1).","Vertex (5, 3) lands on (5 + 3, 3 − 2) = (8, 1).","Vertex (2, 7) lands on (2 + 3, 7 − 2) = (5, 5).","So the image has vertices (5, 1), (8, 1) and (5, 5), and it is the same size and shape as T."],"cues":["What does each number in the vector change?","Where does the corner (2, 3) land?","Where does the corner (5, 3) land?","Where does the corner (2, 7) land?","Has the triangle changed size at all?"]},{"title":"Adding and subtracting two column vectors","setup":"a is the column vector (5, −2) and b is the column vector (−3, 6), each written with the across-component on top and the up-component beneath. Work out a + b and a − b, giving each answer as a column vector.","steps":["Adding works one row at a time: the across-components give 5 + (−3), and the up-components give −2 + 6.","5 + (−3) = 2 and −2 + 6 = 4, so a + b = (2, 4).","For a − b the across-components give 5 − (−3), and taking away a negative adds: 5 + 3 = 8.","The up-components give −2 − 6, and taking 6 away from −2 gives −8.","So a − b = (8, −8)."],"cues":["Which component pairs with which?","What does each pair of components come to?","What does taking away a negative do?","What happens to the up-components here?","How is the second answer written down?"]},{"title":"Multiplying by a scalar and then subtracting","setup":"a is the column vector (4, −1) and b is the column vector (2, −5). Work out 3a − b, giving the answer as a single column vector.","steps":["The scalar 3 multiplies both components of a: 3 × 4 across and 3 × (−1) up.","That gives 3a = (12, −3).","Now take b away row by row: 12 − 2 across, and −3 − (−5) up.","12 − 2 = 10 and −3 + 5 = 2, so 3a − b = (10, 2)."],"cues":["What does the 3 in front of a do?","What is 3a as a column vector?","Which numbers are taken from which?","What does taking away −5 become?"]},{"title":"Two translations combined into one","setup":"A counter on a grid is translated by the column vector (3, −4) and is then translated again by the column vector (−5, 1). Work out the single column vector that would move the counter straight from its starting square to its finishing square.","steps":["One slide followed by another moves the counter across by both top numbers and up by both bottom numbers, so the single vector is the sum.","Across: 3 + (−5) = −2, so the counter finishes 2 squares to the left of where it started.","Up: −4 + 1 = −3, so it finishes 3 squares below where it started.","So one translation by the column vector (−2, −3) does the job of both."],"cues":["Why can the two vectors be combined at all?","What is the total movement across the page?","What is the total movement up or down?","How is that written as one vector?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write the translation '2 squares right and 5 squares down' as a column vector.","a":"(2, −5), with 2 on top and −5 beneath."},{"q":"p = (−4, 3) and q = (6, −1). Work out p + q.","a":"(2, 2)"},{"q":"r = (−2, 5). Work out 3r.","a":"(−6, 15)"},{"q":"A shape is translated by the column vector (7, −2). Which single column vector translates it back to where it started?","a":"(−7, 2)"}]},"room":{"facts":["A column vector has the movement across the page on top and the movement up the page beneath; right and up are positive, left and down are negative. Written on one line here as (across, up).","To describe a translation fully, write the word translation and the column vector. Either one on its own is not a complete description.","The vector that maps an object onto its image is (x of the image − x of the object, y of the image − y of the object); check it on a second pair of matching vertices.","A point (x, y) translated by the column vector (p, q) lands on (x + p, y + q).","Vectors are added and subtracted component by component: (a, b) + (c, d) = (a + c, b + d) and (a, b) − (c, d) = (a − c, b − d).","A scalar multiplies both components: k(a, b) = (ka, kb). The arrow is k times as long, in the same direction unless k is negative, which reverses it.","Two translations one after the other are equivalent to the single translation given by the sum of the two vectors; if that single translation is (p, q), the translation back is (−p, −q), and (p, q) + (−p, −q) = (0, 0).","A translation changes only position: the image is congruent to the object, the same size, the same shape and the same way round, so lengths, angles and area are unchanged."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Write the translation '7 squares left and 3 squares down' as a column vector.","a":"(−7, −3)","sol":"The top number records the movement across the page and left is the negative direction, so the top number is −7. The bottom number records the movement up the page and down is the negative direction, so the bottom number is −3. The column vector is (−7, −3), with −7 on top (B1).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Only one of the two numbers records movement across the page.","Put the across movement on top and the up movement beneath, then decide the sign of each.","Your first line is an empty column vector with the across movement written in the top place."],"codes":["G24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) a = (3, 5) and b = (−4, 2). Work out a + b as a column vector.","a":"(−1, 7)","sol":"Add the across-components: 3 + (−4) = −1. Add the up-components: 5 + 2 = 7. So a + b = (−1, 7), with −1 on top (B1).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The two vectors are combined one row at a time.","Add the two top numbers together, then add the two bottom numbers together.","Your first line is the sum of the two across-components written out as an addition."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"p = (6, −2). Work out 3p as a column vector. A calculator may be used.","a":"(18, −6)","sol":"A scalar multiplies every component of the vector: 3 × 6 = 18 across and 3 × (−2) = −6 up. So 3p = (18, −6) (B1).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The 3 in front of p acts on the whole vector, not just the top number.","Multiply the across-component by 3, then multiply the up-component by 3.","Your first line is 3 multiplied by the across-component of p, written out."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) a = (8, 3) and b = (5, −1). Work out a − b as a column vector.","a":"(3, 4)","sol":"Subtract one row at a time. Across: 8 − 5 = 3. Up: 3 − (−1) = 3 + 1 = 4, because taking away a negative adds. So a − b = (3, 4) (B1).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Subtraction of vectors works the same way round as addition does.","Take the top number of b from the top number of a, then do the same with the bottom numbers.","Your first line is the top number of b taken from the top number of a, written out."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"The point A has coordinates (−2, 6). A is translated by the column vector (5, −4). Work out the coordinates of the image of A. A calculator may be used.","a":"(3, 2)","sol":"Add the top number to the x-coordinate and the bottom number to the y-coordinate: (−2 + 5, 6 + (−4)) (M1). This gives the image (3, 2) (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The vector tells the point how far to move across and how far to move up.","Add the across-component to the x-coordinate and the up-component to the y-coordinate.","Your first line is the x-coordinate of A with the across-component added to it."],"codes":["G24"]},{"q":"Rectangle A has vertices (1, 1), (4, 1), (4, 3) and (1, 3). Rectangle B has vertices (−3, 4), (0, 4), (0, 6) and (−3, 6). Describe fully the single transformation that maps rectangle A onto rectangle B. A calculator may be used.","a":"A translation by the column vector (−4, 3)","sol":"The two rectangles are the same size, the same shape and the same way round, so the transformation is a translation (B1). Matching the vertex (1, 1) with the vertex (−3, 4): across = −3 − 1 = −4 and up = 4 − 1 = 3; the same movement takes (4, 1) to (0, 4), (4, 3) to (0, 6) and (1, 3) to (−3, 6), so the column vector is (−4, 3) with −4 on top (B1). The word translation on its own, or the vector on its own, is not a full description, and naming two transformations scores nothing.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Compare the size, the shape and the way round the two rectangles are before naming anything.","Pair up matching vertices and work out how far the shape has moved across and up.","Your first line pairs the vertex (1, 1) of A with the matching vertex of B."],"codes":["G24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) a = (−2, 5) and b = (4, −3). Work out 2a + b as a column vector.","a":"(0, 7)","sol":"First scale a: 2a = (2 × (−2), 2 × 5) = (−4, 10) (M1). Then add b one row at a time: across −4 + 4 = 0, up 10 + (−3) = 7. So 2a + b = (0, 7) (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Deal with the scalar before anything is added.","Work out 2a as a column vector first, then add b component by component.","Your first line is the scalar multiplying each component of a, written out."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) a = (7, −4) and b = (−2, 6). Work out 3a − 2b as a column vector.","a":"(25, −24)","sol":"3a = (3 × 7, 3 × (−4)) = (21, −12) (M1). 2b = (2 × (−2), 2 × 6) = (−4, 12) (M1). Subtracting row by row: across 21 − (−4) = 21 + 4 = 25, up −12 − 12 = −24. So 3a − 2b = (25, −24) (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Both vectors have to be scaled before they can be combined.","Work out 3a and 2b separately as column vectors, then subtract the second from the first.","Your first line is each component of a multiplied by the scalar in front of it."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"Shape P is translated by the column vector (5, −3) onto shape Q. Shape Q is then translated by the column vector (−2, 6) onto shape R. Work out the column vector of the single translation that maps shape R back onto shape P. A calculator may be used.","a":"(−3, −3)","sol":"One translation followed by another moves the shape across by both top numbers and up by both bottom numbers, so P is mapped onto R by the sum (5 + (−2), −3 + 6) = (3, 3) (M1). The journey back is the negative of that vector, with the sign of each component reversed, so R is mapped onto P by (−3, −3) (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The shape only ends up in one place, however many slides took it there.","Combine the two movements into one vector, then think about what undoes it.","Your first line adds the two across-components of the given vectors."],"codes":["G24"]},{"q":"The point M has coordinates (−1, 4) and the point N has coordinates (5, 1). The translation given by the vector v maps M onto N. Write v as a column vector. A calculator may be used.","a":"(6, −3)","sol":"The vector is the image take away the object in each direction. Across: 5 − (−1) = 5 + 1 = 6. Up: 1 − 4 = −3 (M1). So v = (6, −3), with 6 on top (A1). A check: (−1 + 6, 4 + (−3)) = (5, 1), which is N.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Think about how far the point has to travel across the grid and how far up or down.","Take the coordinates of M from the coordinates of N, one direction at a time.","Your first line takes the x-coordinate of M from the x-coordinate of N."],"codes":["G24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) a = (2, −1) and b = (−3, 4). The vector c is such that a + b + c = (0, 0). Work out c as a column vector.","a":"(1, −3)","sol":"First combine the two known vectors: a + b = (2 + (−3), −1 + 4) = (−1, 3) (M1). For the total to be the zero vector, c must undo this movement, so c is a + b with the sign of each component reversed (M1). That gives c = (1, −3) (A1). A check: (−1 + 1, 3 + (−3)) = (0, 0).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["A total of (0, 0) means the journey finishes exactly where it started.","Add a and b first, then decide what must be added to that result to get back to the start.","Your first line adds the across-components of a and b."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"Triangle P is drawn on a centimetre grid with vertices (1, 2), (6, 2) and (1, 6). P is translated by the column vector (2, −5) onto triangle Q. Work out the area of triangle Q. A calculator may be used.","a":"10 cm²","sol":"A translation changes only the position of a shape, so triangle Q is congruent to triangle P and has the same area (B1). In P the base runs from (1, 2) to (6, 2), a length of 5 cm, and the perpendicular height runs from (1, 2) to (1, 6), a length of 4 cm, so the area is ½ × 5 × 4 (M1). This gives an area of 10 cm² (A1). Working the other way round, Q has vertices (3, −3), (8, −3) and (3, 1), with the same base of 5 cm and height of 4 cm.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what a translation does to the size of a shape before doing any arithmetic.","Find the base and the perpendicular height of the triangle from the coordinates, then use the area formula for a triangle.","Your first line reads the base and the perpendicular height off the coordinates of the two sides that meet at a right angle."],"codes":["G24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) a = (3, −2) and b = (k, n), where k and n are numbers. Given that a + b = (−5, 4), work out the value of k + n.","a":"−2","sol":"The across-components must agree, which gives the equation 3 + k = −5, so k = −5 − 3 = −8 (M1). The up-components must agree, which gives −2 + n = 4, so n = 4 + 2 = 6 (M1). Therefore k + n = −8 + 6 = −2 (A1).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Two column vectors are equal only when both rows match.","Write an equation from the top row and another from the bottom row, then solve each one.","Your first line sets the across-component of a + b equal to the across-component of the given answer."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"A robot starts at the point (−3, 2) on a coordinate grid. It moves by the column vector (5, −1), then by the column vector (−4, −3), and finally by the column vector v. The robot finishes at the point (1, 0). Work out v as a column vector. A calculator may be used.","a":"(3, 2)","sol":"After the first move the robot is at (−3 + 5, 2 + (−1)) = (2, 1) (M1). After the second move it is at (2 + (−4), 1 + (−3)) = (−2, −2) (M1). The last move must take it from (−2, −2) to (1, 0), so v = (1 − (−2), 0 − (−2)) (M1), giving v = (3, 2) (A1). Adding the three vectors to the starting point is an equally valid route and scores the same.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Track where the robot actually is after each move before worrying about the last one.","Translate the starting point by the first vector, then by the second, then compare that position with the finish.","Your first line adds the components of the first vector to the coordinates of the starting point."],"codes":["G24","G25"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write the translation '4 squares right and 6 squares down' as a column vector.","a":"(4, −6)"},{"q":"a = (2, 7) and b = (−5, −2). Work out a + b as a column vector.","a":"(−3, 5)"},{"q":"p = (−1, 3). Work out 4p as a column vector.","a":"(−4, 12)"},{"q":"The point (5, −2) is translated by the column vector (−3, 4). Write down the coordinates of the image.","a":"(2, 2)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Vector","back":"A quantity with a size and a direction. On a grid it is drawn as an arrow; in writing it is given as a column vector. Where the arrow is drawn does not matter, only its size and direction.","code":"G25","open":false},{"front":"Column vector","back":"Two numbers written one above the other: the top number is the movement across the page, the bottom number the movement up the page. Typed on one line here as (across, up).","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"Translation","back":"A transformation that slides every point of a shape the same distance in the same direction. Size, shape and the way round the shape is all stay the same; only its position changes.","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"Describing a translation fully","back":"Write the word translation and the column vector that gives it. The word on its own, or the vector on its own, is not a full description, and naming two transformations scores nothing.","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"Signs in a column vector","back":"Top number: positive for right, negative for left. Bottom number: positive for up, negative for down. So (−5, −2) means 5 squares left and 2 squares down.","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"Vector that maps an object onto its image","back":"Subtract in each direction: across = x of the image − x of the object; up = y of the image − y of the object. Use matching vertices, and test the vector on a second pair.","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"Translating a point by a column vector","back":"The point (x, y) translated by (p, q) lands on (x + p, y + q): add the top number to the x-coordinate and the bottom number to the y-coordinate.","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"Adding two column vectors","back":"Add the matching components: (a, b) + (c, d) = (a + c, b + d). On a diagram this is the journey along the first arrow followed by the journey along the second.","code":"G25","open":false},{"front":"Subtracting two column vectors","back":"Subtract the matching components: (a, b) − (c, d) = (a − c, b − d). Taking away a negative component adds, so 5 − (−3) = 8.","code":"G25","open":false},{"front":"Multiplying a vector by a scalar","back":"Multiply both components by the scalar: k(a, b) = (ka, kb). The arrow becomes k times as long and keeps its direction, unless k is negative, which reverses it.","code":"G25","open":false},{"front":"Directed line segment","back":"A vector drawn as an arrow on a grid: it goes across by the top number and up by the bottom number, and the arrowhead shows the direction of travel.","code":"G25","open":false},{"front":"The negative of a vector, −a","back":"The same length and line as a but pointing the opposite way: every component changes sign. It undoes a, so a + (−a) = (0, 0), the zero vector.","code":"G25","open":false},{"front":"Two translations one after the other","back":"The same as one translation by the sum of the two column vectors, and the order in which the two are carried out makes no difference to where the shape ends up.","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"Name one way to check a column vector written for a translation","back":"Apply it to a second pair of matching vertices and see that the image vertex is reached; count the squares across and up between one vertex and its image; translate an image vertex by the negative vector and land back on the object.","code":"G24","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Preamble: the Exam Aid formulae sheet printed as an insert with every Foundation tier 1MA1 paper prints eight results, and not one of them concerns vectors. (The Higher tier Exam Aid adds the quadratic formula, the sine and cosine rules, the area of a triangle as ½ab sin C and P(A and B) = P(A given B) P(B); it prints nothing about vectors either.) So every result this room uses, listed next, must be written from memory.","Remember: a column vector is written (across, up) with the across-component on top; right and up are positive, left and down are negative.","Remember: a translation is described fully by the word translation together with its column vector.","Remember: the vector mapping an object onto its image is (x of the image − x of the object, y of the image − y of the object).","Remember: the point (x, y) translated by the column vector (p, q) lands on (x + p, y + q).","Remember: (a, b) + (c, d) = (a + c, b + d) — vectors are added component by component.","Remember: (a, b) − (c, d) = (a − c, b − d) — vectors are subtracted component by component, and taking away a negative adds.","Remember: k(a, b) = (ka, kb) — a scalar multiplies both components, multiplying the length of the arrow by k and reversing it when k is negative.","Remember: −(a, b) = (−a, −b) and (a, b) + (−a, −b) = (0, 0), the zero vector, so the negative vector is the translation that undoes the original.","Remember: two translations one after the other are equivalent to the single translation given by the sum of the two vectors.","Remember: a translation produces a congruent image, so lengths, angles and area are unchanged — and the area of a triangle, ½ × base × perpendicular height, is itself not on the Exam Aid."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T12:00:39.598715Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Mathematically clean throughout - all 14 bank questions, 4 exit questions and 4 lesson checks re-solved independently in node and every answer key, unit and solution line is right, both statements stay inside Foundation G25 (no vector proofs), and seven questions are non-calculator with hand-sized numbers; fix the \"nine results\" miscount in the Formulae sheet preamble before publishing."}}},"probability":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"probability","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Probability is the one part of this course where the answer is a claim about what will happen, not a measurement of what has. It runs through all three papers and every question is built from the same four moves: list the equally likely outcomes, count the ones you want, use the fact that a complete set of probabilities adds to 1, and multiply along the branches of a tree when two things happen together. Examiners set it in ordinary contexts — a frequency tree of survey answers, a biased dice tested 500 times, counters taken from a bag without replacement — and the marks sit in the working, not the final fraction. On Paper 1 you have no calculator, so the fractions must be cancelled by hand and left exact; on Papers 2 and 3 the same method is keyed in, and the decimal must be written to the accuracy asked. Get the counting and the sum-to-1 rule secure here and the Higher room's conditional probability is just the same idea with the total restricted.","idea":[{"h":"Recording outcomes in tables and frequency trees","t":"An experiment is recorded by writing the count of each outcome against that outcome in a frequency table; the counts must add to the number of trials, which is the first thing to check. A frequency tree does the same job for two attributes: the total splits down the first pair of branches (walk or do not walk), and each of those splits again down the second pair (Year 11 or not). Every pair of branches adds back to the number it came from, and the four end frequencies add to the original total, so one missing figure is always found by subtraction. Examiners give you three of the four end frequencies, or a total and two splits, and ask you to complete the tree and then read a frequency or a probability off it. Fill every box, including the ones you were not asked for, because the branch totals are where the method marks are.","code":"P1"},{"h":"Expected outcomes of future experiments","t":"If an experiment is repeated n times and one outcome has probability p, the expected number of times that outcome happens is p × n. The probability comes first: from fairness and equally likely sections for a spinner or dice, from a relative frequency for a biased one, or from a share of a group. Then multiply. The wording matters as much as the arithmetic — an expectation is not a promise, so 1/4 × 200 = 50 means about 50 in the long run, and getting 46 reds does not make the spinner unfair. Questions ask this straight ('how many times would you expect it to land on red in 200 spins') or in reverse, giving the expected number and the number of trials and asking for the probability, which is the same equation rearranged as p = expected ÷ n.","code":"P2"},{"h":"Relative frequency and the 0–1 probability scale","t":"The relative frequency of an outcome is the number of times it happened divided by the number of trials: 180 sixes in 400 rolls of a biased dice gives 180 ÷ 400 = 0.45. It is an estimate of the probability, and it is the only estimate available when the object is biased or the situation is not made of equally likely outcomes. Every probability, theoretical or experimental, is a number between 0 and 1, and may be written as a fraction, a decimal or a percentage — never as a ratio or as '1 in 4' when a value is asked for. The scale carries the words too: impossible at 0, unlikely below 1/2, even chance at 1/2, likely above it, certain at 1. Examiners ask you to work out a relative frequency, to place it on a marked scale, and to compare it with the theoretical value and say what the difference suggests.","code":"P3"},{"h":"Exhaustive and mutually exclusive outcomes","t":"Mutually exclusive outcomes cannot both happen in one trial; a set of outcomes is exhaustive if one of them must happen. The probabilities of an exhaustive set of mutually exclusive outcomes add to exactly 1, and that single fact answers two whole families of question. A missing probability in a table of four outcomes is 1 minus the sum of the other three. The probability of an event not happening is P(not A) = 1 − P(A), so P(the bus is late) = 0.12 gives P(the bus is not late) = 0.88. It also gives you a check: if your probabilities do not add to 1, something is wrong before you go any further. The Exam Aid prints P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B); when A and B are mutually exclusive, P(A and B) = 0 and it reduces to simple addition.","code":"P4"},{"h":"Why more trials give a better estimate","t":"A relative frequency from a small unbiased sample can sit a long way from the theoretical probability by pure chance: 13 heads in 20 throws of a fair coin gives 0.65, and proves nothing. As the number of trials grows, the relative frequency of an unbiased experiment settles towards the theoretical probability, so 5120 heads in 10 000 throws (0.512) is much stronger evidence than any short run. Examiners ask this as a written reason — 'explain why Jo cannot be sure the dice is biased' — and the mark is for saying that the sample is too small for the relative frequency to have settled, or that more trials would give a more reliable estimate. Saying only 'it is close to 1/6' or 'she was unlucky' does not score; the reason must mention the number of trials.","code":"P5"},{"h":"Enumerating sets and combinations systematically","t":"Listing everything without repeating or missing anything needs a fixed order and the right diagram. A two-way table or grid suits two experiments (four sandwiches down, three drinks across); a tree diagram suits two or three stages, one branch per outcome; a Venn diagram suits overlapping sets, where the overlap holds the members in both and the space outside the circles holds those in neither. With a Venn diagram, fill the overlap first and subtract it from each subject total, then check that all four regions add to the group size. Where the overlap is not given, the members in at least one set are (first set) + (second set) − (both), because adding the two totals counts the overlap twice. An ordered list works too, as long as the order is visibly systematic: HH, HT, TH, TT.","code":"P6"},{"h":"Possibility spaces and theoretical probability","t":"When every outcome is equally likely, P(event) = (number of outcomes in the event) ÷ (total number of equally likely outcomes). The work is the counting, and a possibility space diagram does it reliably: for two experiments, a grid with the first experiment's outcomes down the side and the second's across the top, with the total or product written in each cell. Two fair six-sided dice give a 6 × 6 grid of 36 equally likely cells, so a single cell has probability 1/36. In general, the number of combined outcomes is (outcomes of the first) × (outcomes of the second). Answers are given as a fraction in its lowest terms unless a decimal or percentage is asked for, and on Paper 1 the cancelling has to be shown. The grid also earns a method mark on its own, even if the final count slips.","code":"P7"},{"h":"Combined events: independent and dependent","t":"On a tree diagram you multiply along a path and add between paths. Each pair of branches from one point adds to 1, which is the quickest check that the tree is right. Events are independent when the first does not change the second — a coin thrown twice, or a counter replaced before the next is taken — and then P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B), with the same probabilities on both sets of branches. They are dependent when the first changes what is left: taking a red counter from 5 red and 3 blue without replacement leaves 4 red out of 7, so the second set of branches changes. State the assumption when asked — that each counter is equally likely to be taken, or that the events are independent. For 'at least one', it is almost always quicker to work out P(none) and subtract from 1.","code":"P8"}],"examples":[{"title":"A possibility space for the total of two dice","setup":"Two fair six-sided dice, one red and one blue, are rolled and the two scores are added. Work out the probability that the total is 9. Give your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms.","steps":["A possibility space grid for the red score 1 to 6 against the blue score 1 to 6 has 6 × 6 = 36 equally likely outcomes.","Reading the grid, the cells with a total of 9 are (3, 6), (4, 5), (5, 4) and (6, 3) — 4 cells out of the 36.","P(total of 9) = cells in the event ÷ all equally likely cells = 4/36.","4/36 cancels by 4, so the probability is 1/9."],"cues":["How many equally likely outcomes are there altogether?","Which pairs of scores add to 9?","What is a theoretical probability divided by?","What is the highest common factor of 4 and 36?"]},{"title":"Completing a frequency tree","setup":"60 students were asked whether they walk to school. 35 of the 60 walk to school. 14 of the students who walk to school are in Year 11, and 9 of the students who do not walk to school are in Year 11. Complete a frequency tree for this information and work out how many of the 60 students are not in Year 11.","steps":["The first pair of branches splits by walking: 35 walk, so 60 − 35 = 25 do not walk.","The walking branch splits again: 14 are in Year 11, so 35 − 14 = 21 are not.","The non-walking branch splits: 9 are in Year 11, so 25 − 9 = 16 are not.","Check the four end frequencies against the total: 14 + 21 + 9 + 16 = 60.","Students not in Year 11 are the two end branches 21 + 16 = 37."],"cues":["How many of the students do not walk to school?","How many of the walkers are not in Year 11?","How many of the non-walkers are not in Year 11?","What should the four end frequencies add up to?","Which end branches hold students who are not in Year 11?"]},{"title":"Relative frequency used to estimate a future count","setup":"A spinner has sections coloured red, green and blue. In a test of 200 spins it lands on red 82 times, on green 64 times and on blue 54 times. Work out the relative frequency of red as a decimal, and use it to estimate the number of reds in 500 spins.","steps":["A relative frequency divides the count of the outcome by how many trials were carried out, giving 82 ÷ 200.","82 ÷ 200 = 0.41, so the relative frequency of red is 0.41.","This estimate is used as the probability, so the estimated number of reds is 0.41 × 500 spins.","0.41 × 500 = 205, so about 205 of the 500 spins would be expected to land on red."],"cues":["What two numbers does a relative frequency compare?","What is that division as a decimal?","How is an expected number worked out from a probability?","Should the result be reported as an exact count or as an expectation?"]},{"title":"A Venn diagram with an unknown overlap","setup":"In a class of 30 students, 18 study French, 14 study Spanish and 5 study neither language. One of the 30 students is chosen at random. Work out the probability that the student studies both French and Spanish. Give your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms.","steps":["Members of at least one set = 30 − 5 = 25.","Adding the two subject totals counts anyone in both of them twice: 18 + 14 = 32.","The excess is the overlap, so 32 − 25 = 7 study both, and the diagram reads 11 French only, 7 both, 7 Spanish only and 5 outside.","P(both) = 7/30, which will not cancel."],"cues":["How many of the class study at least one language?","What happens to anyone in the overlap when the two subject totals are added?","The two totals come to more than the number in at least one set — what does that excess count?","Which region is wanted, and out of how many students?"]},{"title":"A tree diagram without replacement","setup":"A bag contains 5 red counters and 3 blue counters. Two counters are taken at random, one after the other, and the first is not put back. Assume every counter left in the bag is equally likely to be taken. Work out the probability that both counters are red, as a fraction in its lowest terms.","steps":["There are 5 + 3 = 8 counters to start, so the first pair of branches gives P(red) = 5/8.","After a red is removed, 4 of the 7 counters left are red, so the second branch gives 4/7.","Multiply along the path: P(red, red) = 5/8 × 4/7 = 20/56.","20/56 cancels by 4, so P(both red) = 5/14."],"cues":["How many counters are in the bag before anything is taken?","What is left in the bag if the first counter taken is red?","On a tree diagram, do you multiply along a path or add?","What is the highest common factor of 20 and 56?"]}],"check":[{"q":"The probability that a train is late is 0.3. What is the probability that it is not late?","a":"0.7"},{"q":"A fair coin is thrown 80 times. How many heads would you expect?","a":"40"},{"q":"Two fair coins are thrown. List all the equally likely outcomes.","a":"HH, HT, TH, TT — four equally likely outcomes."},{"q":"Ali throws a fair coin 20 times and Beth throws it 2000 times. Whose relative frequency of heads is likely to be closer to 0.5?","a":"Beth's, because the relative frequency settles towards the theoretical probability as the number of trials increases."}]},"room":{"facts":["P(event) = number of equally likely outcomes in the event ÷ total number of equally likely outcomes.","The probabilities of an exhaustive set of mutually exclusive outcomes add to 1, so P(not A) = 1 − P(A).","Relative frequency = number of times the outcome happened ÷ number of trials.","Expected number of successes = probability × number of trials (an expectation, not a guarantee).","On a tree diagram multiply along a path and add between paths; each pair of branches from one point adds to 1.","Independent events: P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). Without replacement the total falls by 1 for the second stage, and the favourable count falls by 1 only if the item taken first was itself a success.","Two combined experiments have (outcomes of the first) × (outcomes of the second) equally likely outcomes.","Members of at least one of two sets = (first set) + (second set) − (members of both)."],"questions":[{"q":"The probability that it rains in Leeds tomorrow is 0.35. Work out the probability that it does not rain in Leeds tomorrow. (non-calculator)","a":"0.65","sol":"Rain and no rain are mutually exclusive and one of them must happen, so their probabilities add to 1. P(no rain) = 1 − 0.35. M1 for 1 − 0.35. = 0.65. A1 for 0.65 (65% or 13/20 also accepted).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Rain and no rain between them cover everything that can happen tomorrow.","The probabilities of an exhaustive set of outcomes add to 1, so subtract.","The first line is P(no rain) = 1 − P(rain)."],"codes":["P4"]},{"q":"A fair eight-sided dice is numbered 1 to 8. It is rolled 120 times. Work out the expected number of times it lands on a number greater than 6. (non-calculator)","a":"30","sol":"Two of the eight equally likely numbers (7 and 8) are greater than 6, so P(greater than 6) = 2/8 = 1/4. Expected number = 1/4 × 120. M1 for 1/4 × 120 or 120 ÷ 4. = 30. A1 for 30.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Each of the eight numbers is equally likely on a fair dice.","An expected number is the probability of the outcome multiplied by the number of trials.","The first line is P(greater than 6) = (how many numbers qualify) ÷ 8."],"codes":["P2","P7"]},{"q":"A biased coin is thrown 500 times. It lands on heads 320 times. Work out the relative frequency of heads. Give your answer as a decimal. (non-calculator)","a":"0.64","sol":"Relative frequency = heads ÷ throws = 320/500. M1 for 320/500 or 64/100. Dividing both parts by 5 gives 64/100 = 0.64. A1 for 0.64.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A biased coin has no equally likely outcomes to count, so its probability can only be estimated from what the 500 throws actually did.","An estimate like that compares how often the outcome happened with how many trials there were, and is then written as a decimal.","The first line is relative frequency = number of heads ÷ number of throws."],"codes":["P3"]},{"q":"A fair six-sided dice numbered 1 to 6 is rolled once. Work out the probability of rolling a number less than 3. Give your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms. (non-calculator)","a":"1/3","sol":"The equally likely outcomes are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6, and two of them (1 and 2) are less than 3. P = 2/6. M1 for 2/6. Cancelling by 2 gives 1/3. A1 for 1/3.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Write out the scores the dice can show; each one is equally likely.","With equally likely outcomes a probability compares how many outcomes are in the event with how many there are in total, and the fraction is then cancelled.","The first line is P(less than 3) = (number of scores below 3) ÷ (number of scores on the dice), then cancel."],"codes":["P7"]},{"q":"A four-sided spinner can land on A, B, C or D. P(A) = 0.2, P(B) = 0.15 and P(C) = 0.4. Work out P(D). (non-calculator)","a":"0.25","sol":"The spinner must land on one of the four letters, so the four probabilities add to 1. 0.2 + 0.15 + 0.4 = 0.75. M1 for 0.75. P(D) = 1 − 0.75 = 0.25. A1 for 0.25.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The spinner has to land on one of the four letters.","An exhaustive set of mutually exclusive probabilities adds to 1, so the missing one is 1 minus the rest.","The first line adds the three probabilities you are given, before subtracting the total from 1."],"codes":["P4"]},{"q":"A dice is rolled 50 times. The scores are recorded as: 1 seven times, 2 nine times, 3 six times, 4 eleven times, 5 nine times and 6 eight times. Write down the relative frequency of a score of 4. Give your answer as a fraction. (non-calculator)","a":"11/50","sol":"Relative frequency = frequency of that score ÷ total rolls = 11/50. B1 for 11/50. It does not cancel, as 11 is prime and is not a factor of 50.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The frequency you need is already in the list; nothing has to be worked out from the other scores.","A relative frequency compares one recorded frequency with the total number of trials.","For a parallel, a score that came up 3 times in 20 rolls would have a relative frequency of 3/20."],"codes":["P1","P3"]},{"q":"80 people took a driving test. 45 of them were women. 33 of the women passed. 21 of the men failed. One of the 80 people is chosen at random. Work out the probability that this person passed the test. Give your answer as a fraction. A calculator may be used.","a":"47/80","sol":"Draw a frequency tree splitting 80 by sex then by pass or fail. Men = 80 − 45 = 35. M1 for 35. Men who passed = 35 − 21 = 14. M1 for 14. Total who passed = 33 + 14 = 47. M1 for 47. P(passed) = 47/80. A1 for 47/80. Check: 33 + 12 + 14 + 21 = 80.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["A frequency tree splits the 80 by sex first, then each branch by pass or fail.","Find the number of men, then how many of the men passed, then add the two groups of passes.","The first line is number of men = total − number of women."],"codes":["P1","P7"]},{"q":"A box contains 4 green pens and 6 black pens. A pen is taken at random, its colour is recorded and it is put back in the box. A second pen is then taken at random. Work out the probability that both pens are green. Give your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms. (non-calculator)","a":"4/25","sol":"There are 4 + 6 = 10 pens, so P(green) = 4/10. M1 for 4/10. The pen is replaced, so the picks are independent and the second probability is also 4/10: P(green, green) = 4/10 × 4/10. M1 for 4/10 × 4/10. = 16/100 = 4/25. A1 for 4/25.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The first pen goes back, so the box is the same for the second pick.","Multiply the probability of a green first by the probability of a green second.","The first line is P(green) = number of green pens ÷ total pens, and the same value is used twice."],"codes":["P8"]},{"q":"Spinner A is fair and has four equal sections numbered 1, 2, 3 and 4. Spinner B is fair and has three equal sections numbered 1, 2 and 3. Both spinners are spun and the two scores are multiplied. Work out the probability that the product is an even number. Give your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms. A calculator may be used.","a":"2/3","sol":"A possibility space grid of A against B holds 4 × 3 = 12 equally likely products. M1 for 12 outcomes or a complete grid. The products are 1, 2, 3 / 2, 4, 6 / 3, 6, 9 / 4, 8, 12, of which the even ones are 2, 2, 4, 6, 6, 4, 8, 12 — 8 in total. M1 for 8 even products. P(even) = 8/12 = 2/3. A1 for 2/3.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Every pair of scores is equally likely, so a grid of all the products shows them all at once.","Count the even products in the grid, then divide by how many products there are.","The first line is the number of equally likely outcomes = sections on A × sections on B."],"codes":["P6","P7"]},{"q":"Jo rolls a dice 60 times. It lands on a six 14 times. (a) Work out the relative frequency of a six, as a fraction in its lowest terms. (b) Jo says the dice must be biased. Explain why 60 rolls is not enough to be sure. A calculator may be used.","a":"(a) 7/30 (b) 60 rolls is too small a sample for the relative frequency to have settled, so it can differ from 1/6 by chance.","sol":"(a) Relative frequency = 14/60. M1 for 14/60. Cancelling by 2 gives 7/30 (about 0.233, against a theoretical 1/6 ≈ 0.167). A1 for 7/30. (b) B1 for saying the sample is too small / more rolls are needed, because the relative frequency of an unbiased experiment only settles towards the theoretical probability as the number of trials increases. 'She was unlucky' on its own scores nothing.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["One of these values is what happened; 1/6 is what theory predicts for a fair dice.","Divide the number of sixes by the number of rolls and cancel, then think about how large 60 is as a sample.","The first line is relative frequency = sixes ÷ number of rolls."],"codes":["P3","P5"]},{"q":"A gym has 40 members. 22 of them use the pool, 18 of them go to the classes and 7 of them do both. One member is chosen at random. Work out the probability that this member uses neither the pool nor the classes. Give your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms. A calculator may be used.","a":"7/40","sol":"Adding 22 and 18 counts the 7 who do both twice, so members using at least one = 22 + 18 − 7 = 33. M1 for 22 + 18 − 7 (or a Venn diagram showing 15, 7 and 11). Members using neither = 40 − 33 = 7. M1 for 40 − 33. P(neither) = 7/40. A1 for 7/40. Check: 15 + 7 + 11 + 7 = 40.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Some members are counted in both of the two totals you are given.","Find how many use at least one facility, then take that from the membership.","The first line is (pool) + (classes) − (both) = number using at least one."],"codes":["P6","P7"]},{"q":"A drawer contains 7 white socks and 5 grey socks. Two socks are taken at random, one after the other, and the first is not put back. Work out the probability that the two socks are different colours. Give your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms. A calculator may be used.","a":"35/66","sol":"There are 7 + 5 = 12 socks. P(white then grey) = 7/12 × 5/11 = 35/132. M1 for 7/12 × 5/11. P(grey then white) = 5/12 × 7/11 = 35/132. M1 for 5/12 × 7/11. Different colours happens either way round, so add the two paths: 35/132 + 35/132 = 70/132. M1 for adding the two paths. Cancelling by 2 gives 35/66. A1 for 35/66. Equivalent method: 1 − (7/12 × 6/11) − (5/12 × 4/11) = 1 − 42/132 − 20/132 = 70/132.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Two different colours can be drawn in two different orders.","Work out the probability of white then grey and of grey then white, then combine them.","The first line is P(white first) = whites ÷ total, with the second sock taken from one fewer sock."],"codes":["P8","P4"]},{"q":"A bag contains 240 beads. The ratio of yellow beads to purple beads is 3 : 5. A bead is taken at random, its colour is recorded and it is put back. This is done 600 times. Work out how many times a yellow bead would be expected. A calculator may be used.","a":"225","sol":"The ratio has 3 + 5 = 8 parts and 240 ÷ 8 = 30 beads per part, so there are 3 × 30 = 90 yellow beads. M1 for 90 (or for going straight to 3/8). P(yellow) = 90/240 = 3/8 = 0.375. A1 for 3/8. Expected number = 3/8 × 600. M1 for 3/8 × 600. = 225. A1 for 225.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The ratio tells you what fraction of the beads are yellow.","Turn the ratio into a probability, then multiply that probability by the number of picks.","The first line is total parts = 3 + 5, then P(yellow) = yellow parts ÷ total parts."],"codes":["P2","P7"]},{"q":"160 people were each asked which one of tea, coffee and juice they preferred. 45% of them preferred tea and 1/4 of them preferred coffee. The rest preferred juice. Work out how many of the 160 people preferred juice. A calculator may be used.","a":"48","sol":"1/4 = 25%, so tea and coffee together take 45% + 25% = 70%. B1 for 70% (or 0.7). The three preferences are mutually exclusive and exhaustive, so juice takes 100% − 70% = 30%, giving P(juice) = 0.3. M1 for 30% or 0.3. Number preferring juice = 0.3 × 160. M1 for 0.3 × 160. = 48. A1 for 48.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Tea, coffee and juice are the only choices, so between them they take the whole group.","Put both known shares in the same form, add them and subtract from the whole.","The first line writes 1/4 as a percentage, then takes (tea% + coffee%) from 100%."],"codes":["P4","P3"]},{"q":"A fair four-sided dice is numbered 1, 2, 3 and 4. It is rolled twice. Work out the probability that at least one of the two rolls is a 4. Give your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms. (non-calculator)","a":"7/16","sol":"Three of the four numbers are not a 4, so P(not a 4) = 3/4 on each roll. M1 for 3/4. The rolls are independent, so P(no 4 at all) = 3/4 × 3/4 = 9/16. M1 for 3/4 × 3/4. 'At least one 4' is the only other possibility, so P(at least one 4) = 1 − 9/16. M1 for 1 − 9/16. = 7/16. A1 for 7/16. Listing the 16 equally likely ordered pairs and counting the 7 containing a 4 scores full marks too.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["'At least one' is far easier to handle through the outcome it rules out.","Find the probability that neither roll is a 4, then take it from 1.","The first line is P(not a 4) on one roll = (numbers that are not 4) ÷ 4."],"codes":["P8","P4","P7"]}],"exit":[{"q":"A bag holds only red and yellow balls. P(red) = 3/7. Work out P(yellow).","a":"4/7"},{"q":"A fair coin is thrown 90 times. How many tails would you expect?","a":"45"},{"q":"Two fair coins are thrown. Work out the probability of getting two tails.","a":"1/4"},{"q":"A spinner lands on green 36 times in 90 spins. Work out the relative frequency of green, as a decimal.","a":"0.4"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The probability scale","back":"Every probability is a number from 0 to 1: 0 impossible, 1 certain, 1/2 an even chance, below 1/2 unlikely, above it likely. Write it as a fraction, decimal or percentage, never as a ratio.","code":"P3","open":false},{"front":"Theoretical probability with equally likely outcomes","back":"P(event) = number of outcomes in the event ÷ total number of equally likely outcomes. On a fair six-sided dice, P(even) = 3/6 = 1/2.","code":"P7","open":false},{"front":"Relative frequency (experimental probability)","back":"Relative frequency = number of times the outcome happened ÷ number of trials. It estimates a probability that cannot be found from equally likely outcomes, such as for a biased dice: 180 sixes in 400 rolls gives 0.45.","code":"P3","open":false},{"front":"Expected number of successes in n trials","back":"Expected number = probability × number of trials: 1/4 × 200 = 50. It is an expectation over the long run, not a guarantee, so the actual count usually differs.","code":"P2","open":false},{"front":"The sum of an exhaustive set of mutually exclusive probabilities","back":"They add to exactly 1, so a missing probability is 1 minus the sum of the others: 0.3 + 0.1 + 0.45 = 0.85, leaving 0.15.","code":"P4","open":false},{"front":"P(not A)","back":"P(not A) = 1 − P(A), because A and 'not A' are mutually exclusive and one of them must happen. P(late) = 0.12 gives P(not late) = 0.88.","code":"P4","open":false},{"front":"Mutually exclusive events","back":"Events that cannot both happen in the same trial, such as rolling a 2 and rolling a 5 on one roll. Then P(A and B) = 0 and P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B).","code":"P4","open":false},{"front":"The effect of sample size on relative frequency","back":"A relative frequency from a small unbiased sample can sit far from the theoretical probability by chance; as the number of trials grows it settles towards that value, so a long run is stronger evidence of bias.","code":"P5","open":false},{"front":"Frequency tree","back":"A diagram splitting a total by one attribute and then by a second, with a frequency at every branch end. Each pair of branches adds back to the figure it came from, and the end frequencies add to the total.","code":"P1","open":false},{"front":"Possibility space diagram","back":"A list, table or grid of every equally likely outcome of one or two experiments. Two fair dice give a 6 × 6 grid of 36 cells, so one cell has probability 1/36.","code":"P7","open":false},{"front":"Reading a Venn diagram","back":"The overlap holds members in both sets, the outer region those in neither, and all regions add to the group size. Members of at least one set = (first) + (second) − (both).","code":"P6","open":false},{"front":"Tree diagram: along and between branches","back":"Multiply the probabilities along a path to get that path's probability; add the probabilities of the different paths that give the event. Each pair of branches from one point adds to 1.","code":"P8","open":false},{"front":"Independent events","back":"Events where the first does not change the probability of the second, as when a counter is replaced. Then P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B), and both sets of branches carry the same probabilities.","code":"P8","open":false},{"front":"Dependent events (without replacement)","back":"The first outcome changes what is left, so the second set of branch probabilities changes. From 6 red and 4 blue, P(red then red) = 6/10 × 5/9 = 1/3.","code":"P8","open":false},{"front":"Give one way of listing every outcome of a combined experiment","back":"A two-way table or possibility space grid; a tree diagram with one branch per outcome; an ordered list written systematically, such as HH, HT, TH, TT.","code":"P6","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B) — printed under Probability on the Foundation Exam Aid (W87990A), so it does not have to be remembered. For mutually exclusive events P(A and B) = 0 and it reduces to P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B).","The probabilities of an exhaustive set of mutually exclusive outcomes add to 1 — not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","P(not A) = 1 − P(A) — not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","P(event) = number of equally likely outcomes in the event ÷ total number of equally likely outcomes — not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Relative frequency = number of times the outcome happened ÷ number of trials — not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Expected number of successes = probability × number of trials — not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Independent events: P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B), which is multiplying along the branches of a tree — not on the Foundation Exam Aid: remember it.","Dependent events: no formula is printed. After the first item is taken and not replaced, drop the total by 1 and drop the favourable count by 1 as well if that item was a success, then multiply along the path.","Combined experiments: total number of equally likely outcomes = (outcomes of the first) × (outcomes of the second) — not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Members of at least one of two sets = (first set) + (second set) − (members of both) — not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","The Higher Exam Aid (W87991A) prints one extra probability line, P(A and B) = P(A given B) P(B). The Foundation sheet (W87990A) does not print it, and conditional probability is Higher-tier content, so nothing in this room relies on it.","Nothing else on the Foundation Exam Aid belongs here: the rest of W87990A is the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, the circumference and area of a circle, a² + b² = c², sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b and Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T11:58:32Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.84,"notes":"Every answer key checks out — all 15 bank questions, the 4 exit questions, the 4 lesson checks and all 5 worked examples were re-solved independently in Python (exact fractions, with the two-dice, two-spinner and two-roll spaces enumerated by brute force), every sol's M/A/B marks add to its tariff, P1–P8 are covered in order with nothing beyond Foundation, and every Formulae sheet claim matches Exam Aid W87990A and W87991A in exam-aid.md exactly; the repairs needed are the decimal acceptances in five solutions, the false without-replacement fact, and the three questions whose answers are already printed on the cards."}}},"statistics":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"statistics","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Statistics carries 12–18% of the Foundation marks alongside probability, and it turns up on all three papers: a frequency table to average, a pie chart or a pictogram to read, two data sets to compare, a scatter graph to describe. Most of these marks are for reasoning rather than arithmetic — saying what an average and a range tell you about the people behind the numbers, saying why a sample might mislead, saying why a prediction beyond the data cannot be trusted. That makes this room one of the cheapest places on the paper to pick marks up, and one of the easiest to throw them away by giving a bare number with no context, no unit and no comparison.","idea":[{"h":"Samples, populations and what a sample can be trusted to say","t":"The population is everyone or everything you want to know about; a sample is the part of it you actually look at. You infer a property of the population from the sample by scaling: if 9 of a sample of 25 say yes, the estimate for a population of 600 is 9/25 × 600 = 216. The exam almost always follows that calculation with a reasoning mark, so know the limitations: a small sample gives an unreliable estimate, and a sample that is not chosen fairly — one class, one morning, volunteers only — is biased and does not represent the population however large it is. Say which limitation applies and why, in the context of the question.","code":"S1"},{"h":"Tables, charts and diagrams, and which one suits the data","t":"Categorical data (colours, sports, drinks) goes in a bar chart, a pictogram or a pie chart; ungrouped discrete numerical data (goals, pets, shoe sizes) goes in a vertical line chart; data collected over time goes in a line graph with time along the horizontal axis. In a pie chart the whole circle is the whole set, so one item is worth 360° ÷ total and a sector angle is (frequency ÷ total) × 360°, with the angles adding to 360°. In a pictogram the key says what one whole symbol stands for and a part symbol stands for that fraction of it. Marks are lost on scales that are not equally spaced, missing labels and a missing key, so label the axes and read values off accurately.","code":"S2"},{"h":"Averages, spread and comparing two distributions","t":"The mean is the total of the values divided by how many there are; from a frequency table it is the total of (value × frequency) divided by the total frequency; for grouped data the exact values are lost, so you use the midpoint of each class and the answer is an estimate of the mean. The median is the middle value once the data is in order — for n values it is in position (n + 1)/2, and with an even n it is halfway between the two middle values. The mode is the most common value, and for grouped data you quote the modal class. The range, largest minus smallest, measures spread, and an outlier — a value far away from the rest — inflates both the range and the mean. To compare two sets you must quote one average and one measure of spread and say what each means in context: 'the boys' mean time was lower, so they were quicker, and their range was smaller, so they were more consistent'.","code":"S4"},{"h":"Using statistics to describe a population","t":"Describing a population means choosing the statistics that suit the data and then saying what they show. Choose the mean when the values are fairly even, because it uses every value; choose the median when there is an outlier or a very lopsided set, because the mean is dragged towards the extreme value; choose the mode when the data is categorical or when you want the most popular value. Pair the average with a measure of spread — the range — so that the description says both what is typical and how much variation there is: 'the rainfall is typically about 45 mm a month, but it varies by 60 mm across the year'. An average on its own describes almost nothing, and a mark for description always wants the context and the units.","code":"S5"},{"h":"Scatter graphs, correlation and lines of best fit","t":"A scatter graph plots two variables for each item so that a relationship can be seen. Points climbing left to right show positive correlation, points falling left to right show negative correlation, and a shapeless cloud shows no correlation; the strength is described as strong or weak. Correlation does not indicate causation — two things can rise together because a third factor drives both. A line of best fit is a single straight line following the trend with roughly equal numbers of points on each side; read from it to predict. A prediction made between the smallest and largest data values is interpolation and is reasonably safe; a prediction outside the data is extrapolation and cannot be trusted, because there is no evidence the trend continues.","code":"S6"}],"examples":[{"title":"All four summary statistics from one list","setup":"Eight students recorded the number of minutes they spent reading one evening: 25, 40, 15, 40, 30, 20, 40, 10. Work out the mean, the median, the mode and the range of these times.","steps":["Put the eight values in order, smallest first: 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 40, 40, 40.","Add the eight times: 10 + 15 + 20 + 25 + 30 + 40 + 40 + 40 = 220 minutes.","Share that total between the eight students: mean = 220 ÷ 8 = 27.5 minutes.","With eight values the median sits halfway between the 4th and the 5th in the ordered list: (25 + 30) ÷ 2 = 27.5 minutes.","The time that appears most often is 40 minutes, which appears three times, so the mode is 40 minutes.","The range is the largest time minus the smallest: 40 − 10 = 30 minutes.","So the mean is 27.5 minutes, the median is 27.5 minutes, the mode is 40 minutes and the range is 30 minutes."],"cues":["What has to happen to a list before a median or a range can be read from it?","What does a mean need before anything can be shared out?","How many students is that total shared between?","With an even number of values, which two sit in the middle?","Which average is found by counting how often each time appears?","Which two of the eight times does a range use?","Which four statistics did the question ask for?"]},{"title":"Turning a frequency table into pie chart angles","setup":"A survey of 45 people asked which drink they chose: tea 20, coffee 15, juice 10. Their answers are to be shown in a pie chart. Work out the angle of the sector for each drink.","steps":["One person is shown by 360° ÷ 45 = 8° of the pie chart.","Tea: 20 × 8° = 160°.","Coffee: 15 × 8° = 120°.","Juice: 10 × 8° = 80°.","The three angles are 160°, 120° and 80°, and they add to 360°, which checks the working."],"cues":["How much of a full turn does a single person take up?","How is a sector angle built once you know the degrees per person?","What does the same multiplication give for the 15 people who chose coffee?","What does it give for the smallest of the three groups?","What should the sector angles add up to?"]},{"title":"Modal class and an estimate of the mean from grouped data","setup":"The table shows the masses, m kg, of 50 dogs at a rescue centre: 0 < m ≤ 10, 8 dogs; 10 < m ≤ 20, 21 dogs; 20 < m ≤ 30, 14 dogs; 30 < m ≤ 40, 7 dogs. Write down the modal class and work out an estimate of the mean mass.","steps":["The class holding more dogs than any other is 10 < m ≤ 20, which holds 21, so that is the modal class.","The exact masses are lost, so each class is represented by its midpoint: 5, 15, 25 and 35 kg.","Multiply each midpoint by its frequency: 5 × 8 = 40, 15 × 21 = 315, 25 × 14 = 350, 35 × 7 = 245.","Adding the products estimates the total mass of all 50 dogs: 40 + 315 + 350 + 245 = 950 kg.","Estimated mean mass = 950 ÷ 50 = 19 kg."],"cues":["Which row holds more dogs than any other?","If you cannot know the exact masses, what single value can stand for a whole class?","How is each midpoint combined with the number of dogs in that class?","What do the four products add up to, and what does that total represent?","How is a mean found once a total and a count are known?"]},{"title":"Reading a prediction from a line of best fit","setup":"A scatter graph shows the engine size, x litres, and the fuel used, y litres per 100 km, for 12 cars whose engines range from 1.0 litres to 3.0 litres. The line of best fit drawn through the points passes through (1.2, 6.0) and (2.8, 12.4). Describe the correlation, use the line to estimate the fuel used by a car with a 2.0 litre engine, and say whether that estimate is interpolation or extrapolation.","steps":["Along the line the points follow, the fuel used goes up from 6.0 at 1.2 litres to 12.4 at 2.8 litres, so right across the 1.0 litre to 3.0 litre spread of the data a bigger engine goes with more fuel used: the correlation is positive.","Gradient of the line of best fit = (12.4 − 6.0) ÷ (2.8 − 1.2) = 6.4 ÷ 1.6 = 4.","Going from x = 1.2 to x = 2.0 is a rise of 0.8 in x, so y rises by 0.8 × 4 = 3.2.","Fuel used by the 2.0 litre car = 6.0 + 3.2 = 9.2 litres per 100 km.","A 2.0 litre engine sits inside the 1.0 litre to 3.0 litre spread of the data, so this is interpolation, which is far safer than reading the line beyond 3.0 litres."],"cues":["Moving along the line of best fit from the smaller engine to the larger one, does the fuel used go up or down?","How much does the fuel used change for each extra litre of engine?","How far along the line do you move from the first given point?","What fuel figure does the line give at that place?","Does the engine asked about sit inside or outside the engines the data covers?"]},{"title":"Scaling a sample up to a population, and its limits","setup":"A library has 3200 members. A sample of 80 members is asked how many books they borrowed last month. The mean for the sample is 3.5 books, and 24 of the 80 borrowed no books at all. Estimate the total number of books borrowed last month by all the members, estimate how many members borrowed no books, and give one limitation of these estimates.","steps":["The sample mean is taken as the mean for every member, so each one is treated as borrowing 3.5 books.","Estimated total = 3.5 × 3200 = 11 200 books.","The fraction of the sample that borrowed nothing is 24 ÷ 80 = 0.3.","Estimated number borrowing no books = 0.3 × 3200 = 960 members.","Only 80 of the 3200 members were asked, so the estimates could be well out; and if those 80 were all asked on one morning they would not represent the whole membership."],"cues":["What has to be assumed about the sample before it can stand for everybody?","How is a total recovered from a mean and a count?","What proportion of the people asked borrowed nothing?","How does that proportion carry across to the whole membership?","What about the size or the choosing of the sample could make these figures wrong?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A list of 6 numbers has a total of 51. Work out the mean.","a":"8.5"},{"q":"In a pie chart showing the choices of 90 people, how many degrees represent one person?","a":"4°"},{"q":"What does negative correlation look like on a scatter graph?","a":"The points fall from top-left to bottom-right: as one variable increases the other decreases."},{"q":"A sample of 25 of a club's 500 members is used to estimate how many members cycle to the club. Give one reason the estimate may be unreliable.","a":"The sample is small (only 25 of 500), so the estimate is rough; or the 25 may not have been chosen fairly, so they may not represent all the members."}]},"room":{"facts":["Mean = total of the values ÷ how many values there are. From a frequency table, mean = total of (value × frequency) ÷ total frequency.","For grouped data you can only estimate the mean: use the midpoint of each class, so estimated mean = total of (midpoint × frequency) ÷ total frequency. The modal class is the class with the highest frequency.","Median = the middle value once the data is in order; for n values it is in position (n + 1)/2, and with an even n it is halfway between the two middle values. Mode = the value that appears most often.","Range = largest value − smallest value. An outlier is a value far from the rest; it inflates the range and drags the mean towards itself, so the median often describes such data better.","Pie chart: one item is worth 360° ÷ total, and a sector angle = (frequency ÷ total) × 360°. The sector angles add up to 360°.","From a sample of size s in which c items have a property, the estimate for a population of size N is (c ÷ s) × N. Correlation does not indicate causation, and a prediction inside the data (interpolation) is far safer than one outside it (extrapolation)."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Here are the shoe sizes of nine pupils: 6, 4, 7, 6, 8, 5, 6, 9, 7. Write down the modal shoe size.","a":"6","sol":"The mode is the size that appears most often. Size 6 appears three times, size 7 twice and every other size once, so the modal shoe size is 6 (B1).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The word 'modal' is about how often a size appears, not about how big it is.","Count how many pupils wear each size, then look for the biggest count.","Your first line is a tally of the nine sizes, one count for each different size."],"codes":["S4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Here are the daily maximum temperatures, in °C, at a weather station for seven days: 12, 17, 9, 14, 11, 17, 13. Work out the median temperature.","a":"13 °C","sol":"Put the seven temperatures in order: 9, 11, 12, 13, 14, 17, 17 (M1). With seven values the median is the 4th one, so the median temperature is 13 °C (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The median is a position in the list, not a calculation on all seven values.","Order the temperatures first, then count in the same number of places from each end.","Your first line is the seven temperatures rewritten from smallest to largest."],"codes":["S4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A pictogram shows the number of letters delivered each day. One full circle represents 8 letters. The row for Wednesday shows 3 full circles and one half circle. Work out the number of letters delivered on Wednesday.","a":"28","sol":"Three full circles stand for 3 × 8 = 24 letters, and a half circle stands for half of 8, which is 4 letters (M1 for either). Total = 24 + 4 = 28 letters (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The key tells you what one whole symbol is worth, so part of a symbol is worth part of that.","Work out the letters shown by the whole circles and the letters shown by the part circle, then combine them.","Your first line is the number of letters the whole circles stand for."],"codes":["S2"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A scatter graph plots the number of hours of revision against the test score for 30 students. The points climb steadily from the bottom-left to the top-right. Write down the type of correlation shown.","a":"Positive correlation","sol":"Points that climb from bottom-left to top-right mean that as one variable increases so does the other, which is positive correlation (B1).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what is happening to the test score as the revision time grows.","Points that climb and points that fall as you read left to right describe opposite relationships.","Your first line is a sentence saying which way the score moves as the revision hours increase."],"codes":["S6"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A club has 800 members. A sample of 40 members is asked when they attend, and 14 of them say Saturday. Estimate the number of the 800 members who attend on Saturday.","a":"280","sol":"The fraction of the sample attending on Saturday is 14/40. Applying that fraction to the whole club gives 14/40 × 800, or equivalently 800 ÷ 40 = 20 members for each member of the sample, then 14 × 20 (M1). This gives an estimate of 280 members (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The sample is meant to behave like the whole club in miniature.","Find the proportion of the sample who said Saturday, then apply that proportion to every member.","Your first line is the number who said Saturday written over the size of the sample."],"codes":["S1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A frequency table shows the number of pets owned by 20 people: 0 pets, 5 people; 1 pet, 8 people; 2 pets, 4 people; 3 pets, 3 people. Work out the mean number of pets.","a":"1.25","sol":"Multiply each number of pets by its frequency: 0 × 5 = 0, 1 × 8 = 8, 2 × 4 = 8, 3 × 3 = 9 (M1 for at least three correct products). Total pets = 0 + 8 + 8 + 9 = 25, and the total frequency is 5 + 8 + 4 + 3 = 20, so the mean = 25 ÷ 20 (M1). Mean = 1.25 pets (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Each row of the table stands for several people, not for one person.","Work out the total number of pets owned by all 20 people, then share that total between them.","Your first line is the four products of pets and frequency, one for each row of the table."],"codes":["S4"]},{"q":"The table shows the amount, £x, spent by each of 25 customers in a café: 0 < x ≤ 4, 5 customers; 4 < x ≤ 8, 9 customers; 8 < x ≤ 12, 7 customers; 12 < x ≤ 16, 4 customers. Work out an estimate of the mean amount spent. A calculator may be used.","a":"£7.60","sol":"The exact amounts are lost once the data is grouped, so each class is represented by its midpoint: £2, £6, £10 and £14 (M1). Multiply each midpoint by its frequency: 2 × 5 = 10, 6 × 9 = 54, 10 × 7 = 70, 14 × 4 = 56 (M1 for at least three correct products). The estimated total spent is 10 + 54 + 70 + 56 = £190, and the total frequency is 25, so the estimated mean = 190 ÷ 25 (M1). Estimated mean amount spent = £7.60 (A1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Once the amounts have been grouped the individual amounts are gone, so each class needs one value to stand for it.","Use a midpoint for each class, multiply by the frequency, total the products and share by the number of customers.","Your first line is the midpoint of each of the four classes."],"codes":["S4","S5"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Two sprinters each ran five races. Ali's times, in seconds, were 12.4, 12.1, 12.6, 12.3, 12.6. Ben's times were 11.9, 12.8, 12.5, 12.1, 12.7. Both sprinters have a mean time of 12.4 seconds. By comparing the range of each sprinter's times, decide which sprinter is the more consistent.","a":"Ali","sol":"Ali: range = 12.6 − 12.1 = 0.5 seconds (B1). Ben: range = 12.8 − 11.9 = 0.9 seconds (B1). The means are equal, so the comparison rests on the spread: Ali's range is the smaller, so Ali's times vary less and Ali is the more consistent sprinter (B1, dependent on both ranges being correct).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Being consistent is about how spread out the times are, not about how fast they are.","Work out the largest time minus the smallest time for each sprinter, then compare the two results.","Your first line is the largest and the smallest of the first sprinter's times."],"codes":["S4"]},{"q":"A scatter graph shows the mass, y grams, of a puppy against its age, x weeks, for puppies aged from 2 to 12 weeks. The line of best fit has equation y = 320x + 900. Use the line to estimate the mass of a puppy aged 7 weeks. A calculator may be used.","a":"3140 g","sol":"Substitute the age into the equation of the line of best fit: y = 320 × 7 + 900 (M1). 320 × 7 = 2240, so y = 2240 + 900 = 3140, giving an estimated mass of 3140 g (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The line of best fit gives a y value for any x inside the range of the data.","Put the age into the equation of the line in place of x.","Your first line is the equation of the line with the age written in place of x."],"codes":["S6"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A vertical line chart shows the number of goals scored in each of 20 matches: 0 goals in 4 matches, 1 goal in 6 matches, 2 goals in 5 matches, 3 goals in 3 matches and 4 goals in 2 matches. Work out the median number of goals.","a":"1.5","sol":"Build running totals of matches: 4, then 10, then 15, then 18, then 20 (M1). With 20 matches the median lies halfway between the 10th and the 11th values; the running totals show the 10th value is 1 goal and the 11th is 2 goals (M1). Median = (1 + 2) ÷ 2 = 1.5 goals (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["There are twenty matches listed in the chart, not five.","Add the frequencies up as you go down the chart and find where the middle of the twenty falls.","Your first line is the running totals of the frequencies, one for each number of goals."],"codes":["S2","S4"]},{"q":"A scatter graph shows arm span, y cm, against height, x cm, for students whose heights run from 140 cm to 180 cm. The line of best fit passes through the points (140, 136) and (180, 180). Use the line to estimate the height of a student whose arm span is 158 cm. A calculator may be used.","a":"160 cm","sol":"Gradient of the line of best fit = (180 − 136) ÷ (180 − 140) = 44 ÷ 40 (M1) = 1.1 (A1), so the arm span rises by 1.1 cm for every 1 cm of height. Working back from the point (140, 136), an arm span of 158 cm is 158 − 136 = 22 cm further up the line, which takes 22 ÷ 1.1 = 20 cm of extra height (M1). The estimated height is 140 + 20 = 160 cm (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Two points on a straight line are enough to fix how steep that line is.","Find how much the arm span changes for each 1 cm of height, then work backwards along the line from one of the given points.","Your first line is the difference in the arm spans written over the difference in the heights."],"codes":["S6"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The mean of six numbers is 14. A seventh number is added to the list and the mean of the seven numbers is 15. Work out the seventh number.","a":"21","sol":"Total of the six numbers = 6 × 14 = 84 (M1). Total of the seven numbers = 7 × 15 = 105 (M1). The seventh number is the difference: 105 − 84 = 21 (A1).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["A mean and a count multiplied together give back a total.","Find the total of the first six numbers and the total of all seven, then compare the two totals.","Your first line is the total of the six numbers, written as their mean multiplied by how many there are."],"codes":["S4"]},{"q":"The masses, in kg, of eight parcels are 2.1, 2.4, 2.2, 9.6, 2.5, 2.3, 2.0 and 2.3. The mean mass of all eight parcels is 3.175 kg. The 9.6 kg parcel is an outlier. Work out the mean mass of the other seven parcels, correct to 2 decimal places. A calculator may be used.","a":"2.26 kg","sol":"Total mass of all eight parcels = 8 × 3.175 = 25.4 kg, so the total mass of the other seven = 25.4 − 9.6 = 15.8 kg (M1; adding the seven masses directly is equally acceptable). Mean of the seven = 15.8 ÷ 7 (M1) = 2.2571… kg, which is 2.26 kg correct to 2 decimal places (A1). The outlier had raised the mean by almost a kilogram.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["The heavy parcel is nothing like the other seven, and the question has already told you to leave it out.","Take the outlier out of the total mass of the parcels, then share what is left between the parcels that remain.","Your first line is the total mass of all eight parcels."],"codes":["S4","S5"]},{"q":"A town has 18 500 residents. A sample of 250 residents is asked how they travel to work, and the answers are shown in a pie chart. The angle of the sector for 'car' is 144°. Estimate the number of the town's 18 500 residents who travel to work by car. A calculator may be used.","a":"7400","sol":"The sector angle as a fraction of the full turn gives the proportion travelling by car: 144/360 = 0.4, or 2/5 (M1). Applying that proportion to the whole town gives 0.4 × 18 500 (M1). The estimate is 7400 residents (A1). The sample size of 250 does not enter the calculation, because the pie chart already shows the proportion.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["The pie chart describes the people who were asked, but the question asks about the whole town.","Turn the sector angle into a proportion of the full circle, then apply that proportion to the town's population.","Your first line is the sector angle written as a fraction of a full turn."],"codes":["S1","S2"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The six salaries at a small firm are £18 000, £19 000, £19 000, £20 000, £21 000 and £96 000. The mean salary is £32 166.67, correct to the nearest penny. Work out how much greater the mean salary is than the median salary.","a":"£12 666.67","sol":"The salaries are already in order, and with six values the middle two are the 3rd and the 4th, £19 000 and £20 000, so the median = (19 000 + 20 000) ÷ 2 = £19 500 (M1). Take the median from the mean: 32 166.67 − 19 500 (M1). The mean is £12 666.67 greater than the median (A1). The single salary of £96 000 is an outlier, which is why the mean sits above five of the six salaries while the median stays among them.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["One of the two averages in this comparison has already been worked out for you; the other has not.","Find the median of the six salaries, then compare it with the mean by subtracting.","Your first line is the six salaries written in order, with the third and the fourth marked."],"codes":["S4","S5"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Work out the median of 3, 5, 6, 8, 9, 11, 14, 20.","a":"8.5"},{"q":"In a sample of 50 club members, 20 are left-handed. Estimate how many of the club's 400 members are left-handed.","a":"160"},{"q":"On a scatter graph the points fall from the top-left to the bottom-right. Write down the type of correlation.","a":"Negative correlation"},{"q":"Work out the pie chart angle for a group of 7 people out of a total of 40 people.","a":"63°"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Population and sample","back":"The population is everyone or everything being studied. A sample is the part of it actually looked at, used to estimate a property of the whole population.","code":"S1","open":false},{"front":"Give one limitation of using a sample to estimate a property of a whole population","back":"The sample may be too small, so the estimate is unreliable; the sample may not be chosen fairly, so it is biased and does not represent the population; the sample may be taken at one time or place only.","code":"S1","open":true},{"front":"Pie chart sector angle","back":"Angle = (frequency ÷ total frequency) × 360°. One item is worth 360° ÷ total. The sector angles always add up to 360°.","code":"S2","open":false},{"front":"Vertical line chart and time series line graph","back":"A vertical line chart shows ungrouped discrete numerical data: a thin line at each value, its height the frequency. A line graph joins points in time order to show a trend over time.","code":"S2","open":false},{"front":"Bar chart and pictogram","back":"Both display categorical data. A bar chart uses equal-width bars with gaps and a labelled frequency scale. A pictogram needs a key saying what one whole symbol represents.","code":"S2","open":false},{"front":"Mean","back":"Mean = total of the values ÷ how many values. From a frequency table, mean = total of (value × frequency) ÷ total frequency. It uses every value, so an outlier distorts it.","code":"S4","open":false},{"front":"Median","back":"The middle value once the data is in order. For n values it is in position (n + 1)/2; with an even n it is halfway between the two middle values. An outlier barely affects it.","code":"S4","open":false},{"front":"Mode and modal class","back":"The mode is the value that appears most often; a set can have no mode or several. For grouped data you quote the modal class: the class with the highest frequency.","code":"S4","open":false},{"front":"Range","back":"Range = largest value − smallest value. It measures spread: a smaller range means the data is more consistent. It uses only the two extreme values, so an outlier inflates it.","code":"S4","open":false},{"front":"Outlier","back":"A value far away from the rest of the data. It inflates the range and drags the mean towards itself, so the median usually describes such data better.","code":"S4","open":false},{"front":"Estimate of the mean from a grouped frequency table","back":"Estimated mean = total of (midpoint × frequency) ÷ total frequency. It is only an estimate because grouping loses the exact values.","code":"S4","open":false},{"front":"Choosing the average that suits the data","back":"Mean when the values are fairly even, because it uses them all; median when there is an outlier or a lopsided set; mode for categorical data or the most popular value.","code":"S5","open":false},{"front":"Comparing two distributions","back":"Quote one average and one measure of spread, and say what each means in context: which set is typically higher, and which set varies less. A bare pair of numbers scores nothing.","code":"S5","open":false},{"front":"Correlation","back":"A relationship between two variables on a scatter graph: positive (both rise), negative (one rises as the other falls) or none. Correlation does not indicate causation — a third factor may cause both.","code":"S6","open":false},{"front":"Line of best fit, interpolation and extrapolation","back":"A straight line following the trend with roughly equal numbers of points each side. Reading inside the data is interpolation and is reliable; reading outside it is extrapolation and may be wrong.","code":"S6","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Preamble: the Exam Aid formulae sheet printed as an insert with every Foundation tier 1MA1 paper gives only the eight results listed next, and not one of them belongs to this room, so every statistical result marked Remember below has to be written from memory.","Given on the Exam Aid: area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, where a and b are the parallel sides and h their perpendicular separation.","Given on the Exam Aid: volume of a prism = area of cross section × length.","Given on the Exam Aid: circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d the diameter.","Given on the Exam Aid: area of a circle = πr².","Given on the Exam Aid: Pythagoras' theorem a² + b² = c², in a right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is c.","Given on the Exam Aid: sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b, in a right-angled triangle ABC whose hypotenuse is c.","Given on the Exam Aid: compound interest total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, where P is the principal, r the interest rate per period and n the number of periods.","Given on the Exam Aid: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B).","Remember: mean = total of the values ÷ how many values there are — no average is printed on the Exam Aid.","Remember: mean from a frequency table = total of (value × frequency) ÷ total frequency.","Remember: estimated mean of grouped data = total of (midpoint of class × frequency) ÷ total frequency, the midpoint being (lower boundary + upper boundary) ÷ 2.","Remember: median = the middle value of the ordered data, in position (n + 1)/2; with an even number of values it is halfway between the two middle ones.","Remember: mode = the value occurring most often; modal class = the class with the highest frequency.","Remember: range = largest value − smallest value.","Remember: pie chart sector angle = (frequency ÷ total frequency) × 360°, and one item is worth 360° ÷ total frequency.","Remember: estimating a population figure from a sample — population estimate = (number in the sample with the property ÷ sample size) × population size.","Remember: a comparison of two data sets quotes one average and one measure of spread, both interpreted in the context of the data and with units.","Remember: correlation does not indicate causation; a prediction inside the range of the data is interpolation, and one outside it is extrapolation and cannot be relied on."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T12:09:34Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Every answer key, mark tally, worked example and Formulae sheet claim checks out against the Foundation Exam Aid, so this is publishable as it stands — but renumber the sample question (4), whose answer is spelled out in the lesson, and the grouped-mean question (6), which repeats the dogs example's midpoints."}}},"number-structure-higher":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"number-structure-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"The Higher papers take everything from the Foundation number room for granted and then add four techniques that only Higher students are assessed on: counting by multiplying instead of listing, estimating a power or a root, fractional indices, and surds. Number is 12–18% of the Higher marks, but these four turn up far beyond the number questions — a surd falls out of Pythagoras, a fractional index out of a growth formula, an exact circle answer out of mensuration. Paper 1 is non-calculator, so an answer such as 6√3 or 2⁶ is what earns the accuracy mark and a rounded decimal does not. They are also cheap marks: each is a short, rule-driven method that can be practised until it is automatic.","idea":[{"h":"The product rule for counting","t":"At Foundation you list possibilities in a system; at Higher you count them without writing the list. If one task can be done in m ways and, for each of those, a second task can be done in n ways, the two tasks together can be done in m × n ways, and the rule extends stage by stage — 4 shirts, 3 ties and 2 jackets give 4 × 3 × 2 = 24 outfits. When an item cannot be used twice the number of choices drops by one at each stage, because the first choice has used one up. Codes, menus, registration plates and seating are the usual contexts, and the method mark is for the product written down, not for the total alone.","code":"N5"},{"h":"Estimating powers and roots","t":"To estimate a root, trap the number between the two nearest known powers. √70 lies between 8 and 9 because 64 < 70 < 81, and since 70 is much nearer 64 the root is nearer 8. The same works for cube roots using 1, 8, 27, 64, 125, 216, 343, 512, 729 and 1000. To estimate a power, round the base to a value whose power you know: 4.9³ is a little under 5³ = 125. The command word Estimate demands that the squares or cubes used are written down — a bare answer scores nothing, even when it is right.","code":"N6"},{"h":"Fractional indices","t":"In a fractional index the denominator is the root and the numerator is the power: xᵐ⁄ⁿ = (ⁿ√x)ᵐ. So 8²⁄³ is the cube root of 8, squared, which is 2² = 4. Always take the root first; going the other way (∛64) is the same answer by a harder route. A negative index takes the reciprocal as well, so 16⁻¹⁄² = 1/16¹⁄² = 1/4, and x¹⁄² = √x. With a fraction as the base, the root and the power act on the numerator and the denominator separately. On Paper 1 the exact value, as a whole number or a fraction in its lowest terms, is what is credited.","code":"N7"},{"h":"Surds and rationalising denominators","t":"A surd is a root that cannot be written exactly as a fraction, so it is left in the answer rather than rounded. Simplify by taking out the largest square factor: √12 = √(4 × 3) = √4 × √3 = 2√3. Like surds then collect like terms, and √a × √a = a turns a product into a whole number. A denominator is never left with a surd in it: for a single surd multiply top and bottom by that surd; for two terms multiply by the conjugate, because (a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b has no surd left. 'Simplify fully' means a partly simplified answer scores no accuracy mark.","code":"N8"}],"examples":[{"title":"Counting lunch deals with the product rule","setup":"A café lunch deal is one sandwich, one drink and one cake. There are 5 different sandwiches, 4 different drinks and 3 different cakes, and every combination is allowed. Work out how many different lunch deals a customer can make.","steps":["The deal is built in three stages — sandwich, then drink, then cake — and every combination is allowed, so the choices can be multiplied instead of listed.","Multiply the first two stages: 5 sandwiches × 4 drinks = 20 different sandwich-and-drink pairs.","Each of those 20 pairs can be finished with any of the 3 cakes, so the total is 20 × 3.","20 × 3 = 60, so there are 60 different lunch deals."],"cues":["Does this need a full list, or can the choices be multiplied?","How many sandwich-and-drink pairs are there?","How many cakes can finish each of those pairs?","What is the total number of deals?"]},{"title":"Estimating the side of a square patio","setup":"A square patio is to have an area of 58 m². The builder has no calculator and needs the side length to the nearest metre. Using square numbers only, work out which whole number of metres the side length is closest to.","steps":["The side of a square is the square root of its area, so the length wanted is √58 metres.","The square numbers on either side are 7² = 49 and 8² = 64, and 49 < 58 < 64, so the side lies between 7 m and 8 m.","Compare the gaps: 58 − 49 = 9 but 64 − 58 = 6, so 58 sits nearer 64 than 49.","The side is therefore nearer 8 m than 7 m, so to the nearest metre it is 8 m."],"cues":["What turns an area into a side length here?","Which square numbers sit either side of 58?","Which of those two squares is 58 nearer to?","So which whole number of metres is the answer?"]},{"title":"Working out a fractional power","setup":"Work out the exact value of 32³⁄⁵ without a calculator, showing both stages of the working.","steps":["The denominator of the index says which root to take, so this is the fifth root of 32, then raised to a power.","2⁵ = 32, so the fifth root of 32 is 2 and 32¹⁄⁵ = 2.","The numerator of the index says which power to apply, so cube that root: 32³⁄⁵ = 2³.","2³ = 8, so 32³⁄⁵ = 8."],"cues":["What does the number on the bottom of the index tell you to do?","Which whole number has a fifth power of 32?","What does the number on the top of the index tell you to do?","What is the exact value?"]},{"title":"Simplifying a surd before multiplying","setup":"Work out the exact value of √48 × √3, giving your answer as a whole number. Simplify √48 fully first by taking out its largest square factor.","steps":["The largest square factor of 48 is 16, because 48 = 16 × 3 and 16 is a square number.","Split the root over that product: √48 = √16 × √3 = 4√3.","The calculation is now 4√3 × √3, and a square root multiplied by itself gives the number back, so √3 × √3 = 3.","So 4√3 × √3 = 4 × 3 = 12."],"cues":["Which square number divides into 48?","What is √48 in its simplest surd form?","What does √3 × √3 come to?","What whole number does the calculation give?"]},{"title":"Clearing a two-term surd from a denominator","setup":"The fraction 14/(4 + √2) has a surd in its denominator. Rationalise the denominator and simplify your answer fully.","steps":["The denominator has two terms, so multiply the numerator and the denominator by its conjugate, 4 − √2.","The denominator becomes (4 + √2)(4 − √2) = 16 − 4√2 + 4√2 − 2; the surd terms cancel, leaving 16 − 2 = 14.","The numerator becomes 14(4 − √2), so the fraction is now 14(4 − √2)/14.","The factor of 14 cancels top and bottom, leaving 4 − √2."],"cues":["What should the top and bottom be multiplied by?","What happens to the two surd terms in the denominator?","What does the numerator become?","What cancels, and what is left?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A lock has three dials, and each dial can show any digit from 0 to 9. How many different settings does the lock have?","a":"1000, from 10 × 10 × 10"},{"q":"Simplify √98 fully.","a":"7√2"},{"q":"Work out 64¹⁄³.","a":"4"},{"q":"Between which two consecutive whole numbers does √30 lie?","a":"5 and 6"},{"q":"Rationalise the denominator of 10/√5.","a":"2√5"}]},"room":{"facts":["The product rule for counting: m ways of doing one task and, for each of those, n ways of doing another give m × n ways altogether; multiply the choices at every stage.","xᵐ⁄ⁿ = (ⁿ√x)ᵐ — the denominator of the index is the root, the numerator is the power; x¹⁄² = √x and x¹⁄³ = ∛x.","x⁻ⁿ = 1/xⁿ and x⁰ = 1, so a negative index means take the reciprocal as well: 16⁻¹⁄² = 1/√16 = 1/4.","√a × √b = √(ab), √a × √a = a and √a ÷ √b = √(a/b); √(a²b) = a√b is the rule behind simplifying a surd.","To rationalise 1/√a multiply top and bottom by √a; for a two-term denominator use the conjugate, since (a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b.","The squares to 15² = 225 and the cubes 1, 8, 27, 64, 125, 216, 343, 512, 729, 1000 are the landmarks an estimated root is trapped between."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify √50 fully.","a":"5√2","sol":"Take out the largest square factor: 50 = 25 × 2, so √50 = √25 × √2 (M1). √25 = 5, so √50 = 5√2 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A surd is simplified by finding a square number hidden inside it.","Look for the largest square number that divides into 50; the root can then be split over the two factors.","Write √50 as √a × √b, where a is the largest square factor of 50."],"codes":["N8"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out the value of 27²⁄³.","a":"9","sol":"The denominator of the index is the root: ∛27 = 3 (M1). The numerator of the index is the power, so square it: 3² = 9 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The two numbers in a fractional index do two different jobs.","The bottom of the index says which root to take and the top says which power to apply; taking the root first keeps the numbers small.","Take the cube root of 27 first, then apply the power on the top of the index to what you get."],"codes":["N7"]},{"q":"A ferry company runs 7 different morning sailings from a harbour to an island and 8 different evening sailings back. A day trip is one morning sailing out and one evening sailing back. Work out how many different day trips are possible. A calculator may be used.","a":"56","sol":"By the product rule for counting there are 7 × 8 = 56 different day trips (B1).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Every morning sailing can be paired with every evening sailing, so nothing needs listing.","The product rule for counting: m ways for one task and n ways for the other give m × n ways altogether.","Multiply the number of morning sailings by the number of evening sailings."],"codes":["N5"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) √70 lies between two consecutive whole numbers. Write down those two numbers.","a":"8 and 9","sol":"The square numbers either side of 70 are 8² = 64 and 9² = 81 (M1). Since 64 < 70 < 81, √70 lies between 8 and 9 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Square numbers are the landmarks a square root is judged against.","Find the square number just below 70 and the square number just above it; their roots trap √70.","Write down the square numbers on either side of 70."],"codes":["N6"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Rationalise the denominator of 6/√2, giving your answer in its simplest form.","a":"3√2","sol":"Multiply numerator and denominator by √2: 6/√2 = 6√2/(√2 × √2) = 6√2/2 (M1). Cancelling the 2 gives 3√2 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A denominator should never be left with a surd in it.","A surd multiplied by itself gives a whole number, so multiply the top and the bottom of the fraction by that same surd.","Multiply the numerator and the denominator by √2, then simplify the fraction that results."],"codes":["N8"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out the value of 49¹⁄² + 16⁻¹⁄², giving your answer as a mixed number.","a":"7 1/4","sol":"49¹⁄² = √49 = 7 (B1). A negative index takes the reciprocal, so 16⁻¹⁄² = 1/16¹⁄² = 1/√16 (M1). √16 = 4, so 16⁻¹⁄² = 1/4 and the total is 7 + 1/4 = 7 1/4 (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Deal with the two powers one at a time before adding anything.","An index of ¹⁄² is a square root, and a minus sign in the index means take the reciprocal of the result.","Write 49¹⁄² as a square root, and write 16⁻¹⁄² as 1 divided by 16¹⁄², then work each one out."],"codes":["N7"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify fully √75 + √27 − √12.","a":"6√3","sol":"√75 = √25 × √3 = 5√3 (M1). In the same way √27 = √9 × √3 = 3√3 and √12 = √4 × √3 = 2√3 (A1). These are now like surds, so 5√3 + 3√3 − 2√3 = 6√3 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The three surds look different, but each one hides the same smaller surd.","Simplify each surd by taking out its largest square factor; they then become like terms that can be collected.","Write each of √75, √27 and √12 as √a × √b, with a the largest square factor in each case."],"codes":["N8"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Expand and simplify (3 + √5)(2 − √5), giving your answer in the form a + b√5 where a and b are integers.","a":"1 − √5","sol":"Multiply each term in the first bracket by each term in the second: 3 × 2 = 6, 3 × (−√5) = −3√5, √5 × 2 = 2√5 and √5 × (−√5) = −5 (M1). This gives 6 − 3√5 + 2√5 − 5 (A1). Collecting, 6 − 5 = 1 and −3√5 + 2√5 = −√5, so the answer is 1 − √5 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This is an ordinary expansion of two brackets; the surd behaves like any other term.","Multiply each term in the first bracket by each term in the second, remembering that √5 × √5 is a whole number.","Write out all four products from the expansion, then collect the whole numbers and the surd terms separately."],"codes":["N8"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Rationalise the denominator of 1/(3 − √5), giving your answer in its simplest form.","a":"(3 + √5)/4","sol":"Multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator (M1). The denominator becomes (3 − √5)(3 + √5) = 9 + 3√5 − 3√5 − 5 = 4 (A1). The numerator becomes 1 × (3 + √5), so the fraction is (3 + √5)/4 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Multiplying a two-term denominator by the right partner clears the surd out of it.","Use the conjugate: (a − √b)(a + √b) contains no surd, because the two middle terms cancel.","Multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of 3 − √5, then expand the new denominator."],"codes":["N8"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out the value of (16/81)³⁄⁴, giving your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"8/27","sol":"The denominator of the index is the root, and a root of a fraction acts on the top and the bottom separately (M1). Since 2⁴ = 16 and 3⁴ = 81, (16/81)¹⁄⁴ = 2/3 (A1). The numerator of the index is the power, so cube it: (2/3)³ = 8/27 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A root or a power of a fraction acts on the numerator and the denominator separately.","The bottom of the index gives the root and the top gives the power; take the root first so the numbers stay small.","Find the fourth root of 16 and the fourth root of 81, then apply the power from the top of the index."],"codes":["N7"]},{"q":"A car park ticket code is made of 2 letters followed by 3 digits. Each letter is any of the 26 capital letters and each digit is any of the digits 0 to 9; letters and digits may be repeated. Work out how many different codes are possible. A calculator may be used.","a":"676 000","sol":"Each of the five positions is a separate choice, so by the product rule the total is 26 × 26 × 10 × 10 × 10 (M1) = 676 000 (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Each position in the code is its own choice, and choices multiply.","Count how many possibilities each of the five positions has, then apply the product rule across all five.","Write the calculation as 26 × 26 × … , with one factor for every position in the code."],"codes":["N5"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Estimate the value of 2.9³ + √80. Round 2.9 to the nearest whole number, and use the nearer of the two square numbers on either side of 80.","a":"36","sol":"2.9 rounds to 3, and 3³ = 27 (M1). The square numbers either side of 80 are 8² = 64 and 9² = 81, and 80 is nearer 81, so √80 is about √81 = 9 (M1). The estimate is 27 + 9 = 36 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Neither part has to be worked out exactly — each one sits close to a power that is already known.","Round the base of the cube to a whole number and cube it, and trap 80 between the two square numbers on either side, taking the root of the closer one.","Write down the cube of the rounded base, and write down the square numbers just below and just above 80."],"codes":["N6"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Here are four numbers: √72, 6, ∛150 and 8.5. Write the four numbers in order of size, starting with the smallest. You must show how you decided.","a":"∛150, 6, √72, 8.5","sol":"8² = 64 and 9² = 81, and 64 < 72 < 81, so √72 lies between 8 and 9 and is bigger than 6 (M1). 8.5² = 72.25, which is bigger than 72, so √72 < 8.5 (M1). 5³ = 125 and 6³ = 216, and 125 < 150 < 216, so ∛150 lies between 5 and 6 and is smaller than 6 (M1). The order, smallest first, is ∛150, 6, √72, 8.5 (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Neither root has to be worked out exactly — each one only has to be placed against the whole numbers and decimals already in the list.","Trap √72 between the two square numbers on either side of it and ∛150 between the two cube numbers on either side of it, then square 8.5 to settle the last comparison.","Write down the square numbers just below and just above 72, the cube numbers just below and just above 150, and the square of 8.5."],"codes":["N6"]},{"q":"A password is 4 characters long. The first two characters are capital letters chosen from the 26 letters of the alphabet and the last two characters are digits chosen from 0 to 9. No letter may be used twice and no digit may be used twice. Work out how many different passwords are possible. A calculator may be used.","a":"58 500","sol":"The first letter can be chosen in 26 ways, and because it cannot be repeated the second letter can be chosen in 25 ways (M1). The first digit can be chosen in 10 ways and the second in 9 ways (M1). By the product rule the total is 26 × 25 × 10 × 9 (M1) = 58 500 (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Each character that is used up changes what is left for the next one.","Count the possibilities position by position, dropping the count by one where a character cannot be repeated, then multiply.","Write down how many choices there are for the first letter and then for the second letter, and do the same for the two digits."],"codes":["N5"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Rationalise the denominator of (√5 + 2)/(√5 − 2), giving your answer in the form a + b√5 where a and b are integers.","a":"9 + 4√5","sol":"Multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator, √5 + 2 (M1). The denominator becomes (√5 − 2)(√5 + 2) = 5 + 2√5 − 2√5 − 4 = 1 (A1). The numerator becomes (√5 + 2)² = 5 + 2√5 + 2√5 + 4 = 9 + 4√5 (M1). Dividing by 1 leaves 9 + 4√5 (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The denominator has two terms, so multiplying top and bottom by a single surd will not clear it.","Rationalise the denominator with the conjugate, then expand the numerator, which becomes the square of a two-term surd.","Multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of √5 − 2, then expand the new denominator."],"codes":["N8"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out the value of 8²⁄³ × 4⁻¹⁄² × 2⁵, giving your answer as a power of 2.","a":"2⁶","sol":"∛8 = 2, so 8²⁄³ = 2² (B1). √4 = 2, so 4⁻¹⁄² = 1/2 = 2⁻¹ (B1). Multiplying powers of the same base adds the indices: 2 + (−1) + 5 = 6 (M1). So the value is 2⁶ (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Every base in this product is itself a power of 2, so the whole calculation can be written in one base.","Write 8 and 4 as powers of 2, apply the fractional indices to them, then add the indices when the powers are multiplied.","Work out 8²⁄³ and 4⁻¹⁄² separately as powers of 2 before combining the three terms."],"codes":["N7"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Simplify √32 fully.","a":"4√2"},{"q":"Work out the value of 125¹⁄³.","a":"5"},{"q":"A spinner has 4 different colours and an ordinary dice has 6 faces. The spinner is spun once and the dice is rolled once. How many different colour-and-number outcomes are possible?","a":"24"},{"q":"√40 lies between two consecutive whole numbers. Write down the smaller of those two numbers.","a":"6"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The product rule for counting","back":"If one task can be done in m ways and, for each of those, a second task in n ways, the two together can be done in m × n ways. Extra stages multiply on in the same way.","code":"N5","open":false},{"front":"Counting when an item cannot be repeated","back":"Each choice uses one item up, so the number of ways falls by one at each stage: 5 different books in a row give 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 120 orders.","code":"N5","open":false},{"front":"Name two contexts where the product rule replaces a full list","back":"A code or password built position by position; a menu where one item is chosen from each course; a registration plate of letters then digits.","code":"N5","open":true},{"front":"Estimating a square root","back":"Trap the number between the two nearest square numbers; the root lies between their roots and is nearer the root of the closer square. 100 < 110 < 121, so √110 is between 10 and 11.","code":"N6","open":false},{"front":"The cube numbers used for estimating a cube root","back":"1, 8, 27, 64, 125, 216, 343, 512, 729 and 1000. Trap the number between two of them and the cube root lies between the matching whole numbers.","code":"N6","open":false},{"front":"Estimating a power of a decimal","back":"Round the base to a value whose power is known, then say which side the true value falls: 3.1³ is a little more than 3³ = 27. The rounding used must be written down.","code":"N6","open":false},{"front":"The meaning of a fractional index","back":"xᵐ⁄ⁿ = (ⁿ√x)ᵐ: the denominator of the index is the root and the numerator is the power. Take the root first to keep the numbers small.","code":"N7","open":false},{"front":"x¹⁄² and x¹⁄³","back":"x¹⁄² = √x and x¹⁄³ = ∛x, so 36¹⁄² = 6 and 27¹⁄³ = 3.","code":"N7","open":false},{"front":"A negative index","back":"x⁻ⁿ = 1/xⁿ, so the minus sign means take the reciprocal as well: 16⁻¹⁄² = 1/16¹⁄² = 1/4. Also x⁰ = 1.","code":"N7","open":false},{"front":"Simplifying a surd","back":"Take out the largest square factor and split the root over the product: √12 = √(4 × 3) = √4 × √3 = 2√3. Only a fully simplified answer earns the accuracy mark.","code":"N8","open":false},{"front":"The rules for multiplying and dividing surds","back":"√a × √b = √(ab), √a ÷ √b = √(a/b) and √a × √a = a, so √3 × √6 = √18 = 3√2.","code":"N8","open":false},{"front":"Rationalising a denominator that is a single surd","back":"Multiply the numerator and the denominator by that surd, because the surd multiplied by itself is a whole number: 8/√2 = 8√2/2 = 4√2.","code":"N8","open":false},{"front":"The conjugate of a + √b","back":"a − √b. Their product (a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b contains no surd, which is what clears a two-term denominator.","code":"N8","open":false},{"front":"Name two ways of rationalising a denominator","back":"Multiply top and bottom by the single surd in the denominator, as with 8/√2; multiply top and bottom by the conjugate when the denominator has two terms, as with 1/(4 − √3).","code":"N8","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Nothing used in this room is printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: the product rule for counting, the laws of indices, the surd rules and the method for rationalising a denominator are not on the sheet, so remember them.","Remember: xᵐ⁄ⁿ = (ⁿ√x)ᵐ — the denominator of the index is the root and the numerator is the power (N7).","Remember: x⁻ⁿ = 1/xⁿ and x⁰ = 1, so a negative index takes the reciprocal as well (N7).","Remember: √a × √b = √(ab), √a × √a = a and √a ÷ √b = √(a/b) (N8).","Remember: √(a²b) = a√b — the rule behind simplifying a surd by its largest square factor, as in √12 = 2√3 (N8).","Remember: (a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b — the conjugate product that clears a two-term surd from a denominator (N8).","Remember: the product rule for counting — m ways of doing one task and, for each, n ways of doing another give m × n ways (N5).","Remember: the square numbers to 15² = 225 and the cube numbers to 10³ = 1000, the landmarks an estimated root is trapped between (N6).","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid, though not needed in this room: area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, volume of a prism = area of cross section × length, circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd, area of a circle = πr², the quadratic formula, a² + b² = c², the three trigonometric ratios, the sine and cosine rules, area of a triangle = ½ab sin C, total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B) and P(A and B) = P(A given B)P(B)."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T12:07:16Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Mathematically clean - every answer key, solution line, mark tally (each sol totals exactly its m), card and the Higher Exam Aid formulae claims check out, so the only work needed before publishing is to re-set question 2 (and ideally question 12) in a context that does not repeat a worked example."}}},"number-fdp-higher":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"number-fdp-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Every fraction you meet turns into a decimal that either stops or repeats for ever, and at Higher tier you are expected to move both ways between the two. Recurring decimals are one of the few places where the exam asks you to prove a conversion rather than press a button, so the multiply-and-subtract line is worth marks in itself and often appears as a 'show that' on Paper 1, where no calculator is allowed. The skill also settles arguments you meet elsewhere in the course: whether two quantities really are equal, how to take an exact share of an amount when the share is written as a recurring decimal, and why a denominator with a factor of 3, 7 or 11 can never give a decimal that stops. Knowing the test for a terminating decimal saves you from long fruitless division in the exam.","idea":[{"h":"Recurring decimals into fractions","code":"N10 (i)","t":"A recurring decimal repeats a fixed block of digits for ever, so multiplying by the right power of 10 produces a number with exactly the same tail. Subtract the original and the tail cancels, leaving whole numbers. Call the decimal x, count the digits in the repeating block, and multiply by 10 for one digit, 100 for two, 1000 for three. For 0.3̇9̇: x = 0.393939..., 100x = 39.393939..., so 99x = 39 and x = 39/99 = 13/33. If some digits come before the block, shift past them first: for 0.25̇ take 10x = 2.555... and 100x = 25.555..., so 90x = 23 and x = 23/90. The examiner wants the two lines and the subtraction written down, because a 'show that' question gives you the answer and marks only the method; always cancel to lowest terms at the end."},{"h":"Fractions into recurring decimals","code":"N10 (ii)","t":"Going the other way is division: a/b means a divided by b, and you keep bringing down zeros. The remainders can only be 0, 1, 2, ..., b − 1, so within b steps either a remainder of 0 appears and the decimal stops, or a remainder repeats and from there the digits cycle for ever. That is why every fraction gives a terminating or a recurring decimal and nothing else. You can tell which without dividing: cancel to lowest terms, then look at the prime factors of the denominator — only 2s and 5s means it terminates, anything else means it recurs. So 21/40 terminates because 40 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 5, giving 0.525, while 10/27 recurs because 27 = 3 × 3 × 3, giving 0.3̇70̇. Mark the block with a dot over its first and last digit; a missing dot loses the accuracy mark."}],"examples":[{"title":"A two-digit block turned into a fraction","setup":"Change the recurring decimal 0.4̇5̇ into a fraction, giving your answer in its lowest terms.","steps":["The dots in 0.4̇5̇ sit over both digits, so the block 45 repeats: write x = 0.454545...","The block is two digits long, so multiply by 100 to shift it exactly one block along: 100x = 45.454545...","Subtract the first line from the second: 100x − x = 45.454545... − 0.454545..., so 99x = 45.","Divide by 99 and cancel by 9: x = 45/99, and 45 ÷ 9 = 5 with 99 ÷ 9 = 11, so the fraction is 5/11."],"cues":["How many digits are in the block that repeats?","Which power of 10 shifts the decimal along by exactly one block?","What happens to the two recurring tails when one line is taken from the other?","What is the highest common factor of 45 and 99?"]},{"title":"The same fraction turned back into a decimal","setup":"Use division to write 5/11 as a decimal, and write your answer using dot notation.","steps":["Set out 5.000000 divided by 11: 11 does not go into 5, so the units figure is 0 and the 5 is carried into the tenths column to make 50.","11 into 50 goes 4 times with 6 left over, so the first decimal digit is 4 and the remainder 6 is carried.","11 into 60 goes 5 times with 5 left over, so the next digit is 5 — and that remainder 5 is the one we started with, so the digits must now cycle for ever.","The quotient is 0.454545..., a block of two repeating digits, each of which takes a dot: 0.4̇5̇."],"cues":["How many times does 11 go into 5, and what is carried across?","How many elevens fit into 50, and what is left over?","Why does a repeated remainder mean the digits repeat?","Where do the dots go when the block is two digits long?"]},{"title":"A decimal where only the last digit recurs","setup":"Write the recurring decimal 0.28̇ as a fraction in its lowest terms.","steps":["Only the 8 in 0.28̇ carries a dot, so just that digit repeats: x = 0.2888...","Shift past the 2, which does not repeat, by multiplying by 10: 10x = 2.888...","Shift one more place so the two recurring tails line up: 100x = 28.888...","Subtract: 100x − 10x = 28.888... − 2.888..., so 90x = 26 and x = 26/90 = 13/45."],"cues":["Which digit does the dot sit on, and which digit does not repeat?","How many places must you shift to get past the digits that do not repeat?","Why multiply by 10 a second time rather than by 100 straight away?","Which two lines have identical tails, so are worth subtracting?"]},{"title":"Deciding which fractions recur","setup":"Three fractions are written on a board: 7/40, 13/25 and 7/15. Each is already in its lowest terms. Decide which of them give terminating decimals and which give recurring decimals, and write each one as a decimal.","steps":["Break each denominator into prime factors: 40 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 5, 25 = 5 × 5, and 15 = 3 × 5.","A fraction in its lowest terms stops only if its denominator has no prime factor except 2 and 5, so 7/40 and 13/25 terminate, while 15 has a factor of 3, so 7/15 recurs.","Divide for the two that stop: 7 ÷ 40 = 0.175 and 13 ÷ 25 = 0.52.","Divide for the last one: 7 ÷ 15 = 0.4666..., in which only the 6 repeats, so it is written 0.46̇."],"cues":["What are the prime factors of each denominator?","Which prime factors are allowed in the denominator of a decimal that stops?","How do you turn a fraction into its decimal once you know it terminates?","Which digits in the last quotient repeat, and which does not?"]},{"title":"A three-digit repeating block","setup":"Change the recurring decimal 0.2̇16̇ into a fraction, giving your answer in its lowest terms.","steps":["The dots on the 2 and the 6 mark a block of three digits in 0.2̇16̇, so x = 0.216216216...","A block of three digits needs a shift of three places: 1000x = 216.216216...","Subtract the original: 1000x − x = 216, so 999x = 216 and x = 216/999.","Both parts divide by 27: 216 ÷ 27 = 8 and 999 ÷ 27 = 37, so the fraction is 8/37."],"cues":["Which digits lie between the two dots, and how many are there?","Which power of 10 moves the decimal on by one whole block?","What is left once the recurring tails cancel?","What is the highest common factor of 216 and 999?"]},{"title":"A recurring share of a quantity","setup":"A bag contains 220 counters. Exactly 0.2̇7̇ of the counters are red and the rest are blue. Work out how many red counters are in the bag.","steps":["The dots in 0.2̇7̇ mean the block 27 repeats, so let x = 0.272727... and shift two places: 100x = 27.272727...","Subtracting removes the tail: 100x − x = 27, so 99x = 27 and x = 27/99.","Cancel by 9: 27 ÷ 9 = 3 and 99 ÷ 9 = 11, so the red counters are exactly 3/11 of the 220 counters.","One eleventh of the bag is 220 ÷ 11 = 20 counters, so 3/11 of the bag is 3 × 20 = 60 red counters."],"cues":["How long is the block that repeats, and what does that tell you to multiply by?","What is left on the right-hand side once the two tails cancel?","What does the cancelled fraction now act on?","How many counters make up one eleventh of the bag?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write 0.5̇ as a fraction in its lowest terms.","a":"5/9"},{"q":"Write 0.8̇1̇ as a fraction in its lowest terms.","a":"9/11"},{"q":"Write 1/6 as a decimal, using dot notation.","a":"0.16̇"},{"q":"Does 7/20 give a terminating or a recurring decimal? Give a reason.","a":"Terminating: 20 = 2 × 2 × 5 has no prime factor other than 2 or 5 (7/20 = 0.35)."},{"q":"How many digits are in the repeating block of 0.3̇14̇?","a":"Three (the block 314 repeats)."}]},"room":{"facts":["A block of n repeating digits: let x be the decimal, multiply by 10ⁿ, subtract x, then divide — so 0.ȧḃ = ab/99, 0.ȧbċ = abc/999, and the fraction is then cancelled.","Digits before the block: shift past them first — 0.aḃ = (ab − a)/90, and in general the denominator is one 9 for each repeating digit followed by one 0 for each digit before the block.","Fraction to decimal: divide the numerator by the denominator; as soon as a remainder comes round again, the digits from that point repeat for ever.","Dot notation: one dot above a single repeating digit, and a dot above the first and last digit of a longer block — 0.4̇ = 0.444..., 0.4̇17̇ = 0.417417...","Test for a terminating decimal: cancel to lowest terms, then a decimal stops only if the denominator has no prime factor other than 2 or 5; any other prime factor makes it recur.","Every fraction gives either a terminating or a recurring decimal, and a denominator b can repeat a block of at most b − 1 digits.","0.9̇ = 1 exactly, because 10x − x = 9 gives 9x = 9."],"questions":[{"q":"Write the recurring decimal 0.6̇3̇ as a fraction in its lowest terms. (non-calculator)","a":"7/11","m":2,"d":1,"codes":["N10 (i)"],"sol":"Let x = 0.636363... The repeating block 63 is two digits long, so multiply by 100: 100x = 63.636363... Subtracting the original removes the tail: 100x − x = 63, so 99x = 63 and x = 63/99. M1 for 99x = 63 or for 63/99 seen. Both parts divide by 9: 63 ÷ 9 = 7 and 99 ÷ 9 = 11, so x = 7/11. A1 for 7/11 (63/99 left uncancelled scores only the method mark).","hints":["The dots tell you how long the block that repeats is.","Multiply the decimal by the power of 10 that shifts it along by exactly one block, then subtract the original so the tails cancel.","The first line is x = 0.636363..., and the second line is 100x = ..."]},{"q":"Write 5/12 as a decimal. Use dot notation in your answer. (non-calculator)","a":"0.416̇","m":2,"d":1,"codes":["N10 (ii)"],"sol":"5/12 means 5 divided by 12. Short division of 5.0000 by 12: 12 into 50 goes 4 remainder 2; 12 into 20 goes 1 remainder 8; 12 into 80 goes 6 remainder 8, and that remainder 8 has come round again. M1 for the division reaching 0.4166... So only the 6 repeats: 5/12 = 0.416̇. A1 for 0.416̇ with a single dot on the 6 (0.4166... written without a dot scores only the method mark).","hints":["The fraction line is a division sign.","Divide the numerator by the denominator, bringing down zeros, and watch the remainders until one comes round again.","The first line is 5.0000 divided by 12, taken one digit at a time."]},{"q":"Write the recurring decimal 0.2̇4̇ as a fraction in its lowest terms. (non-calculator)","a":"8/33","m":2,"d":1,"codes":["N10 (i)"],"sol":"Let x = 0.242424... The block 24 is two digits long, so 100x = 24.242424... and 100x − x = 24, giving 99x = 24. M1 for 99x = 24 or for 24/99. Dividing both parts by 3 gives 24 ÷ 3 = 8 and 99 ÷ 3 = 33, so x = 8/33. A1 for 8/33.","hints":["A two-digit block needs a two-place shift.","Write the decimal as x, make a second line whose recurring tail matches, and subtract.","The first line is x = 0.242424..., with 100x written underneath it."]},{"q":"Write 4/15 as a decimal. Use dot notation in your answer. (a calculator may be used)","a":"0.26̇","m":2,"d":1,"codes":["N10 (ii)"],"sol":"4/15 means 4 divided by 15, so divide 4.0000 by 15 and follow the remainders: 15 into 40 goes 2 remainder 10; 15 into 100 goes 6 remainder 10, and that remainder 10 has come round again, so from there the 6 repeats for ever. M1 for the repeated remainder 10, or for the division written out far enough to show the 6 recurring (a calculator can be used to check the digits, but the returning remainder is what shows the block). The 2 is reached once and never again, so 4/15 = 0.26̇. A1 for 0.26̇ with a single dot on the 6.","hints":["Only the digits that keep coming back get a dot.","Divide the numerator by the denominator and look at which digits repeat from some point onwards.","The first line is 4 divided by 15, written out to several decimal places."]},{"q":"Write the recurring decimal 0.0̇6̇ as a fraction in its lowest terms. (non-calculator)","a":"2/33","m":2,"d":1,"codes":["N10 (i)"],"sol":"Let x = 0.060606... The repeating block is the two digits 06, so 100x = 6.060606... and 100x − x = 6, giving 99x = 6. M1 for 99x = 6 or for 6/99. Dividing both parts by 3 gives x = 2/33. A1 for 2/33.","hints":["A zero inside the block still counts as one of its digits.","Count the digits between the dots to choose the power of 10, then subtract the original decimal.","The first line is x = 0.060606..., and the shifted line comes next."]},{"q":"Write the recurring decimal 0.13̇ as a fraction in its lowest terms. (non-calculator)","a":"2/15","m":3,"d":2,"codes":["N10 (i)"],"sol":"Only the 3 carries a dot, so x = 0.13333... First shift past the 1, which does not repeat: 10x = 1.3333... M1 for 10x = 1.3333... Now shift one more place so the tails line up: 100x = 13.3333... M1 for 100x = 13.3333... Subtracting, 100x − 10x = 13 − 1, so 90x = 12 and x = 12/90 = 2/15 after dividing both parts by 6. A1 for 2/15.","hints":["Not every digit after the point repeats here.","Shift past the digits that do not repeat first, then shift again by the length of the block so two tails match.","The first line is 10x = 1.3333..., and a second shifted line is needed before any subtraction."]},{"q":"Show that the recurring decimal 0.1̇35̇ can be written as the fraction 5/37. (non-calculator)","a":"5/37","m":3,"d":2,"codes":["N10 (i)"],"sol":"Let x = 0.135135135... The block 135 is three digits long, so multiply by 1000: 1000x = 135.135135... M1 for the shifted line 1000x = 135.135135... Subtract the original: 1000x − x = 135, so 999x = 135 and x = 135/999. M1 for 999x = 135 or for 135/999. Both parts divide by 27: 135 ÷ 27 = 5 and 999 ÷ 27 = 37, so x = 5/37 as required. A1 for the complete chain, ending with the cancelling to 5/37 (the answer is given, so every line must be seen).","hints":["The answer is given, so every line of the method has to be written down.","Set the decimal equal to x, shift it by one whole block, subtract, then cancel the fraction you get.","The first line is x = 0.135135135..., with 1000x underneath it."]},{"q":"Write 4/7 as a decimal. Use dot notation in your answer. (a calculator may be used)","a":"0.5̇71428̇","m":2,"d":2,"codes":["N10 (ii)"],"sol":"4/7 means 4 divided by 7. Dividing 4.000000 by 7 gives the digits 5, 7, 1, 4, 2, 8 with remainders 5, 1, 3, 2, 6, 4 in turn, and that last remainder 4 is the one the division started from, so the six digits 571428 cycle from there. M1 for the remainder chain coming back to 4, or for the digits written out far enough to show the block 571428 beginning again (a calculator can be used to check them). A dot goes on the first and the last digit of the block, so 4/7 = 0.5̇71428̇. A1 for 0.5̇71428̇ with both dots correctly placed.","hints":["A calculator display shows the block more than once if you look carefully.","Divide until the digits start again from the beginning, then mark the block that repeats.","The first line is 4.000000 divided by 7, taken one digit at a time with the remainder written down at each step."]},{"q":"Priya says, 'The fraction 9/24 must give a recurring decimal, because 24 has a factor of 3.' Is Priya right? You must show your working. (non-calculator)","a":"No: 9/24 = 3/8 = 0.375, which terminates","m":3,"d":2,"codes":["N10 (ii)"],"sol":"The test only works on a fraction in its lowest terms, so cancel first: 9 and 24 both divide by 3, giving 9/24 = 3/8. M1 for 3/8. The denominator 8 = 2 × 2 × 2 has no prime factor other than 2, so the decimal terminates and Priya is wrong. B1 for the reason — only 2s (and 5s) are allowed in the denominator. Dividing, 3 ÷ 8 = 0.375. A1 for 'no' supported by 0.375.","hints":["Whether a decimal stops or recurs is settled by the prime factors of the denominator: any prime other than 2 or 5 makes it recur.","Cancel the fraction to its lowest terms before you look at the prime factors of the denominator.","The first line is 9/24 cancelled by the common factor of 9 and 24."]},{"q":"Write 2.1̇8̇ as a mixed number in its lowest terms. (non-calculator)","a":"2 2/11","m":3,"d":2,"codes":["N10 (i)"],"sol":"Deal with the whole number separately: 2.1̇8̇ = 2 + 0.181818... B1 for splitting off the 2 (or for working on 0.181818... alone). Let x = 0.181818..., so 100x = 18.181818... and 100x − x = 18, giving 99x = 18 and x = 18/99. M1 for 99x = 18 or for 18/99. Dividing both parts by 9 gives 2/11, so 2.1̇8̇ = 2 2/11. A1 for 2 2/11.","hints":["The 2 in front of the point is not affected by the recurring part.","Convert the decimal part on its own, then put the whole number back in front of it.","The first line is 2.1̇8̇ = 2 + 0.181818..., and the recurring part is then called x."]},{"q":"Work out 0.6̇ − 0.1̇2̇. Give your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms. (non-calculator)","a":"6/11","m":4,"d":2,"codes":["N10 (i)"],"sol":"Change each decimal into a fraction first. For 0.6̇: x = 0.666..., 10x = 6.666..., so 9x = 6 and x = 6/9 = 2/3. M1 for 2/3 or 6/9. For 0.1̇2̇: y = 0.121212..., 100y = 12.121212..., so 99y = 12 and y = 12/99 = 4/33. M1 for 4/33 or 12/99. A common denominator of 3 and 33 is 33, and 2/3 = 22/33. M1 for 22/33. Subtracting, 22/33 − 4/33 = 18/33 = 6/11 after dividing both parts by 3. A1 for 6/11.","hints":["Recurring decimals are not safe to subtract column by column.","Turn each decimal into a fraction by the multiply-and-subtract method, then use a common denominator.","The first line is x = 0.666... with 10x underneath, and the second decimal is then treated the same way."]},{"q":"In a survey of 330 people, exactly 0.2̇1̇ of the people said that they cycle to work. Write the ratio of the people who cycle to work to the people who do not, giving your answer in its simplest form. (a calculator may be used)","a":"7 : 26","m":5,"d":3,"codes":["N10 (i)"],"sol":"First make the decimal exact. Let x = 0.212121..., so 100x = 21.212121... and 100x − x = 21, giving 99x = 21. M1 for 99x = 21 or for 21/99. Dividing both parts by 3 gives x = 7/33. A1 for 7/33. The number who cycle is 7/33 of 330: one thirty-third is 330 ÷ 33 = 10, so 7 × 10 = 70 people. M1 for 70. The rest are 330 − 70 = 260 people. M1 for 260. So the ratio is 70 : 260, and dividing both parts by 10 gives 7 : 26. A1 for 7 : 26.","hints":["A recurring decimal has to be made exact before it can act on a quantity.","Convert the decimal to a fraction in its lowest terms, take that fraction of the 330 people, and subtract to find the rest.","The first line is x = 0.212121..., with 100x written underneath."]},{"q":"A rope is 96 cm long. Exactly 0.583̇ of the rope is cut off. Work out the length of rope that is cut off. (non-calculator)","a":"56 cm","m":5,"d":3,"codes":["N10 (i)"],"sol":"Change 0.583̇ into a fraction. Let x = 0.58333... Two digits come before the repeating 3, so shift past them: 100x = 58.333... M1 for 100x = 58.333... Shift one more place so the tails line up: 1000x = 583.333... M1 for 1000x = 583.333... Subtracting, 1000x − 100x = 583 − 58, so 900x = 525. M1 for 900x = 525 or for 525/900. Dividing both parts by 75 gives x = 7/12. A1 for 7/12. The length cut off is 7/12 of 96 cm: one twelfth is 96 ÷ 12 = 8 cm, so 7 × 8 = 56 cm. A1 for 56 cm with the unit.","hints":["Only the last digit of this decimal keeps coming back.","Shift past the digits that do not repeat, shift again by the length of the block, subtract, then use the fraction on the rope.","The first line is 100x = 58.333..., and a further shifted line is needed before subtracting."]},{"q":"x is the recurring decimal 0.4̇2̇. Work out 3x, giving your answer as a recurring decimal in dot notation. (non-calculator)","a":"1.2̇7̇","m":5,"d":3,"codes":["N10 (i)","N10 (ii)"],"sol":"Write x as a fraction first: 100x = 42.424242... and 100x − x = 42, so 99x = 42. M1 for 99x = 42. Dividing both parts by 3, x = 42/99 = 14/33. A1 for 14/33. Then 3x = 3 × 14/33 = 42/33 = 14/11. M1 for 14/11 or 42/33. Now divide back: 14 ÷ 11 = 1 remainder 3, then 11 into 30 goes 2 remainder 8, then 11 into 80 goes 7 remainder 3 — the remainder 3 has come round again, so the digits 2 and 7 repeat. M1 for 1.2727... A1 for 1.2̇7̇ with a dot on the 2 and on the 7.","hints":["Multiplying a recurring decimal by 3 is only safe once it is an exact fraction.","Convert to a fraction, multiply that fraction by 3, then divide back to get the decimal.","The first line is 100x = 42.424242..., with x subtracted from it."]},{"q":"Put these four numbers in order, starting with the smallest: 0.234, 0.23, 7/30, 0.2̇3̇. (non-calculator)","a":"0.23, 0.2̇3̇, 7/30, 0.234","m":4,"d":3,"codes":["N10 (i)","N10 (ii)"],"sol":"Write everything as a decimal to enough places to separate them. The block 23 repeats, so 0.2̇3̇ = 0.232323... M1 for 0.2323... Dividing, 7 ÷ 30 = 0.23333... M1 for 0.2333... Pad the two terminating decimals to the same length: 0.230000 and 0.234000. Comparing the thousandths digit of each value gives 0, 2, 3 and 4, so 0.230 < 0.232323... < 0.233333... < 0.234. M1 for a correct comparison of all four values. Written in the forms given, the order is 0.23, 0.2̇3̇, 7/30, 0.234. A1 for the correct order in the original forms.","hints":["Numbers can only be compared once they are all written the same way.","Turn the recurring decimal and the fraction into decimals, pad every value to the same number of decimal places, then compare column by column.","The first line is 0.2̇3̇ written out as a string of digits, with 7 divided by 30 after it."]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 0.42̇ as a fraction in its lowest terms.","a":"19/45"},{"q":"Write 0.5̇1̇ as a fraction in its lowest terms.","a":"17/33"},{"q":"Write 5/6 as a decimal, using dot notation.","a":"0.83̇"},{"q":"Does 3/16 give a terminating or a recurring decimal? Give a reason.","a":"Terminating: 16 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 has no prime factor other than 2 or 5 (3/16 = 0.1875)."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Recurring decimal","code":"N10 (ii)","open":false,"back":"A decimal in which a block of digits repeats for ever, such as 0.3̇ = 0.333... It comes from a fraction whose denominator, in lowest terms, has a prime factor other than 2 or 5."},{"front":"Dot notation","code":"N10 (ii)","open":false,"back":"One dot above a single repeating digit; for a longer block, a dot above its first and last digit only. So 0.4̇ = 0.444... and 0.4̇27̇ = 0.427427427..."},{"front":"Turning a purely recurring decimal into a fraction","code":"N10 (i)","open":false,"back":"Let x be the decimal, multiply by 10ⁿ where n is the number of digits in the block, subtract x so the tails cancel, then divide and cancel to lowest terms."},{"front":"Which power of 10 to multiply by","code":"N10 (i)","open":false,"back":"One digit repeating: multiply by 10. Two digits: by 100. Three digits: by 1000. The shift must move the decimal on by exactly one whole block."},{"front":"1/3 and 1/9 as recurring decimals","code":"N10 (ii)","open":false,"back":"1/3 = 0.3̇ and 1/9 = 0.1̇, so the ninths run 0.1̇, 0.2̇, 0.3̇, ... For example 4/9 = 0.4̇."},{"front":"The rule of nines for a repeating block","code":"N10 (i)","open":false,"back":"0.0̇1̇ = 1/99, so a two-digit block ab gives 0.ȧḃ = ab/99, for example 0.3̇8̇ = 38/99. Cancel afterwards."},{"front":"A block of three repeating digits as a fraction","code":"N10 (i)","open":false,"back":"The block sits over 999: 0.ȧbċ = abc/999. For example 0.5̇04̇ = 504/999 = 56/111 after dividing both parts by 9."},{"front":"A decimal that starts before it recurs","code":"N10 (i)","open":false,"back":"Shift past the digits that do not repeat first: 0.aḃ = (ab − a)/90, for example 0.47̇ = (47 − 4)/90 = 43/90. One 9 per repeating digit, one 0 per digit before the block."},{"front":"Test for a terminating decimal","code":"N10 (ii)","open":false,"back":"Cancel to lowest terms, then prime factorise the denominator. Only 2s and 5s means the decimal stops; any other prime factor means it recurs."},{"front":"Why every fraction stops or recurs","code":"N10 (ii)","open":false,"back":"Dividing by b leaves a remainder from 0 to b − 1. Within b steps either 0 appears and the decimal stops, or a remainder comes round again and the digits cycle."},{"front":"The longest a repeating block can be","code":"N10 (ii)","open":false,"back":"For a denominator b the block is at most b − 1 digits long, because only b − 1 non-zero remainders exist. For example 1/13 = 0.0̇76923̇, a block of six."},{"front":"0.9̇ as a fraction","code":"N10 (i)","open":false,"back":"0.9̇ = 1 exactly. With x = 0.999..., 10x = 9.999..., so 9x = 9 and x = 1."},{"front":"Fraction to decimal: the method","code":"N10 (ii)","open":false,"back":"Divide the numerator by the denominator, bringing down zeros. Stop when the remainder is 0 or when a remainder repeats; the digits from the repeat onwards form the block."},{"front":"A recurring decimal with a whole-number part","code":"N10 (i)","open":false,"back":"Split it off and convert only the decimal part: 3.4̇ = 3 + 4/9 = 31/9. The whole number never enters the multiply-and-subtract lines."},{"front":"Give one example of a fraction whose decimal recurs, and say why","code":"N10 (ii)","open":true,"back":"1/3 = 0.3̇, since 3 is a prime factor other than 2 or 5; 8/9 = 0.8̇, since 9 = 3 × 3; 1/12 = 0.083̇, since 12 = 2 × 2 × 3. Any fraction whose cancelled denominator has such a factor is acceptable."}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Purely recurring decimal to a fraction: with a block of n digits, 10ⁿ times x, minus x, leaves the block over n nines — 0.ȧḃ = ab/99 — then cancel. Not on the Higher Exam Aid: remember it.","Recurring decimal with digits before the block: 0.aḃ = (ab − a)/90, and in general one 9 for each repeating digit followed by one 0 for each digit before the block. Not on the Higher Exam Aid: remember it.","Recurring decimal with a whole-number part: split it off, convert the decimal part, then add — 3.4̇ = 3 + 4/9 = 31/9. Not on the Higher Exam Aid: remember it.","Fraction to decimal: numerator divided by denominator, bringing down zeros until the remainder is 0 or repeats. Not on the Higher Exam Aid: remember it.","Test for a terminating decimal: in lowest terms the denominator has no prime factor other than 2 or 5. Not on the Higher Exam Aid: remember it.","Dot notation: one dot over a single repeating digit, dots over the first and last digit of a longer block. Not on the Higher Exam Aid: remember it.","Remember: 1/3 = 0.3̇. Not on the Higher Exam Aid: remember it.","Remember: 1/9 = 0.1̇. Not on the Higher Exam Aid: remember it.","Remember: 1/11 = 0.0̇9̇. Not on the Higher Exam Aid: remember it.","Remember: 1/6 = 0.16̇. Not on the Higher Exam Aid: remember it.","Remember: 0.9̇ = 1. Not on the Higher Exam Aid: remember it."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T12:07:41.946782Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.89,"notes":"Mathematically clean throughout — all 15 room questions, 4 exit questions, 5 lesson checks, 6 worked examples and every fact and card re-solved and confirmed correct, with the Higher Exam Aid claims matching W87991A exactly — so the only work left is tightening the two calculator-flagged questions and the first-step/cue of the 5/11 division example."}}},"number-measures-higher":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"number-measures-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Bounds are where Higher tier turns a rounded measurement back into the range of values it really stands for, and the marks are worth protecting: every measured value in a question has been rounded, so any length, mass, time or rate worked out from it is itself only known within limits. Papers 2 and 3 set bound calculations inside area, speed, density and percentage questions, and Paper 1 sets them as short non-calculator writing-down questions or as a reasoning question — will the load be safe, will the piece fit, can the claim be true. The command words are 'write down the upper bound', 'calculate the upper and lower bounds' and 'give your answer to a suitable degree of accuracy', and each has a fixed method. Choosing the wrong end of one interval loses every accuracy mark in the question, so the habit of asking which value makes the answer largest and which makes it smallest is what this room builds.","idea":[{"h":"Upper and lower bounds of a measurement","t":"A measurement is never exact: a value given as a, correct to the nearest u, was rounded, so the true value lies within half of u either side. The lower bound is a − u/2 and the upper bound is a + u/2, and the limits of accuracy are written (a − u/2) ≤ x < (a + u/2) — the lower bound is included because it rounds up to the reading, the upper bound is not because it rounds up to the next reading instead. First find u, the accuracy the measurement is given to: 'to the nearest metre' gives u = 1 m, 'to the nearest 0.1 s' gives u = 0.1 s, 'to the nearest 10 g' gives u = 10 g, and a value written to a number of decimal places or significant figures is rounded to the place value of its last figure, so 5.83 to 3 significant figures has u = 0.01 and bounds 5.825 and 5.835. The exam asks for these as 'write down' answers carrying B marks, so no working can rescue the wrong half-unit.","code":"N16 (i)"},{"h":"Calculating with upper and lower bounds","t":"Once each measurement has been replaced by its interval, the bounds are carried through the calculation, and the only question at each step is which end makes the answer as large as possible and which makes it as small as possible. A sum is largest from the two upper bounds and smallest from the two lower bounds. A product behaves the same way. A difference a − b is largest from the upper bound of a with the lower bound of b, and smallest from the lower bound of a with the upper bound of b. A quotient a ÷ b is largest from the upper bound of a over the lower bound of b, and smallest from the lower bound of a over the upper bound of b — dividing by the smallest possible divisor gives the biggest answer. An exact number in the calculation, such as the 4 in the perimeter of a square, has no bounds of its own. Finally the two answers are interpreted: 'give your answer to a suitable degree of accuracy' means round the upper and lower bounds to the same accuracy and quote the value they agree on, and in a decision question the bound that works against you is the one that settles it.","code":"N16 (ii)"}],"examples":[{"title":"Bounds of a length measured to the nearest centimetre","setup":"A piece of wood has a length of 24 cm, measured to the nearest centimetre. Write down the lower bound and the upper bound of the length L of the wood, and then write the limits of accuracy as an inequality in cm.","steps":["Measuring to the nearest centimetre means the true value lies within half of 1 cm of the reading, and half of 1 cm is 0.5 cm.","Lower bound = 24 − 0.5 = 23.5 cm, and a length of exactly 23.5 cm would round up to the reading, so it is included.","Upper bound = 24 + 0.5 = 24.5 cm, but a length of exactly 24.5 cm would round up to 25 cm instead, so the true length stays below it.","The limits of accuracy are therefore 23.5 ≤ L < 24.5, in cm."],"cues":["How far either side of a reading can the true value be?","What is the smallest length that still rounds to this reading?","Does a value at the top of the range round to this reading or to the next one?","Which end of the interval takes ≤ and which takes <?"]},{"title":"Bounds of an area, and a suitable degree of accuracy","setup":"A rectangle measures 8.2 cm by 5.6 cm, each length correct to the nearest 0.1 cm. Calculate the upper bound and the lower bound of the area of the rectangle, and then give the area to a suitable degree of accuracy, explaining why that accuracy is the right one.","steps":["Half of 0.1 cm is 0.05 cm, so the length lies in 8.15 ≤ l < 8.25 and the width in 5.55 ≤ w < 5.65, in cm.","A product is largest when both factors are largest, so the upper bound of the area = 8.25 × 5.65 = 46.6125 cm².","A product is smallest when both factors are smallest, so the lower bound of the area = 8.15 × 5.55 = 45.2325 cm².","Rounding both bounds to 2 significant figures gives 47 cm² and 45 cm²; they disagree, so 2 significant figures cannot be justified.","Rounding both bounds to 1 significant figure gives 50 cm² each time, so the area is 50 cm² to 1 significant figure."],"cues":["How wide is the interval for each side of the rectangle?","Which end of each interval makes the area as large as it can be?","Which end of each interval makes the area as small as it can be?","Do the two bounds agree once they are rounded to two significant figures?","How accurate can the answer be if the two bounds are to agree?"]},{"title":"Upper and lower bounds of a speed","setup":"A runner covers a distance measured as 96 m, correct to the nearest metre, in a time measured as 7.5 seconds, correct to the nearest 0.1 second. Calculate the upper bound and the lower bound of the runner's average speed in m/s, each correct to 2 decimal places. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Distance: half of 1 m is 0.5 m, so 95.5 ≤ d < 96.5 in m. Time: half of 0.1 s is 0.05 s, so 7.45 ≤ t < 7.55 in s.","Average speed = distance ÷ time, and a quotient is largest when the number being divided is largest and the divider is smallest.","Upper bound of the speed = 96.5 ÷ 7.45 = 12.9530… = 12.95 m/s (2 d.p.).","The quotient is smallest the other way round, so the lower bound = 95.5 ÷ 7.55 = 12.6490… = 12.65 m/s (2 d.p.)."],"cues":["What is the interval for each of the two measurements?","Which quantity is divided by which, and what makes a quotient big?","Which pair of values goes into the division for the largest speed?","Which pair of values is left for the smallest speed?"]},{"title":"Deciding with bounds: will it certainly fit?","setup":"A doorway is 76 cm wide, correct to the nearest centimetre. A wardrobe is 75.8 cm wide, correct to the nearest 0.1 cm. Decide whether the wardrobe is certain to fit through the doorway without being turned, and justify the decision using bounds.","steps":["To be certain, the narrowest the doorway could be must still be wider than the widest the wardrobe could be.","Doorway: half of 1 cm is 0.5 cm, so its lower bound is 76 − 0.5 = 75.5 cm.","Wardrobe: half of 0.1 cm is 0.05 cm, so its upper bound is 75.8 + 0.05 = 75.85 cm.","75.85 cm is greater than 75.5 cm, so the wardrobe could be wider than the doorway and it is not certain to fit."],"cues":["Which extreme of each measurement is the worst case here?","What is the least the doorway could measure?","What is the most the wardrobe could measure?","Which of those two values is the larger, and what does that mean?"]},{"title":"Bounds of a difference","setup":"A plank of wood is 240 cm long, correct to the nearest centimetre. A piece of length 86.5 cm, correct to the nearest 0.5 cm, is cut from the plank. Calculate the upper bound and the lower bound of the length of plank that is left. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Plank: half of 1 cm is 0.5 cm, so its length p satisfies 239.5 ≤ p < 240.5, in cm.","Piece cut off: half of 0.5 cm is 0.25 cm, so its length c satisfies 86.25 ≤ c < 86.75, in cm.","A difference is largest when the starting value is largest and the amount taken away is smallest: upper bound = 240.5 − 86.25 = 154.25 cm.","A difference is smallest the other way round: lower bound = 239.5 − 86.75 = 152.75 cm."],"cues":["What is half of the accuracy the plank is given to?","What is half of the accuracy the cut piece is given to?","Which two values leave as much plank as possible?","Which two values leave as little plank as possible?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A length is 45 cm, correct to the nearest centimetre. Write down its lower bound.","a":"44.5 cm"},{"q":"A mass is 3.20 kg, correct to the nearest 0.01 kg. Write down its upper bound.","a":"3.205 kg"},{"q":"u = 9.6 and v = 4.1, each correct to 1 decimal place. Work out the upper bound of u ÷ v, correct to 3 significant figures.","a":"2.38 (from 9.65 ÷ 4.05)"},{"q":"Which bounds of p and q give the smallest possible value of p − q?","a":"The lower bound of p minus the upper bound of q"}]},"room":{"facts":["A value given as a, correct to the nearest u, has lower bound a − u/2 and upper bound a + u/2, and limits of accuracy (a − u/2) ≤ x < (a + u/2).","The accuracy u is the place value of the last figure given: 1 dp means u = 0.1, 2 dp means u = 0.01, and 5.83 to 3 significant figures means u = 0.01.","Sums and products: largest from the two upper bounds, smallest from the two lower bounds.","Differences: a − b is largest from UB(a) − LB(b) and smallest from LB(a) − UB(b).","Quotients: a ÷ b is largest from UB(a) ÷ LB(b) and smallest from LB(a) ÷ UB(b).","A suitable degree of accuracy: round the upper and lower bounds to the same accuracy and give the most accurate value they both round to."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) A steel rod has a length of 36 cm, correct to the nearest centimetre. Write down the lower bound of the length of the rod.","a":"35.5 cm","sol":"Correct to the nearest centimetre means the true length lies within half of 1 cm, that is 0.5 cm, of the reading. Lower bound = 36 − 0.5 = 35.5 cm (B1, unit needed). A rod of exactly 35.5 cm rounds up to 36 cm, so this value is possible.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The figure given is a rounded length, not the exact length.","For a value rounded to the nearest centimetre the true length can be up to half a centimetre either side.","Your first line is half of the unit the length was rounded to."],"codes":["N16 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The mass of a parcel is 4.6 kg, correct to the nearest 0.1 kg. Write down the lower bound and the upper bound of the mass of the parcel.","a":"Lower bound 4.55 kg, upper bound 4.65 kg","sol":"Half of the accuracy is 0.1 ÷ 2 = 0.05 kg. Lower bound = 4.6 − 0.05 = 4.55 kg (B1). Upper bound = 4.6 + 0.05 = 4.65 kg (B1). The true mass is at least 4.55 kg and below 4.65 kg.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The mass on the label stands for a whole range of masses.","Halve the accuracy the mass is given to, then go that far below and above the reading.","Your first line is 0.1 ÷ 2."],"codes":["N16 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The length of a tunnel is 8400 m, correct to the nearest 100 m. Write down the limits of accuracy of the length d metres, as an inequality.","a":"8350 ≤ d < 8450","sol":"Half of 100 m is 50 m, so the bounds are 8400 − 50 = 8350 m and 8400 + 50 = 8450 m (B1 for both values). A length of 8350 m rounds up to 8400 m so it is included, while 8450 m rounds up to 8500 m so it is not: 8350 ≤ d < 8450 (B1 for the fully correct inequality, including the two signs).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The limits of accuracy are the two ends of the range the true length lies in.","Halve the unit the length was rounded to, then take it from and add it to the reading.","Your first line is half of the rounding unit, in metres."],"codes":["N16 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A number x is 5.83, correct to 3 significant figures. Write down the limits of accuracy of x as an inequality.","a":"5.825 ≤ x < 5.835","sol":"The third significant figure of 5.83 is in the hundredths place, so x was rounded to the nearest 0.01 (B1 for using 0.01, or for either bound). Half of 0.01 is 0.005, giving 5.83 − 0.005 = 5.825 and 5.83 + 0.005 = 5.835, so 5.825 ≤ x < 5.835 (B1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Decide first what place value the last figure given sits in.","Three significant figures here is the same as rounding to the nearest hundredth.","Your first line is half of the place value of the last figure."],"codes":["N16 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) a = 6.4 and b = 2.5, each correct to 1 decimal place. Work out the upper bound of a + b.","a":"9","sol":"Each value is rounded to the nearest 0.1, so half of that is 0.05: the upper bounds are 6.45 and 2.55. A sum is largest when both values are largest, so the calculation is 6.45 + 2.55 (M1). 6.45 + 2.55 = 9, so the upper bound of a + b is 9 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Replace each letter by the largest value it could stand for.","Find the upper bound of a and the upper bound of b, then add them.","Your first line writes the two upper bounds as an addition."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) x = 8.5 and y = 2.4, each correct to 1 decimal place. Work out the lower bound of x − y.","a":"6","sol":"Each value is rounded to the nearest 0.1, so the bounds are 8.45 ≤ x < 8.55 and 2.35 ≤ y < 2.45. A difference is smallest when the first value is as small as possible and the value taken away is as large as possible, so the calculation is 8.45 − 2.45 (M1). 8.45 − 2.45 = 6, so the lower bound of x − y is 6 (A1). Pairing the two lower bounds, 8.45 − 2.35 = 6.1, is the wrong choice of ends and scores nothing.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The two letters do not use the same end of their intervals here.","To make a subtraction as small as possible, start small and take away as much as possible.","Your first line writes the lower bound of x and the upper bound of y as a subtraction."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"A triangle has a base of 12.4 cm and a perpendicular height of 7.8 cm, each correct to the nearest 0.1 cm. Work out the upper bound of the area of the triangle. Give your answer exactly. A calculator may be used.","a":"48.86625 cm²","sol":"Half of 0.1 cm is 0.05 cm, so the upper bounds are 12.45 cm for the base and 7.85 cm for the height (M1). Area of a triangle = ½ × base × height, and the product is largest when both lengths are largest, so the upper bound = ½ × 12.45 × 7.85 (M1). This gives 48.86625, so the upper bound of the area is 48.86625 cm² (A1, unit needed).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Each length stands for a range, and only one end of each range is wanted here.","Write down the largest possible base and the largest possible height, then use the area formula.","Your first line is the two upper bounds, 12.45 cm and 7.85 cm."],"codes":["N16 (i)","N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"A force of 480 N, correct to the nearest 10 N, acts on an area of 62 cm², correct to the nearest 1 cm². Pressure = force ÷ area. Work out the upper bound of the pressure, in N/cm², correct to 3 significant figures. A calculator may be used.","a":"7.89 N/cm²","sol":"Force: half of 10 N is 5 N, so its upper bound is 485 N. Area: half of 1 cm² is 0.5 cm², so its lower bound is 61.5 cm² (M1 for both of these values). A quotient is largest when the number divided is largest and the divider is smallest, so the upper bound of the pressure = 485 ÷ 61.5 (M1). This gives 7.8861…, which is 7.89 N/cm² to 3 significant figures (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The two measurements do not both take their upper bound here.","For the biggest possible pressure, make the force as large as possible and the area as small as possible.","Your first line is the upper bound of the force and the lower bound of the area."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"Water flows into a tank. The volume collected is 245 litres, correct to the nearest 5 litres, and the time taken is 31.2 seconds, correct to the nearest 0.1 second. Work out the lower bound of the rate of flow, in litres per second. A calculator may be used.","a":"7.76 litres per second","sol":"Volume: half of 5 litres is 2.5 litres, so its lower bound is 242.5 litres. Time: half of 0.1 s is 0.05 s, so its upper bound is 31.25 s (M1 for both values). Rate of flow = volume ÷ time, and a quotient is smallest when the number divided is smallest and the divider is largest, so the lower bound = 242.5 ÷ 31.25 (M1). This gives exactly 7.76, so the lower bound of the rate of flow is 7.76 litres per second (A1, unit needed).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A rate of flow is one quantity divided by another, so each has its own interval.","For the smallest possible rate, collect as little water as possible in as long a time as possible.","Your first line is the lower bound of the volume and the upper bound of the time."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Each of 40 identical crates has a mass of 12 kg, correct to the nearest kilogram. A lift has a maximum safe load of 495 kg. Can the 40 crates be carried in the lift in one go without any risk of passing the safe load? You must show your working.","a":"No — the total mass could be up to 500 kg, which is more than 495 kg","sol":"The worst case is the heaviest the crates could be: half of 1 kg is 0.5 kg, so the upper bound of one crate is 12 + 0.5 = 12.5 kg (M1). Upper bound of the total mass = 40 × 12.5 = 500 kg (M1). 500 kg is greater than the safe load of 495 kg, so the load might not be safe and the crates cannot be carried in one go with certainty (A1 for the comparison and the conclusion).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Only one end of the crates' interval can cause a problem here.","Work out the heaviest one crate could be, then the heaviest all of them could be together.","Your first line is the upper bound of the mass of one crate."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A square tile has sides of length 7.0 cm, correct to the nearest 0.1 cm. Work out the upper bound of the perimeter of the tile.","a":"28.2 cm","sol":"Half of 0.1 cm is 0.05 cm, so the upper bound of the side is 7.0 + 0.05 = 7.05 cm (M1). The perimeter is 4 sides, and 4 is an exact number with no bounds of its own, so the upper bound of the perimeter = 4 × 7.05 = 28.2 cm (A1, unit needed).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The number of sides is exact; only the length of a side has been rounded.","Find the longest a side could be, then use it in the perimeter of the square.","Your first line is the upper bound of the length of one side."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"A car travels at a constant speed of 12.53 m/s, correct to 2 decimal places, for 4.13 seconds, correct to 2 decimal places. Work out the distance travelled, in metres, giving your answer to a suitable degree of accuracy. You must show your working and explain why your answer is given to that accuracy. A calculator may be used.","a":"52 m (correct to 2 significant figures)","sol":"Correct to 2 decimal places means rounded to the nearest 0.01, and half of that is 0.005, so 12.525 ≤ speed < 12.535 and 4.125 ≤ time < 4.135 (M1). Distance = speed × time, so the upper bound = 12.535 × 4.135 = 51.832225 m (M1) and the lower bound = 12.525 × 4.125 = 51.665625 m (M1). Both bounds round to 52 to 2 significant figures, while to 3 significant figures they give 51.8 and 51.7 and disagree, so the distance is 52 m to 2 significant figures (A1 for 52 m with the comparison of the rounded bounds).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A calculation on rounded values cannot give an answer more accurate than the values allow.","Work out the upper and lower bounds of the distance, then round both to the same accuracy.","Your first line is half of 0.01, the amount each measurement can be out by."],"codes":["N16 (i)","N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"A metal block is a cuboid with edges of length 6.2 cm, 4.5 cm and 3.8 cm, each correct to the nearest 0.1 cm. The mass of the block is 640 g, correct to the nearest 10 g. Density = mass ÷ volume. Work out the upper bound of the density of the metal, in g/cm³, correct to 3 significant figures. A calculator may be used.","a":"6.28 g/cm³","sol":"Mass: half of 10 g is 5 g, so its upper bound is 645 g (M1). For the largest density the volume must be as small as possible, so use the lower bound of each edge, 6.15 cm, 4.45 cm and 3.75 cm: lower bound of the volume = 6.15 × 4.45 × 3.75 = 102.628125 cm³ (M1). Upper bound of the density = 645 ÷ 102.628125 (M1) = 6.2848…, which is 6.28 g/cm³ to 3 significant figures (A1, unit needed).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The mass and the volume pull in opposite directions when the density is to be as large as possible.","Take the heaviest possible mass and the smallest possible volume, which needs the shortest possible edges.","Your first line is the upper bound of the mass, in grams."],"codes":["N16 (i)","N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A strip of ribbon is 50 cm long, correct to the nearest centimetre. The strip is cut into 6 equal lengths. Show that each of these lengths must be more than 8.2 cm.","a":"Each length is at least 49.5 ÷ 6 = 8.25 cm, and 8.25 > 8.2","sol":"The shortest each piece could be comes from the shortest the ribbon could be: half of 1 cm is 0.5 cm, so the lower bound of the ribbon is 50 − 0.5 = 49.5 cm (M1). The 6 pieces are equal and 6 is exact, so the lower bound of one piece = 49.5 ÷ 6 = 8.25 cm (M1). Every possible length is at least 8.25 cm, and 8.25 cm is greater than 8.2 cm, so each piece must be more than 8.2 cm (A1 for the comparison and the conclusion).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["'Show that' means the worst case has to be tested, not a typical case.","Find the shortest the whole ribbon could be, then share that length between the pieces.","Your first line is the lower bound of the length of the ribbon."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"The price of a share rises from 240 pence, correct to the nearest 10 pence, to 315 pence, correct to the nearest 5 pence. Work out the upper bound of the percentage increase in the price, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"35.1%","sol":"Old price: half of 10 p is 5 p, so 235 ≤ old < 245. New price: half of 5 p is 2.5 p, so 312.5 ≤ new < 317.5 (M1 for both intervals). Percentage increase = (new − old) ÷ old × 100, which is largest when the new price is largest and the old price is smallest, so use 317.5 p and 235 p: increase = 317.5 − 235 = 82.5 p (M1). Percentage increase = 82.5 ÷ 235 × 100 (M1) = 35.106…, so the upper bound is 35.1% (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A percentage increase compares the rise with the price it started from, and both prices are rounded.","The smallest possible starting price makes the percentage increase as large as possible; pair it with the largest possible new price.","Your first line is the lower bound of the old price and the upper bound of the new price."],"codes":["N16 (i)","N16 (ii)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"A width is 18 cm, correct to the nearest centimetre. Write down the upper bound of the width.","a":"18.5 cm"},{"q":"A mass is 250 g, correct to the nearest 10 g. Write down the limits of accuracy of the mass m grams, as an inequality.","a":"245 ≤ m < 255"},{"q":"p = 3.6 and q = 1.2, each correct to 1 decimal place. Work out the upper bound of p + q.","a":"4.9 (from 3.65 + 1.25)"},{"q":"x = 7.4 and y = 2.5, each correct to 1 decimal place. Work out the upper bound of xy.","a":"18.9975 (from 7.45 × 2.55)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Upper bound of a measurement","back":"The value the true measurement must stay below: for a value a given to the nearest u, the upper bound is a + u/2. A measurement exactly there would round up to the next reading.","code":"N16 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Lower bound of a measurement","back":"The smallest value the true measurement could take. For a value a given to the nearest u, the lower bound is a − u/2, and the true value can equal it, because that value rounds up to the reading.","code":"N16 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Limits of accuracy of a rounded measurement","back":"The two bounds the true value lies between, written (a − u/2) ≤ x < (a + u/2) for a value a given to the nearest u. Example: 24 cm to the nearest cm gives 23.5 ≤ L < 24.5.","code":"N16 (i)","open":false},{"front":"The rounding unit u of a measurement","back":"The accuracy the value is given to: 'nearest metre' means u = 1 m, 'nearest 10 g' means u = 10 g, 1 decimal place means u = 0.1. Halving u gives the distance to each bound.","code":"N16 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Bounds of a value given to a number of significant figures","back":"The rounding unit is the place value of the last significant figure. So 5.83 to 3 significant figures was rounded to the nearest 0.01, giving bounds 5.825 and 5.835.","code":"N16 (i)","open":false},{"front":"The inequality signs in the limits of accuracy","back":"≤ at the lower bound and < at the upper bound. The lower bound rounds up to the stated value so it is included; the upper bound rounds up to the next value so it is excluded.","code":"N16 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Bounds of a sum a + b","back":"Upper bound = UB(a) + UB(b); lower bound = LB(a) + LB(b). Both measurements use the same end of their intervals.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Bounds of a difference a − b","back":"Upper bound = UB(a) − LB(b); lower bound = LB(a) − UB(b). The two measurements use opposite ends, because taking away less leaves more.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Bounds of a product ab","back":"Upper bound = UB(a) × UB(b); lower bound = LB(a) × LB(b). Used for areas and volumes, where every length takes the same end of its interval.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Bounds of a quotient a ÷ b","back":"Upper bound = UB(a) ÷ LB(b); lower bound = LB(a) ÷ UB(b). Dividing by the smallest possible divider gives the largest answer, so the two measurements use opposite ends.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"An exact number inside a bounds calculation","back":"A counted or defined number, such as the 4 sides of a square or the 6 equal pieces, has not been rounded, so it has no bounds: use it unchanged in both the upper and lower bound calculations.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Giving an answer to a suitable degree of accuracy","back":"Round the upper and lower bounds of the answer to the same accuracy; the answer is the most accurate value both bounds round to. If they disagree, round both less accurately and compare again.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"The worst case in a 'will it fit?' question","back":"The pair of bounds that makes the outcome least favourable: the smallest gap with the largest object, or the largest load against a safe limit. If the worst case passes, the outcome is certain.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Name one situation where the lower bound is the value that decides the answer","back":"Checking a shelf or gap is long enough for something to fit; checking a shared quantity gives everyone at least a stated amount; finding the smallest possible speed from a rounded distance and time, where the lower bound of the distance is divided by the upper bound of the time.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":true},{"front":"Bounds of a percentage change from rounded values","back":"Percentage increase = (new − old) ÷ old × 100 is largest when the new value takes its upper bound and the old value its lower bound, and smallest the other way round.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Preamble: the Exam Aid formulae sheet printed as an insert with every Higher tier 1MA1 paper prints nothing this room uses — no bound, no limit of accuracy and no compound measure — so every result below must be written from memory.","Remember: a value a given correct to the nearest u has lower bound a − u/2 and upper bound a + u/2 — no bound formula appears on the Exam Aid.","Remember: the limits of accuracy are written (a − u/2) ≤ x < (a + u/2), with ≤ at the lower bound and < at the upper bound.","Remember: the rounding unit u is the place value of the last figure given, so 1 decimal place gives u = 0.1 and 3 significant figures in 5.83 gives u = 0.01.","Remember: upper bound of a + b = UB(a) + UB(b); lower bound of a + b = LB(a) + LB(b).","Remember: upper bound of a − b = UB(a) − LB(b); lower bound of a − b = LB(a) − UB(b).","Remember: upper bound of ab = UB(a) × UB(b); lower bound of ab = LB(a) × LB(b).","Remember: upper bound of a ÷ b = UB(a) ÷ LB(b); lower bound of a ÷ b = LB(a) ÷ UB(b).","Remember: average speed = distance ÷ time, density = mass ÷ volume and pressure = force ÷ area — the Exam Aid prints no compound measure, and each of these is a quotient, so its bounds use opposite ends of the two intervals.","Remember: area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height, which the Exam Aid gives only in the form ½ab sin C, for two sides and the angle between them.","Remember: a suitable degree of accuracy is the most accurate value that both the upper bound and the lower bound of the answer round to.","Remember: an exact number in a calculation, such as the 4 in the perimeter of a square, has no bounds and is used unchanged."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T12:01:36Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Every answer key, solution line and worked-example calculation checks out exactly (bounds, the 2 s.f. 'suitable accuracy' judgement in Q11 and example 2, and all quotient/difference end-pairings), and the Formulae sheet matches the Higher Exam Aid line for line; fix the missing accuracy instruction on the triangle-area question and the self-contradicting upper-bound card and this is ready to publish."}}},"algebra-notation-higher":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"algebra-notation-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"At Higher tier the Algebra topic area carries 27–33% of the marks, and this room holds the manipulation Foundation never meets: cancelling an algebraic fraction, expanding a product of three binomials, factorising ax² + bx + c, setting an argument out as an algebraic proof, and using formal function notation for composite and inverse functions. Each of these is asked for in its own right, and each is also a step buried inside harder work on quadratics, graphs and circle proof, so a weakness here quietly costs marks across the whole of the algebra you meet. Much of it is examined without a calculator, where the written method is what earns the method mark, and a proof scores nothing at all unless every line of the reasoning is set down.","idea":[{"h":"Algebraic fractions, three brackets and ax² + bx + c","t":"The Higher list widens A4 to expressions 'including those involving surds and algebraic fractions', to 'expanding products of two or more binomials' and to 'factorising quadratic expressions of the form ax² + bx + c'. To cancel an algebraic fraction, factorise the numerator and the denominator fully and cancel a whole bracket that appears in both — never a single term. To expand three brackets, multiply two of them first, then multiply that quadratic by the third bracket term by term. To factorise ax² + bx + c, find two numbers with product a × c and sum b, split the middle term into those two x terms, and factorise the four terms in pairs. Surds behave like any other terms once you know √a × √a = a.","code":"A4"},{"h":"Algebraic proof","t":"A6 at Higher ends 'use algebra to support and construct arguments and proofs'. Testing a few numbers is not a proof: it only shows the statement works for the values you tried. A proof represents the general case with a letter — n for any integer, 2n for an even number, 2n + 1 for an odd number, n, n + 1, n + 2 for consecutive integers — then expands and collects until the required form is visible, usually by taking out a common factor such as 3(…) or 4(…). It ends with a conclusion in words saying that this holds for every value of n. Because the result is given, every line of the argument has to be written down: marks are for the chain of reasoning, not the answer.","code":"A6"},{"h":"Inverse and composite functions","t":"A7 at Higher adds 'interpret the reverse process as the inverse function; interpret the succession of two functions as a composite function (the use of formal function notation is expected)'. f(x) is the output of the machine f for the input x, so f(3) means substitute 3 into the rule. gf(x) means g(f(x)): apply f first and feed its output into g, so the order matters and fg(x) is usually different from gf(x). The inverse f⁻¹ undoes f — write y = f(x), rearrange to make x the subject, then rewrite the result using x as the input, and answer in f⁻¹(x) = … form. Examiners expect that notation, not words.","code":"A7"}],"examples":[{"title":"Simplifying an algebraic fraction","setup":"Simplify fully the algebraic fraction (x² + 2x − 15)/(x² − 25). You may assume that x ≠ 5 and x ≠ −5.","steps":["Factorise the top: two numbers with product −15 and sum +2 are +5 and −3, so x² + 2x − 15 = (x + 5)(x − 3)","Factorise the bottom: x² − 25 is a difference of two squares, so x² − 25 = (x + 5)(x − 5)","Rewrite the fraction with both factorisations in place: ((x + 5)(x − 3))/((x + 5)(x − 5))","The bracket (x + 5) appears above and below, so it cancels, leaving (x − 3)/(x − 5)"],"cues":["Which pair of numbers multiplies to the constant and adds to the coefficient of x?","What kind of quadratic has no x term and a subtracted square?","What does the fraction look like once neither part is a single quadratic?","Which bracket appears in both parts of the fraction?"]},{"title":"Expanding a product of three binomials","setup":"Expand and simplify the product of three binomials (x − 3)(x + 2)(x − 1), giving your answer as a cubic expression with the terms written in descending powers of x.","steps":["Multiply the first two brackets: (x − 3)(x + 2) = x² + 2x − 3x − 6 = x² − x − 6","Now multiply every term of x² − x − 6 by x: x³ − x² − 6x","Multiply every term of x² − x − 6 by −1: −x² + x + 6","Add the two lines and collect like terms: x³ − 2x² − 5x + 6"],"cues":["Which two brackets are easiest to deal with first?","What does each term of the quadratic give when multiplied by the x in the last bracket?","What does the same quadratic give when multiplied by the number in the last bracket?","Which terms in the two lines are like terms?"]},{"title":"Factorising when the coefficient of x² is not 1","setup":"Factorise fully the quadratic expression 4x² − 4x − 15, in which the coefficient of x² is not 1. Give your answer as a product of two brackets.","steps":["Multiply the coefficient of x² by the constant term: 4 × (−15) = −60, while the middle coefficient is −4","Look for two numbers with product −60 and sum −4: they are −10 and +6","Split the middle term using them: 4x² − 10x + 6x − 15","Factorise the first pair and the second pair separately: 2x(2x − 5) + 3(2x − 5)","Both parts now share the bracket (2x − 5), so take it out: (2x − 5)(2x + 3)"],"cues":["Which two numbers in the expression have to be multiplied together first?","What must the pair of numbers do to that product and to the middle coefficient?","How is the −4x rewritten so there are four terms?","What is the highest common factor of each pair of terms?","What do the two parts have in common now?"]},{"title":"Multiplying two brackets that contain a surd","setup":"Expand and simplify (2 + √3)(5 − √3), giving your answer in the form a + b√3, where a and b are integers.","steps":["Multiply each term in the first bracket by each term in the second: 2 × 5, 2 × (−√3), √3 × 5 and √3 × (−√3)","That gives 10 − 2√3 + 5√3 − (√3)²","A square root multiplied by itself gives the number back, so (√3)² = 3","Collect the whole numbers and the surd terms separately: 10 − 3 = 7 and −2√3 + 5√3 = 3√3","So the expansion simplifies to 7 + 3√3"],"cues":["How many products are there when two two-term brackets are multiplied?","What is the product of the two numbers at the front of each bracket?","What happens to a square root multiplied by itself?","Which of the four terms are like terms?","How is the result written in the form the question asks for?"]},{"title":"Proving a statement about consecutive even numbers","setup":"Let n stand for any integer. Prove that the sum of any three consecutive even numbers is always a multiple of 6.","steps":["Write the three numbers algebraically: the first even number is 2n, the next is 2n + 2 and the one after is 2n + 4","Add them together: 2n + (2n + 2) + (2n + 4)","Collect like terms: 6n + 6","Take out the common factor: 6(n + 1)","6(n + 1) is 6 times an integer, so the sum of any three consecutive even numbers is a multiple of 6"],"cues":["How is an even number written using n, and how do you step to the next even number?","What does the statement 'the sum of' tell you to do with the three expressions?","How many n terms and how many units are there altogether?","What number divides both terms of that expression?","Why does this form prove the statement for every integer, not just one case?"]},{"title":"Composite and inverse functions in formal notation","setup":"Two functions are defined by f(x) = 2x + 7 and g(x) = x − 4. Work out gf(3), work out fg(3), and find an expression for f⁻¹(x).","steps":["gf means f acts first, so start with f(3) = 2 × 3 + 7 = 13","Feed that output into g: g(13) = 13 − 4 = 9, so gf(3) = 9","fg means g acts first: g(3) = 3 − 4 = −1, and then f(−1) = 2 × (−1) + 7 = 5, so fg(3) = 5","For the inverse, write y = 2x + 7, undo the + 7 to get y − 7 = 2x, then divide by 2 to get x = (y − 7)/2","Rewrite with x as the input: f⁻¹(x) = (x − 7)/2"],"cues":["In gf(3), which function is applied to the 3?","What is done with the number the first function produced?","Which function acts first in fg(3), and why is that the other way round?","Which operations have to be undone, and in what order?","How is the rearranged rule written in function notation?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Factorise 2x² + 11x + 5.","a":"(2x + 1)(x + 5)"},{"q":"Simplify fully (x² − 4)/(x² + 5x + 6).","a":"(x − 2)/(x + 3)"},{"q":"f(x) = 4x + 1 and g(x) = x². Work out fg(2).","a":"17"},{"q":"Prove that the sum of any two consecutive odd numbers is a multiple of 4.","a":"(2n + 1) + (2n + 3) = 4n + 4 = 4(n + 1), which is 4 times an integer, so the sum is always a multiple of 4"}]},"room":{"facts":["To factorise ax² + bx + c, find two numbers with product a × c and sum b, split the middle term into those two x terms, then factorise the four terms in pairs.","To simplify an algebraic fraction, factorise the numerator and the denominator fully and cancel a whole bracket that appears in both; a single term never cancels on its own.","The difference of two squares: x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a).","To expand three binomials, multiply two brackets first, then multiply that quadratic by each term of the remaining bracket and collect like terms.","√a × √a = a, so brackets containing surds expand in the ordinary way and the surd terms are collected at the end.","In an algebraic proof: n is any integer, 2n is even, 2n + 1 is odd, and consecutive integers are n, n + 1, n + 2; finish with a conclusion in words covering every value of n.","gf(x) means g(f(x)), so f is applied first; f⁻¹ undoes f and is found by writing y = f(x), making x the subject and rewriting the rule as f⁻¹(x) = …"],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Factorise 3x² + 10x + 8.","a":"(3x + 4)(x + 2)","sol":"Multiply the coefficient of x² by the constant: 3 × 8 = 24, and the middle coefficient is 10. Two numbers with product 24 and sum 10 are 6 and 4, so split the middle term: 3x² + 6x + 4x + 8 (M1). Factorise in pairs: 3x(x + 2) + 4(x + 2) = (3x + 4)(x + 2) (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The coefficient of x² is not 1, so the pair of numbers you look for is not simply a pair of factors of the constant term.","Find two numbers whose product is the coefficient of x² times the constant term and whose sum is the coefficient of x, then use them to split the middle term into two.","Work out the coefficient of x² multiplied by the constant term, and list the factor pairs of that product before splitting 10x."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The function f is defined by f(x) = 5x + 2. Find f⁻¹(x).","a":"f⁻¹(x) = (x − 2)/5","sol":"Write y = 5x + 2 and rearrange to make x the subject: y − 2 = 5x, so x = (y − 2)/5 (M1). Rewrite with x as the input, in function notation: f⁻¹(x) = (x − 2)/5 (A1). Total 2 marks.","hints":["The inverse is the function that undoes f, so think about what f does to an input and in what order.","Write y = f(x), then rearrange the equation so that x stands alone on one side.","Set the rule equal to y, then undo the addition first and the multiplication second, keeping both sides balanced."],"m":2,"d":1,"codes":["A7"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) f(x) = 4x − 3 and g(x) = x + 5. Work out the value of gf(2).","a":"10","sol":"gf means f is applied first: f(2) = 4 × 2 − 3 = 5 (M1). Apply g to that output: g(5) = 5 + 5 = 10 (A1). Total 2 marks.","hints":["In a composite function written gf, the two functions are not used in the order they are read.","Work out the output of the function nearer the bracket first, then put that number into the other function.","Substitute the given input into f to get a single number before you go anywhere near g."],"m":2,"d":1,"codes":["A7"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify fully (x² − 49)/(x² + 7x).","a":"(x − 7)/x","sol":"Factorise both parts: x² − 49 is a difference of two squares, so it is (x − 7)(x + 7), and x² + 7x = x(x + 7) (M1). The bracket (x + 7) appears above and below, so it cancels, leaving (x − 7)/x (A1). Total 2 marks.","hints":["An algebraic fraction only simplifies once both parts are written as products.","Factorise the numerator and the denominator, then look for a bracket common to both.","The numerator is a square subtract a square, and the denominator has a common factor in every term — factorise each before doing anything else."],"m":2,"d":1,"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Expand and simplify (2x + 1)(x + 3)(x − 2).","a":"2x³ + 3x² − 11x − 6","sol":"Multiply the first two brackets: (2x + 1)(x + 3) = 2x² + 6x + x + 3 = 2x² + 7x + 3 (M1). Multiply that by (x − 2): 2x³ + 7x² + 3x − 4x² − 14x − 6 (M1). Collect like terms: 2x³ + 3x² − 11x − 6 (A1). Total 3 marks.","hints":["Three brackets cannot be multiplied all at once; deal with them in stages.","Expand any two of the brackets and simplify, then multiply the quadratic you get by the bracket that is left.","Start by expanding the first two brackets and collecting the two x terms they produce."],"m":3,"d":2,"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Factorise 6x² + x − 12.","a":"(3x − 4)(2x + 3)","sol":"6 × (−12) = −72 and the middle coefficient is 1. Two numbers with product −72 and sum 1 are 9 and −8, so split the middle term: 6x² + 9x − 8x − 12 (M1). Factorise in pairs: 3x(2x + 3) − 4(2x + 3) = (3x − 4)(2x + 3) (A1). Total 2 marks.","hints":["The coefficient of x² is not 1, so use the splitting method rather than guessing a pair of brackets.","Find two numbers with product equal to the coefficient of x² times the constant term and sum equal to the coefficient of x.","Multiply 6 by −12 first, then hunt through the factor pairs of that product for the pair with the right sum."],"m":2,"d":2,"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify fully (2x² + 5x − 3)/(x² + 6x + 9).","a":"(2x − 1)/(x + 3)","sol":"Factorise the numerator by splitting the middle term: 2 × (−3) = −6, and 6 and −1 have product −6 and sum 5, so 2x² + 6x − x − 3 = 2x(x + 3) − 1(x + 3) = (2x − 1)(x + 3) (M1). Factorise the denominator: x² + 6x + 9 = (x + 3)(x + 3) (M1). Cancel the common bracket (x + 3): (2x − 1)/(x + 3) (A1). Total 3 marks.","hints":["Neither part of this fraction can be cancelled until both are written as products of brackets.","Factorise the numerator with the splitting method, and look at the denominator for a repeated bracket.","Multiply the coefficient of x² by the constant term in the numerator, then find the pair of numbers that splits its middle term."],"m":3,"d":2,"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Expand and simplify (3 + √5)(4 − 2√5), giving your answer in the form a + b√5 where a and b are integers.","a":"2 − 2√5","sol":"Multiply each term in the first bracket by each term in the second: 12 − 6√5 + 4√5 − 2(√5)² (M1). Use √5 × √5 = 5, so the last term is −2 × 5 = −10 (M1). Collect: 12 − 10 = 2 and −6√5 + 4√5 = −2√5, giving 2 − 2√5 (A1). Total 3 marks.","hints":["Brackets containing surds expand exactly like any other pair of brackets.","Write out all four products first, then deal with the term in which a root is multiplied by itself.","Multiply each term of the first bracket by each term of the second and set the four products down before simplifying any of them."],"m":3,"d":2,"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) f(x) = 3x + 2 and g(x) = x² − 1. Express fg(x) in its simplest form.","a":"fg(x) = 3x² − 1","sol":"fg means g is applied first, so the input to f is x² − 1: fg(x) = 3(x² − 1) + 2 (M1). Expand and collect: 3x² − 3 + 2 = 3x² − 1 (A1). Total 2 marks.","hints":["In fg(x) the two functions are not applied in the order in which the letters are written.","Work out which function acts first, then substitute its whole rule in place of x in the other function.","Replace the x in the rule of f by the entire expression that g produces, keeping it inside brackets."],"m":2,"d":2,"codes":["A7"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Prove that the sum of the squares of any two consecutive integers is always an odd number.","a":"n² + (n + 1)² = 2n² + 2n + 1 = 2(n² + n) + 1, which is one more than an even number, so the sum is always odd","sol":"Let the two consecutive integers be n and n + 1, where n is any integer (B1). Sum of the squares: n² + (n + 1)² = n² + n² + 2n + 1 = 2n² + 2n + 1 (M1). Write this as 2(n² + n) + 1, which is twice an integer plus 1, so the sum is one more than an even number and is therefore odd for every integer n (A1). Total 3 marks.","hints":["Trying a few pairs of numbers shows the statement works for those pairs only, so start from a letter that stands for any integer.","Write the two integers in terms of n, square each of them and add, then expand and collect the terms.","Give the smaller of the two integers a letter, write the larger one in terms of that same letter, then set down the sum of their two squares before expanding anything."],"m":3,"d":2,"codes":["A6"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Prove that (n + 3)² − (n − 3)² is a multiple of 12 for all integer values of n.","a":"(n + 3)² − (n − 3)² = 12n, which is 12 times an integer, so it is always a multiple of 12","sol":"Expand both squares: (n + 3)² = n² + 6n + 9 and (n − 3)² = n² − 6n + 9 (M1). Subtract, taking care with the signs: n² + 6n + 9 − n² + 6n − 9 = 12n (A1). 12n is 12 times an integer, so the expression is a multiple of 12 for every integer n (A1). Total 3 marks.","hints":["The two brackets are squares, so nothing can be said about the difference until each one is multiplied out in full.","Expand each square separately, then subtract the second expansion from the first, remembering that every term of it changes sign.","Write out (n + 3)(n + 3) and (n − 3)(n − 3) as expansions before attempting the subtraction."],"m":3,"d":2,"codes":["A6"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify fully (2x² − 8)/(x² + x − 6).","a":"2(x + 2)/(x + 3)","sol":"Take the common factor out of the numerator: 2x² − 8 = 2(x² − 4) (M1). x² − 4 is a difference of two squares, so the numerator is 2(x − 2)(x + 2) (A1). Factorise the denominator: two numbers with product −6 and sum 1 are 3 and −2, so x² + x − 6 = (x + 3)(x − 2) (M1). Cancel the common bracket (x − 2): 2(x + 2)/(x + 3) (A1). Total 4 marks.","hints":["Fully means the numerator may need more than one stage of factorising before anything cancels.","Take out the numerical common factor of the numerator first, then factorise what is left inside the bracket and factorise the denominator.","Look at the numerator for a factor common to both terms, and take it outside a bracket before going any further."],"m":4,"d":2,"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) f(x) = 2x − 3 and g(x) = x². Solve gf(x) = 25.","a":"x = 4 or x = −1","sol":"gf means f first, so gf(x) = (2x − 3)², giving the equation (2x − 3)² = 25 (M1). Take the square root of both sides, keeping both signs: 2x − 3 = 5 or 2x − 3 = −5 (M1). From the first, 2x = 8 so x = 4 (A1). From the second, 2x = −2 so x = −1 (A1). Total 4 marks. (Expanding to 4x² − 12x − 16 = 0 and factorising scores the same marks.)","hints":["Build the composite rule as an expression in x before trying to solve anything.","Apply f to x, square the result because that is what g does, then set the squared bracket equal to the given value.","Write down gf(x) as a squared bracket and form the equation; remember a square can come from a positive or a negative quantity."],"m":4,"d":3,"codes":["A7"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Prove that the product of any two consecutive odd numbers is always one less than a multiple of 4.","a":"(2n + 1)(2n + 3) = 4n² + 8n + 3 = 4(n + 1)² − 1, which is one less than a multiple of 4","sol":"Let the two consecutive odd numbers be 2n + 1 and 2n + 3, where n is any integer (B1). Their product is (2n + 1)(2n + 3) = 4n² + 6n + 2n + 3 = 4n² + 8n + 3 (M1). Rewrite this with 4 as a factor: 4n² + 8n + 3 = 4(n² + 2n + 1) − 1 = 4(n + 1)² − 1 (M1). Since (n + 1)² is an integer, 4(n + 1)² is a multiple of 4, so the product is always one less than a multiple of 4 (A1). Total 4 marks.","hints":["Odd numbers have a standard algebraic form, and the next odd number after one of them is two more.","Write both numbers in terms of n, expand the product, then rearrange the result so that 4 is a factor of all but a constant.","Give the smaller odd number its general algebraic form, write the next odd number in terms of the same letter, then set down their product before expanding."],"m":4,"d":3,"codes":["A6"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) A rectangle has area (6x² + 11x − 10) cm² and length (3x − 2) cm. Work out the perimeter of the rectangle when x = 3.","a":"36 cm","sol":"The length is one factor of the area, so factorise the area: 6 × (−10) = −60 and 15 and −4 have product −60 and sum 11, giving 6x² + 15x − 4x − 10 = 3x(2x + 5) − 2(2x + 5) (M1) = (3x − 2)(2x + 5), so the width is (2x + 5) cm (A1). Perimeter = 2[(3x − 2) + (2x + 5)] = 2(5x + 3) = (10x + 6) cm (M1). Substitute x = 3: 10 × 3 + 6 (M1) = 36 cm (A1). Total 5 marks.","hints":["Area of a rectangle is length × width, so the given length must divide the area exactly.","Factorise the area into two brackets, identify which one is the width, then build the perimeter expression before substituting.","Multiply the coefficient of x² by the constant term of the area and find the pair of numbers that splits the middle term."],"m":5,"d":3,"codes":["A4"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Factorise 2x² + 9x + 4.","a":"(2x + 1)(x + 4)"},{"q":"Simplify fully (x² − 16)/(x² − 3x − 4).","a":"(x + 4)/(x + 1)"},{"q":"f(x) = 6x − 5. Find f⁻¹(x).","a":"f⁻¹(x) = (x + 5)/6"},{"q":"Expand and simplify (x + 1)(x + 2)(x + 3).","a":"x³ + 6x² + 11x + 6"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Factorising ax² + bx + c","back":"Find two numbers with product a × c and sum b, split the middle term into those two x terms, factorise the four terms in pairs and take out the common bracket.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Simplifying an algebraic fraction","back":"Factorise the numerator and the denominator fully, then cancel a bracket that appears in both. Whole factors cancel; single terms never do.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Difference of two squares","back":"x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a). It is often the factorisation hiding in one part of an algebraic fraction, for example x² − 25 = (x + 5)(x − 5).","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Expanding a product of three binomials","back":"Multiply two brackets and simplify, then multiply that quadratic by each term of the third bracket and collect like terms. The result is a cubic.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Multiplying brackets that contain surds","back":"Expand as usual and use √a × √a = a. For example (1 + √2)(3 + √2) = 3 + √2 + 3√2 + 2 = 5 + 4√2.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Checking a factorisation","back":"Expand the brackets again: the expansion must reproduce every term of the original expression, including the sign of the constant.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Function notation f(x)","back":"f(x) is the output of the function f for the input x. f(3) means substitute x = 3 into the rule; the f names the function and is not a multiplier.","code":"A7","open":false},{"front":"Composite function gf(x)","back":"gf(x) = g(f(x)): apply f first and put its output into g. Order matters, so fg(x) is usually not the same as gf(x).","code":"A7","open":false},{"front":"Inverse function f⁻¹(x)","back":"The function that undoes f: if f(a) = b then f⁻¹(b) = a. Find it by writing y = f(x), making x the subject, then rewriting the rule as f⁻¹(x) = …","code":"A7","open":false},{"front":"Representing numbers algebraically in a proof","back":"n is any integer; 2n is even; 2n + 1 is odd; consecutive integers are n, n + 1, n + 2; consecutive even numbers are 2n, 2n + 2, 2n + 4.","code":"A6","open":false},{"front":"The conclusion line of an algebraic proof","back":"After simplifying, state in words that the expression has the required form — a multiple of 3, one less than a multiple of 4 — and that this holds for every value of n.","code":"A6","open":false},{"front":"Testing numbers is not a proof","back":"Checking examples shows only that the statement works for the values tried. A proof uses a letter for the general case, so the conclusion covers all values.","code":"A6","open":false},{"front":"Showing an expression is a multiple of k","back":"Expand and collect, then take k out as a common factor, leaving an integer expression inside the bracket: 6n + 6 = 6(n + 1) is a multiple of 6.","code":"A6","open":false},{"front":"Name one way to write an even number and one way to write an odd number in an algebraic proof","back":"2n is even; 2n + 2 is the next even number; 2n + 1 or 2n − 1 is odd, where n stands for any integer.","code":"A6","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Preamble: none of this room's own results — the factorising rules, the rule for cancelling an algebraic fraction, the proof forms and the function notation — is printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid (W87991A), the formulae insert in every Higher paper, so everything marked Remember below must be written from memory. The items marked Printed are the whole of that sheet; the quadratic formula it prints solves ax² + bx + c = 0, and its roots p and q give the factorisation a(x − p)(x − q), so it is a route to a factorisation as well as a check on one.","Remember: to factorise ax² + bx + c, find two numbers with product a × c and sum b, split the middle term and factorise the four terms in pairs. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q), where p × q = c and p + q = b. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: the difference of two squares, x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a). Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: to simplify an algebraic fraction, factorise the numerator and the denominator fully and cancel a bracket common to both. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: to expand three binomials, multiply two brackets first and then multiply that quadratic by each term of the third bracket. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: √a × √a = a, the step that closes a bracket expansion containing surds. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: an even number is written 2n, for any integer n. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: an odd number is written 2n + 1 (or 2n − 1). Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: consecutive integers are written n, n + 1, n + 2, and consecutive even numbers 2n, 2n + 2, 2n + 4. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: an algebraic proof ends with a conclusion in words stating that the result holds for every value of n. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: gf(x) = g(f(x)) — the function nearer the bracket acts first. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: to find f⁻¹, write y = f(x), make x the subject, then write the answer as f⁻¹(x) = … Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: the quadratic formula, x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/2a, for ax² + bx + c = 0 where a ≠ 0.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, where a and b are the parallel sides and h their perpendicular separation.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: volume of a prism = area of cross section × length.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d the diameter.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: area of a circle = πr², where r is the radius.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: Pythagoras' theorem, a² + b² = c², in a right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is c.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: sin A = a/c, in a right-angled triangle ABC whose hypotenuse is c and whose side a is opposite angle A.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: cos A = b/c, where b is the side adjacent to angle A and c the hypotenuse.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: tan A = a/b, where a is opposite angle A and b adjacent to it.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: the sine rule, a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C, in any triangle ABC.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: the cosine rule, a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A, in any triangle ABC.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: area of a triangle = ½ab sin C.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, where P is the principal, r the interest rate per period and n the number of periods.","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B).","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid: P(A and B) = P(A given B) P(B)."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T12:07:11.826177+00:00","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.93,"notes":"All 15 bank questions, the 4 exit-ticket items, the 4 lesson check items and all 6 worked examples re-solved in sympy and every answer key, solution line and mark tally (M/A/B) is correct, with 11 of 15 questions non-calculator and the Higher Exam Aid claims exact — publish as is, optionally splitting the rectangle question's two-part answer key."}}},"algebra-graphs-higher":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"algebra-graphs-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Algebra carries 27–33% of the Higher papers, and the graph work in this room is where most of the grade 6 to 9 marks in it are found. A Higher paper expects you to turn a gradient upside down and change its sign for a perpendicular line, to complete the square and read the turning point straight off, to sketch exponential and trigonometric curves and the translations and reflections of any function, to estimate a gradient or an area on a curve when no calculus is allowed, and to handle the circle x² + y² = r² and its tangents. Every one of these is printed in bold or added to the Foundation statement in the Higher list, so none of it can be avoided at this tier, and all three papers can ask for it.","idea":[{"h":"Perpendicular lines","t":"Two lines are perpendicular when their gradients multiply to −1, so each gradient is the negative reciprocal of the other: 4 and −1/4, 2/3 and −3/2. At Higher tier the statement reads 'use the form y = mx + c to identify parallel and perpendicular lines', so you read the gradient from the equation, turn it upside down and change its sign, then substitute the given point to find c. Rearrange first if the line is given as, say, 2y = 6x + 5, because the gradient is only visible once y stands alone.","code":"A9"},{"h":"Turning points by completing the square","t":"The Higher list adds 'and turning points by completing the square' to A11. Halve the coefficient of x to get p, write (x + p)², then correct the constant: (x + p)² = x² + 2px + p², so you must add q = c − p². In the form (x + p)² + q the turning point is exactly (−p, q) and the line of symmetry is x = −p, because a square is never negative and is zero only when x = −p. This is the exact method, so it works when factorising fails and it also proves whether a curve meets the x axis at all.","code":"A11"},{"h":"Exponential and trigonometric graphs","t":"A12 at Higher tier adds 'exponential functions y = kˣ for positive values of k, and the trigonometric functions (with arguments in degrees) y = sin x, y = cos x and y = tan x for angles of any size'. y = kˣ with k > 1 passes through (0, 1), climbs ever more steeply and hugs the negative x axis as an asymptote, never reaching zero; the other positive values behave differently, because 0 < k < 1 gives the mirror image, still through (0, 1) but falling as x increases with the positive x axis as its asymptote, and k = 1 gives the flat line y = 1. y = sin x and y = cos x are waves of period 360° running between −1 and 1, sin starting at (0, 0) and cos at (0, 1). y = tan x repeats every 180° with vertical asymptotes at 90° and 270°.","code":"A12"},{"h":"Translations and reflections of a function","t":"A13 is printed in bold, so it is Higher tier only: 'sketch translations and reflections of a given function'. Changes outside the bracket do what they say — y = f(x) + a moves the graph a up, and y = −f(x) reflects it in the x axis. Changes inside the bracket do the opposite of what they look like: y = f(x + a) moves the graph a to the left, and y = f(−x) reflects it in the y axis. Exam questions rarely give you f(x); they give one point, a turning point or an intercept, and ask where it goes.","code":"A13"},{"h":"Exponential graphs in real contexts","t":"A14 at Higher tier widens to '(including reciprocal graphs and exponential graphs)', so growth and decay are read off a curve whose steepness keeps changing. A model y = a × bᵗ has a as the value at t = 0, the intercept on the vertical axis, and b as the multiplier for each unit of time: b > 1 is growth, 0 < b < 1 is decay. Read across from a value to the curve and down to the time axis for an approximate solution, and say what the answer means in the context, with its unit, because the interpretation carries the mark.","code":"A14"},{"h":"Gradients of and areas under non-linear graphs","t":"A15 is bold, so it is Higher tier only: 'calculate or estimate gradients of graphs and areas under graphs (including quadratic and other non-linear graphs), and interpret results in cases such as distance-time graphs, velocity-time graphs and graphs in financial contexts (this does not include calculus)'. For a gradient, draw a tangent touching the curve at the point and work out change in y ÷ change in x for two points on that tangent. For an area, split the region into strips — trapezia, triangles and rectangles — and add them. Both are estimates, and each must be interpreted: the gradient of a velocity–time graph is acceleration, and the area under it is distance.","code":"A15"},{"h":"The circle and its tangent","t":"A16 is bold, so it is Higher tier only: 'recognise and use the equation of a circle with centre at the origin; find the equation of a tangent to a circle at a given point'. A circle centred on the origin with radius r has equation x² + y² = r², so x² + y² = 36 has radius 6, and a point lies on the circle exactly when its coordinates satisfy the equation. The tangent at a point is perpendicular to the radius drawn to it, so find the gradient of that radius, take the negative reciprocal, and substitute the point into y = mx + c to find c.","code":"A16"}],"examples":[{"title":"The line perpendicular to a given line through a point","setup":"The line L has equation y = 3x − 5. Find the equation of the line perpendicular to L that passes through the point (6, 1). Give your answer in the form y = mx + c.","steps":["The number multiplying x gives the steepness of L, so its gradient is 3.","Perpendicular gradients multiply to −1, so the new gradient is −1 ÷ 3 = −1/3.","The new line is therefore y = −(1/3)x + c, which is the line the method mark is given for.","Substitute the point: 1 = −(1/3) × 6 + c, and −(1/3) × 6 = −2, so 1 = −2 + c.","Adding 2 to both sides gives c = 3, so the equation is y = −(1/3)x + 3."],"cues":["Where in the equation of L is its steepness written?","What must the two gradients multiply to make?","With the new gradient known, which part of y = mx + c is still missing?","Which pair of values can be put into the equation to pin down c?","What is left once −2 has been moved to the other side?"]},{"title":"Completing the square to find a turning point","setup":"A curve has equation y = x² + 8x + 21. Write the equation in the form (x + p)² + q, then write down the coordinates of the turning point of the curve and say whether it is a maximum or a minimum.","steps":["Halve the coefficient of x: half of 8 is 4, so begin with the bracket (x + 4)².","Expanding, (x + 4)² = x² + 8x + 16, which is 5 short of the constant needed.","So the equation is y = (x + 4)² + 5, because 16 + 5 = 21.","A square is never negative, and (x + 4)² is 0 only when x = −4, where y takes its smallest value, 5.","The turning point is (−4, 5) and, since y is smallest there, it is a minimum."],"cues":["Which number inside the bracket comes from the coefficient of x?","What constant does that bracket produce when it is expanded?","What must be added to the bracket to rebuild the original equation?","What is the smallest a squared bracket can ever be, and when?","Which pair of numbers in the completed form gives the coordinates?"]},{"title":"Where a minimum point goes under four changes","setup":"The curve y = f(x) has a single minimum point at (2, −5). Write down the coordinates of the point that the minimum point maps to on each of these curves: (a) y = f(x) + 4, (b) y = f(x − 3), (c) y = −f(x), (d) y = f(−x).","steps":["Adding 4 outside the bracket lifts every point 4 up, so the minimum goes to (2, −1).","Subtracting 3 inside the bracket shifts the curve 3 to the right, so the minimum goes to (5, −5).","The minus sign outside reverses every y coordinate, reflecting the curve in the x axis: the point becomes (2, 5) and is now a maximum.","The minus sign inside reverses every x coordinate, reflecting the curve in the y axis: the point becomes (−2, −5) and is still a minimum."],"cues":["Does a change outside the bracket move the curve up or sideways?","Which way does a change inside the bracket move the curve?","What happens to the sign of every y coordinate under a minus sign outside?","Which coordinate changes sign when the minus sign is inside the bracket?"]},{"title":"Estimating a distance from a speed–time curve","setup":"A car pulls away from traffic lights. Its speed v m/s is recorded every 2 seconds: t (s): 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 and v (m/s): 0, 5, 12, 18, 22, 24. The readings are plotted and joined with a smooth curve. Estimate the distance the car travels in the first 10 seconds, and say what the area under the graph represents.","steps":["The area under a speed–time graph is the distance travelled, so divide the region into five strips, each 2 s wide.","Each strip is a trapezium whose parallel sides are two readings, so its area is ½(a + b)h with h = 2 — the trapezium formula printed on the Exam Aid.","Adding the five trapezia gives ½ × 2 × [0 + 24 + 2 × (5 + 12 + 18 + 22)].","The bracketed readings total 57, so the area is 1 × (24 + 114) = 138.","The distance travelled is about 138 m, which is the area between the curve and the time axis."],"cues":["What quantity does the region under a speed–time graph measure?","What shape is each strip once the curve is treated as straight across it?","Which two readings are the ends, and which are counted twice?","What do the four inner readings add up to?","What unit does the answer take, and what does it measure?"]},{"title":"The tangent to a circle at a given point","setup":"A circle has centre the origin O and passes through the point P(−3, 4). Find the equation of the circle, and find the equation of the tangent to the circle at P, giving it in the form y = mx + c.","steps":["By Pythagoras the radius satisfies OP² = (−3)² + 4² = 9 + 16 = 25, so the circle is x² + y² = 25.","The gradient of the radius OP is (4 − 0) ÷ (−3 − 0) = −4/3.","The tangent is perpendicular to the radius, so its gradient is −1 ÷ (−4/3) = 3/4.","Substituting P into y = (3/4)x + c gives 4 = (3/4) × (−3) + c = −9/4 + c.","So c = 4 + 9/4 = 16/4 + 9/4 = 25/4, and the tangent is y = (3/4)x + 25/4."],"cues":["How is the radius found from the coordinates of P and the origin?","Which two points give the gradient of the radius?","What is the relationship between a tangent and the radius at the point of contact?","Which point can be substituted to find the intercept c?","What is 4 written as a number of quarters?"]},{"title":"Reading an exponential growth graph","setup":"The number of bacteria N in a dish t hours after the start is modelled by N = 50 × 2ᵗ. The graph of N against t is drawn for 0 ≤ t ≤ 4 hours. (a) Work out the number of bacteria after 3 hours. (b) Use the graph to estimate, to the nearest 0.1 hour, the time when there are 300 bacteria. (c) Say what the intercept on the N axis tells you.","steps":["For (a), put t = 3 into the model: N = 50 × 2³ = 50 × 8 = 400 bacteria.","For (b), 300 lies between the plotted values 200 at t = 2 and 400 at t = 3, so read across from the N axis to the curve and down to the t axis.","Because the curve rises ever more steeply, 300 is reached later than halfway between those times: the reading is t ≈ 2.6 hours.","For (c), t = 0 gives N = 50 × 2⁰ = 50, so the intercept is the number of bacteria at the start."],"cues":["Which power of 2 is needed for 3 hours?","Between which two plotted readings must the answer lie?","Does the steepening curve push the reading earlier or later than the midpoint?","What does t = 0 stand for in this situation?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write down the gradient of a line perpendicular to a line of gradient 2/3.","a":"−3/2"},{"q":"Write x² + 6x + 10 in the form (x + p)² + q.","a":"(x + 3)² + 1"},{"q":"Write down the coordinates of the turning point of y = (x − 4)² − 7.","a":"(4, −7)"},{"q":"The graph of y = f(x) passes through (1, 8). Which point must the graph of y = f(x) + 5 pass through?","a":"(1, 13)"},{"q":"Write down the radius of the circle x² + y² = 36.","a":"6"},{"q":"What does the area under a speed–time graph represent?","a":"The distance travelled"}]},"room":{"facts":["Perpendicular lines: m₁ × m₂ = −1, so each gradient is the negative reciprocal of the other (4 and −1/4, 2/3 and −3/2).","Completing the square: x² + bx + c = (x + b/2)² + c − (b/2)², and the turning point of y = (x + p)² + q is (−p, q) with line of symmetry x = −p.","y = kˣ with k > 1 passes through (0, 1), rises ever more steeply and approaches the negative x axis as an asymptote; y = a × bᵗ is growth when b > 1 and decay when 0 < b < 1.","y = sin x and y = cos x have period 360° and run between −1 and 1; sin x starts at (0, 0), cos x at (0, 1). y = tan x has period 180° and vertical asymptotes at 90° and 270°.","Transformations: y = f(x) + a moves the graph a up, y = f(x + a) moves it a to the left, y = −f(x) reflects it in the x axis and y = f(−x) reflects it in the y axis.","The gradient of a curve at a point is the gradient of the tangent drawn there: change in y ÷ change in x for two points on the tangent.","The area under a curve is estimated by splitting it into strips (trapezia, triangles, rectangles) and adding them; under a speed–time graph that area is the distance travelled and the gradient is the acceleration.","A circle of radius r centred on the origin has equation x² + y² = r², and the tangent at a point on it is perpendicular to the radius to that point."],"questions":[{"q":"The line L has equation y = 4x − 7. Write down the gradient of a line perpendicular to L. (non-calculator)","a":"−1/4","sol":"The gradient of L is the number multiplying x, which is 4. Perpendicular gradients multiply to −1, so the gradient wanted is −1 ÷ 4 = −1/4. B1 for −1/4 (accept −0.25).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The steepness of L is already written in its equation; perpendicular changes that steepness in a fixed way.","The two gradients must multiply to −1, so turn the gradient of L upside down and change its sign.","The first line is the gradient of L, read off the equation, ready to be divided into −1."],"codes":["A9"]},{"q":"Write x² + 10x + 27 in the form (x + p)² + q. (non-calculator)","a":"(x + 5)² + 2","sol":"Halve the coefficient of x: half of 10 is 5, so the bracket is (x + 5)². M1 for (x + 5)² seen. Expanding, (x + 5)² = x² + 10x + 25, which is 2 less than x² + 10x + 27, so 2 must be added: x² + 10x + 27 = (x + 5)² + 2. A1 for (x + 5)² + 2.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The number inside the bracket comes from the coefficient of x, not from the constant.","Halve the coefficient of x for p, expand your bracket, then correct the constant term.","The first line is the bracket (x + p)² with p taken as half of 10."],"codes":["A11"]},{"q":"Write down the equation of the circle with centre the origin and radius 9. (a calculator may be used)","a":"x² + y² = 81","sol":"A circle of radius r with centre the origin has equation x² + y² = r². Here r = 9, so r² = 81 and the equation is x² + y² = 81. B1 for x² + y² = 81.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A point on the circle is always the same distance from the origin, and Pythagoras turns that distance into an equation.","Use the standard form x² + y² = r² and put the radius in the right place.","The first line is x² + y² = r² with r replaced by the given radius."],"codes":["A16"]},{"q":"Here are four equations. A: y = 3ˣ   B: y = sin x   C: y = tan x   D: y = 1/x (x ≠ 0). (a) Write down the letter of the graph that passes through (0, 1) and gets closer and closer to the negative x axis without ever meeting it. (b) Write down the letter of the graph that has vertical asymptotes at x = 90° and x = 270°. (a calculator may be used)","a":"(a) A (b) C","sol":"(a) y = 3ˣ passes through (0, 1) because 3⁰ = 1, and as x becomes large and negative 3ˣ gets ever closer to 0 without reaching it, so the negative x axis is an asymptote: the answer is A. B1 for A. (b) tan x is undefined at 90° and at 270°, where the graph shoots away to a vertical asymptote: the answer is C. B1 for C.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Only one of these four curves cuts the y axis at 1, and only one repeats every 180°.","Test x = 0 in each equation, then recall which curve is undefined at 90°.","The first line is the value of each function at x = 0, starting with 3⁰."],"codes":["A12"]},{"q":"The graph of y = f(x) has a maximum point at (−1, 6). Write down the coordinates of the maximum point of (a) y = f(x) − 4, (b) y = f(x + 2). (a calculator may be used)","a":"(a) (−1, 2) (b) (−3, 6)","sol":"(a) Subtracting 4 outside the bracket lowers every point by 4, so the y coordinate falls from 6 to 2 and the maximum is at (−1, 2). B1 for (−1, 2). (b) f(x + 2) translates the graph 2 to the left, so the x coordinate falls from −1 to −3 while the height is unchanged: the maximum is at (−3, 6). B1 for (−3, 6).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["One of these changes moves the curve vertically and the other moves it sideways.","A change outside the bracket moves the graph up or down; a change inside moves it the opposite way along the x axis.","The first line is the vertical move: the y coordinate of the maximum with 4 taken from it."],"codes":["A13"]},{"q":"Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to y = 5x − 2 and passes through the point (10, 3). Give your answer in the form y = mx + c. (non-calculator)","a":"y = −(1/5)x + 5","sol":"The gradient of y = 5x − 2 is 5, so the perpendicular gradient is −1 ÷ 5 = −1/5. M1 for −1/5. The line is therefore y = −(1/5)x + c; substituting (10, 3) gives 3 = −(1/5) × 10 + c, that is 3 = −2 + c. M1 for substituting the point into y = −(1/5)x + c. So c = 5 and the line is y = −(1/5)x + 5. A1 for y = −(1/5)x + 5 (accept y = −0.2x + 5).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Two things are needed for the equation: how steep the line is, and where it crosses the y axis.","Take the negative reciprocal of the gradient given, then substitute the point to find c.","The first line is −1 divided by the gradient of the line given, which fixes m."],"codes":["A9"]},{"q":"By completing the square, find the coordinates of the turning point of the curve y = x² + 4x − 7, and state whether it is a maximum or a minimum. (non-calculator)","a":"(−2, −11), a minimum","sol":"Half of 4 is 2, so start with (x + 2)², and (x + 2)² = x² + 4x + 4. M1 for (x + 2)² seen. That constant is 11 more than the −7 required, so y = (x + 2)² − 11. A1 for (x + 2)² − 11. A square is never negative and (x + 2)² is 0 only when x = −2, where y is at its smallest, −11, so the turning point is (−2, −11) and it is a minimum (the x² term is positive). A1 for (−2, −11) and minimum.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The completed square form shows the smallest value the curve can take and where it happens.","Halve the coefficient of x, square the bracket, correct the constant, then read the turning point off.","The first line is the bracket (x + p)² with p taken as half of 4."],"codes":["A11"]},{"q":"A ball is released from rest on a slope that gets steeper. Its speed v m/s is recorded every second: t (s): 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 and v (m/s): 0, 3, 7, 12, 20. The points are joined with a smooth curve. (a) Using strips 1 second wide, estimate the distance the ball rolls in the 4 seconds. (b) State what the area under this graph represents. (a calculator may be used)","a":"(a) 32 m (b) the distance travelled","sol":"(a) Split the region into four trapezium strips of width 1 s and use ½(a + b)h on each: ½ × 1 × (0 + 3) + ½ × 1 × (3 + 7) + ½ × 1 × (7 + 12) + ½ × 1 × (12 + 20). M1 for a complete method of strips covering 0 to 4. The four areas are 1.5, 5, 9.5 and 16. M1 for all four strip areas correct. Their total is 32, so the distance is about 32 m. A1 for 32 m. (b) The area between the curve and the t axis on a speed–time graph is the distance travelled. B1 for distance travelled.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The readings are 1 second apart, which divides the region under the curve into equal slices.","Treat each slice as a trapezium whose parallel sides are the two speeds at its ends, then add the slices.","The first line is the area of the first strip, ½ × 1 × (0 + 3)."],"codes":["A15"]},{"q":"The point P(6, 8) lies on the circle x² + y² = 100. Find the equation of the tangent to the circle at P. (non-calculator)","a":"y = −(3/4)x + 12.5","sol":"The radius OP runs from the origin to P, so its gradient is (8 − 0) ÷ (6 − 0) = 8/6 = 4/3. M1 for 4/3. The tangent is perpendicular to the radius, so its gradient is −1 ÷ (4/3) = −3/4. M1 for −3/4. Substituting P(6, 8) into y = −(3/4)x + c gives 8 = −(3/4) × 6 + c, that is 8 = −4.5 + c. M1 for substituting P into y = −(3/4)x + c. So c = 12.5 and the tangent is y = −(3/4)x + 12.5. A1 for y = −(3/4)x + 12.5 (accept y = −0.75x + 25/2).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The radius drawn to the point of contact and the tangent there meet at a right angle.","Find the gradient of the radius from the origin to P, take its negative reciprocal, then substitute P to find c.","The first line is the gradient of OP as the change in y over the change in x from the origin to P."],"codes":["A16"]},{"q":"A machine is bought for £12 000. Its value £V after t years is modelled by V = 12000 × 0.8ᵗ, and V is plotted against t as a smooth curve. (a) Work out the value of the machine after 3 years. (b) Write down the first whole number of years after which the value has fallen below £6000. (a calculator may be used)","a":"(a) £6144 (b) 4 years","sol":"(a) Put t = 3 into the model: V = 12000 × 0.8³ = 12000 × 0.512. M1 for 12000 × 0.8³. That gives V = £6144. A1 for £6144. (b) After 3 years the value is £6144, which is still above £6000; after 4 years V = 12000 × 0.8⁴ = £4915.20, which is below £6000. So the value first falls below £6000 after 4 years. B1 for 4 years.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Each year the value is multiplied by the same number, which is why the graph is a curve.","Substitute t = 3 into the model for part (a), then test whole values of t until the value drops below the target.","The first line is 12000 × 0.8³ written out ready to be evaluated."],"codes":["A14"]},{"q":"The curve y = x² is translated 3 units to the right and 4 units down. Write the equation of the new curve in the form y = x² + bx + c. (non-calculator)","a":"y = x² − 6x + 5","sol":"A translation of 3 to the right replaces x by (x − 3), and a translation of 4 down subtracts 4 from the whole function, so the new curve is y = (x − 3)² − 4. M1 for (x − 3)² − 4. Expanding the bracket, (x − 3)² = x² − 6x + 9. M1 for a correct expansion. So y = x² − 6x + 9 − 4 = x² − 6x + 5. A1 for y = x² − 6x + 5.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A sideways move is made inside the bracket and a vertical move outside it.","Write the translated curve in completed square form first, then expand and collect the terms.","The first line is y = x² with x replaced by (x − 3), before the vertical move is applied."],"codes":["A13","A11"]},{"q":"The circle C has equation x² + y² = 169 and the point A(5, 12) lies on C. The tangent to C at A crosses the x axis at B, and O is the origin. Work out the area of triangle OAB. (non-calculator)","a":"202.8 square units","sol":"The radius OA has gradient (12 − 0) ÷ (5 − 0) = 12/5, so the tangent at A has gradient −1 ÷ (12/5) = −5/12. M1 for −5/12. Substituting A(5, 12) into y = −(5/12)x + c gives 12 = −25/12 + c, so c = 144/12 + 25/12 = 169/12 and the tangent is y = −(5/12)x + 169/12. M1 for the tangent equation. At B, y = 0, so (5/12)x = 169/12 and x = 169/5 = 33.8: B is (33.8, 0). A1 for B = (33.8, 0). Triangle OAB has base OB = 33.8 along the x axis, and its perpendicular height is the y coordinate of A, 12. M1 for ½ × 33.8 × 12. Area = 202.8 square units. A1 for 202.8.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The tangent has to be found before the triangle can be drawn, and the radius to A is the key to it.","Find the tangent at A from the negative reciprocal of the gradient of OA, set y = 0 to reach B, then use the base along the x axis with the height of A.","The first line is the gradient of OA as the change in y over the change in x from the origin to A."],"codes":["A16","A9"]},{"q":"(a) Write y = x² − 10x + 32 in the form (x − p)² + q. (b) Hence explain why the curve never crosses the x axis. (c) Write down the equation of the line of symmetry of the curve. (non-calculator)","a":"(a) (x − 5)² + 7 (b) the smallest value of y is 7, which is above 0, so y is never 0 (c) x = 5","sol":"(a) Half of −10 is −5, so the bracket is (x − 5)², and (x − 5)² = x² − 10x + 25. M1 for (x − 5)² seen. That is 7 less than x² − 10x + 32, so y = (x − 5)² + 7. A1 for (x − 5)² + 7. (b) (x − 5)² is a square, so it is never negative; the least it can be is 0, when x = 5. M1 for stating (x − 5)² ≥ 0 or that its least value is 0. Then the least value of y is 0 + 7 = 7, which is greater than 0, so y is never 0 and the curve never meets the x axis. A1 for a complete reason. (c) The curve is symmetrical about the vertical line through the turning point (5, 7), so the line of symmetry is x = 5. B1 for x = 5.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The completed square form shows in one line how low the curve ever gets.","Complete the square, then argue from the fact that a square is never negative.","The first line is the bracket (x − p)² with p taken as half of 10."],"codes":["A11"]},{"q":"A cyclist sets off from rest. Her speed v m/s is recorded every 5 seconds: t (s): 0, 5, 10, 15, 20 and v (m/s): 0, 6, 9, 11, 12. The points are joined with a smooth curve. (a) Use four strips to estimate the distance she travels in the 20 seconds. (b) A tangent drawn to the curve at t = 5 passes through (0, 2.8) and (10, 9.2). Estimate her acceleration at t = 5 s. (c) Is the estimate in part (a) an underestimate or an overestimate of the distance? Give a reason. (a calculator may be used)","a":"(a) 160 m (b) 0.64 m/s² (c) an underestimate, because each straight strip top lies below the curve","sol":"(a) Each strip is a trapezium of width 5 s: ½ × 5 × (0 + 6) + ½ × 5 × (6 + 9) + ½ × 5 × (9 + 11) + ½ × 5 × (11 + 12). M1 for a complete strip method across 0 to 20. The areas are 15, 37.5, 50 and 57.5, and their total is 160, so the distance is about 160 m. A1 for 160 m. (b) The acceleration is the gradient of the tangent: (9.2 − 2.8) ÷ (10 − 0). M1 for the gradient of the tangent from its two points. = 6.4 ÷ 10 = 0.64 m/s². A1 for 0.64 m/s². (c) It is an underestimate. B1 for underestimate. The curve bends over — it is steepest at the start and flattens — so the straight top of each strip lies below the curve and a sliver of area is missed each time. B1 for the reason.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["One part is about how much lies under the curve, the other about how steep it is at a single instant.","Use trapezium strips for the area, the gradient of the given tangent for the acceleration, and think about which side of the curve each strip top lies.","The first line is the area of the first strip, ½ × 5 × (0 + 6)."],"codes":["A15"]},{"q":"The point (30°, 0.5) lies on the curve y = sin x. (a) Write down the coordinates of the corresponding point on y = sin x − 2. (b) Write down the coordinates of the corresponding point on y = −sin x. (c) Write down the coordinates of the corresponding point on y = sin(x − 40°). (d) Use the symmetry of y = sin x to write down another angle between 0° and 360° at which y = 0.5. (non-calculator)","a":"(a) (30°, −1.5) (b) (30°, −0.5) (c) (70°, 0.5) (d) 150°","sol":"(a) Subtracting 2 lowers every point by 2, so the point is (30°, −1.5). B1 for (30°, −1.5). (b) −sin x reflects the curve in the x axis, reversing the sign of the y coordinate: (30°, −0.5). B1 for (30°, −0.5). (c) sin(x − 40°) translates the curve 40° to the right, so the x coordinate rises by 40°: (70°, 0.5). B1 for (70°, 0.5). (d) Between 0° and 180° the sine curve is symmetrical about its maximum at x = 90°, and 30° is 60° below 90°, so the matching angle is 90° + 60° = 150°. B1 for 150°.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Three parts move one known point; the last uses the shape of the wave itself.","Decide for each change whether it moves the curve vertically, sideways or reflects it, then use the symmetry of the wave about its peak.","The first line is the y coordinate of the given point with 2 taken from it."],"codes":["A12","A13"]},{"q":"£4000 is invested in an account paying 6% compound interest a year. The value £V after t years is plotted against t as a smooth curve. (a) Write down a formula for V in terms of t. (b) Use the graph, or otherwise, to find the least whole number of years after which the value is more than £6000. (c) Explain why the graph is a curve and not a straight line. (a calculator may be used)","a":"(a) V = 4000 × 1.06ᵗ (b) 7 years (c) each year's interest is worked out on a larger amount, so the yearly rise keeps growing","sol":"(a) A rise of 6% multiplies the value by 1.06 each year, so V = 4000 × 1.06ᵗ — the compound interest form P(1 + r/100)ⁿ printed on the Exam Aid, with P = 4000 and r = 6. B1 for V = 4000 × 1.06ᵗ. (b) Reading the curve, or testing values: 4000 × 1.06⁶ = £5674.08, which is still below £6000. M1 for a correct trial or reading either side of £6000. 4000 × 1.06⁷ = £6014.52, which is above £6000, so 7 whole years are needed. A1 for 7. (c) The interest each year is 6% of a larger amount than the year before, so the increase per year grows and the graph gets steeper: the growth is exponential, not a constant rate of change. B1 for a correct reason.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Interest is added to the amount already there, so each year starts from a bigger figure than the last.","Write the yearly multiplier as a power of t, then test whole years against the target value.","The first line is 4000 multiplied by the yearly multiplier raised to the power t."],"codes":["A14"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write down the gradient of a line perpendicular to y = −3x + 2.","a":"1/3"},{"q":"Write x² − 4x + 9 in the form (x − p)² + q.","a":"(x − 2)² + 5"},{"q":"Write down the equation of the circle with centre the origin that passes through (0, 7).","a":"x² + y² = 49"},{"q":"The graph of y = f(x) has a minimum point at (4, −1). Write down the minimum point of y = f(x) + 5.","a":"(4, 4)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Perpendicular gradients","back":"Two lines are perpendicular when their gradients multiply to −1: m₁ × m₂ = −1. Each gradient is the negative reciprocal of the other, such as 3 and −1/3. Not printed on either Exam Aid.","code":"A9","open":false},{"front":"Negative reciprocal","back":"Turn the fraction upside down and change the sign: the negative reciprocal of 2/5 is −5/2. A horizontal line has gradient 0 and is perpendicular to a vertical line, whose gradient is undefined.","code":"A9","open":false},{"front":"Completed square form of x² + bx + c","back":"(x + p)² + q, where p is half of b and q = c − p², because (x + p)² = x² + 2px + p². Check by expanding.","code":"A11","open":false},{"front":"Turning point from the completed square","back":"y = (x + p)² + q has its turning point at (−p, q) and its line of symmetry at x = −p. It is a minimum when the x² term is positive and a maximum when it is negative.","code":"A11","open":false},{"front":"The graph of y = kˣ for positive values of k","back":"Always through (0, 1). For k > 1 it rises ever more steeply, with the negative x axis as an asymptote; for 0 < k < 1 it falls, with the positive x axis as an asymptote. Never negative.","code":"A12","open":false},{"front":"The graphs of y = sin x and y = cos x","back":"Waves of period 360° running between −1 and 1. y = sin x starts at (0, 0) and peaks at 90°; y = cos x starts at (0, 1) and peaks at 0° and 360°.","code":"A12","open":false},{"front":"The graph of y = tan x","back":"Repeats every 180°, passing through (0, 0), (180°, 0) and (360°, 0). Vertical asymptotes come every 180°, at every odd multiple of 90°: 90°, 270°, 450°, −90° and so on. It takes every value.","code":"A12","open":false},{"front":"y = f(x) + a and y = f(x + a)","back":"f(x) + a translates the graph a units up, a change outside the bracket. f(x + a) translates it a units to the left — a change inside the bracket moves the graph the opposite way.","code":"A13","open":false},{"front":"Name one way to change y = f(x) so that the point (2, 5) moves to (2, −5)","back":"y = −f(x), a reflection in the x axis, because every y coordinate changes sign; or y = f(x) − 10, a translation 10 units down. Note that y = f(−x) reflects in the y axis instead, changing every x coordinate, so it sends the point to (−2, 5).","code":"A13","open":true},{"front":"The exponential model y = a × bᵗ","back":"a is the starting value, at t = 0, and b is the multiplier for each unit of time: growth when b > 1, decay when 0 < b < 1. A 6% yearly rise means b = 1.06.","code":"A14","open":false},{"front":"Reading an exponential graph in context","back":"The intercept on the vertical axis is the starting value. Read across from a value to the curve and down for the time. The steepness keeps changing, so no single gradient describes the whole curve.","code":"A14","open":false},{"front":"Gradient of a curve at a point","back":"Draw a tangent touching the curve at that point and find its gradient, change in y ÷ change in x, from two points on the tangent. The answer is an estimate and depends on the tangent drawn.","code":"A15","open":false},{"front":"Area under a curve","back":"Split the region into strips — trapezia, triangles and rectangles — and add their areas. The strip formula ½(a + b)h is printed on the Exam Aid. Under a speed–time graph the area is the distance travelled.","code":"A15","open":false},{"front":"Equation of a circle centred on the origin","back":"x² + y² = r², where r is the radius, so x² + y² = 49 is the circle of radius 7. A point lies on the circle exactly when its coordinates fit the equation. Not on the Exam Aid.","code":"A16","open":false},{"front":"Tangent to a circle at a point on it","back":"The tangent is perpendicular to the radius drawn to that point, so its gradient is the negative reciprocal of the gradient of that radius; substitute the point into y = mx + c to find c.","code":"A16","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Perpendicular gradients: m₁ × m₂ = −1, so the perpendicular gradient is the negative reciprocal. The Higher Exam Aid (W87991A) does not print it, so remember it.","Equation of a straight line: y = mx + c, with m the gradient and c the y intercept. Not on the Higher Exam Aid: remember it.","Gradient from two points: m = change in y ÷ change in x = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁). Not on the Higher Exam Aid: remember it, and use it for the gradient of a tangent too.","Completing the square: x² + bx + c = (x + b/2)² + c − (b/2)², giving the turning point (−p, q) and the line of symmetry x = −p for y = (x + p)² + q. Not on the Higher Exam Aid: remember it.","Equation of a circle centred on the origin: x² + y² = r². Not on the Higher Exam Aid: remember it, along with the fact that the tangent at a point is perpendicular to the radius to that point.","Shapes to remember: y = kˣ through (0, 1) with the negative x axis as an asymptote; y = sin x and y = cos x of period 360° between −1 and 1; y = tan x of period 180° with asymptotes at 90° and 270°. The Exam Aid prints no graph shapes and no exact trigonometric values, so these are recall.","Transformations to remember: y = f(x) + a is a translation a up, y = f(x + a) is a translation a to the left, y = −f(x) is a reflection in the x axis and y = f(−x) is a reflection in the y axis. Not on the Exam Aid: remember them.","Speed = distance ÷ time, read as the gradient of a distance–time graph, and acceleration = change in speed ÷ time, read as the gradient of a speed–time graph; the area under a speed–time graph is the distance travelled. None of these is on the Higher Exam Aid sheet, so remember all of these.","What the Higher Exam Aid (W87991A) does print that this room uses: Area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, which is the area of each strip when you estimate the area under a curve, and Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, the compound interest model behind an exponential growth graph in a financial context.","The rest of the Higher sheet belongs to other rooms: volume of a prism, circumference = 2πr = πd and area = πr² of a circle, the quadratic formula x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/2a, a² + b² = c², sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b, the sine and cosine rules, Area of triangle = ½ab sin C, P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B) and P(A and B) = P(A given B) P(B)."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T12:07:15.428449Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Every answer key is correct and every worked example sound — all seven Higher ideas (A9, A11–A16) are covered in order at genuine Higher standard with nine non-calculator questions — so this is publishable once the Formulae sheet stops crediting the Exam Aid with a list of what it does not print."}}},"algebra-equations-higher":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"algebra-equations-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Algebra carries 27–33% of the marks on the Higher papers, and this room holds the solving methods that separate a Higher answer from a Foundation one. At Foundation you factorise a quadratic and hope it factorises; at Higher every quadratic can be solved, because completing the square and the quadratic formula work whatever the numbers are, and the formula is printed on your Exam Aid. On top of that the Higher papers ask you to solve a line and a curve together, to find a root by iteration when no exact method exists, and to solve quadratic inequalities and write the solution set properly in set notation or as a shaded region. These techniques then reappear inside longer problems — a tangent question, an area that leads to a quadratic, a time interval in a real context — so they have to be automatic.","idea":[{"h":"Completing the square and the quadratic formula","t":"The Higher statement is 'solve quadratic equations (including those that require rearrangement) algebraically by factorising, by completing the square and by using the quadratic formula'. The first move is always the same: rearrange until one side is zero, in the form ax² + bx + c = 0. Then choose. Factorising is quickest when it works, and at Higher you are expected to factorise ax² + bx + c with a ≠ 1 as well. Completing the square writes x² + bx + c as (x + b/2)² − (b/2)² + c; solving from there gives exact answers in surd form, which is what Paper 1 wants. The formula x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/2a always works and is printed on the Higher Exam Aid, so the marks are for substituting a, b and c with their signs, not for recalling it. The number under the root, b² − 4ac, is the discriminant: positive gives two roots, zero gives one repeated root, negative gives none. A question that says 'give your answer to 2 decimal places' is telling you a calculator method is expected; 'give an exact answer' or 'in the form p ± √q' is telling you to complete the square or to leave the surd in the formula.","code":"A18"},{"h":"Solving a line and a curve together","t":"The Higher version of A19 reads 'solve two simultaneous equations in two variables (linear/linear or linear/quadratic) algebraically'. Elimination does not work here, so substitution is the method: rearrange the linear equation for one letter if it is not already in the form y = …, put that expression into the quadratic, and you are left with a single quadratic in one letter. Rearrange it to = 0 and solve it with any method from A18. Each solution for x gives a value of y — always substitute back into the linear equation, which is easier and safer — and the answers must be paired, not listed separately. Geometrically the pairs are the points where the line crosses the curve. Two solutions means the line cuts the curve twice; one repeated solution (a discriminant of zero) means it touches once, so the line is a tangent; no real solutions means they never meet. Examiners often ask exactly that question the other way round: find the value of k for which the line is a tangent.","code":"A19"},{"h":"Finding a root numerically by iteration","t":"A20 is printed in bold in the Higher list and does not appear at Foundation at all: 'find approximate solutions to equations numerically using iteration'. It is used when an equation, usually a cubic, cannot be solved by any exact method you have. First locate the root: work out f(a) and f(b); if one is negative and the other positive, and the graph is continuous, the curve must cross the x-axis somewhere between a and b, and that change of sign is the whole argument. Then rearrange the equation into the form x = g(x) — the paper usually gives you the rearrangement — and iterate: put x₀ into g, call the answer x₁, put x₁ back in for x₂, and keep going. Use the calculator's answer key so the full value is carried each time; rounding as you go drifts the answer. Stop when consecutive terms agree to the accuracy asked for, and write the values down as x₁ = …, x₂ = …, because the method marks live in those lines.","code":"A20"},{"h":"Quadratic inequalities, set notation and regions","t":"The Higher A22 reads 'solve linear inequalities in one or two variable(s), and quadratic inequalities in one variable; represent the solution set on a number line, using set notation and on a graph'. A quadratic inequality is solved through its graph, not by dividing: rearrange to make one side zero, factorise to find the critical values where the expression is zero, then picture the U-shaped curve. A positive x² curve is below the axis between the roots and above it outside them, so x² + bx + c < 0 gives one interval between the critical values while > 0 gives two intervals, one either side. Write the answer as an inequality or in set notation, {x : 1 < x < 6}, using ∪ to join two separate intervals. For two variables the solution is a region: draw each boundary line, dashed for a strict inequality and solid for ≤ or ≥, test a point such as the origin to see which side to take, and label or shade the region satisfying all of them.","code":"A22"}],"examples":[{"title":"Completing the square for an exact answer","setup":"Solve the equation x² + 6x + 1 = 0 by completing the square. Give your solutions in surd form, in their simplest form. No calculator is allowed.","steps":["Halve the coefficient of x: 6 ÷ 2 = 3, so the bracket to build is (x + 3).","(x + 3)² = x² + 6x + 9, which is 9 too big, so x² + 6x + 1 = (x + 3)² − 9 + 1 = (x + 3)² − 8.","The equation is now (x + 3)² − 8 = 0, so (x + 3)² = 8.","Square root both sides, keeping both signs: x + 3 = ±√8, and √8 = √4 × √2 = 2√2.","Subtract 3: x = −3 + 2√2 or x = −3 − 2√2."],"cues":["Which number belongs inside the bracket when the square is completed?","What does that bracket expand to, and how does it differ from the expression given?","What should be done to get the squared bracket on its own?","How is the bracket undone, and why does that produce two answers?","What is the last operation needed to leave x on its own?"]},{"title":"A quadratic that must be rearranged, solved by the formula","setup":"Solve the equation 5x² = 3x + 4. Give your solutions correct to 2 decimal places. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Collect everything on one side so that the equation has the form ax² + bx + c = 0: 5x² − 3x − 4 = 0.","Read off the coefficients with their signs: a = 5, b = −3, c = −4.","Substitute into the formula printed on the Exam Aid: x = (−(−3) ± √((−3)² − 4 × 5 × (−4)))/(2 × 5).","Work out the discriminant: (−3)² − 4 × 5 × (−4) = 9 + 80 = 89, so x = (3 ± √89)/10.","√89 = 9.4339811…, so x = (3 + 9.4339811…)/10 = 1.2433981… or x = (3 − 9.4339811…)/10 = −0.6433981…","Rounding each value, the solutions are x = 1.24 and x = −0.64."],"cues":["Why can the formula not be used on the equation exactly as it is printed?","What are a, b and c, and which sign does each one carry?","Where do a = 5, b = −3 and c = −4 each go now, and which of them needs brackets to keep its sign?","What is the value of the expression under the square root?","What two values come out of the ± sign?","To what accuracy does the question want the answers?"]},{"title":"A line meeting a curve","setup":"Solve the simultaneous equations y = x² − 4x + 5 and y = 2x − 3 algebraically, and say what the solutions tell you about the graphs of the two equations. No calculator is allowed.","steps":["Both right-hand sides are equal to y, so they are equal to each other: x² − 4x + 5 = 2x − 3.","Move every term to the left so that one side is zero: x² − 6x + 8 = 0.","Factorise: (x − 2)(x − 4) = 0, so x = 2 or x = 4.","Substitute each value into the linear equation y = 2x − 3: x = 2 gives y = 1, and x = 4 gives y = 5.","The pairs are x = 2, y = 1 and x = 4, y = 5, so the line cuts the curve at the two points (2, 1) and (4, 5)."],"cues":["What can be done with the two right-hand sides, and why?","What form does a quadratic equation need to be in before it is solved?","Which two numbers multiply to give the constant and add to give the coefficient of x?","Which of the two original equations is easier to substitute back into?","What do the two pairs of values represent on the graphs?"]},{"title":"Locating a root and iterating to it","setup":"Show that the equation x³ + 2x − 7 = 0 has a solution between x = 1 and x = 2. Then, starting with x₀ = 1.5, use the iterative formula xₙ₊₁ = ∛(7 − 2xₙ) to find that solution correct to 3 decimal places. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Write f(x) = x³ + 2x − 7 and substitute the two end values: f(1) = 1 + 2 − 7 = −4 and f(2) = 8 + 4 − 7 = 5.","One value is negative and the other positive, and the graph of a cubic is unbroken, so the curve must cross the x-axis somewhere between them.","First iteration: x₁ = ∛(7 − 2 × 1.5) = ∛4 = 1.5874011…","Feed each answer back in: x₂ = ∛(7 − 2 × 1.5874011…) = 1.5639324… and x₃ = ∛(7 − 2 × 1.5639324…) = 1.5703032…","Carry on: x₄ = 1.5685789… and x₅ = 1.5690459…","x₄ and x₅ agree once rounded, so the solution is x = 1.569."],"cues":["What has to be worked out at each end of the interval?","What do the two values you have just found tell you about the graph between them?","What goes into the right-hand side of the formula first?","Which value is substituted to produce the next term?","How many terms are needed before the digits settle down?","To what accuracy can the solution now be written?"]},{"title":"A quadratic inequality and its solution set","setup":"Solve the inequality x² + 2x − 8 ≤ 0. Write the solution set in set notation and describe how it is shown on a number line. No calculator is allowed.","steps":["One side is already zero, so factorise the other: x² + 2x − 8 = (x + 4)(x − 2).","The critical values are where the product is zero: x + 4 = 0 gives x = −4, and x − 2 = 0 gives x = 2.","Sketch y = (x + 4)(x − 2): the coefficient of x² is positive, so the curve is U-shaped and cuts the axis at the two critical values.","A U-shaped curve is on or below the axis between its two crossings, so the inequality holds there.","In set notation the answer is {x : −4 ≤ x ≤ 2}; on a number line it is drawn with filled circles at each end and the line between them shaded, because ≤ includes both end values."],"cues":["What has to happen to the expression before any critical value can be found?","Which values of x make that product equal to zero?","Which way up is the curve, and where does it cut the axis?","Is the part of the curve that satisfies the inequality between the crossings or outside them?","How is 'includes the end values' shown in set notation and on a number line?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write x² + 4x − 6 in the form (x + a)² + b.","a":"(x + 2)² − 10"},{"q":"How many real solutions does 3x² − 4x + 2 = 0 have, and how do you know?","a":"None: the discriminant is (−4)² − 4 × 3 × 2 = −8, which is negative."},{"q":"Solve the inequality (x + 2)(x − 5) > 0.","a":"x < −2 or x > 5"},{"q":"Solve the simultaneous equations y = x² − 2x and y = x − 2.","a":"x = 1, y = −1 and x = 2, y = 0"}]},"room":{"facts":["Completing the square: x² + bx + c = (x + b/2)² − (b/2)² + c; when the coefficient of x² is not 1, take it out of the first two terms first, as in 2x² + 12x + 7 = 2(x² + 6x) + 7 = 2(x + 3)² − 11.","The quadratic formula: the solution of ax² + bx + c = 0, a ≠ 0, is x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/2a. It is printed on the Higher Exam Aid, but the equation must be rearranged to = 0 before a, b and c can be read off.","The discriminant b² − 4ac: positive gives two different real roots, zero gives one repeated root (and a tangent), negative gives no real roots.","Linear/quadratic simultaneous equations: substitute the linear equation into the quadratic, rearrange to = 0, solve for x, then substitute each x back into the linear equation and pair the answers as coordinates.","Iteration: if f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs and the graph is continuous there is a root between a and b; then apply xₙ₊₁ = g(xₙ) from x₀, keeping the full calculator value, until consecutive terms agree to the accuracy asked.","Quadratic inequalities: rearrange to zero, factorise to find the critical values, and use the U-shaped sketch — the expression is negative between the roots and positive outside them. Set notation: {x : 1 < x < 6}, with ∪ joining two separate intervals.","Regions: a strict inequality (< or >) has a dashed boundary line and ≤ or ≥ has a solid one; test a point such as (0, 0) to decide which side of the line to take."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve 2x² + 7x + 3 = 0.","a":"x = −1/2 or x = −3","sol":"The equation is already in the form ax² + bx + c = 0. Factorise: the brackets must multiply to give 2x² and +3, and (2x + 1)(x + 3) expands to 2x² + 6x + x + 3 = 2x² + 7x + 3 (M1 for a pair of brackets giving 2x² and +3, A1 for the correct factorisation). A product is zero only if a factor is zero: 2x + 1 = 0 gives x = −1/2, and x + 3 = 0 gives x = −3 (A1 for both solutions).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The left-hand side is a product of two brackets waiting to be found.","With a coefficient of 2 in front of x², one bracket must begin 2x and the other x; the numbers in them multiply to give the constant term.","Write the left-hand side as (2x + …)(x + …) and check the expansion gives the middle term."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write x² + 10x + 2 in the form (x + a)² + b, where a and b are integers.","a":"(x + 5)² − 23","sol":"Halve the coefficient of x: 10 ÷ 2 = 5, so the bracket is (x + 5) (M1). (x + 5)² = x² + 10x + 25, which is 23 more than x² + 10x + 2, so x² + 10x + 2 = (x + 5)² − 23 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The number inside the bracket comes straight from the coefficient of x.","Halve the coefficient of x to fill the bracket, then compare the expansion of the bracket with the expression you were given.","Expand (x + a)² with your value of a and see how far the constant term is out."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"(a calculator may be used) Solve 2x² + 5x − 4 = 0, giving your solutions correct to 2 decimal places.","a":"x = 0.64 or x = −3.14","sol":"Here a = 2, b = 5 and c = −4, so the quadratic formula (printed on the Higher Exam Aid) gives x = (−5 ± √(5² − 4 × 2 × (−4)))/(2 × 2) (M1 for a correct substitution). The discriminant is 25 + 32 = 57, so x = (−5 ± √57)/4 = (−5 ± 7.5498344…)/4 (A1). This gives x = 0.6374586… and x = −3.1374586…, so x = 0.64 or x = −3.14 to 2 decimal places (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["This one does not factorise, so use the method that works on every quadratic.","Identify a, b and c with their signs and use the formula from your Exam Aid; take care that c is negative.","Substitute your values into x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/2a and work out the number under the root first."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve the inequality (x − 1)(x − 6) < 0, giving the solution set in set notation.","a":"{x : 1 < x < 6}","sol":"The critical values are where the product is zero: x = 1 and x = 6. The coefficient of x² is positive, so the sketch of y = (x − 1)(x − 6) is a U-shaped curve cutting the x-axis at 1 and 6, and it lies below the axis between those crossings (M1 for the critical values with a correct U-shaped argument). The inequality is satisfied there, so the solution set is {x : 1 < x < 6}, with strict inequalities because the end values give zero, not a negative value (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A sketch of the curve settles which values of x make the product negative.","Find the two x values that make the product zero, then decide whether the U-shaped curve dips below the axis between them or outside them.","Mark the two critical values on a sketch of y = (x − 1)(x − 6) and look at where the curve is below the axis."],"codes":["A22"]},{"q":"(a calculator may be used) f(x) = x³ + x − 5. Work out f(1) and f(2), and explain how your values show that the equation x³ + x − 5 = 0 has a solution between x = 1 and x = 2.","a":"f(1) = −3 and f(2) = 5; the change of sign shows a root between 1 and 2","sol":"f(1) = 1³ + 1 − 5 = −3 and f(2) = 2³ + 2 − 5 = 5 (M1 for both values correct). f(1) is negative and f(2) is positive, and the graph of a cubic is continuous, so the curve must cross the x-axis between x = 1 and x = 2; that crossing is a solution of x³ + x − 5 = 0 (A1 for the sign-change reasoning and conclusion).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Substituting the two end values tells you which side of the x-axis the curve is on at each end.","Work out both values, then compare their signs and think about whether a cubic graph can jump from one side of the axis to the other without touching it.","Substitute x = 1 and then x = 2 into x³ + x − 5 and write both results down."],"codes":["A20"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve x² − 8x + 11 = 0 by completing the square. Give your solutions in the form p ± √q, where p and q are integers.","a":"x = 4 ± √5","sol":"Halve the coefficient of x: −8 ÷ 2 = −4, so x² − 8x + 11 = (x − 4)² − 16 + 11 = (x − 4)² − 5 (M1). The equation becomes (x − 4)² − 5 = 0, so (x − 4)² = 5 and x − 4 = ±√5 (M1). Adding 4 gives x = 4 + √5 or x = 4 − √5 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The answer is exact, so no decimals and no calculator method are wanted here.","Rewrite the left-hand side as a squared bracket plus a number, then get the squared bracket on its own.","Halve the coefficient of x to build the bracket, and adjust the constant so the expression is unchanged."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write 2x² + 12x + 7 in the form a(x + b)² + c, where a, b and c are integers.","a":"2(x + 3)² − 11","sol":"Take the 2 out of the terms in x: 2x² + 12x + 7 = 2(x² + 6x) + 7 (M1). Complete the square inside the bracket: x² + 6x = (x + 3)² − 9, so the expression is 2[(x + 3)² − 9] + 7 = 2(x + 3)² − 18 + 7 (M1). Collecting the numbers gives 2(x + 3)² − 11, so a = 2, b = 3 and c = −11 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The coefficient of x² is not 1, so it has to be dealt with before the square can be completed.","Take the coefficient of x² out of the first two terms only, complete the square inside the bracket, then multiply back out and tidy the constants.","Write the expression as 2(x² + 6x) + 7 and work on the bracket."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve the simultaneous equations y = x² + 2x − 3 and y = x + 3 algebraically.","a":"x = −3, y = 0 and x = 2, y = 5","sol":"Both expressions equal y, so x² + 2x − 3 = x + 3 (M1 for the substitution). Rearranging to make one side zero gives x² + x − 6 = 0 (M1). Factorising, (x + 3)(x − 2) = 0, so x = −3 or x = 2 (A1). Substituting each into y = x + 3 gives y = 0 when x = −3 and y = 5 when x = 2, so the solutions are the pairs x = −3, y = 0 and x = 2, y = 5 (A1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Elimination is no help when one equation is a curve; make the two expressions for y meet instead.","Set the two right-hand sides equal, collect everything on one side and solve the quadratic that appears, then find the matching y values.","Write x² + 2x − 3 = x + 3 and collect all the terms on the left."],"codes":["A19"]},{"q":"(a calculator may be used) The equation x³ − 4x − 9 = 0 has a solution between x = 2 and x = 3. Using the iterative formula xₙ₊₁ = ∛(4xₙ + 9) with x₀ = 2, work out x₁, x₂ and x₃, giving each value correct to 3 decimal places.","a":"x₁ = 2.571, x₂ = 2.682, x₃ = 2.702","sol":"x₁ = ∛(4 × 2 + 9) = ∛17 = 2.5712816… = 2.571 (M1 for a correct first substitution, A1 for the value). Feeding each answer back in, x₂ = ∛(4 × 2.5712816… + 9) = 2.6816834… = 2.682 and x₃ = ∛(4 × 2.6816834… + 9) = 2.7019982… = 2.702 (A1 for both, using the unrounded values).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Each term is made by putting the previous term into the right-hand side of the formula.","Start with the given value of x₀, then use the calculator's answer key so the full value, not the rounded one, goes back in each time.","Substitute x₀ into ∛(4x + 9) to get x₁, and keep that number on the display."],"codes":["A20"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve the inequality x² − 2x − 15 ≥ 0, giving the solution set in set notation.","a":"{x : x ≤ −3} ∪ {x : x ≥ 5}","sol":"Factorise: x² − 2x − 15 = (x − 5)(x + 3), so the critical values are x = 5 and x = −3 (M1). The coefficient of x² is positive, so the curve is U-shaped and lies on or above the x-axis outside the two crossings (M1). The solution set is therefore {x : x ≤ −3} ∪ {x : x ≥ 5}, with ≤ and ≥ because the end values make the expression zero, which satisfies ≥ 0 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["One side is already zero, so the shape of the curve decides the answer.","Factorise to find the two critical values, then decide whether the U-shaped curve is above the axis between them or outside them.","Factorise x² − 2x − 15 and write down the values of x that make each bracket zero."],"codes":["A22"]},{"q":"(a calculator may be used) x and y are integers with x ≥ 1, y > 2 and x + y < 6. Write down all the points (x, y) that satisfy all three inequalities.","a":"(1, 3), (1, 4) and (2, 3)","sol":"Take the possible values of x in turn. For x = 1: y > 2 and y < 5, so y = 3 or y = 4 (M1 for a correct systematic test of one value of x). For x = 2: y > 2 and y < 4, so y = 3; for x = 3: y > 2 and y < 3, which no integer satisfies, and any larger x leaves no room at all (M1). The points inside the region are therefore (1, 3), (1, 4) and (2, 3) (A1 for all three and no extras).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The three inequalities cut out a small triangular region, and only whole-number points inside it count.","Work through the possible values of x one at a time, using the third inequality to put an upper limit on y each time; take care that two of the signs are strict.","Start with x = 1 and write down the range of y that the other two inequalities allow."],"codes":["A22"]},{"q":"(a calculator may be used) A rectangle has length (2x + 1) cm and width (x − 3) cm. Its area is 50 cm². Work out the value of x, correct to 2 decimal places.","a":"x = 6.55","sol":"Area = length × width, so (2x + 1)(x − 3) = 50 (M1). Expanding gives 2x² − 5x − 3 = 50, so 2x² − 5x − 53 = 0 (M1). With a = 2, b = −5 and c = −53 the formula gives x = (5 ± √(25 + 424))/4 = (5 ± √449)/4 (M1). √449 = 21.1896201…, so x = 6.5474050… or x = −4.0474050…; the width x − 3 must be positive, so the negative root is rejected and x = 6.55 (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Turn the area statement into an equation before doing anything else.","Multiply the two bracketed lengths together, set the product equal to the area and collect everything on one side; then choose a solving method that works on any quadratic.","Write (2x + 1)(x − 3) = 50 and expand the left-hand side."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The line y = 2x + k is a tangent to the curve y = x² + 6x + 10. Work out the value of k.","a":"k = 6","sol":"Where the line meets the curve, x² + 6x + 10 = 2x + k (M1). Rearranging to make one side zero gives x² + 4x + (10 − k) = 0 (M1). A tangent touches the curve exactly once, so this quadratic has one repeated root and its discriminant is zero: 4² − 4 × 1 × (10 − k) = 0 (M1). So 16 − 40 + 4k = 0, giving 4k = 24 and k = 6 (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A tangent meets a curve at one point only, so think about how many solutions the simultaneous equations have.","Set the two expressions equal and rearrange to a quadratic in x; the number of times the line meets the curve is controlled by the expression under the root in the quadratic formula.","Write x² + 6x + 10 = 2x + k and collect all the terms on the left."],"codes":["A19","A18"]},{"q":"(a calculator may be used) f(x) = x³ + 3x² − 2. Show that f(x) = 0 has a solution between x = 0 and x = 1, and then use the iterative formula xₙ₊₁ = √(2 ÷ (xₙ + 3)) with x₀ = 1 to find that solution correct to 3 decimal places.","a":"x = 0.732","sol":"f(0) = −2 and f(1) = 1 + 3 − 2 = 2 (M1 for both values). The signs are opposite and the graph of a cubic is continuous, so the curve crosses the x-axis between x = 0 and x = 1 (A1 for the sign-change conclusion). Then x₁ = √(2 ÷ (1 + 3)) = √0.5 = 0.7071068… (M1 for a correct first iteration). Continuing, x₂ = √(2 ÷ 3.7071068…) = 0.7345096…, x₃ = 0.7318098… and x₄ = 0.7320744… (A1 for a correct chain as far as x₃). The terms x₃ and x₄ agree to 3 decimal places, so the solution is x = 0.732 (A1).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The question has two separate jobs: first place the root, then close in on it.","Substitute both end values into f(x) and compare the signs; then run the iteration from the given starting value, keeping the full display value each time.","Work out f(0) and f(1), then substitute x₀ into √(2 ÷ (x + 3))."],"codes":["A20"]},{"q":"(a calculator may be used) A ball is thrown upwards. Its height h metres above the ground after t seconds is given by h = 20t − 5t², for 0 ≤ t ≤ 4. Find the set of times for which the ball is more than 15 m above the ground, giving your answer as an inequality in t.","a":"1 < t < 3","sol":"More than 15 m means 20t − 5t² > 15 (M1 for forming the inequality). Collecting on one side, 0 > 5t² − 20t + 15, and dividing by the positive number 5 gives t² − 4t + 3 < 0 (M1). Factorising, (t − 1)(t − 3) < 0, so the critical values are t = 1 and t = 3 (M1). The coefficient of t² is positive, so the curve is U-shaped and lies below the axis between the critical values; the ball is therefore above 15 m for 1 < t < 3 (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The condition in the question turns into an inequality with t² in it.","Put the height expression against 15, collect everything on one side and factorise; then use the shape of the curve to pick out the right interval.","Write 20t − 5t² > 15 and move every term to one side."],"codes":["A22"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The line y = 4x − 3 crosses the curve y = x² + 2x − 3 at the points A and B. Work out the exact length of AB, giving your answer in the form k√n with n as small as possible.","a":"AB = 2√17","sol":"At the crossing points x² + 2x − 3 = 4x − 3 (M1). Rearranging gives x² − 2x = 0, so x(x − 2) = 0 (M1) and x = 0 or x = 2 (A1). Substituting into y = 4x − 3 gives y = −3 and y = 5, so A is (0, −3) and B is (2, 5) (A1). By Pythagoras, AB² = (2 − 0)² + (5 − (−3))² = 4 + 64 = 68, so AB = √68 = √4 × √17 = 2√17 (A1).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Find where the two graphs meet before thinking about any distance.","Solve the line and the curve together to get the coordinates of both points, then treat the gap between them as the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle.","Set x² + 2x − 3 equal to 4x − 3 and collect the terms on one side."],"codes":["A19"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write x² − 6x + 1 in the form (x + a)² + b.","a":"(x − 3)² − 8"},{"q":"Solve the simultaneous equations y = x² − 3x + 4 and y = x + 1 algebraically.","a":"x = 1, y = 2 and x = 3, y = 4"},{"q":"Solve the inequality x² − 16 < 0, giving the solution set in set notation.","a":"{x : −4 < x < 4}"},{"q":"Using the iterative formula xₙ₊₁ = ∛(2xₙ + 5) with x₀ = 2, work out x₁ correct to 3 decimal places.","a":"x₁ = 2.080"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Completing the square for x² + bx + c","back":"x² + bx + c = (x + b/2)² − (b/2)² + c. Halve the coefficient of x to fill the bracket, then subtract the square of that half to undo what the bracket added.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"Completing the square when the coefficient of x² is not 1","back":"Take a out of the x² and x terms first, complete the square inside, then multiply back out: 2x² + 12x + 7 = 2(x² + 6x) + 7 = 2(x + 3)² − 11.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"Solving from the completed square","back":"(x + p)² = k gives x + p = ±√k, so x = −p ± √k. Both signs must be kept, and leaving √k as a surd gives the exact answer.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"The quadratic formula","back":"The solution of ax² + bx + c = 0, where a ≠ 0, is x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/2a. It is printed on the Higher Exam Aid.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"The discriminant b² − 4ac","back":"Positive: two different real roots. Zero: one repeated root, so the curve touches the x-axis. Negative: no real roots, and the formula cannot be finished.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"The first move with any quadratic equation","back":"Rearrange until one side is zero, in the form ax² + bx + c = 0. Only then can it be factorised, completed or substituted into the formula.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"Choosing a method for a quadratic equation","back":"Factorise if it factorises; complete the square when an exact answer or the form p ± √q is asked for; use the formula when neither works or a decimal answer is wanted.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"Solving linear/quadratic simultaneous equations","back":"Substitute the linear equation into the quadratic, rearrange to = 0 and solve it; then put each value of x back into the linear equation and pair the answers.","code":"A19","open":false},{"front":"What a linear/quadratic solution means on the graphs","back":"Each pair of values is a point where the line meets the curve. Two pairs: the line cuts the curve twice. One repeated pair: the line is a tangent. No real solutions: they never meet.","code":"A19","open":false},{"front":"The sign-change test for a root","back":"If f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs and the graph of f is continuous between them, the curve crosses the x-axis, so f(x) = 0 has a solution between a and b.","code":"A20","open":false},{"front":"Using an iterative formula","back":"Put x₀ into the right-hand side of xₙ₊₁ = g(xₙ) to get x₁, then feed each answer back. Keep the full calculator value each time and stop when consecutive terms agree to the accuracy asked.","code":"A20","open":false},{"front":"Give one rearrangement of x³ + 5x − 12 = 0 into the form x = g(x)","back":"x = (12 − x³)/5; x = ∛(12 − 5x); x = 12/(x² + 5). Any rearrangement that leaves a single x on the left and the rest in terms of x on the right can be iterated.","code":"A20","open":true},{"front":"Solving a quadratic inequality","back":"Rearrange to zero, factorise for the critical values, then read a U-shaped sketch: the expression is negative between the roots and positive outside them.","code":"A22","open":false},{"front":"Set notation for a solution set","back":"{x : x > 3} means all values of x greater than 3. Two separate intervals are joined with ∪, as in {x : x ≤ −3} ∪ {x : x ≥ 5}.","code":"A22","open":false},{"front":"Showing inequalities in two variables on a graph","back":"Draw each boundary line dashed for < or > and solid for ≤ or ≥, test a point such as (0, 0) to find the right side, and shade or label the region satisfying all of them.","code":"A22","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Given on the Higher Tier Exam Aid (W87991A): the quadratic formula — the solution of ax² + bx + c = 0 where a ≠ 0 is x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/2a. It is one of the two things on the Higher sheet that this room uses, and it is not on the Foundation sheet at all.","Given, but still your work: the formula is printed, not the setting up. Rearranging the equation into ax² + bx + c = 0 and reading off a, b and c with their signs earns the method mark, and a sign slip in c loses it.","Given on the Higher Tier Exam Aid (W87991A): Pythagoras' theorem — in any right-angled triangle where a, b and c are the lengths of the sides and c is the hypotenuse, a² + b² = c². This room needs it when a line meets a curve and the question asks for the distance between the two points of intersection.","Recall: the length of the line segment joining (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂) is √((x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²) — Pythagoras applied to the horizontal and the vertical gap. This form is on neither Exam Aid; only the a² + b² = c² it is built from is printed.","Recall: completing the square, x² + bx + c = (x + b/2)² − (b/2)² + c — not on either Exam Aid.","Recall: when the coefficient of x² is not 1, take it out of the x² and x terms first — a(x² + (b/a)x) + c — then complete the square inside the bracket.","Recall: (x + p)² = k gives x = −p ± √k, keeping both signs.","Recall: the discriminant b² − 4ac and what it tells you — two roots if positive, one repeated root (a tangent) if zero, none if negative. The Exam Aid prints the formula but says nothing about the discriminant.","Recall: for linear/quadratic simultaneous equations, substitute the linear equation into the quadratic, solve, and substitute back into the linear equation to pair the values.","Recall: the sign-change test — opposite signs for f(a) and f(b) on a continuous graph mean a root lies between a and b.","Recall: the iteration process xₙ₊₁ = g(xₙ) and the habit of keeping the full calculator value between terms. No iterative formula is printed; a question that needs one gives it to you.","Recall: the quadratic-inequality rule — a positive x² expression is negative between its roots and positive outside them.","Recall: set notation, {x : a < x < b}, with ∪ joining two intervals, and the number-line convention of an open circle for < or > and a filled circle for ≤ or ≥.","Recall: the boundary conventions for a region — dashed line for a strict inequality, solid line for ≤ or ≥, with a test point deciding which side is wanted.","The rest of the Higher Exam Aid — area of a trapezium, volume of a prism, circumference and area of a circle, the trigonometric ratios, the sine and cosine rules, area = ½ab sin C, compound interest and the two probability results — has nothing to offer this room, except where a question wraps its equation inside one of those contexts."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T12:08:38Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Mathematically clean — all 16 bank questions, the 4 exit items and the 4 lesson checks re-solved and every answer key, form and accuracy is right, with mark totals matching the M/A lines; the only fixes needed are on the Formulae sheet, which under-claims what the Higher Exam Aid gives the last question and omits the coordinate-distance result that question needs."}}},"algebra-sequences-higher":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"algebra-sequences-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Everything in this room is Higher-tier only, and at Higher tier Algebra carries 27–33% of the marks. Sequences appear in every series: a quadratic sequence to be turned into an nth term, or a geometric progression whose common ratio is a surd. The Foundation work on linear sequences, the named families and geometric progressions with a rational ratio is assumed here; what is added is the surd ratio, sequences set by any consistent rule, and the second-difference method that produces an² + bn + c. These questions are also a favourite home for reasoning and problem-solving marks — AO2 and AO3 together carry 60% of the Higher marks — so you are asked to find a position, show that a term is a whole number, or match two sequences, not just to continue a list.","idea":[{"h":"Geometric progressions with a surd ratio, and other sequences","t":"At Foundation the common ratio of a geometric progression is a positive rational number; the Higher list adds \"or a surd\", so r can be √2, √5 or 1/√2. The method does not change — r = any term ÷ the term before it, and each new term is the previous term × r — but the arithmetic needs the surd rules. Because √a × √a = a, two steps along a progression with ratio √a multiply a term by a, which is why 2, 2√2, 4, 4√2, 8, … alternates between whole numbers and surds and why you can jump two places at a time when a much later term is wanted. A ratio of 1/√2 is handled the same way: two steps halve a term, and a first term found by dividing, such as 6 ÷ √3, is written exactly by rationalising. The Higher list also adds \"and other sequences\", so a question may set any sequence with a consistent rule — fractions such as n/(2n + 1), alternating signs, or a term-to-term rule of its own. With fractions, read the numerators and the denominators as two separate sequences, and always test a rule you have deduced against a term you were given.","code":"A24"},{"h":"Deducing the nth term of a quadratic sequence","t":"The Higher list extends A25 to \"the nth term of linear and quadratic sequences\". A quadratic sequence is the one whose first differences are not constant but whose second differences are, and that second difference D is twice the coefficient of n²: the n² part of the nth term is (D/2)n². The method follows from that fact. Work out the row of first differences, then the second difference; write down an² with a = D/2; subtract an² from every term; what is left is always a linear sequence, and its nth term bn + c completes the answer as an² + bn + c. A falling sequence has a negative second difference and so a negative a. Mark schemes give a method mark for reaching the an² part, a second for subtracting it and finding the linear remainder, and the accuracy mark for the full expression, so the difference rows are written down, not done in your head. Check by substituting a position back in, and note that the same expression answers \"which term equals V?\" — set an² + bn + c = V, solve the quadratic, and keep only a positive whole-number n.","code":"A25"}],"examples":[{"title":"Continuing a geometric progression whose ratio is a surd","setup":"The first three terms of a geometric progression are 3, 3√5 and 15. Work out the common ratio of the progression and the 5th term, giving each as an exact value.","steps":["Divide one term by the term before it: 3√5 ÷ 3 = √5, so r = √5.","Test that ratio on the next pair: 3√5 × √5 = 3 × 5 = 15, which is the third term given, so the ratio is right.","The 4th term is the 3rd term × the ratio: 15 × √5 = 15√5.","The 5th term is 15√5 × √5 = 15 × 5 = 75, so the common ratio is √5 and the 5th term is 75."],"cues":["How is the common ratio of any geometric progression found?","What does 3√5 × √5 simplify to, and does it match the term you were given?","What must be done to the 3rd term to reach the 4th?","What happens to the surd when 15√5 is multiplied by √5, and which two values does the setup ask you to give?"]},{"title":"The nth term of a quadratic sequence from its second difference","setup":"Here are the first five terms of a quadratic sequence: 5, 12, 23, 38, 57. Find an expression, in terms of n, for the nth term of this sequence.","steps":["First differences: 12 − 5 = 7, 23 − 12 = 11, 38 − 23 = 15, 57 − 38 = 19.","That row rises by 4 each time, so the second difference is 4 and the n² part is (4 ÷ 2)n² = 2n².","Write 2n² for n = 1 to 5: 2, 8, 18, 32, 50, and subtract term by term: 3, 4, 5, 6, 7.","3, 4, 5, 6, 7 is linear with difference 1 and would be 2 at position zero, so its nth term is n + 2.","Putting the parts together, the nth term is 2n² + n + 2; check n = 4: 32 + 4 + 2 = 38 ✓."],"cues":["What do you work out first when a sequence is not going up by a fixed amount?","By how much does the row of differences change, and what does that make the coefficient of n²?","What is left when 2n² is taken away from each term?","What is the nth term of the linear sequence that remains?","How are the two parts combined, and how can the rule be checked?"]},{"title":"A quadratic sequence that turns downwards","setup":"The first five terms of a quadratic sequence are 10, 8, 2, −8, −22. Find an expression, in terms of n, for the nth term of this sequence.","steps":["First differences: 8 − 10 = −2, 2 − 8 = −6, −8 − 2 = −10, −22 − (−8) = −14.","Each first difference falls by 4, so the second difference is −4 and the n² part is (−4 ÷ 2)n² = −2n².","Subtracting −2n² means adding 2n² = 2, 8, 18, 32, 50 to the terms, which leaves 12, 16, 20, 24, 28.","12, 16, 20, 24, 28 is linear with difference 4 and would be 8 at position zero, so its nth term is 4n + 8.","So the nth term is −2n² + 4n + 8; check n = 3: −18 + 12 + 8 = 2 ✓."],"cues":["What is the first row to write down for a sequence that is not linear?","The differences are falling — what does that do to the sign of the coefficient of n²?","What must be done to each term to strip the −2n² part out?","What is the nth term of the linear sequence left behind?","How do the two parts combine, and does the rule reproduce a term you were given?"]},{"title":"Reading the nth term of a sequence of fractions","setup":"Here are the first four terms of a sequence: 1/3, 2/5, 3/7, 4/9. Find an expression, in terms of n, for the nth term of this sequence, and use it to work out the 20th term.","steps":["Take the numerators on their own: 1, 2, 3, 4 are the position numbers, so the numerator is n.","Now the denominators: 3, 5, 7, 9 go up by 2 each time, a linear sequence with nth term 2n + 1.","Combining them, the nth term is n/(2n + 1); check n = 3: 3/(6 + 1) = 3/7 ✓, so the 20th term can be found by substitution.","Substituting n = 20 gives 20/(2 × 20 + 1) = 20/41."],"cues":["What pattern do the tops of the fractions follow?","The bottoms go up in equal steps — what is their nth term?","How do the two rules combine into a single expression?","Which value of n gives the 20th term?"]},{"title":"A tile pattern that grows as a quadratic sequence","setup":"A pattern of tiles grows so that pattern 1 uses 2 tiles, pattern 2 uses 6 tiles, pattern 3 uses 12 tiles and pattern 4 uses 20 tiles. The numbers of tiles form a quadratic sequence. Work out how many tiles pattern 15 uses.","steps":["First differences: 6 − 2 = 4, 12 − 6 = 6, 20 − 12 = 8.","That row rises by 2, so the second difference is 2 and the n² part is (2 ÷ 2)n² = n².","Subtract n² = 1, 4, 9, 16 from the terms and 1, 2, 3, 4 is left, a linear sequence with nth term n.","So pattern n uses n² + n tiles; check pattern 4: 16 + 4 = 20 ✓, so pattern 15 can now be found by substitution.","Substituting n = 15: 15² + 15 = 225 + 15 = 240 tiles."],"cues":["What do the differences between the numbers of tiles do?","What does a second difference of 2 make the coefficient of n²?","What is left after n² is taken from each term?","What rule gives the tiles in pattern n, and does it reproduce a pattern you were given?","Which value of n is needed for pattern 15?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A geometric progression has common ratio √3. What happens to a term when you move two places along the progression?","a":"It is multiplied by 3, because √3 × √3 = 3."},{"q":"The second difference of a quadratic sequence is 10. What is the n² part of its nth term?","a":"5n², because the coefficient of n² is 10 ÷ 2 = 5."},{"q":"After subtracting the an² part from a quadratic sequence, what kind of sequence is left and what does it give you?","a":"A linear sequence; its nth term is the bn + c part of the answer."},{"q":"Find an expression, in terms of n, for the nth term of the quadratic sequence 6, 15, 28, 45, 66.","a":"2n² + 3n + 1 (second difference 4, so 2n²; the remainder 4, 7, 10, 13, 16 has nth term 3n + 1)."}]},"room":{"facts":["In a geometric progression each term is the previous term × the common ratio r, and r = any term ÷ the term before it; at Higher tier r may be a surd such as √2, √5 or 1/√2.","√a × √a = a, so in a progression with ratio √a each term is a times the term two places before it — and when the first term is a whole number and √a is irrational, the terms alternate between whole numbers and multiples of √a.","A linear sequence has a constant first difference; a quadratic sequence has a constant second difference D, and the n² part of its nth term is (D/2)n².","Method for the nth term of a quadratic sequence: find D; write an² with a = D/2; subtract an² from every term; find the nth term bn + c of the linear sequence left; the answer is an² + bn + c.","The nth term of a quadratic sequence always has the form an² + bn + c with a ≠ 0, and a deduced rule is checked by substituting a position back in.","To find the position of a value V, solve an² + bn + c = V; only a positive whole-number solution is a position, and any other solution means V is not a term.","At Higher tier a sequence may be set by any consistent rule, so with fractional terms read the numerators and the denominators as two separate sequences."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) The first three terms of a geometric progression are 3, 3√7 and 21. Write down the common ratio of the progression and work out the 4th term.","a":"r = √7 and the 4th term is 21√7","sol":"Divide a term by the one before it: 3√7 ÷ 3 = √7, so r = √7 (B1). (Check: 3√7 × √7 = 3 × 7 = 21 ✓.) The 4th term is 21 × √7 = 21√7 (B1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Consecutive terms of a geometric progression are linked by the same multiplier, even when that multiplier is a surd.","Divide one term by the term before it to get the ratio, then multiply the last term you are given by that ratio.","Work out 3√7 ÷ 3, then multiply the third term by whatever that comes to."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Here are the first five terms of a quadratic sequence: 4, 9, 18, 31, 48. Work out the second difference of this sequence.","a":"4","sol":"First differences: 9 − 4 = 5, 18 − 9 = 9, 31 − 18 = 13, 48 − 31 = 17 (M1). That row goes up by the same amount each time, so the second difference is 4 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A quadratic sequence is recognised by what happens to its differences, not to its terms.","Work out the differences between consecutive terms, then the differences between those differences.","Write down 9 − 4, 18 − 9, 31 − 18 and 48 − 31, then look at how that row changes."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The nth term of a quadratic sequence is n² + 3n − 1. Work out the 6th term of this sequence.","a":"53","sol":"Substitute n = 6 into the rule: 6² + 3 × 6 − 1 (M1) = 36 + 18 − 1 = 53 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["An nth term rule gives any term straight from its position, with no need for the terms in between.","Replace every n in the expression by the position number, then work the arithmetic out in the usual order.","Write n² + 3n − 1 with n replaced by 6, and deal with the power first."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A geometric progression has first term 7 and common ratio √2. Write down the 2nd term of the progression, and work out the 3rd term as an integer.","a":"2nd term 7√2; 3rd term 14","sol":"2nd term = 7 × √2 = 7√2 (B1). 3rd term = 7√2 × √2 = 7 × 2 = 14 (B1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Each new term of a geometric progression comes from multiplying the term before it by the common ratio.","Multiply the first term by √2 for the second term, then multiply that answer by √2 again.","The third term is 7 × √2 × √2, and a surd multiplied by itself simplifies."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The nth term of a sequence is n/(n + 3). Work out the 5th term of this sequence, giving your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"5/8","sol":"Substitute the position into the top and the bottom: 5/(5 + 3) (M1) = 5/8, which has no common factor to cancel (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The rule gives a term directly from its position number, top and bottom together.","Substitute the position into the numerator and into the denominator, then cancel the fraction if you can.","Write n/(n + 3) with n replaced by 5, then work the bottom out."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Here are the first five terms of a quadratic sequence: 3, 10, 21, 36, 55. Find an expression, in terms of n, for the nth term of this sequence.","a":"2n² + n","sol":"First differences 7, 11, 15, 19, so the second difference is 4 and the n² part is 4 ÷ 2 = 2, giving 2n² (M1). Subtract 2n² = 2, 8, 18, 32, 50 from the terms to leave 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, a linear sequence with nth term n (M1). So the nth term is 2n² + n (A1); check n = 3: 18 + 3 = 21 ✓.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The terms do not go up by the same amount, so the differences of the differences are the way in.","Halve the second difference for the coefficient of n², subtract that part from every term, then find the nth term of what is left.","Write down the first differences 7, 11, 15, 19 and then the second difference."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Find an expression, in terms of n, for the nth term of the quadratic sequence 2, 11, 26, 47, 74.","a":"3n² − 1","sol":"First differences 9, 15, 21, 27, so the second difference is 6 and the n² part is 6 ÷ 2 = 3, giving 3n² (M1). Subtract 3n² = 3, 12, 27, 48, 75 from the terms: every result is −1, so the linear remainder is the constant −1 (M1). The nth term is 3n² − 1 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A sequence rising by ever larger steps is quadratic, and its second difference fixes the n² part.","Halve the second difference for the coefficient of n², subtract that part from each term, and read off what is left.","Write down the first differences 9, 15, 21, 27 and then the second difference."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The first three terms of a geometric progression are 4, 4√3 and 12. Work out the 6th term of the progression, giving your answer in the form a√3.","a":"36√3","sol":"r = 4√3 ÷ 4 = √3 (M1). Continuing: 4th term = 12 × √3 = 12√3, 5th term = 12√3 × √3 = 36, 6th term = 36 × √3 (M1). So the 6th term is 36√3 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The ratio here is a surd, so the terms alternate between whole numbers and multiples of that surd.","Find the ratio by dividing the second term by the first, then keep multiplying until you reach the term asked for.","Work out 4√3 ÷ 4, then multiply the third term by that ratio."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A geometric progression has first term 32 and common ratio 1/√2. Work out the 5th term of this progression.","a":"8","sol":"Multiplying twice by 1/√2 multiplies by 1/2, because √2 × √2 = 2 (M1). Going from the 1st term to the 5th term is four multiplications, that is two halvings: 32 ÷ 2 = 16 and 16 ÷ 2 = 8 (M1). The 5th term is 8 (A1). [Full progression: 32, 16√2, 16, 8√2, 8.]","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A ratio of 1/√2 shrinks the progression, so think about what two of those steps do together.","Either multiply by 1/√2 four times, or use the fact that two steps make one halving and count the steps from the 1st term to the 5th.","Write down 1/√2 × 1/√2 and simplify it before counting the steps."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The nth term of a sequence is 60 − n². Work out the first term of this sequence that is negative, and write down its position.","a":"−4, which is the 8th term","sol":"A term is negative when 60 − n² < 0, that is when n² > 60 (M1). 7² = 49 and 8² = 64, so n = 8 is the first position where n² is greater than 60 (M1). That term is 60 − 64 = −4, the 8th term (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Squaring grows quickly, so at some position n² overtakes the 60 in the rule.","Decide which values of n make 60 − n² negative, take the smallest of them, and then work that term out.","Set up the condition 60 − n² < 0 and rearrange it to compare n² with 60."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Here are the first four terms of a sequence: 1/4, 2/7, 3/10, 4/13. Find an expression, in terms of n, for the nth term of this sequence.","a":"n/(3n + 1)","sol":"The numerators 1, 2, 3, 4 are the position numbers, so the numerator is n (B1). The denominators 4, 7, 10, 13 go up by 3 each time and would be 1 at position zero, so their nth term is 3n + 1 (M1). The nth term is therefore n/(3n + 1) (A1); check n = 4: 4/(12 + 1) = 4/13 ✓.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Treat the tops and the bottoms of the fractions as two sequences of their own.","Find the nth term of the numerators and the nth term of the denominators, then write them as one fraction.","The numerators are 1, 2, 3, 4; now look at the constant difference of the denominators 4, 7, 10, 13."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The first three terms of a geometric progression are 2, 2√6 and 12. Show that the 7th term of the progression is a whole number, and work out its value.","a":"432","sol":"r = 2√6 ÷ 2 = √6 (M1). Since √6 × √6 = 6, moving two places along multiplies a term by the whole number 6, so every odd-numbered term is a whole number (M1). The 7th term is three such double steps from the 1st term: 2 × 6³ (M1) = 2 × 216 = 432 (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Whether a term is a whole number depends on how many times the surd ratio has been applied to it.","Find the ratio, work out what two steps along the progression do to a term, then count how many double steps reach the 7th term.","Work out 2√6 ÷ 2, then square that ratio."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) A shop records its sales of a new game. In month 1 it sells 4 copies, in month 2 it sells 10 copies, in month 3 it sells 18 copies and in month 4 it sells 28 copies. The monthly sales form a quadratic sequence. (a) Find an expression, in terms of n, for the number of copies sold in month n. (b) Work out the month in which the shop sells 130 copies.","a":"(a) n² + 3n  (b) month 10","sol":"(a) First differences 6, 8, 10 give a second difference of 2, so the n² part is 2 ÷ 2 = 1, that is n² (M1). Subtract n² = 1, 4, 9, 16 from the terms to leave 3, 6, 9, 12, a linear sequence with nth term 3n (M1). Month n has sales n² + 3n (A1). (b) Set n² + 3n = 130, so n² + 3n − 130 = 0, which factorises as (n + 13)(n − 10) = 0 (M1). A month number must be positive, so n = 10: the shop sells 130 copies in month 10 (A1).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The monthly sales are not going up by a fixed amount, so the differences between them hold the pattern.","Use the second difference to build the nth term in part (a), then set that expression equal to the number of copies and solve for n in part (b).","Write down the first differences 6, 8, 10 and then the second difference."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) Sequence A is the quadratic sequence 4, 13, 26, 43, 64, … Sequence B has nth term 13n + 11. There is exactly one position at which the two sequences have the same term. Work out that position and the value of the term.","a":"Position 6; the term is 89","sol":"Sequence A has first differences 9, 13, 17, 21, so the second difference is 4 and the n² part is 2n² (M1). Subtracting 2n² = 2, 8, 18, 32, 50 leaves 2, 5, 8, 11, 14, a linear sequence with nth term 3n − 1 (M1), so A has nth term 2n² + 3n − 1 (A1). Setting the nth terms equal: 2n² + 3n − 1 = 13n + 11 (M1), which rearranges to 2n² − 10n − 12 = 0, or n² − 5n − 6 = 0, so (n − 6)(n + 1) = 0 (M1). A position must be positive, so n = 6 and the common term is 13 × 6 + 11 = 89 (A1).","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["The two sequences can only meet where their nth terms are equal, so sequence A needs a rule of its own first.","Deduce the nth term of A from its second difference, set it equal to the rule for B, and solve the quadratic equation that appears.","Write down A's first differences 9, 13, 17, 21 and then the second difference."],"codes":["A25","A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The 2nd term of a geometric progression is 6 and the 4th term is 18. The common ratio is positive. Work out the common ratio and the first term, giving each as an exact value.","a":"r = √3 and the first term is 2√3","sol":"Going from the 2nd term to the 4th term multiplies by r twice, so r² = 18 ÷ 6 = 3 (M1). The ratio is positive, so r = √3 (A1). The first term is the 2nd term divided by the ratio: 6 ÷ √3 (M1). Rationalising, 6/√3 = 6√3/3 = 2√3 (A1). [Check: 2√3 × √3 = 2 × 3 = 6 ✓.]","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Two steps along a geometric progression multiply a term by the ratio twice over.","Divide the 4th term by the 2nd to get r², take the positive square root, then step backwards from the 2nd term to the 1st.","Write down 18 ÷ 6 as the value of r², and remember that stepping backwards means dividing, not multiplying."],"codes":["A24"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write down the common ratio of the geometric progression 5, 5√2, 10, 10√2, …","a":"√2"},{"q":"The nth term of a quadratic sequence is n² + 4n. Work out the position of the term whose value is 45.","a":"n = 5 (the 5th term)"},{"q":"Find an expression, in terms of n, for the nth term of the quadratic sequence 2, 8, 18, 32, 50.","a":"2n²"},{"q":"The nth term of a sequence is n/(3n + 2). Work out the 4th term, giving your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"2/7"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Common ratio of a geometric progression","back":"The number each term is multiplied by to reach the next: r = any term ÷ the term before it. At Higher tier r may be a fraction or a surd.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"A geometric progression with common ratio √k","back":"Its terms are a, a√k, ak, ak√k, ak², … Two steps along multiply a term by k, because √k × √k = k; if a is a whole number and √k irrational, whole numbers and multiples of √k alternate.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"The result of √a × √a","back":"It equals a. This is why a surd common ratio produces a whole-number term every second step of the progression.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"The sequence rⁿ","back":"The geometric progression r, r², r³, r⁴, … whose nth term is rⁿ; its first term and its common ratio are both r, and at Higher tier r may be a surd.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"\"And other sequences\" — the Higher tier addition to A24","back":"Any sequence set by a consistent rule may be examined, not only the named families: fractions such as n/(2n + 1), alternating signs, or a term-to-term rule of the question's own.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Rationalising a surd ratio such as 1/√2","back":"Multiply top and bottom by √2: 1/√2 = √2/2. Two steps at this ratio halve a term, since √2 × √2 = 2.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Second difference of a quadratic sequence","back":"The constant difference between consecutive first differences. It is twice the coefficient of n², so the n² part of the nth term is (second difference ÷ 2)n².","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"First differences of a quadratic sequence","back":"They are not constant: they themselves form a linear sequence, rising or falling by the same amount each time.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"Method for the nth term of a quadratic sequence","back":"Find the second difference D; take an² with a = D/2; subtract an² from every term; find the nth term bn + c of the linear sequence left; write an² + bn + c.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"General form of the nth term of a quadratic sequence","back":"an² + bn + c with a ≠ 0, where a comes from the second difference and b and c from the linear sequence left after an² is removed.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"Sign of the coefficient of n² when a quadratic sequence turns downwards","back":"Negative. Falling first differences give a negative second difference, so a = D/2 is negative, as in −3n² + 2n + 7.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"Checking a deduced nth term","back":"Substitute n = 1 and one other position and confirm the rule reproduces the terms given. A slip in the second difference shows up immediately.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"Finding the position of a value in a quadratic sequence","back":"Set the nth term equal to the value and solve the quadratic. Only a positive whole-number solution is a position; anything else means the value is not a term.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"Give one sequence whose nth term is quadratic","back":"The square numbers, nth term n²; the triangular numbers, nth term ½n(n + 1); the sequence 2, 6, 12, 20 with nth term n² + n.","code":"A25","open":true},{"front":"Name two ways to spot that a sequence is geometric","back":"Each term ÷ the one before it gives the same number every time; the terms follow the shape a, ar, ar², ar³, …; the first differences are not constant but grow in the same proportion as the terms.","code":"A24","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["No sequence result of any kind is printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid (W87991A), or on either tier's sheet, so every rule below must be written from memory; the one printed result that can be used in this room is the quadratic formula.","Remember: in a geometric progression each term is the previous term × r, and r = any term ÷ the term before it; at Higher tier r may be a surd (A24).","Remember: √a × √a = a, so two steps along a progression with ratio √a multiply a term by a; and 1/√a = √a/a when a ratio has to be written exactly (A24).","Remember: the sequence rⁿ is r, r², r³, r⁴, … (A24).","Remember: a quadratic sequence has a constant second difference D, and the n² part of its nth term is (D/2)n² (A25).","Remember: the nth term of a quadratic sequence is an² + bn + c — find a = D/2, subtract an² from every term, and take bn + c from the linear sequence left (A25).","Remember: to find a position, set an² + bn + c equal to the value and solve; only a positive whole-number n is a position (A25).","What the Higher Tier Exam Aid does print: the quadratic formula x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/2a, which is the one printed result that can be used in this room; nothing else on the sheet is needed here — area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h; volume of a prism = area of cross section × length; circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd; area of a circle = πr²; a² + b² = c²; sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b; the sine rule a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C; the cosine rule a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A; area of a triangle = ½ab sin C; total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ; P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B); P(A and B) = P(A given B)P(B).","The quadratic formula x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/2a is printed on the Higher sheet, so a position question whose equation will not factorise can still be finished from the sheet; factorising is quicker where it works."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T12:10:37Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Mathematically clean throughout - all 15 answer keys, every sol line, the five worked examples, the lesson checks and the exit ticket re-solved and confirmed, with 12 non-calculator items whose numbers work by hand and genuine Higher-only content (surd common ratios, second-difference nth terms); fix the self-contradictory sentence in the Formulae sheet and swap the duplicated exit-ticket question before publishing."}}},"geometry-properties-higher":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"geometry-properties-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Higher tier adds three pieces of geometry that Foundation never meets: enlargement by a negative scale factor, describing what a combination of transformations changes and what it leaves invariant, and the standard circle theorems with their proofs. Together they carry marks on all three papers, and the circle theorems in particular are the board's favourite setting for AO2 chains of reasoning, where a correct angle with no theorem quoted loses the reasoning marks. This room assumes the Foundation properties room: angle facts, special quadrilaterals, congruence, the four transformations and the parts of a circle are the tools these new results are built from.","idea":[{"h":"Enlargement by a negative scale factor","code":"G7","t":"At Higher tier the scale factor of an enlargement may be negative as well as fractional. The image of a point P under an enlargement of scale factor k about centre C is C + k × (P − C): the step from the centre to the point is multiplied by k and taken again from the centre. When k is negative that step reverses, so the image lands on the opposite side of the centre and the whole shape is turned through 180°, while every length is multiplied by the size of k. Scale factor −2 doubles the lengths and inverts the shape; −1/2 halves them. A full description still names three things — enlargement, the scale factor with its sign, and the centre — and the centre is found by joining each point to its image and extending until the lines cross, which for a negative factor happens between the object and the image."},{"h":"Combining transformations, and what stays put","code":"G8","t":"G8 is Higher only: describe the changes and the invariance achieved by combinations of rotations, reflections and translations. Each of the three is a congruence, so lengths, angles and area survive every combination and the image is always congruent to the object; a reflection also reverses the sense, so an odd number of reflections leaves the shape mirror-imaged. An invariant point maps to itself and an invariant line is one whose points all stay on it — the centre of a rotation, every point of a mirror line, and the centre of an enlargement are the usual ones. Learn the standard results: two reflections in parallel mirrors a apart give a translation of 2a perpendicular to them, two reflections in mirrors crossing at angle θ give a rotation of 2θ about the crossing point, and two rotations about the same centre add their angles. The safe method in the exam is to follow the general point (x, y) through each stage and read off the single transformation from the rule you end with."},{"h":"The circle theorems and their proofs","code":"G10","t":"G10 is Higher only and asks you to apply and prove the standard circle theorems, and to use them to prove further results. The eight to know are: the angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference on the same arc; the angle in a semicircle is 90°; angles in the same segment are equal; opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral add to 180°; a tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact; the two tangents from an external point are equal; the perpendicular from the centre to a chord bisects the chord; and the alternate segment theorem. Every step of an angle chase must quote the theorem it rests on, because the reasoning marks are for the named property, not the arithmetic. The proofs are built from radii (which make isosceles triangles), the angle sum of a triangle and the angles at a point, so an unfamiliar 'prove that' question is usually one of these arguments run again on a new diagram."}],"examples":[{"title":"Enlarging a triangle by scale factor −2","setup":"Triangle ABC has vertices A(3, 1), B(5, 1) and C(3, 4) on a coordinate grid. The triangle is enlarged by scale factor −2 with centre of enlargement (1, 1). Work out the coordinates of the images A′, B′ and C′.","steps":["Measure from the centre of enlargement to A by subtracting coordinates: (3 − 1, 1 − 1) = (2, 0), a step of 2 right and 0 up.","Multiply that step by −2, which reverses its direction: (2, 0) × −2 = (−4, 0), so A′ = (1 − 4, 1 + 0) = (−3, 1).","Repeat for B: the step from the centre is (5 − 1, 1 − 1) = (4, 0), and × −2 gives (−8, 0), so B′ = (1 − 8, 1) = (−7, 1).","Repeat for C: the step from the centre is (3 − 1, 4 − 1) = (2, 3), and × −2 gives (−4, −6), so C′ = (1 − 4, 1 − 6) = (−3, −5).","So the images are A′(−3, 1), B′(−7, 1) and C′(−3, −5): the triangle has landed on the far side of (1, 1), turned upside down, with every length twice as long."],"cues":["How far across and up is A from the centre?","What does multiplying that step by a negative number do to its direction?","What step takes the centre to B?","What step takes the centre to C?","Which side of the centre has the image landed on, and how big is it?"]},{"title":"Describing an enlargement with a negative scale factor","setup":"Triangle P has vertices (4, 4), (8, 4) and (4, 10). Triangle Q has vertices (1, 1), (−1, 1) and (1, −2). A single enlargement maps triangle P onto triangle Q. Describe that enlargement fully.","steps":["Compare matching sides: the base of P runs from (4, 4) to (8, 4) and is 4 units long, while the base of Q runs from (1, 1) to (−1, 1) and is 2 units long, so every length has been halved.","Q is turned through 180° relative to P — its base points the other way and its third vertex is below the base instead of above it — so the scale factor is negative: −1/2.","Join each point to its image and extend: the line through (4, 4) and (1, 1) has gradient 1 and passes through the origin, so its equation is y = x.","The line through (8, 4) and (−1, 1) has gradient (4 − 1)/(8 + 1) = 1/3, so its equation is y = (x + 1)/3 + 1.","Solve the two together: x = (x + 1)/3 + 1 gives 3x = x + 4, so x = 2 and y = 2, and the joining lines cross at (2, 2).","The full description is therefore an enlargement, scale factor −1/2, centre (2, 2)."],"cues":["How do the lengths of the two bases compare?","Which way round is Q compared with P?","What can be drawn through a point and its image?","What is the gradient of the second joining line?","Where do the two joining lines cross?","Which three things must a full description give?"]},{"title":"Combining a rotation and a reflection","setup":"Triangle T has vertices A(1, 1), B(4, 1) and C(1, 3). T is rotated 90° clockwise about the origin to give triangle T′, and T′ is then reflected in the y axis to give triangle T″. Describe fully the single transformation that maps T onto T″, and say which points are invariant under it.","steps":["A 90° clockwise turn about the origin sends (x, y) to (y, −x), so A → (1, −1), B → (1, −4) and C → (3, −1).","Reflecting in the y axis changes the sign of the x coordinate, so those three vertices become (−1, −1), (−1, −4) and (−3, −1).","Compare start with finish: (1, 1) → (−1, −1), (4, 1) → (−1, −4) and (1, 3) → (−3, −1), so every point (x, y) has ended at (−y, −x).","Swapping the coordinates and changing both signs is exactly what a reflection in the line y = −x does, so the single transformation is a reflection in y = −x.","Under that reflection every point of the mirror line y = −x is invariant, and lengths and angles are unchanged, but the sense of the triangle has been reversed."],"cues":["Where does a 90° clockwise turn about the origin send a point?","What does reflecting in the y axis do to each coordinate?","What single rule takes each starting vertex to its final position?","Which transformation has the rule (x, y) → (−y, −x)?","Which points have not moved at all?"]},{"title":"An angle chase from a tangent to the circumference","setup":"A circle has centre O. The line TA is a tangent to the circle, touching it at A, and AB is a chord of the circle. Angle TAB = 55°. The point C lies on the major arc AB. Work out the size of angle ACB, giving a reason at each step.","steps":["A tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact, so angle OAT = 90° and angle OAB = 90° − 55° = 35°.","OA and OB are both radii, so triangle OAB is isosceles and its base angles are equal: angle OBA = 35°.","The angles of triangle OAB add to 180°, so angle AOB = 180° − 35° − 35° = 110°.","The angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference standing on the same arc AB, so angle ACB = 110° ÷ 2 = 55°.","Angle ACB has come out equal to the tangent–chord angle, which is the alternate segment theorem doing its work: the angle between a tangent and a chord equals the angle in the alternate segment."],"cues":["What angle does the tangent make with the radius OA?","Why must angle OBA equal angle OAB?","What do the three angles of triangle OAB add to?","How is the angle at O related to the angle at C?","What do you notice about angle ACB and angle TAB?"]},{"title":"Proving the angle in a semicircle is 90°","setup":"A, B and C are points on a circle with centre O. AB is a diameter, so AOB is a straight line, and the radii OA, OB and OC are all drawn. Prove that angle ACB is a right angle.","steps":["OA = OC because both are radii, so triangle OAC is isosceles; call each of its base angles x, giving angle OAC = angle OCA = x.","OB = OC for the same reason, so triangle OBC is isosceles; call each of its base angles y, giving angle OBC = angle OCB = y.","In triangle ABC the angle at A is x, the angle at B is y and the angle at C is angle OCA + angle OCB = x + y.","The angles of triangle ABC add to 180°, so x + y + (x + y) = 180°, that is 2x + 2y = 180°, and dividing by 2 gives x + y = 90°.","Angle ACB = x + y, so angle ACB = 90°: the angle in a semicircle is a right angle, as required."],"cues":["Which two sides of triangle OAC must be equal, and why?","Which other triangle in the diagram is isosceles?","What is the angle at C made up of?","What do the three angles of triangle ABC add to?","What does that make angle ACB?"]}],"check":[{"q":"The point P(6, 3) is enlarged by scale factor −2 with centre the origin. What are the coordinates of its image?","a":"(−12, −6)"},{"q":"Two mirror lines are parallel and 5 cm apart. A shape is reflected in one and the image is reflected in the other. What single transformation has the same effect?","a":"A translation of 10 cm perpendicular to the mirror lines, towards the second mirror (twice the gap between them)."},{"q":"A, B and C are points on a circle with centre O. Angle AOB = 80° and C is on the major arc AB. What is angle ACB, and which theorem gives it?","a":"40°; the angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference on the same arc."},{"q":"PQRS is a cyclic quadrilateral and angle PQR = 112°. What is angle PSR?","a":"68°"}]},"room":{"facts":["Enlargement, scale factor k, centre C: the image of P is C + k × (P − C) — take the step from the centre to the point, multiply it by k, and step out from the centre again.","A negative scale factor puts the image on the opposite side of the centre and turns it through 180°; every length is multiplied by the size of k, so −2 doubles lengths and −1/2 halves them.","A full description of an enlargement gives the word enlargement, the scale factor with its sign, and the centre; the centre is where the lines through each point and its image cross.","Two reflections in parallel mirrors a apart make a translation of 2a perpendicular to them; two reflections in mirrors crossing at angle θ make a rotation of 2θ about the crossing point; two rotations about the same centre add their angles.","Rotations, reflections and translations all preserve length, angle and area, so the final image is congruent to the object; invariant points are points that map to themselves, such as the centre of a rotation or any point of a mirror line.","Angle at the centre = 2 × angle at the circumference on the same arc; angle in a semicircle = 90°; angles in the same segment are equal; opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral add to 180°.","A tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact; the two tangents from an external point are equal in length; the perpendicular from the centre to a chord bisects the chord.","Alternate segment theorem: the angle between a tangent and a chord equals the angle subtended by that chord in the alternate segment."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) A, B and C are points on a circle with centre O. The angle AOB at the centre, standing on the minor arc AB, is 130°. The point C lies on the major arc AB. Work out the size of angle ACB.","a":"65°","sol":"Angle AOB and angle ACB stand on the same arc AB, and the angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference, so angle ACB = 130° ÷ 2 (M1). 130 ÷ 2 = 65, so angle ACB = 65° (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Both angles stand on the same arc AB — one is at the centre, the other at the circumference.","One circle theorem links an angle at the centre with an angle at the circumference on the same arc: one of them is double the other.","The line to write down is angle ACB = angle AOB ÷ 2."],"codes":["G10"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) ABCD is a cyclic quadrilateral. Angle ABC = 84°. Work out the size of angle ADC.","a":"96°","sol":"All four vertices lie on the circle, so ABC and ADC are opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral and add to 180°: angle ADC = 180° − 84° (M1). 180 − 84 = 96, so angle ADC = 96° (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Angle ABC and angle ADC are at opposite corners of a quadrilateral whose vertices all lie on the circle.","There is a circle theorem about what a pair of opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral must add to.","The line to write down is angle ADC = 180° − angle ABC."],"codes":["G10"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Triangle T has vertices (1, 2), (3, 2) and (1, 5). T is enlarged by scale factor −1 with centre the origin to give triangle T′. Write down the coordinates of the three vertices of T′.","a":"(−1, −2), (−3, −2) and (−1, −5)","sol":"With the origin as centre, an enlargement of scale factor k sends (x, y) to (kx, ky), so every coordinate is multiplied by −1 (M1). This gives (−1, −2), (−3, −2) and (−1, −5) (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A negative scale factor sends the image to the other side of the centre.","The centre is the origin, so each vertex is a step straight out from (0, 0) and that step is multiplied by the scale factor.","Write each vertex in the form (−1 × x, −1 × y)."],"codes":["G7"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A shape is reflected in the x axis, and the image is then reflected in the y axis. Describe fully the single transformation that maps the original shape onto the final image.","a":"A rotation of 180° about the origin","sol":"Reflecting in the x axis sends (x, y) to (x, −y), and reflecting that image in the y axis sends it to (−x, −y) (M1). The rule (x, y) → (−x, −y) is a half turn about the point where the mirrors cross, so the single transformation is a rotation of 180° about the origin (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The two mirror lines are not parallel — they cross.","Two reflections in mirrors that cross give a rotation about the crossing point, through twice the angle between the mirrors.","Write down the image of the general point (x, y) after the first reflection, then apply the second to it."],"codes":["G8"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A, B and C are points on a circle and AB is a diameter of the circle. Angle ABC = 34°. Work out the size of angle BAC, giving a reason for each step.","a":"56°","sol":"AB is a diameter and C is on the circle, so angle ACB = 90° — the angle in a semicircle is a right angle (B1). The angles of triangle ABC add to 180°, so angle BAC = 180° − 90° − 34° (M1). 180 − 90 − 34 = 56, so angle BAC = 56° (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Angle ACB is subtended by a diameter, and one circle theorem is about exactly that.","Once angle ACB is known, triangle ABC has two known angles.","After the semicircle theorem has fixed angle ACB, write angle BAC = 180° − angle ACB − angle ABC."],"codes":["G10"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) TA and TB are tangents to a circle with centre O, touching the circle at A and at B. Angle ATB = 48°. Work out the size of angle AOB.","a":"132°","sol":"A tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact, so angle OAT = angle OBT = 90° (B1). OATB is a quadrilateral, so its four angles add to 360°: angle AOB = 360° − 90° − 90° − 48° (M1). 360 − 228 = 132, so angle AOB = 132° (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Think about the angle a tangent makes with the radius drawn to the point where it touches.","OATB is a quadrilateral containing two right angles and the angle at T.","Write angle AOB = 360° − angle OAT − angle OBT − angle ATB."],"codes":["G10"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A circle has centre O and radius 13 cm. AB is a chord of the circle of length 24 cm, and M is the midpoint of AB. Work out the length of OM.","a":"5 cm","sol":"The perpendicular from the centre to a chord bisects the chord, so OM is perpendicular to AB and AM = 24 ÷ 2 = 12 cm (B1). Triangle OMA is right-angled at M with hypotenuse the radius OA, so by Pythagoras' theorem OM² = 13² − 12² = 169 − 144 = 25 (M1). OM = √25 = 5, so OM = 5 cm (A1).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Joining the centre to the midpoint of a chord creates a right angle.","That makes a right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is a radius and whose short side is half the chord.","Write OM² = OA² − AM², where OA is a radius and AM is the part of the chord lying on one side of M."],"codes":["G10"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Triangle A has vertices (2, 2), (6, 2) and (2, 4). Triangle B has vertices (−1, −1), (−3, −1) and (−1, −2). Describe fully the single transformation that maps triangle A onto triangle B.","a":"An enlargement, scale factor −1/2, centre (0, 0)","sol":"The base of A is 4 units long and the base of B is 2 units long, so every length has been halved, and B is turned through 180° relative to A, so the transformation is an enlargement (B1) with scale factor −1/2 (B1). Every vertex satisfies image = −1/2 × vertex, for example (2, 2) → (−1, −1) and (6, 2) → (−3, −1), and the lines joining each vertex to its image meet at the origin, so the centre is (0, 0) (B1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The image is smaller than the object and the wrong way up.","A pair of matching sides gives the size of the scale factor and the orientation gives its sign.","Write down the ratio of one side of B to the matching side of A, then join a vertex to its image and extend the line."],"codes":["G7"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A shape is reflected in the line x = 1, and the image is then reflected in the line x = 4. Describe fully the single transformation that maps the original shape onto the final image.","a":"A translation of 6 units in the positive x direction (column vector 6 across, 0 up)","sol":"The mirror lines are parallel, so the combination is a translation perpendicular to them of twice the distance between them: 2 × (4 − 1), or by tracking a point, x = 0 → x = 2 → x = 6 (M1). 2 × 3 = 6, so the transformation is a translation of 6 units in the positive x direction, column vector 6 across and 0 up (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The two mirror lines never meet.","Reflecting twice in parallel mirrors slides the shape, and the size of the slide depends only on the gap between the mirrors.","Take one point, say the point with x = 0, and write down where it lands after each reflection in turn."],"codes":["G8"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Shape S is rotated 90° anticlockwise about the origin to give shape T, and shape T is then rotated 90° anticlockwise about the origin to give shape U. Describe fully the single transformation that maps S onto U, and write down the coordinates of the only invariant point.","a":"A rotation of 180° about the origin; the invariant point is (0, 0)","sol":"A 90° anticlockwise turn about the origin sends (x, y) to (−y, x), so doing it twice sends (x, y) to (−y, x) and then to (−x, −y) (M1). Two rotations about the same centre make one rotation about that centre through the sum of the angles, 90° + 90° = 180°, so S is mapped onto U by a rotation of 180° about the origin (A1). The centre of a rotation is the only point that does not move, so the invariant point is (0, 0) (B1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Both turns are about the same point, so nothing needs to be redrawn twice.","Rotations about a common centre combine into one rotation by adding their angles.","Follow the general point (x, y) through the first rotation and write down where it goes."],"codes":["G8"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Triangle P has a vertex at (4, 1). Triangle P is enlarged by scale factor −3 with centre of enlargement (1, 2). Work out the coordinates of the image of the vertex (4, 1).","a":"(−8, 5)","sol":"The step from the centre to the vertex is (4 − 1, 1 − 2) = (3, −1) (M1). Multiplying that step by the scale factor −3 gives (−9, 3) (M1). Stepping out from the centre again: (1 − 9, 2 + 3) = (−8, 5), so the image is (−8, 5) (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The centre is not the origin, so the coordinates cannot simply be multiplied.","Work out the step from the centre to the point, scale that step, then take it again from the centre.","Write the step from the centre to the vertex as (4 − 1, 1 − 2)."],"codes":["G7"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A, B and C are points on a circle with centre O. Angle OAB = 28°, and C lies on the major arc AB. Work out the size of angle ACB, giving reasons.","a":"62°","sol":"OA = OB because both are radii, so triangle OAB is isosceles and its base angles are equal: angle OBA = 28° (B1). The angles of triangle OAB add to 180°, so angle AOB = 180° − 28° − 28° (M1) = 124° (A1). The angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference on the same arc AB, so angle ACB = 124° ÷ 2 = 62° (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Two sides of triangle OAB are radii of the circle, which makes that triangle a special one.","Find the angle at the centre first, then connect it to the angle at the circumference.","Write down what the two radii OA and OB tell you about angles OAB and OBA, then use the angle sum of triangle OAB."],"codes":["G10"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) ABCD is a cyclic quadrilateral drawn in a circle with centre O. Prove that angle ABC + angle ADC = 180°.","a":"2x + 2y = 360°, so x + y = 180°, where x = angle ABC and y = angle ADC","sol":"Let angle ABC = x and angle ADC = y, and draw the radii OA and OC (M1). The angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference on the same arc, so the angle AOC on the same side of AC as D is 2x (M1), and the angle AOC on the same side of AC as B is 2y (M1). Those two angles at O make a full turn, so 2x + 2y = 360°, and dividing by 2 gives x + y = 180°, that is angle ABC + angle ADC = 180° as required (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Give the two opposite angles letters, and draw the radii to the two vertices they both stand on.","The angle-at-the-centre theorem can be used twice, once for each of the two angles at O.","Write angle ABC = x and angle ADC = y, then write down each angle at the centre in terms of x or y."],"codes":["G10"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Shape P is enlarged by scale factor −2 with centre the origin to give shape Q. Shape Q is then rotated 180° about the origin to give shape R. Describe fully the single transformation that maps shape P onto shape R.","a":"An enlargement, scale factor 2, centre (0, 0)","sol":"The enlargement sends the general point (x, y) to (−2x, −2y) (M1). A rotation of 180° about the origin sends (−2x, −2y) to (2x, 2y) (M1). The rule (x, y) → (2x, 2y) doubles the step from the origin to every point without turning the shape, so P is mapped onto R by an enlargement of scale factor 2, centre (0, 0) (A1).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Follow a general point (x, y) through both stages rather than drawing anything.","An enlargement with a negative scale factor already contains a half turn, so a second half turn undoes the turning and leaves only the scaling.","Write down the image of (x, y) after the enlargement alone."],"codes":["G7","G8"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) PQRS is a cyclic quadrilateral. Angle PQR = (3x + 10)° and angle PSR = (2x − 5)°. Work out the size of angle PQR.","a":"115°","sol":"PQR and PSR are opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral, so they add to 180°: (3x + 10) + (2x − 5) = 180 (M1). Collecting like terms gives 5x + 5 = 180, so 5x = 175 (M1) and x = 35 (A1). Angle PQR = 3 × 35 + 10 = 115, so angle PQR = 115° (A1). (Check: angle PSR = 2 × 35 − 5 = 65 and 115 + 65 = 180.)","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The two angles given are at opposite corners of the cyclic quadrilateral.","The circle theorem turns the two expressions into an equation, which can then be solved for x.","Write (3x + 10) + (2x − 5) = 180 and collect the like terms on the left."],"codes":["G10"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A, B and C are points on a circle. The tangent to the circle at A makes an angle of 47° with the chord AB, and C lies in the alternate segment. Angle ABC = 65°. Work out the size of angle BAC.","a":"68°","sol":"By the alternate segment theorem the angle between the tangent and the chord AB equals the angle subtended by AB in the alternate segment, so angle ACB = 47° (B1). The angles of triangle ABC add to 180°, so angle BAC = 180° − 65° − 47° (M1). 180 − 112 = 68, so angle BAC = 68° (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The angle between a tangent and a chord is equal to an angle drawn on the circle itself.","Once that angle at C is known, triangle ABC has two known angles.","Write angle BAC = 180° − angle ABC − angle ACB."],"codes":["G10"]}],"exit":[{"q":"A and B are points on a circle with centre O, and C lies on the major arc AB. Angle AOB = 96°. Work out angle ACB.","a":"48°"},{"q":"The point (5, −2) is enlarged by scale factor −2 with centre the origin. What are the coordinates of the image?","a":"(−10, 4)"},{"q":"A shape is reflected in the line y = 2, and the image is reflected in the line y = 5. Describe fully the single transformation with the same effect.","a":"A translation of 6 units in the positive y direction (column vector 0 across, 6 up)"},{"q":"The tangent to a circle at A makes an angle of 40° with the chord AB, and C is a point in the alternate segment. Write down the size of angle ACB.","a":"40°"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Enlargement with a negative scale factor","code":"G7","open":false,"back":"The image lands on the opposite side of the centre, turned through 180°, with every length multiplied by the size of the scale factor: −2 doubles each length, −1/2 halves it."},{"front":"Image of a point under an enlargement, centre C, scale factor k","code":"G7","open":false,"back":"Take the step from C to the point, multiply that step by k, and step out from C again: image = C + k × (P − C)."},{"front":"Describing an enlargement fully","code":"G7","open":false,"back":"Three things are needed: the word enlargement, the scale factor with its sign, and the centre of enlargement as coordinates. Naming two transformations instead of a single one does not describe the mapping."},{"front":"Finding the centre of an enlargement","code":"G7","open":false,"back":"Join each point to its image with a straight line and extend; all such lines meet at the centre. With a negative scale factor the centre lies between the object and the image."},{"front":"Give one way to tell that the scale factor of an enlargement is negative","code":"G7","open":true,"back":"The image is on the opposite side of the centre from the object; the image is turned upside down, a half turn relative to the object; the line joining a point to its image passes through the centre between them."},{"front":"Invariant point and invariant line","code":"G8","open":false,"back":"An invariant point maps to itself; an invariant line is one whose points all stay on that line. The centre of a rotation is invariant, as is every point of a mirror line."},{"front":"Two reflections combined","code":"G8","open":false,"back":"In parallel mirror lines a apart: a translation of 2a perpendicular to them. In mirror lines crossing at angle θ: a rotation of 2θ about the crossing point."},{"front":"Two rotations about the same centre","code":"G8","open":false,"back":"They combine into a single rotation about that centre through the sum of the angles: 90° + 90° anticlockwise gives 180°, and the centre stays invariant throughout."},{"front":"Angle at the centre of a circle","code":"G10","open":false,"back":"The angle subtended at the centre by an arc is twice the angle subtended at the circumference by the same arc."},{"front":"Angle in a semicircle","code":"G10","open":false,"back":"The angle subtended at the circumference by a diameter is 90°, so a triangle drawn on a diameter with its third vertex on the circle is right-angled."},{"front":"Angles in the same segment","code":"G10","open":false,"back":"Angles subtended at the circumference by the same arc, on the same side of that arc, are equal to one another."},{"front":"Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral","code":"G10","open":false,"back":"In a quadrilateral whose four vertices all lie on a circle, each pair of opposite angles adds to 180°."},{"front":"Tangent facts in a circle","code":"G10","open":false,"back":"A tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact, and the two tangents drawn from a point outside the circle are equal in length."},{"front":"Perpendicular from the centre to a chord","code":"G10","open":false,"back":"The perpendicular from the centre of a circle to a chord bisects that chord, so the centre, the midpoint of the chord and a right angle always go together."},{"front":"Alternate segment theorem","code":"G10","open":false,"back":"The angle between a tangent and a chord drawn from the point of contact equals the angle subtended by that chord in the alternate segment."}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["The Higher Tier Exam Aid prints no circle theorem and no transformation rule, so every result in this room has to be remembered; the sheet's geometry is limited to areas, volumes, Pythagoras and trigonometry.","Remember: image of P under an enlargement of scale factor k, centre C, is C + k × (P − C); a negative k puts the image on the opposite side of C and turns it through 180° (G7).","Remember: a full description of an enlargement = the word enlargement + the scale factor with its sign + the centre (G7).","Remember: two reflections in parallel mirrors a apart = a translation of 2a perpendicular to them; two reflections in mirrors crossing at θ = a rotation of 2θ about the crossing point; two rotations about one centre add their angles (G8).","Remember: angle at the centre = 2 × angle at the circumference on the same arc (G10).","Remember: the angle in a semicircle is 90° (G10).","Remember: angles in the same segment are equal (G10).","Remember: opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral add to 180° (G10).","Remember: a tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact, and the two tangents from an external point are equal in length (G10).","Remember: the perpendicular from the centre of a circle to a chord bisects the chord (G10).","Remember: the alternate segment theorem — the angle between a tangent and a chord equals the angle in the alternate segment (G10).","Printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid and worth using alongside this room: a² + b² = c² in any right-angled triangle where c is the hypotenuse (used with the chord-bisecting theorem), circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd and area of a circle = πr², where r is the radius and d the diameter.","Also printed on the Higher Tier Exam Aid but not needed here: area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, volume of a prism = area of cross section × length, the quadratic formula, the three trigonometric ratios, the sine and cosine rules, area of a triangle = ½ab sin C, total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B) and P(A and B) = P(A given B)P(B)."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T12:13:24.827928Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Mathematically clean: all 16 bank questions, 4 exit-ticket items and 4 lesson check questions re-solved independently (coordinate models for the enlargements and combined transformations, and arc/inscribed-angle models for every circle chase) and every answer key, including the angle configurations in Q4, Q11 and Q15, came out exactly as the kit states; the Higher Exam Aid claims in the Formulae sheet are also correct item for item, so the only work left is tightening the cyclic-quadrilateral proof's answer key and reflex-angle wording."}}},"geometry-mensuration-higher":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"geometry-mensuration-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Geometry and measures carries 17–23% of the Higher tier marks, and these four additions are where most of them sit at grades 6–9. The sine rule, the cosine rule and Area = ½ab sin C are bold, Higher-only content: a Foundation student never meets them, so they separate the grades. The similar-figures scale factors and trigonometry inside a solid turn up again inside volume, ratio and problem-solving questions, so a Higher paper rarely asks for them on their own — it hides them inside a longer AO2 or AO3 question where you must choose the rule for yourself.","idea":[{"h":"Area and volume in similar figures","t":"The Higher list extends G19 to 'the relationships between lengths, areas and volumes in similar figures'. If the lengths of two similar figures are in the ratio a : b, then every pair of corresponding areas — surface area, cross section, a painted face — is in the ratio a² : b², and the volumes are in the ratio a³ : b³. The scale factors are linked, so an area ratio of 9 : 16 gives a length ratio of 3 : 4 by square rooting, and a volume ratio of 27 : 64 by cubing. Exams set this both ways: forwards from a length ratio, and backwards from a given area or volume ratio, often with a mass or capacity that scales with volume.","code":"G19"},{"h":"Trigonometry in general triangles and in three dimensions","t":"The Higher list extends G20 to right-angled triangles 'and, where possible, general triangles in two and three dimensional figures'. In two dimensions that means a triangle with no right angle is still open to you: drop a perpendicular from a vertex to split it into two right-angled triangles, or reach for the sine and cosine rules of G22 when no convenient split exists. In three dimensions the method is always the same: find a triangle inside the solid — right-angled where you can, general where you cannot — and draw it as a flat triangle on its own. Pythagoras' theorem applied twice gives the diagonal of a cuboid, √(a² + b² + c²), because the base diagonal becomes one of the two shorter sides of the second triangle. The angle between a line and a plane is the angle between the line and its shadow on that plane — drop a perpendicular to the plane, and the foot of that perpendicular, the point where the line meets the plane and the point directly above it form the triangle you need. A general triangle inside a solid, such as a sloping face of a pyramid, is handled the same way: draw it flat, then use the cosine rule or ½ab sin C on it.","code":"G20"},{"h":"The sine rule and the cosine rule","t":"G22 is bold, Higher-only content: 'know and apply the sine rule a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C, and cosine rule a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A, to find unknown lengths and angles'. The lower-case letter is the side opposite the capital-letter angle. Use the sine rule when you have a matched pair — a side and the angle opposite it — plus one more side or angle. Use the cosine rule when you have three sides (rearranged as cos A = (b² + c² − a²)/(2bc)) or two sides and the angle between them. A negative cosine means the angle is obtuse, and when the sine rule gives an angle there may be a second, obtuse answer, 180° − the first, so the question must tell you which one to take.","code":"G22"},{"h":"The area of any triangle","t":"G23 is bold, Higher-only content: 'know and apply Area = ½ ab sin C to calculate the area, sides or angles of any triangle'. The two lengths a and b must be the sides enclosing the angle C — the included angle — so no perpendicular height is needed. Rearranged, the same formula finds a side, b = 2 × Area ÷ (a sin C), or the included angle, sin C = 2 × Area ÷ (ab). Since sin(180° − x) = sin x, the angle found from a given area has an acute and an obtuse possibility, and a question that wants the obtuse one will say so. It pairs naturally with the cosine rule: find the included angle from the area, then the third side.","code":"G23"}],"examples":[{"title":"Volume from a length ratio in similar cones","setup":"Two mathematically similar cones have heights in the ratio 2 : 5. The smaller cone has a volume of 56 cm³. Work out the volume of the larger cone.","steps":["Volumes of similar solids scale as the cube of the length ratio, so the volume ratio is 2³ : 5³.","2³ : 5³ = 8 : 125 — this cubing is the line the mark scheme awards the method mark for.","The 8 parts stand for 56 cm³, so one part is 56 ÷ 8 = 7 cm³.","The larger cone is 125 parts: 125 × 7 = 875, so its volume is 875 cm³."],"cues":["Do volumes scale by the length ratio itself, or by some power of it?","What does the ratio 2³ : 5³ simplify to?","What is one part of that ratio worth in cm³?","How many parts does the larger cone have, and what is its volume?"]},{"title":"The angle between a cuboid's diagonal and its base","setup":"A cuboid measures 3 cm by 4 cm by 12 cm, with the 12 cm edges vertical. Work out the angle that a diagonal of the cuboid makes with the base, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Start on the base: its diagonal is the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle with legs 3 cm and 4 cm, so it is √(3² + 4²) = √25 = 5 cm.","The base diagonal, the vertical edge and the cuboid's diagonal form a right-angled triangle, with the right angle where the vertical edge meets the base.","In that triangle the vertical edge is opposite the angle and the base diagonal is adjacent, so tan θ = 12/5.","tan θ = 2.4, so θ = tan⁻¹(2.4) = 67.38…°, which is 67.4° to 1 decimal place."],"cues":["Which flat triangle inside the solid can you work out first?","Which three lines make the right-angled triangle containing the angle you want?","Which ratio uses the opposite and the adjacent sides?","What angle has that tangent?"]},{"title":"A side by the sine rule","setup":"In triangle ABC, angle A = 47°, angle B = 62° and side c = AB = 8.5 cm. Side a is BC. Work out the length of a, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Side c is opposite angle C, which is not given, so find it first: C = 180° − 47° − 62° = 71°.","Now a pairs with A and c pairs with C, so the sine rule gives a/sin 47° = 8.5/sin 71°.","Multiply both sides by sin 47°: a = 8.5 × sin 47° ÷ sin 71°.","a = 6.574…, so a = 6.6 cm to 1 decimal place."],"cues":["Which angle is opposite the side you have been given, and do you know it yet?","Which two side–angle pairs go into the sine rule here?","How do you make a the subject of that equation?","What is the value of a to 1 decimal place?"]},{"title":"The largest angle from three sides","setup":"A triangle has sides of length 6 cm, 9 cm and 11 cm. Work out the size of its largest angle, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["The largest angle lies opposite the longest side, so label a = 11 cm, with b = 6 cm and c = 9 cm enclosing the angle A that is wanted.","Three sides and no angle means the cosine rule, rearranged: cos A = (b² + c² − a²)/(2bc) = (36 + 81 − 121)/(2 × 6 × 9).","cos A = −4/108 = −0.0370…, and a negative cosine tells us the angle is obtuse.","A = cos⁻¹(−0.0370…) = 92.12…°, which is 92.1° to 1 decimal place."],"cues":["Which side is the largest angle opposite?","With three sides and no angles, which rule applies, and in which form?","What does the sign of the cosine tell you about the angle?","What is the angle to 1 decimal place?"]},{"title":"An included angle from a given area","setup":"A triangle has two sides of length 8 cm and 11 cm. The angle between these two sides is acute, and the area of the triangle is 35 cm². Work out the size of that angle, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["The two lengths enclose the unknown angle, so Area = ½ab sin C gives ½ × 8 × 11 × sin C = 35.","½ × 8 × 11 = 44, so the equation is 44 sin C = 35.","Divide by 44: sin C = 35/44 = 0.7954…","C = sin⁻¹(0.7954…) = 52.69…°, which is 52.7° to 1 decimal place; this is the acute answer the question asks for."],"cues":["Which area formula works when no perpendicular height is given?","What does the constant part of the left-hand side come to?","How do you leave sin C on its own?","Which angle has that sine, and is it the one asked for?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Two similar solids have lengths in the ratio 5 : 2. What is the ratio of their surface areas?","a":"25 : 4"},{"q":"A triangle has sides 4 cm and 7 cm with an included angle of 90°. Use Area = ½ab sin C to find its area.","a":"14 cm² (because sin 90° = 1)"},{"q":"You are given all three sides of a triangle and asked for one of its angles. Which rule do you use?","a":"The cosine rule, rearranged as cos A = (b² + c² − a²)/(2bc)"},{"q":"What does it mean if the cosine rule gives cos A = −0.6?","a":"Angle A is obtuse; A = 126.9° to 1 d.p."}]},"room":{"facts":["In similar figures with lengths in the ratio a : b, areas are in the ratio a² : b² and volumes are in the ratio a³ : b³.","From an area ratio, square root to get the length ratio; from a volume ratio, cube root to get the length ratio.","The diagonal of a cuboid with edges a, b and c is √(a² + b² + c²) — Pythagoras' theorem used twice.","The angle between a line and a plane is the angle between the line and its projection (its shadow) on that plane.","Sine rule: a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C — use it with a matched side–angle pair.","Cosine rule: a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A, rearranged for an angle as cos A = (b² + c² − a²)/(2bc).","Area of any triangle = ½ab sin C, where C is the angle included between sides a and b.","sin(180° − x) = sin x, so an angle found from a sine may have an obtuse second value; a negative cosine always means an obtuse angle."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Two mathematically similar bottles have heights in the ratio 2 : 3. Write down the ratio of their volumes.","a":"8 : 27","sol":"Volumes of similar solids are in the ratio of the cubes of the lengths, so the ratio is 2³ : 3³ = 8 : 27 (B1).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The bottles are similar, so one scale factor links every length on them.","Areas and volumes do not scale by the length ratio itself — each uses a power of it.","Write the ratio as 2 to the power ? : 3 to the power ?, choosing the power that volume needs."],"codes":["G19"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Shape B is an enlargement of the mathematically similar shape A with a length scale factor of 4. The area of shape A is 5 cm². Work out the area of shape B.","a":"80 cm²","sol":"Area scale factor = (length scale factor)² = 4² = 16 (M1). Area of B = 5 × 16 = 80 cm² (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Doubling every length does not double the area — think what happens to a 1 cm square.","Square the length scale factor to get the area scale factor, then multiply.","Work out 4² first, then use it on the 5 cm²."],"codes":["G19"]},{"q":"A triangle has two sides of length 6.2 cm and 9.5 cm, and the angle between them is 47°. Work out the area of the triangle, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"21.5 cm²","sol":"The angle is the one included between the two given sides, so Area = ½ab sin C = ½ × 6.2 × 9.5 × sin 47° (M1). = 29.45 × 0.73135… = 21.538… = 21.5 cm² (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["No perpendicular height is given, but the angle sits between the two sides you have.","Use the formula for the area of any triangle, with the two sides and the angle enclosed by them.","Write down ½ × 6.2 × 9.5 × sin of the given angle."],"codes":["G23"]},{"q":"A triangular sail ABC has angle ABC = 35° and angle ACB = 84°. The side AC is 6.9 m long. Work out the length of AB, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"12.0 m","sol":"AC = b is opposite angle B and AB = c is opposite angle C, so the sine rule gives c/sin 84° = 6.9/sin 35° (M1). Rearranging, c = 6.9 × sin 84° ÷ sin 35° (M1). c = 11.963… = 12.0 m (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The sail has a side and the angle facing it, together with one more angle — that is the pattern one of the two triangle rules needs.","Pair each side with the angle opposite it and use the sine rule.","Write AB/sin(angle ACB) = AC/sin(angle ABC) with the values in place, then make AB the subject."],"codes":["G22"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A cuboid measures 2 cm by 6 cm by 9 cm. Work out the length of a diagonal of the cuboid.","a":"11 cm","sol":"The diagonal of the 2 cm by 6 cm base is √(2² + 6²) = √40 (M1). That base diagonal and the 9 cm edge are the two shorter sides of a right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is the cuboid's diagonal, so the diagonal² = 40 + 9² = 40 + 81 = 121 (M1). Diagonal = √121 = 11 cm (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["A diagonal of a cuboid is the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle standing on a diagonal of the base.","Use Pythagoras' theorem twice: first across the base, then up to the opposite corner.","There is no need to simplify the surd from the base: square it straight back at the second stage."],"codes":["G20"]},{"q":"In triangle PQR, PQ = 7.4 cm, QR = 9.1 cm and angle PQR = 108°. Work out the length of PR, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"13.4 cm","sol":"Two sides and the included angle are known, so use the cosine rule with PR opposite the 108° angle: PR² = 7.4² + 9.1² − 2 × 7.4 × 9.1 × cos 108° (M1). = 54.76 + 82.81 − 134.68 × (−0.30901…) = 137.57 + 41.61… = 179.188… (M1). PR = √179.188… = 13.386… = 13.4 cm (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The angle you are given lies between the two sides you are given, and the side you want is opposite it.","That is the pattern for the cosine rule, not the sine rule — there is no matched side–angle pair.","Write PR² = PQ² + QR² − 2 × PQ × QR × cos(angle PQR) before substituting anything."],"codes":["G22"]},{"q":"In triangle PQR, PQ = 7 cm, QR = 9 cm and PR = 12 cm. Work out the size of angle PQR, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"96.4°","sol":"Angle PQR is opposite PR = 12 cm, so take a = 12, b = 7 and c = 9 and use the cosine rule rearranged: cos Q = (7² + 9² − 12²)/(2 × 7 × 9) (M1). = (49 + 81 − 144)/126 = −14/126 = −0.1111… (M1). Q = cos⁻¹(−0.1111…) = 96.379… = 96.4° (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["All three sides are known and no angle is, so only one of the two triangle rules can start you off.","Rearrange the cosine rule to make the cosine of the angle the subject.","The side opposite angle PQR must take the place of a: write cos Q = (b² + c² − a²)/(2bc) with the right side in each slot."],"codes":["G22"]},{"q":"A vertical mast stands at corner A of a horizontal rectangular field ABCD, where AB = 40 m and BC = 30 m. From corner C the angle of elevation of the top of the mast is 18°. Work out the height of the mast, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"16.2 m","sol":"The mast stands at A, so the horizontal distance from C to its foot is the diagonal AC = √(40² + 30²) = √2500 (M1) = 50 m (A1). The mast, that diagonal and the line of sight from C form a right-angled triangle standing in a vertical plane, so tan 18° = h/50 (M1). h = 50 × tan 18° = 50 × 0.32491… = 16.245… = 16.2 m (A1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The angle of elevation is measured at C, so the right-angled triangle you need stands on the line from C to the foot of the mast.","Find that horizontal distance from the rectangle first, then use the angle of elevation with the tangent ratio.","Apply Pythagoras' theorem to the two sides of the rectangle before any trigonometry."],"codes":["G20"]},{"q":"A triangle has an area of 40 cm². One of the sides enclosing an angle of 38° is 12 cm long. Work out the length of the other side enclosing that angle, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"10.8 cm","sol":"Using Area = ½ab sin C with a = 12 and C = 38°: ½ × 12 × b × sin 38° = 40 (M1). So b = 2 × 40 ÷ (12 × sin 38°) = 80 ÷ 7.3879… (M1) = 10.828… = 10.8 cm (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The 38° angle sits between the known side and the side you want.","Put what you know into the area formula for any triangle and solve the equation for the missing side.","Write ½ × 12 × b × sin 38° = area, then make b the subject."],"codes":["G23"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Two mathematically similar solid statues have surface areas of 45 cm² and 80 cm². The smaller statue has a volume of 54 cm³. Work out the volume of the larger statue.","a":"128 cm³","sol":"Area ratio = 45 : 80 = 9 : 16 (M1). Square rooting each part, the length ratio is 3 : 4 (M1). Cubing, the volume ratio is 27 : 64, so the volume of the larger statue = 54 ÷ 27 × 64 (M1) = 2 × 64 = 128 cm³ (A1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Areas and volumes cannot be compared directly — both are tied to the same length ratio.","Work back from the area ratio to the length ratio, then forward to the volume ratio.","Simplify 45 : 80 by dividing both parts by 5 before taking any roots."],"codes":["G19"]},{"q":"In triangle ABC, BC = 14 cm, AC = 9 cm and angle A = 58°. Work out the size of angle B, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"33.0°","sol":"BC = a = 14 is opposite A and AC = b = 9 is opposite B, a matched pair, so the sine rule gives sin B/9 = sin 58°/14 (M1). sin B = 9 × sin 58° ÷ 14 = 0.5451… (M1). B = sin⁻¹(0.5451…) = 33.036… = 33.0° (A1). B is acute because b < a, so B < A.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["One side is given together with the angle facing it, which sets up a matched pair.","Use the sine rule with the sines on top when you are looking for an angle.","Write sin B/AC = sin A/BC before putting any numbers in."],"codes":["G22"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) In triangle ABC, AB = 5 cm, AC = 8 cm and angle BAC = 60°. Work out the exact length of BC.","a":"7 cm","sol":"Two sides and the included angle are known, so BC² = 5² + 8² − 2 × 5 × 8 × cos 60° (M1). cos 60° = ½, so BC² = 25 + 64 − 80 × ½ = 89 − 40 = 49 (M1). BC = √49 = 7 cm (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The angle given lies between the two given sides, so no matched side–angle pair is available.","Use the cosine rule, and recall the exact value of the cosine of the angle rather than reaching for a decimal.","Write BC² = AB² + AC² − 2 × AB × AC × cos 60° before substituting."],"codes":["G22"]},{"q":"A triangular plot of land has two sides of length 28 m and 22 m. The angle between these two sides is obtuse and the area of the plot is 250 m². Work out the length of the third side of the plot, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"44.6 m","sol":"Area = ½ab sin C gives ½ × 28 × 22 × sin C = 250 (M1), so sin C = 500/616 = 0.81168… (M1). sin⁻¹(0.81168…) = 54.2612…°, and since C is obtuse, C = 180° − 54.2612…° = 125.7387…°, that is 125.7° to 1 d.p. (A1). Now the cosine rule, keeping the angle unrounded (cos C = −0.58409…): third side² = 28² + 22² − 2 × 28 × 22 × cos 125.7387…° = 784 + 484 + 719.59… = 1987.59… (M1). Third side = √1987.59… = 44.5825… = 44.6 m (A1).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The area ties the two given sides to the angle between them; once that angle is known, the triangle is fully determined.","Find the included angle from the area formula first, then use the cosine rule for the third side.","Write ½ × 28 × 22 × sin C = area and make sin C the subject, remembering that a second, obtuse angle shares that sine."],"codes":["G23","G22"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A solid cone has a height of 24 cm and a volume of 864 cm³. A cut is made parallel to the base, 8 cm from the vertex, and the small cone above the cut is removed. Work out the volume of the frustum that is left.","a":"832 cm³","sol":"The small cone is similar to the whole cone, with heights in the ratio 8 : 24 = 1 : 3 (M1). Volumes are in the ratio 1³ : 3³ = 1 : 27, so the small cone has volume 864 ÷ 27 (M1) = 32 cm³ (A1). Frustum = 864 − 32 = 832 cm³ (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The piece cut off is not a new shape to measure — it is a smaller copy of the original cone.","Find the length ratio of the two cones, use it to get the volume of the small cone, then subtract.","Simplify the ratio of the two heights, 8 : 24, before doing anything with volumes."],"codes":["G19"]},{"q":"A solid pyramid has a square base of side 10 cm and four equal slant edges of length 13 cm. Work out the angle between a slant edge and the base, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"57.0°","sol":"The slant edge meets the base at a corner, and the apex is vertically above the centre of the square, so the required triangle has the slant edge as hypotenuse and half a base diagonal as the side adjacent to the angle. Base diagonal = √(10² + 10²) = √200 = 14.142… cm (M1), so half of it is 7.071… cm (A1). cos θ = 7.071…/13 = 0.5439… (M1). θ = cos⁻¹(0.5439…) = 57.048… = 57.0° (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The apex sits directly above the centre of the square base, so the foot of the vertical is the centre.","The slant edge, its shadow on the base and the vertical height form a right-angled triangle; that shadow is half a diagonal of the square.","Find the full diagonal of the square base with Pythagoras' theorem before halving it."],"codes":["G20"]},{"q":"Two ships leave port P at the same time. Ship A sails 12 km on a bearing of 040°. Ship B sails 9 km on a bearing of 145°. Work out the distance between the two ships, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"16.8 km","sol":"The angle APB between the two courses is 145° − 40° = 105° (M1). Two sides and the included angle are known, so AB² = 12² + 9² − 2 × 12 × 9 × cos 105° (M1) = 144 + 81 − 216 × (−0.25881…) = 225 + 55.90… = 280.90… (M1). AB = √280.90… = 16.760… = 16.8 km (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Sketch the two courses from the port; the bearings are both measured clockwise from the same north line.","Find the angle at the port between the two tracks, then use the rule for two sides and the angle between them.","Subtract the smaller bearing from the larger to get the angle at P."],"codes":["G22"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Two similar solids have lengths in the ratio 3 : 4. Write down the ratio of their volumes.","a":"27 : 64"},{"q":"A triangle has sides of 9 cm and 4 cm with an included angle of 30°. Work out its area.","a":"9 cm²"},{"q":"In triangle ABC, a = 10 cm, angle A = 50° and angle B = 35°. Work out the length of b, correct to 1 decimal place.","a":"7.5 cm"},{"q":"Work out the length of the longest straight rod that will fit inside a cuboid measuring 1 cm by 4 cm by 8 cm.","a":"9 cm"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Area scale factor of two similar figures","back":"If the lengths are in the ratio a : b, the areas are in the ratio a² : b². The area scale factor is the square of the length scale factor.","code":"G19","open":false},{"front":"Volume scale factor of two similar solids","back":"If the lengths are in the ratio a : b, the volumes are in the ratio a³ : b³. Mass and capacity scale with volume when the material is the same.","code":"G19","open":false},{"front":"Getting the length ratio back from an area or a volume ratio","back":"Square root each part of an area ratio; cube root each part of a volume ratio. So 9 : 16 gives lengths 3 : 4, and 8 : 125 gives lengths 2 : 5.","code":"G19","open":false},{"front":"The sine rule","back":"a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C, where each lower-case side is opposite the capital-letter angle. Turn it upside down, sin A/a = sin B/b, when hunting an angle.","code":"G22","open":false},{"front":"The cosine rule for a side","back":"a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A, where A is the angle enclosed by sides b and c and a is the side opposite it.","code":"G22","open":false},{"front":"The cosine rule rearranged for an angle","back":"cos A = (b² + c² − a²)/(2bc). Use it when all three sides are known; a is always the side opposite the angle being found.","code":"G22","open":false},{"front":"The two cases that need the cosine rule","back":"Three sides and no angle, or two sides and the angle included between them. Both lack a matched side–angle pair, so the sine rule cannot start.","code":"G22","open":false},{"front":"The case that needs the sine rule","back":"A side together with the angle opposite it — a matched pair — plus one more side or angle. Find a missing third angle by subtracting from 180° first if needed.","code":"G22","open":false},{"front":"The ambiguous case of the sine rule","back":"sin(180° − x) = sin x, so an angle found from its sine has an acute and an obtuse value. Take the obtuse one only when the question or the diagram says the angle is obtuse.","code":"G22","open":false},{"front":"What a negative cosine means for an angle","back":"cos A < 0 means A is obtuse, between 90° and 180°. It is a useful check after the cosine rule: the largest angle of a triangle is the one opposite the longest side.","code":"G22","open":false},{"front":"Area of any triangle","back":"Area = ½ab sin C, where a and b are two sides and C is the angle included between them. No perpendicular height is needed.","code":"G23","open":false},{"front":"The included angle","back":"The angle enclosed by the two sides used in a formula — the angle whose arms are those two sides. ½ab sin C and the cosine rule both fail if the angle is not the included one.","code":"G23","open":false},{"front":"Rearranging Area = ½ab sin C","back":"For a side: b = 2 × Area ÷ (a sin C). For the included angle: sin C = 2 × Area ÷ (ab), then take the inverse sine, choosing the acute or obtuse value as the question states.","code":"G23","open":false},{"front":"The diagonal of a cuboid","back":"For edges a, b and c the diagonal is √(a² + b² + c²): Pythagoras' theorem across the base, then again from the base diagonal up to the opposite corner.","code":"G20","open":false},{"front":"Give one way to find the angle between a line and a plane in a solid","back":"Use the line's projection on the plane, so the angle sits in a right-angled triangle; drop a perpendicular from the far end of the line to the plane and work in that triangle; in a pyramid, join the apex to the centre of the base and use the half-diagonal.","code":"G20","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["How to read this sheet: every result below is tagged Printed — given to you in the exam, on the Higher Tier Exam Aid inserted in each paper unless the item says otherwise — or Remember, meaning it is not given and you must know it. The Exam Aid also prints the quadratic formula, compound interest and the probability results, which belong to other rooms, and it never tells you which rule to choose.","Printed: sine rule a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C, in any triangle ABC where a, b and c are the lengths of the sides (G22).","Printed: cosine rule a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A, in any triangle ABC (G22). The rearrangement cos A = (b² + c² − a²)/(2bc) is not printed, so you must do that rearranging yourself.","Printed: Area of triangle = ½ab sin C (G23). Which angle counts as C — the included one — is not printed with it.","Printed: Pythagoras' theorem a² + b² = c², in any right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is c (G20).","Printed: sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b in a right-angled triangle ABC with hypotenuse c — that is, sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse and tan θ = opposite/adjacent (G20).","Printed: area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, where a and b are the lengths of the parallel sides and h their perpendicular separation.","Printed: volume of a prism = area of cross section × length.","Printed: circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d the diameter.","Printed: area of a circle = πr².","Remember: in similar figures with lengths a : b, areas are in the ratio a² : b² and volumes in the ratio a³ : b³ (G19). Nothing about similarity appears on the Exam Aid.","Remember: to go back from an area ratio to the length ratio, square root each part; from a volume ratio, cube root each part (G19).","Remember: the diagonal of a cuboid with edges a, b and c is √(a² + b² + c²), which is Pythagoras' theorem used twice (G20).","Remember: the angle between a line and a plane is the angle between the line and its projection on that plane (G20).","Remember: sin(180° − x) = sin x, the identity behind the ambiguous case of the sine rule (G22).","Remember: the exact values sin 30° = 1/2, cos 60° = 1/2, sin 45° = cos 45° = √2/2, sin 60° = cos 30° = √3/2 and tan 45° = 1 — the Exam Aid does not print them, and Paper 1 expects them (G21).","Remember: area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height — not on the Exam Aid.","Remember: volume of a cylinder = πr²h — not on the Exam Aid.","Remember: volume of a cuboid = length × width × height — not on the Exam Aid.","Printed: volume of a cone = 1/3 πr²h — given inside the question when it is needed, not on the Exam Aid (Appendix 3 of the specification).","Printed: curved surface area of a cone = πrl — given inside the question when it is needed, not on the Exam Aid (Appendix 3).","Printed: volume of a sphere = 4/3 πr³ — given inside the question when it is needed, not on the Exam Aid (Appendix 3).","Printed: surface area of a sphere = 4πr² — given inside the question when it is needed, not on the Exam Aid (Appendix 3)."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T12:10:15Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Mathematically clean and properly Higher-tier: all 16 bank questions, the 4 exit-ticket questions and the 4 check questions re-solve to exactly the printed keys, in the form and accuracy asked, with six well-chosen non-calculator items; only the formulae sheet's presentation (prose first item, bundled entry, a G21 result tagged G22/G23) and one prematurely rounded solution line need tidying before publishing."}}}} };
